window.CCDV = {"blueprint":{"exam":{"name":"Claude Certified Architect – Foundations","code":"CCA-F","version":"Foundations","effective":"March 2026","items":60,"minutes":120,"format":"Multiple choice — every item has one correct answer and three plausible distractors; unanswered items are scored as incorrect and there is no penalty for guessing","scenarioFormat":"Scenario-based: 4 of the 6 published scenarios are drawn at random for each sitting, and each scenario frames a group of items","delivery":"Proctored; registration through Anthropic Academy (Skilljar) for Claude Partner Network organisations","passing":"Scaled score of 720 on a scale of 100–1,000","fee":"Reported as $99 USD per attempt (complimentary for the first 5,000 Claude Partner Network employees at launch) — confirm on the Academy listing","validity":"See the current Certification Terms on Anthropic Academy","reporting":"Pass/fail with a scaled score (100–1,000)","retakes":"See the current Certification Exam Policy on Anthropic Academy","renewal":null,"scenariosPerExam":4,"scenarios":[{"id":"S1","title":"Customer Support Resolution Agent","text":"You are building a customer support resolution agent with the Claude Agent SDK. It handles high-ambiguity requests — returns, billing disputes, account issues — through custom MCP tools such as get_customer, lookup_order, process_refund and escalate_to_human. The goal is 80%+ first-contact resolution while knowing when to escalate to a human."},{"id":"S2","title":"Code Generation with Claude Code","text":"Your team uses Claude Code to generate code, refactor and write documentation. You configure CLAUDE.md files, custom slash commands and skills for the team, and decide when to use plan mode versus direct execution."},{"id":"S3","title":"Multi-Agent Research System","text":"You are building a research system with the Claude Agent SDK in which a coordinator agent delegates to specialized subagents — web search, document analysis, synthesis and report generation — and produces comprehensive reports with citations."},{"id":"S4","title":"Developer Productivity with Claude","text":"You are building developer productivity tooling with the Claude Agent SDK. The agent helps engineers explore unfamiliar codebases, understand legacy systems, generate boilerplate and automate repetitive tasks using the built-in tools (Read, Write, Edit, Bash, Grep, Glob) and MCP servers."},{"id":"S5","title":"Claude Code for CI/CD","text":"You are integrating Claude Code into a CI/CD pipeline for automated code review, test generation and pull-request feedback. The output must be actionable, machine-parseable where needed, and low on false positives."},{"id":"S6","title":"Structured Data Extraction","text":"You are building a system that extracts information from unstructured documents (contracts, invoices, papers, forms), validates it against JSON schemas, handles edge cases gracefully and feeds downstream systems with high accuracy."}]},"ui":{"track":"architect","storagePrefix":"ccar:","siteTitle":"CCA-F Study Guide","skill":"Task","skills":"tasks","guideLabel":"Task statement — what the exam measures","heroEyebrow":"Claude Certification Program · Claude Partner Network · Launched March 2026 · Exam code CCA-F","heroTitle":"Claude Certified Architect – Foundations: everything you need to pass.","heroLead":"A complete prep track built on the published blueprint: lessons for all 30 task statements, {questions} original scenario-based questions, drill mode, flashcards, a diagnostic and timed 60-question mock exams that draw 4 of the 6 exam scenarios — scored on the 100–1,000 scale.","facts":[["60","Items"],["120","Minutes"],["720","Pass (100–1,000)"],["5 / 30","Domains / tasks"],["4 of 6","Scenarios per sitting"],["Mar 2026","Launched"]],"steps":[["Diagnose","Take the 24-question diagnostic to see which domains and scenarios need the most work.","#/diagnostic"],["Learn","Work through the 30 task lessons. Each ends with exam traps, scenario questions and a build exercise.","#/learn"],["Drill & memorize","Drill weak task statements with instant feedback, and run flashcards daily for patterns and anti-patterns.","#/drill"],["Simulate","Sit full 60-question, 4-scenario mocks under the 120-minute clock. Aim for 800+ consistently before booking.","#/mock"]],"blueprintNote":"Anthropic publishes weights per domain; agentic architecture alone is more than a quarter of the exam. Task weights shown here split each domain weight evenly across its task statements — an estimate used to size lessons, mocks and the question bank.","learnLead":"The CCA-F exam covers 5 domains and 30 task statements. Each lesson maps 1:1 to a task statement. The exam tests architectural judgment in realistic scenarios — which pattern, which control, which trade-off — not API trivia.","coverageNote":"Every task statement maps to one lesson. Question counts are sized by the estimated task weight, and every question is set in one of the six exam scenarios.","recommendedOrder":"Follow the numbering (1.1 → 5.6): Domain 1 builds the agent mental model the other domains rely on. Read 2.x before 3.x, because Claude Code configuration reuses tool and MCP concepts. Short on time? Prioritize Domain 1 (27%) end to end, then 4.3, 4.4, 3.1, 2.1, 2.2 and 5.2.","navCurriculum":"5 domains · 30 task statements","exams":{"full":{"n":60,"minutes":120,"scenarios":4,"note":"The real exam's format and pace (2 min per item), with 4 of the 6 scenarios drawn at random and items grouped by scenario."},"half":{"n":30,"minutes":60,"scenarios":2},"diagnostic":{"n":24,"minutes":45,"scenarios":6}},"mockLead":"Sit an exam-realistic simulation: 4 of the 6 scenarios are drawn at random, questions are allocated in proportion to the task weights and grouped by scenario, options are shuffled, and there is no feedback until you submit. Scored on the 100–1,000 scale with 720 to pass.","diagnosticLead":"24 questions in 45 minutes, sampled across all 5 domains and all 6 scenarios in proportion to the blueprint. Take it before you start studying to find your weak areas, then again halfway through.","exercisesLead":"The exam rewards hands-on experience with the Claude API, the Agent SDK, Claude Code and MCP. Every lesson ends with a build exercise; together they cover all six exam scenarios — a support agent, a research system, CI review, extraction pipelines and team Claude Code configuration.","exercisesNote":"The repository folder <code>architect/exercises/</code> contains tested reference implementations for the core patterns — stop_reason-driven agent loop with a hard budget (1.1), prerequisite gate (1.4), structured MCP-style errors (2.2), validation-retry extraction with tool_choice (4.3/4.4), batch failure resubmission by custom_id (4.5) and escalation routing (5.2). Run <code>python architect/exercises/test_solutions.py</code> (offline, no API key) to verify them.","glossaryLead":"terms for agent architecture, the Agent SDK, MCP, Claude Code configuration, structured output and production reliability.","searchPlaceholder":"e.g. stop_reason, tool_choice, custom_id, .claude/rules, fork_session","progressFile":"cca-progress"},"domains":[{"id":1,"slug":"agentic-architecture-orchestration","name":"Agentic Architecture & Orchestration","weight":27,"summary":"The largest domain: stop_reason-driven agent loops, coordinator–subagent orchestration, context passing between agents, enforced multi-step workflows, Agent SDK hooks, task decomposition and session management.","skills":[{"id":"1.1","slug":"agentic-loops","name":"Agentic Loops","weight":3.9,"guide":"Design and implement agentic loops for autonomous task execution: continue on stop_reason \"tool_use\", stop on \"end_turn\", append tool results to the conversation, and avoid anti-patterns such as parsing natural language for completion or using iteration caps as the primary stop signal."},{"id":"1.2","slug":"multi-agent-orchestration","name":"Multi-Agent Orchestration","weight":3.9,"guide":"Orchestrate multi-agent systems with coordinator–subagent (hub-and-spoke) patterns: the coordinator decomposes, delegates, routes all communication, aggregates results and refines; subagents work in isolated contexts; overly narrow decomposition causes coverage gaps."},{"id":"1.3","slug":"subagent-invocation-context","name":"Subagent Invocation & Context Passing","weight":3.9,"guide":"Configure subagent invocation, context passing and spawning: the Task/Agent tool and allowedTools, AgentDefinition (description, prompt, tools), explicit context in the delegation prompt, structured hand-back data with metadata, and parallel spawning in a single response."},{"id":"1.4","slug":"workflow-enforcement-handoff","name":"Workflow Enforcement & Handoff","weight":3.9,"guide":"Implement multi-step workflows with enforcement and handoff patterns: programmatic prerequisite gates instead of prompt-only guidance when compliance must be deterministic, decomposing multi-concern requests, and structured handoff summaries for human agents."},{"id":"1.5","slug":"agent-sdk-hooks","name":"Agent SDK Hooks","weight":3.8,"guide":"Apply Agent SDK hooks for tool-call interception and data normalization: PostToolUse to normalize heterogeneous tool results before the model sees them, PreToolUse to block or redirect policy-violating calls, and choosing hooks over prompts when guarantees are required."},{"id":"1.6","slug":"task-decomposition","name":"Task Decomposition Strategies","weight":3.8,"guide":"Design task decomposition strategies for complex workflows: fixed prompt chains for predictable multi-aspect work versus dynamic, adaptive decomposition for open-ended investigation; per-file local passes plus a cross-file integration pass for large reviews."},{"id":"1.7","slug":"session-state-resumption","name":"Session State, Resumption & Forking","weight":3.8,"guide":"Manage session state, resumption and forking: named sessions and --resume, fork_session for divergent explorations from a shared baseline, informing a resumed session about changed files, and starting fresh with a structured summary when prior tool results are stale."}]},{"id":2,"slug":"tool-design-mcp-integration","name":"Tool Design & MCP Integration","weight":18,"summary":"Tool interfaces the model can choose between reliably, structured error responses, scoping tools per agent and configuring tool_choice, integrating MCP servers into Claude Code and agents, and using the built-in tools well.","skills":[{"id":"2.1","slug":"tool-interface-design","name":"Tool Interface Design","weight":3.6,"guide":"Design effective tool interfaces with clear descriptions and boundaries: descriptions are the primary signal for tool selection; include input formats, example queries, edge cases and when to use one tool versus a similar one; split generic tools and rename overlapping ones."},{"id":"2.2","slug":"structured-tool-errors","name":"Structured Error Responses for MCP Tools","weight":3.6,"guide":"Implement structured error responses for MCP tools: the isError flag, error categories (transient, validation, business, permission), retryability and human-readable descriptions; distinguish an access failure from a valid empty result; recover locally before propagating."},{"id":"2.3","slug":"tool-distribution-tool-choice","name":"Tool Distribution & tool_choice","weight":3.6,"guide":"Distribute tools appropriately across agents and configure tool choice: too many tools degrade selection; scope each agent to the tools its role needs, add narrow cross-role tools for frequent needs; use tool_choice auto, any or a forced tool to control behavior."},{"id":"2.4","slug":"mcp-server-integration","name":"MCP Server Integration","weight":3.6,"guide":"Integrate MCP servers into Claude Code and agent workflows: project-scoped .mcp.json versus user-scoped configuration, environment-variable expansion for secrets, tools from all servers available at once, MCP resources for content catalogs, and preferring existing servers for standard integrations."},{"id":"2.5","slug":"built-in-tools","name":"Built-in Tools","weight":3.6,"guide":"Select and apply the built-in tools (Read, Write, Edit, Bash, Grep, Glob) effectively: Grep for content, Glob for path patterns, Edit with unique anchor text and Read + Write as the fallback, and incremental exploration instead of reading everything up front."}]},{"id":3,"slug":"claude-code-configuration-workflows","name":"Claude Code Configuration & Workflows","weight":20,"summary":"Configure Claude Code for teams: the CLAUDE.md hierarchy and modular rules, custom slash commands and skills, path-scoped conventions, plan mode versus direct execution, iterative refinement and CI/CD integration.","skills":[{"id":"3.1","slug":"claude-md-hierarchy","name":"CLAUDE.md Hierarchy & Organization","weight":3.4,"guide":"Configure CLAUDE.md files with appropriate hierarchy, scoping and modular organization: user-level versus project-level versus directory-level files, what is shared through version control, @import for modularity, .claude/rules/ topic files, and diagnosing what is loaded with /memory."},{"id":"3.2","slug":"slash-commands-skills","name":"Custom Slash Commands & Skills","weight":3.4,"guide":"Create and configure custom slash commands and skills: project-scoped (.claude/) versus personal (~/.claude/) locations, SKILL.md frontmatter such as context: fork, allowed-tools and argument-hint, and choosing skills (on-demand workflows) versus CLAUDE.md (always-on conventions)."},{"id":"3.3","slug":"path-specific-rules","name":"Path-Specific Rules","weight":3.3,"guide":"Apply path-specific rules for conditional convention loading: .claude/rules/ files with paths glob frontmatter that load only when matching files are edited, and choosing them over directory-level CLAUDE.md when conventions span many directories (e.g. all test files)."},{"id":"3.4","slug":"plan-mode-vs-direct","name":"Plan Mode vs Direct Execution","weight":3.3,"guide":"Determine when to use plan mode versus direct execution: plan mode for large-scale, multi-file, architectural or ambiguous changes; direct execution for small, well-scoped changes; the Explore subagent to keep verbose discovery out of the main context; combining planning with execution."},{"id":"3.5","slug":"iterative-refinement","name":"Iterative Refinement","weight":3.3,"guide":"Apply iterative refinement techniques for progressive improvement: concrete input/output examples, test-driven iteration with failing tests as feedback, the interview pattern for unfamiliar domains, and batching interacting issues versus fixing independent ones sequentially."},{"id":"3.6","slug":"ci-cd-integration","name":"CI/CD Integration","weight":3.3,"guide":"Integrate Claude Code into CI/CD pipelines: non-interactive -p mode, --output-format json and --json-schema for machine-parseable results, CLAUDE.md as CI context, an independent review instance rather than self-review, and passing prior findings to avoid duplicate comments."}]},{"id":4,"slug":"prompt-engineering-structured-output","name":"Prompt Engineering & Structured Output","weight":20,"summary":"Precise prompts with explicit criteria, targeted few-shot examples, schema-enforced output through tool use, validation–retry loops, batch processing strategy, and multi-instance or multi-pass review architectures.","skills":[{"id":"4.1","slug":"explicit-criteria","name":"Explicit Criteria & Precision","weight":3.4,"guide":"Design prompts with explicit criteria to improve precision and reduce false positives: concrete categorical criteria instead of vague instructions such as 'be conservative', defining what to report and what to skip, severity definitions with examples, and temporarily disabling high false-positive categories to restore trust."},{"id":"4.2","slug":"few-shot-prompting","name":"Few-Shot Prompting","weight":3.3,"guide":"Apply few-shot prompting to improve output consistency and quality: 2–4 targeted examples for ambiguous cases that show the reasoning behind the choice, demonstrating output format, generalizing to novel patterns, and reducing hallucination for varied document layouts."},{"id":"4.3","slug":"structured-output-tool-use","name":"Structured Output via Tool Use & JSON Schemas","weight":3.4,"guide":"Enforce structured output using tool use and JSON schemas: schemas eliminate syntax errors but not semantic errors; tool_choice any versus forced tool; required versus nullable fields to prevent fabrication; enums with 'other' plus detail and 'unclear' values; normalization rules."},{"id":"4.4","slug":"validation-retry-loops","name":"Validation, Retry & Feedback Loops","weight":3.3,"guide":"Implement validation, retry and feedback loops for extraction quality: retry with the document, the failed output and the specific validation error; recognize when retries cannot help because information is absent; self-correction fields such as calculated versus stated totals; detected_pattern fields for analyzing dismissals."},{"id":"4.5","slug":"batch-processing","name":"Batch Processing Strategy","weight":3.3,"guide":"Design efficient batch processing strategies: the Message Batches API's 50% cost saving and up-to-24-hour window without latency guarantees, matching it to latency-tolerant workloads only, custom_id correlation, resubmitting only failed items, and refining prompts on a sample before large runs."},{"id":"4.6","slug":"multi-pass-review","name":"Multi-Instance & Multi-Pass Review","weight":3.3,"guide":"Design multi-instance and multi-pass review architectures: why a model reviewing its own output in the same session is less effective, independent reviewer instances without the generator's reasoning, per-file local passes plus cross-file integration passes, and confidence self-reports for routing review."}]},{"id":5,"slug":"context-management-reliability","name":"Context Management & Reliability","weight":15,"summary":"Preserve critical facts across long interactions, escalate and resolve ambiguity correctly, propagate errors across agents, explore large codebases without context degradation, calibrate human review, and keep provenance in multi-source synthesis.","skills":[{"id":"5.1","slug":"conversation-context","name":"Preserving Critical Context","weight":2.5,"guide":"Manage conversation context to preserve critical information across long interactions: progressive summarization loses numbers, dates and commitments; keep a persistent case-facts block; the lost-in-the-middle effect; trim verbose tool outputs to the fields that matter; place key findings at the start."},{"id":"5.2","slug":"escalation-ambiguity","name":"Escalation & Ambiguity Resolution","weight":2.5,"guide":"Design effective escalation and ambiguity resolution patterns: honor explicit requests for a human, escalate on policy gaps and when progress stalls, avoid unreliable triggers such as sentiment or self-reported confidence, and ask for identifiers when a lookup returns multiple matches."},{"id":"5.3","slug":"error-propagation","name":"Error Propagation in Multi-Agent Systems","weight":2.5,"guide":"Implement error propagation strategies across multi-agent systems: return structured error context (failure type, attempted query, partial results, alternatives) to the coordinator; never silently suppress errors as empty success or abort the whole workflow on one failure; annotate coverage gaps."},{"id":"5.4","slug":"large-codebase-context","name":"Context in Large Codebase Exploration","weight":2.5,"guide":"Manage context effectively in large codebase exploration: recognize context degradation in long sessions, keep scratchpad files of findings, delegate verbose exploration to subagents, summarize before moving to the next phase, use /compact deliberately, and keep crash-recovery manifests."},{"id":"5.5","slug":"human-review-calibration","name":"Human Review & Confidence Calibration","weight":2.5,"guide":"Design human review workflows and confidence calibration: aggregate accuracy hides weak document types and fields; validate per type and per field, use stratified random sampling of automated results, and calibrate field-level confidence thresholds on labeled validation sets."},{"id":"5.6","slug":"information-provenance","name":"Information Provenance & Uncertainty","weight":2.5,"guide":"Preserve information provenance and uncertainty in multi-source synthesis: claim–source mappings that survive summarization, annotating conflicting values with their sources instead of picking one, carrying publication and collection dates, and rendering each content type appropriately."}]}]},"lessons":[{"skill":"1.1","slug":"agentic-loops","title":"Agentic Loops","summary":"The stop_reason-driven loop at the heart of every Claude agent — what continues it, what ends it, where tool results go, and the anti-patterns the exam uses as distractors.","minutes":6,"html":"<h2 id=\"what-you-need-to-know\">What you need to know</h2>\n<p>Every Claude agent — whether hand-written against the Messages API or running inside the Agent SDK — is the same loop: <strong>send the conversation, inspect why the model stopped, execute the tools it asked for, feed the results back, repeat</strong>. The exam does not ask you to type this loop from memory; it asks you to recognise <em>correct</em> control flow and to spot the subtle bugs that make agents stop too early, run forever or reason about stale data.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-key\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key concept</div><div class=\"callout-title\">The control signal is <code>stop_reason</code></div><div class=\"callout-body\"><ul><li><code>\"tool_use\"</code> → Claude wants one or more tools executed. Run them, append the results, call the API again.</li><li><code>\"end_turn\"</code> → Claude has finished. Exit the loop and return the final answer.</li></ul>\n<p>Everything else is a special case you handle explicitly — never as \"done by default\".</p></div></aside>\n<h2 id=\"the-loop-step-by-step\">The loop, step by step</h2>\n<div class=\"codeblock\"><div class=\"codeblock-head\"><span>manual agentic loop (Python SDK)</span><button class=\"copy-btn\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></div><pre><code class=\"lang-python\">messages = [{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": task}]\nfor turn in range(MAX_TURNS):                      # safety net, not the control flow\n    resp = client.messages.create(model=MODEL, max_tokens=4096,\n                                  system=SYSTEM, tools=TOOLS, messages=messages)\n    messages.append({\"role\": \"assistant\", \"content\": resp.content})\n    if resp.stop_reason == \"end_turn\":\n        return final_text(resp)\n    if resp.stop_reason != \"tool_use\":\n        return handle_special(resp)                # max_tokens, pause_turn, refusal …\n    results = []\n    for block in resp.content:\n        if block.type == \"tool_use\":\n            out = execute(block.name, block.input)\n            results.append({\"type\": \"tool_result\", \"tool_use_id\": block.id,\n                            \"content\": out.text, \"is_error\": out.failed})\n    messages.append({\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": results})\nraise BudgetExceeded(\"agent did not finish within MAX_TURNS\")</code></pre></div>\n<p>Four details carry most of the exam weight:</p>\n<ol><li><strong>The assistant turn is appended unchanged</strong> — including its <code>tool_use</code> blocks. Results without the matching request are rejected by the API.</li><li><strong>All tool calls in a response are answered</strong>, each with a <code>tool_result</code> whose <code>tool_use_id</code> matches. Claude may request several tools in one turn (parallel tool use); answer them in <strong>one</strong> user message.</li><li><strong>Tool results go back into the conversation history.</strong> That is how the model \"sees\" the environment and decides the next step. An agent that executes tools but doesn't append results is reasoning blind.</li><li><strong>Errors are results too.</strong> Return failures as <code>tool_result</code> with <code>is_error: true</code> and a useful message, so Claude can adapt — don't crash the loop and don't hide the failure.</li></ol>\n<h2 id=\"other-stop-reasons\">Other stop reasons</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th><code>stop_reason</code></th><th>What happened</th><th>Correct handling</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>max_tokens</code></td><td>Output hit the <code>max_tokens</code> limit mid-response</td><td>The answer (or a tool call) is <strong>truncated</strong>. Raise the limit or continue — never treat it as finished</td></tr><tr><td><code>stop_sequence</code></td><td>A custom stop sequence was produced</td><td>Application-specific</td></tr><tr><td><code>pause_turn</code></td><td>A long-running server-tool turn was paused</td><td>Send the assistant content back unchanged so Claude continues</td></tr><tr><td><code>refusal</code></td><td>Claude declined for safety reasons</td><td>Surface it; do not loop retrying the same request</td></tr><tr><td><code>model_context_window_exceeded</code></td><td>The context window filled</td><td>Compact/trim context, then continue</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"anti-patterns-the-distractors-are-built-from\">Anti-patterns the distractors are built from</h2>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-trap\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Exam trap</div><div class=\"callout-title\">Parsing natural language to decide completion</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>\"Stop when the reply contains 'task complete'\" or \"stop when Claude stops mentioning tools\" is fragile — wording varies, and Claude may explain its next tool call in prose. <code>stop_reason</code> is the contract.</p></div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-trap\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Exam trap</div><div class=\"callout-title\">\"Stop when a text block appears\"</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Claude often returns text <strong>and</strong> <code>tool_use</code> blocks in the same turn (\"Let me check the order…\"). Exiting on the first text block skips the requested tool call.</p></div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-trap\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Exam trap</div><div class=\"callout-title\">An iteration cap as the primary stop condition</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>A <code>MAX_TURNS</code> limit is a good <strong>safety net</strong> against runaway loops and cost blow-ups. But an agent that \"finishes\" because it hit turn 10 has not finished — it was cut off. If a question offers \"set max iterations to 10\" as the fix for an agent that stops too early or too late, it's almost always the distractor.</p></div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-trap\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Exam trap</div><div class=\"callout-title\">Rebuilding the prompt instead of appending</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Some designs re-send only the original task plus the latest tool result. Claude then loses its own earlier reasoning and earlier results, repeats calls and contradicts itself. Keep the full history (and manage its size deliberately — see 5.1).</p></div></aside>\n<h2 id=\"model-driven-vs-pre-configured-decisions\">Model-driven vs. pre-configured decisions</h2>\n<p>In an agent loop, <strong>Claude decides</strong> which tool to call next based on the context — that is the point of an agent. Pre-configured decision trees (\"always call A, then B, then C\") belong in a workflow (see 1.6). Exam items often contrast the two: if the order of steps is genuinely fixed and known, a workflow is simpler and more reliable; if the next step depends on what the last step returned, the model-driven loop is right — with <strong>hooks and gates</strong> (1.4, 1.5) enforcing the few steps that must never be skipped.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-scenario\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Scenario</div><div class=\"callout-title\">S1 — the agent stops after its first sentence</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>A support agent replies \"I'll look up your order now.\" and the conversation ends without a lookup. Logs show <code>stop_reason: \"tool_use\"</code> on that response. The loop exits when it finds a text block. <strong>Fix:</strong> branch on <code>stop_reason</code>, not on content types.</p></div></aside>\n<h2 id=\"exam-traps\">Exam traps</h2>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-remember\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Remember for the exam</div><div class=\"callout-title\">Checklist for any loop question</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><ul><li>Does the loop continue on <code>tool_use</code> and stop on <code>end_turn</code>?</li><li>Are <strong>all</strong> tool calls answered, with matching IDs, in one user message?</li><li>Are results (including errors) <strong>appended to history</strong>?</li><li>Is the iteration cap a <strong>safety net</strong> that raises/flags, not a silent \"done\"?</li><li>Are <code>max_tokens</code> and other stop reasons handled explicitly?</li></ul></div></aside>\n<h2 id=\"practice-questions\">Practice questions</h2>\n<div class=\"quiz-embed\" data-qid=\"a1-loop-01\"></div>\n<div class=\"quiz-embed\" data-qid=\"a1-loop-02\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"build-exercise\">Build exercise</h2>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-exercise\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Build exercise</div><div class=\"callout-title\">Build a stop_reason-driven support agent loop</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><ol><li>Define two tools: <code>lookup_order(order_id)</code> and <code>get_customer(email)</code>, backed by an in-memory dictionary.</li><li>Implement the loop above with the Messages API. Branch only on <code>stop_reason</code>.</li><li>Return an <code>is_error: true</code> tool result for unknown order IDs and observe how Claude asks the user for the correct ID.</li><li>Add a <code>MAX_TURNS</code> safety net that raises a <code>BudgetExceeded</code> exception (and logs the transcript) instead of silently returning.</li><li>Force <code>max_tokens=50</code> once and confirm your loop reports a truncated answer instead of treating it as done.</li><li>Compare with the reference solution in <code>architect/exercises/solutions.py</code> (<code>run_agent</code>).</li></ol></div></aside>","headings":[{"id":"what-you-need-to-know","text":"What you need to know"},{"id":"the-loop-step-by-step","text":"The loop, step by step"},{"id":"other-stop-reasons","text":"Other stop reasons"},{"id":"anti-patterns-the-distractors-are-built-from","text":"Anti-patterns the distractors are built from"},{"id":"model-driven-vs-pre-configured-decisions","text":"Model-driven vs. pre-configured decisions"},{"id":"exam-traps","text":"Exam traps"},{"id":"practice-questions","text":"Practice questions"},{"id":"build-exercise","text":"Build exercise"}],"sources":["Handling stop reasons | https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/handling-stop-reasons","How to implement tool use | https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/agents-and-tools/tool-use/implement-tool-use","Building effective agents — Anthropic | https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/building-effective-agents","Agent SDK overview | https://code.claude.com/docs/en/agent-sdk/overview"],"text":" What you need to know Every Claude agent — whether hand written against the Messages API or running inside the Agent SDK — is the same loop send the conversation, inspect why the model stopped, execute the tools it asked for, feed the results back, repeat . The exam does not ask you to type this loop from memory; it asks you to recognise correct control flow and to spot the subtle bugs that make agents stop too early, run forever or reason about stale data. key The control signal is stop_reason \"tool_use\" → Claude wants one or more tools executed. Run them, append the results, call the API again. \"end_turn\" → Claude has finished. Exit the loop and return the final answer. Everything else is a special case you handle explicitly — never as \"done by default\". The loop, step by step Four details carry most of the exam weight 1. The assistant turn is appended unchanged — including its tool_use blocks. Results without the matching request are rejected by the API. 2. All tool calls in a response are answered , each with a tool_result whose tool_use_id matches. Claude may request several tools in one turn (parallel tool use); answer them in one user message. 3. Tool results go back into the conversation history. That is how the model \"sees\" the environment and decides the next step. An agent that executes tools but doesn't append results is reasoning blind. 4. Errors are results too. Return failures as tool_result with is_error true and a useful message, so Claude can adapt — don't crash the loop and don't hide the failure. Other stop reasons stop_reason What happened Correct handling max_tokens Output hit the max_tokens limit mid response The answer (or a tool call) is truncated . Raise the limit or continue — never treat it as finished stop_sequence A custom stop sequence was produced Application specific pause_turn A long running server tool turn was paused Send the assistant content back unchanged so Claude continues refusal Claude declined for safety reasons Surface it; do not loop retrying the same request model_context_window_exceeded The context window filled Compact/trim context, then continue Anti patterns the distractors are built from trap Parsing natural language to decide completion \"Stop when the reply contains 'task complete'\" or \"stop when Claude stops mentioning tools\" is fragile — wording varies, and Claude may explain its next tool call in prose. stop_reason is the contract. trap \"Stop when a text block appears\" Claude often returns text and tool_use blocks in the same turn (\"Let me check the order…\"). Exiting on the first text block skips the requested tool call. trap An iteration cap as the primary stop condition A MAX_TURNS limit is a good safety net against runaway loops and cost blow ups. But an agent that \"finishes\" because it hit turn 10 has not finished — it was cut off. If a question offers \"set max iterations to 10\" as the fix for an agent that stops too early or too late, it's almost always the distractor. trap Rebuilding the prompt instead of appending Some designs re send only the original task plus the latest tool result. Claude then loses its own earlier reasoning and earlier results, repeats calls and contradicts itself. Keep the full history (and manage its size deliberately — see 5.1). Model driven vs. pre configured decisions In an agent loop, Claude decides which tool to call next based on the context — that is the point of an agent. Pre configured decision trees (\"always call A, then B, then C\") belong in a workflow (see 1.6). Exam items often contrast the two if the order of steps is genuinely fixed and known, a workflow is simpler and more reliable; if the next step depends on what the last step returned, the model driven loop is right — with hooks and gates (1.4, 1.5) enforcing the few steps that must never be skipped. scenario S1 — the agent stops after its first sentence A support agent replies \"I'll look up your order now.\" and the conversation ends without a lookup. Logs show stop_reason \"tool_use\" on that response. The loop exits when it finds a text block. Fix branch on stop_reason , not on content types. Exam traps remember Checklist for any loop question Does the loop continue on tool_use and stop on end_turn ? Are all tool calls answered, with matching IDs, in one user message? Are results (including errors) appended to history ? Is the iteration cap a safety net that raises/flags, not a silent \"done\"? Are max_tokens and other stop reasons handled explicitly? Practice questions quiz a1 loop 01 quiz a1 loop 02 Build exercise exercise Build a stop_reason driven support agent loop 1. Define two tools lookup_order(order_id) and get_customer(email) , backed by an in memory dictionary. 2. Implement the loop above with the Messages API. Branch only on stop_reason . 3. Return an is_error true tool result for unknown order IDs and observe how Claude asks the user for the correct ID. 4. Add a MAX_TURNS safety net that raises a BudgetExceeded exception (and logs the transcript) instead of silently returning. 5. Force max_tokens=50 once and confirm your loop reports a truncated answer instead of treating it as done. 6. Compare with the reference solution in architect/exercises/solutions.py ( run_agent ). "},{"skill":"1.2","slug":"multi-agent-orchestration","title":"Multi-Agent Orchestration","summary":"Coordinator–subagent (hub-and-spoke) systems — what the coordinator owns, why subagents are isolated, how decomposition causes coverage gaps, and when multi-agent is the wrong answer.","minutes":5,"html":"<h2 id=\"what-you-need-to-know\">What you need to know</h2>\n<p>The reference architecture for multi-agent Claude systems is <strong>hub-and-spoke</strong>: one <strong>coordinator</strong> (lead agent) and several specialised <strong>subagents</strong>. The Multi-Agent Research System scenario (S3) is built entirely on it, and parts of the support and productivity scenarios use it too.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-key\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key concept</div><div class=\"callout-title\">Responsibilities of the coordinator</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><ol><li><strong>Decompose</strong> the request into subtasks.</li><li><strong>Select</strong> which subagents to invoke (and how many — scale effort to complexity).</li><li><strong>Delegate</strong> with complete context (1.3).</li><li><strong>Route all communication</strong> — subagents report only to the coordinator.</li><li><strong>Aggregate</strong> and reconcile results, resolve conflicts, keep provenance.</li><li><strong>Evaluate coverage</strong> and iterate: re-delegate if gaps or contradictions remain.</li></ol></div></aside>\n<h2 id=\"why-hub-and-spoke\">Why hub-and-spoke</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Context isolation.</strong> Each subagent starts with a clean context containing only its subtask. Verbose searching and reading stays in the subagent; the coordinator receives a condensed result.</li><li><strong>Observability and control.</strong> All information flows through one place, so you can log it, apply consistent error handling (5.3) and enforce policy.</li><li><strong>Specialisation.</strong> Subagents can have different prompts, tools and models — a web-search agent with search tools, a document agent with file tools, a cheaper model for simple extraction.</li><li><strong>Parallelism.</strong> Independent subtasks run concurrently, reducing wall-clock time.</li></ul>\n<p>Subagents talking directly to each other (\"mesh\") loses these properties: duplicated work, untraceable information flow and no single place to resolve conflicts. On the exam, <strong>\"route through the coordinator\"</strong> beats \"let subagents share a channel\".</p>\n<h2 id=\"decomposition-decides-quality\">Decomposition decides quality</h2>\n<p>The most frequently tested failure: a report that is well-written and well-sourced but <strong>covers only part of the question</strong>. Each subagent did exactly what it was asked — the coordinator asked for too little.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-scenario\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Scenario</div><div class=\"callout-title\">S3 — \"impact of AI on creative industries\"</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>The final report discusses only visual art. Logs show the coordinator created three subtasks: \"AI in digital art\", \"AI in graphic design\", \"AI in photography\". The search and synthesis agents performed well. <strong>Root cause:</strong> overly narrow decomposition by the coordinator — music, writing, film and games were never delegated. <strong>Fix:</strong> improve the coordinator's decomposition (enumerate the dimensions of the topic, check coverage before synthesis), not the subagents.</p></div></aside>\n<p>Good coordinator prompts therefore include: identify the dimensions of the question, map each to a subtask with a clear objective and output format, avoid overlap, and <strong>check coverage against the original question</strong> before synthesising.</p>\n<h2 id=\"iterative-refinement-by-the-coordinator\">Iterative refinement by the coordinator</h2>\n<p>A coordinator should not just fan out once and concatenate. After results return it evaluates them: are claims supported, do sources conflict, are there gaps? If so it issues <strong>targeted follow-up delegations</strong> (\"find primary data for the 2025 figure\", \"search for counter-evidence on X\") and re-synthesises. This is the orchestrator-workers pattern with an evaluation step.</p>\n<h2 id=\"when-multi-agent-is-wrong\">When multi-agent is wrong</h2>\n<p>Multi-agent systems cost more tokens (Anthropic measured research agents at many times the tokens of a chat) and add coordination overhead. They pay off for <strong>broad, parallelisable, high-value</strong> work. They are a poor fit when:</p>\n<ul><li>subtasks are <strong>tightly coupled</strong> and each needs the full shared context (many coding tasks);</li><li>the task is a <strong>fixed sequence</strong> a workflow can handle;</li><li>the request is <strong>simple</strong> — one agent with the right tools suffices.</li></ul>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-compare\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Comparison</div><div class=\"callout-title\">Coordinator–subagent vs. prompt chain</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>A <strong>prompt chain</strong> has steps fixed in code. A <strong>coordinator</strong> decides subtasks at runtime from the input. If the question says \"the sub-questions vary by request\", choose the coordinator; if they are always the same, choose the chain.</p></div></aside>\n<h2 id=\"exam-traps\">Exam traps</h2>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-trap\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Exam trap</div><div class=\"callout-title\">Blaming the subagents for a coverage gap</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>If every subagent performed its assigned task well, the gap is in <strong>what was assigned</strong>. The fix is the coordinator's decomposition logic.</p></div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-trap\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Exam trap</div><div class=\"callout-title\">Letting subagents communicate directly to \"save a hop\"</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>It removes observability and conflict resolution. Keep communication coordinator-mediated.</p></div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-trap\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Exam trap</div><div class=\"callout-title\">\"Always run every subagent\"</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Effort should scale with the query. A simple factual question needs one subagent (or none), not the full pipeline.</p></div></aside>\n<h2 id=\"practice-questions\">Practice questions</h2>\n<div class=\"quiz-embed\" data-qid=\"a1-orch-01\"></div>\n<div class=\"quiz-embed\" data-qid=\"a1-orch-02\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"build-exercise\">Build exercise</h2>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-exercise\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Build exercise</div><div class=\"callout-title\">Build a coordinator with two research subagents</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><ol><li>With the Agent SDK, define a coordinator with <code>allowed_tools</code> including <code>Task</code>, and two subagents: <code>web-researcher</code> (web search/fetch) and <code>doc-analyst</code> (Read/Grep on a local folder of PDFs converted to text).</li><li>Prompt the coordinator to list the <strong>dimensions</strong> of the question first, then delegate one subtask per dimension.</li><li>Ask subagents to return JSON findings with <code>claim</code>, <code>source</code>, <code>date</code>.</li><li>Add a coverage check: the coordinator compares findings to its dimension list and re-delegates for any uncovered dimension.</li><li>Run a broad question (\"impact of AI on creative industries\") with and without the dimension step; compare coverage.</li></ol></div></aside>","headings":[{"id":"what-you-need-to-know","text":"What you need to know"},{"id":"why-hub-and-spoke","text":"Why hub-and-spoke"},{"id":"decomposition-decides-quality","text":"Decomposition decides quality"},{"id":"iterative-refinement-by-the-coordinator","text":"Iterative refinement by the coordinator"},{"id":"when-multi-agent-is-wrong","text":"When multi-agent is wrong"},{"id":"exam-traps","text":"Exam traps"},{"id":"practice-questions","text":"Practice questions"},{"id":"build-exercise","text":"Build exercise"}],"sources":["How we built our multi-agent research system — Anthropic | https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/multi-agent-research-system","Building effective agents — Anthropic | https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/building-effective-agents","Agent SDK: Subagents | https://code.claude.com/docs/en/agent-sdk/subagents","Effective context engineering for AI agents | https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/effective-context-engineering-for-ai-agents"],"text":" What you need to know The reference architecture for multi agent Claude systems is hub and spoke one coordinator (lead agent) and several specialised subagents . The Multi Agent Research System scenario (S3) is built entirely on it, and parts of the support and productivity scenarios use it too. key Responsibilities of the coordinator 1. Decompose the request into subtasks. 2. Select which subagents to invoke (and how many — scale effort to complexity). 3. Delegate with complete context (1.3). 4. Route all communication — subagents report only to the coordinator. 5. Aggregate and reconcile results, resolve conflicts, keep provenance. 6. Evaluate coverage and iterate re delegate if gaps or contradictions remain. Why hub and spoke Context isolation. Each subagent starts with a clean context containing only its subtask. Verbose searching and reading stays in the subagent; the coordinator receives a condensed result. Observability and control. All information flows through one place, so you can log it, apply consistent error handling (5.3) and enforce policy. Specialisation. Subagents can have different prompts, tools and models — a web search agent with search tools, a document agent with file tools, a cheaper model for simple extraction. Parallelism. Independent subtasks run concurrently, reducing wall clock time. Subagents talking directly to each other (\"mesh\") loses these properties duplicated work, untraceable information flow and no single place to resolve conflicts. On the exam, \"route through the coordinator\" beats \"let subagents share a channel\". Decomposition decides quality The most frequently tested failure a report that is well written and well sourced but covers only part of the question . Each subagent did exactly what it was asked — the coordinator asked for too little. scenario S3 — \"impact of AI on creative industries\" The final report discusses only visual art. Logs show the coordinator created three subtasks \"AI in digital art\", \"AI in graphic design\", \"AI in photography\". The search and synthesis agents performed well. Root cause overly narrow decomposition by the coordinator — music, writing, film and games were never delegated. Fix improve the coordinator's decomposition (enumerate the dimensions of the topic, check coverage before synthesis), not the subagents. Good coordinator prompts therefore include identify the dimensions of the question, map each to a subtask with a clear objective and output format, avoid overlap, and check coverage against the original question before synthesising. Iterative refinement by the coordinator A coordinator should not just fan out once and concatenate. After results return it evaluates them are claims supported, do sources conflict, are there gaps? If so it issues targeted follow up delegations (\"find primary data for the 2025 figure\", \"search for counter evidence on X\") and re synthesises. This is the orchestrator workers pattern with an evaluation step. When multi agent is wrong Multi agent systems cost more tokens (Anthropic measured research agents at many times the tokens of a chat) and add coordination overhead. They pay off for broad, parallelisable, high value work. They are a poor fit when subtasks are tightly coupled and each needs the full shared context (many coding tasks); the task is a fixed sequence a workflow can handle; the request is simple — one agent with the right tools suffices. compare Coordinator–subagent vs. prompt chain A prompt chain has steps fixed in code. A coordinator decides subtasks at runtime from the input. If the question says \"the sub questions vary by request\", choose the coordinator; if they are always the same, choose the chain. Exam traps trap Blaming the subagents for a coverage gap If every subagent performed its assigned task well, the gap is in what was assigned . The fix is the coordinator's decomposition logic. trap Letting subagents communicate directly to \"save a hop\" It removes observability and conflict resolution. Keep communication coordinator mediated. trap \"Always run every subagent\" Effort should scale with the query. A simple factual question needs one subagent (or none), not the full pipeline. Practice questions quiz a1 orch 01 quiz a1 orch 02 Build exercise exercise Build a coordinator with two research subagents 1. With the Agent SDK, define a coordinator with allowed_tools including Task , and two subagents web researcher (web search/fetch) and doc analyst (Read/Grep on a local folder of PDFs converted to text). 2. Prompt the coordinator to list the dimensions of the question first, then delegate one subtask per dimension. 3. Ask subagents to return JSON findings with claim , source , date . 4. Add a coverage check the coordinator compares findings to its dimension list and re delegates for any uncovered dimension. 5. Run a broad question (\"impact of AI on creative industries\") with and without the dimension step; compare coverage. "},{"skill":"1.3","slug":"subagent-invocation-context","title":"Subagent Invocation & Context Passing","summary":"How subagents are defined and spawned in the Agent SDK, why they inherit nothing, what a complete delegation prompt contains, and how to get structured, attributable results back — in parallel.","minutes":5,"html":"<h2 id=\"what-you-need-to-know\">What you need to know</h2>\n<p>In Claude Code and the Agent SDK, an agent spawns a subagent through the <strong>Task tool</strong> (renamed the <strong>Agent</strong> tool in newer releases — exam material uses \"Task\"). Each subagent is described by an <strong><code>AgentDefinition</code></strong>, runs in its <strong>own context window</strong>, and returns a single result to the caller.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-key\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key concept</div><div class=\"callout-title\">Three facts the exam keeps returning to</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><ol><li>The coordinator can only delegate if the Task/Agent tool is in its <strong><code>allowedTools</code></strong>.</li><li>A subagent <strong>does not inherit</strong> the coordinator's conversation, tool results or reasoning. It sees its system prompt (from the definition), its tools, and the <strong>delegation prompt</strong> — nothing else.</li><li>To run subagents <strong>in parallel</strong>, the coordinator emits <strong>multiple Task tool calls in a single response</strong>.</li></ol></div></aside>\n<h2 id=\"defining-subagents\">Defining subagents</h2>\n<div class=\"codeblock\"><div class=\"codeblock-head\"><span>Agent SDK — coordinator with two subagents</span><button class=\"copy-btn\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></div><pre><code class=\"lang-python\">from claude_agent_sdk import ClaudeAgentOptions, AgentDefinition\n\noptions = ClaudeAgentOptions(\n    system_prompt=COORDINATOR_PROMPT,\n    allowed_tools=[\"Task\", \"Read\"],\n    agents={\n        \"web-researcher\": AgentDefinition(\n            description=\"Finds current external sources for ONE sub-question. \"\n                        \"Use for facts that need web evidence.\",\n            prompt=\"Search, read and return JSON: [{claim, source_url, title, \"\n                   \"published, excerpt}]. Include dates. Do not synthesize.\",\n            tools=[\"WebSearch\", \"WebFetch\"],\n        ),\n        \"doc-analyst\": AgentDefinition(\n            description=\"Analyzes the internal reports in ./reports. Use for company data.\",\n            prompt=\"Return JSON findings with file name, page and excerpt for every claim.\",\n            tools=[\"Read\", \"Grep\", \"Glob\"],\n        ),\n    },\n)</code></pre></div>\n<ul><li><strong><code>description</code></strong> tells the coordinator <em>when</em> to use the subagent — write it like a tool description (2.1).</li><li><strong><code>prompt</code></strong> is the subagent's system prompt: role, method, output format.</li><li><strong><code>tools</code></strong> restricts the subagent to what its role needs (least privilege, better selection — 2.3).</li></ul>\n<p>Subagents can also be defined as Markdown files in <code>.claude/agents/</code> (project) or <code>~/.claude/agents/</code> (personal) for Claude Code.</p>\n<h2 id=\"writing-the-delegation-prompt\">Writing the delegation prompt</h2>\n<p>Because nothing is inherited, the delegation prompt must be <strong>self-contained</strong>:</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Include</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>The objective and how it fits the overall task</td><td>Prevents drift and duplicated scope</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Prior findings</strong> relevant to this subtask (verbatim, with sources)</td><td>The synthesis agent can't cite what it never received</td></tr><tr><td>Constraints and boundaries (time range, regions, what <em>not</em> to cover)</td><td>Avoids overlap with sibling subagents</td></tr><tr><td>Required <strong>output format</strong> — ideally structured</td><td>Reliable aggregation</td></tr><tr><td>Metadata to keep: source URL, document name, page, date</td><td>Provenance survives synthesis (5.6)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-scenario\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Scenario</div><div class=\"callout-title\">S3 — the synthesis agent can't cite sources</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>The coordinator passes the synthesis subagent a prose summary of findings. The report has no citations. <strong>Fix:</strong> pass the search and analysis results <strong>with their source metadata</strong> (structured: claim, source, date, excerpt) in the delegation prompt, and require citations in the output format.</p></div></aside>\n<h2 id=\"getting-results-back\">Getting results back</h2>\n<p>Ask subagents to return <strong>structured data separating content from metadata</strong> — e.g. JSON objects with <code>claim</code>, <code>evidence</code>, <code>source</code>, <code>date</code>, <code>confidence</code>. The coordinator can then merge, de-duplicate, detect conflicts and keep attribution. Free-form prose forces the coordinator to re-parse and loses detail.</p>\n<h2 id=\"spawning-parallel-vs-sequential\">Spawning: parallel vs. sequential</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Independent</strong> subtasks (search three regions) → emit three Task calls <strong>in one response</strong>; they run concurrently.</li><li><strong>Dependent</strong> subtasks (analyse documents the search found) → sequential: wait for the result, then delegate with that result included.</li><li>Spawning across <strong>separate turns</strong> when the work is independent wastes wall-clock time.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"exam-traps\">Exam traps</h2>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-trap\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Exam trap</div><div class=\"callout-title\">\"Increase the subagent's context window / use a bigger model\"</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>When a subagent lacks information the coordinator had, size and model are irrelevant — the information was never passed. Put it in the delegation prompt.</p></div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-trap\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Exam trap</div><div class=\"callout-title\">Assuming subagents share memory or files with the coordinator</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>They share the filesystem if tools allow it, but not the conversation. Don't rely on \"it saw it earlier\".</p></div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-trap\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Exam trap</div><div class=\"callout-title\">Forgetting the Task tool in allowedTools</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>A coordinator that \"never delegates\" may simply not be allowed to.</p></div></aside>\n<h2 id=\"practice-questions\">Practice questions</h2>\n<div class=\"quiz-embed\" data-qid=\"a1-sub-01\"></div>\n<div class=\"quiz-embed\" data-qid=\"a1-sub-02\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"build-exercise\">Build exercise</h2>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-exercise\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Build exercise</div><div class=\"callout-title\">Parallel subagents with structured hand-back</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><ol><li>Reuse the coordinator from 1.2. Change the coordinator prompt to emit all independent delegations <strong>in one turn</strong>.</li><li>Measure wall-clock time for three sequential vs. three parallel delegations.</li><li>Define a JSON contract for findings (<code>claim</code>, <code>source_url</code>, <code>published</code>, <code>excerpt</code>, <code>confidence</code>).</li><li>Add a synthesis subagent and pass it the <strong>full structured findings</strong> — verify every sentence in its report carries a citation.</li><li>Remove <code>Task</code> from <code>allowed_tools</code> and observe that the coordinator can no longer delegate.</li></ol></div></aside>","headings":[{"id":"what-you-need-to-know","text":"What you need to know"},{"id":"defining-subagents","text":"Defining subagents"},{"id":"writing-the-delegation-prompt","text":"Writing the delegation prompt"},{"id":"getting-results-back","text":"Getting results back"},{"id":"spawning-parallel-vs-sequential","text":"Spawning: parallel vs. sequential"},{"id":"exam-traps","text":"Exam traps"},{"id":"practice-questions","text":"Practice questions"},{"id":"build-exercise","text":"Build exercise"}],"sources":["Agent SDK: Subagents | https://code.claude.com/docs/en/agent-sdk/subagents","Claude Code subagents | https://code.claude.com/docs/en/sub-agents","Agent SDK Python reference | https://code.claude.com/docs/en/agent-sdk/python","How we built our multi-agent research system — Anthropic | https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/multi-agent-research-system"],"text":" What you need to know In Claude Code and the Agent SDK, an agent spawns a subagent through the Task tool (renamed the Agent tool in newer releases — exam material uses \"Task\"). Each subagent is described by an AgentDefinition , runs in its own context window , and returns a single result to the caller. key Three facts the exam keeps returning to 1. The coordinator can only delegate if the Task/Agent tool is in its allowedTools . 2. A subagent does not inherit the coordinator's conversation, tool results or reasoning. It sees its system prompt (from the definition), its tools, and the delegation prompt — nothing else. 3. To run subagents in parallel , the coordinator emits multiple Task tool calls in a single response . Defining subagents description tells the coordinator when to use the subagent — write it like a tool description (2.1). prompt is the subagent's system prompt role, method, output format. tools restricts the subagent to what its role needs (least privilege, better selection — 2.3). Subagents can also be defined as Markdown files in .claude/agents/ (project) or ~/.claude/agents/ (personal) for Claude Code. Writing the delegation prompt Because nothing is inherited, the delegation prompt must be self contained Include Why The objective and how it fits the overall task Prevents drift and duplicated scope Prior findings relevant to this subtask (verbatim, with sources) The synthesis agent can't cite what it never received Constraints and boundaries (time range, regions, what not to cover) Avoids overlap with sibling subagents Required output format — ideally structured Reliable aggregation Metadata to keep source URL, document name, page, date Provenance survives synthesis (5.6) scenario S3 — the synthesis agent can't cite sources The coordinator passes the synthesis subagent a prose summary of findings. The report has no citations. Fix pass the search and analysis results with their source metadata (structured claim, source, date, excerpt) in the delegation prompt, and require citations in the output format. Getting results back Ask subagents to return structured data separating content from metadata — e.g. JSON objects with claim , evidence , source , date , confidence . The coordinator can then merge, de duplicate, detect conflicts and keep attribution. Free form prose forces the coordinator to re parse and loses detail. Spawning parallel vs. sequential Independent subtasks (search three regions) → emit three Task calls in one response ; they run concurrently. Dependent subtasks (analyse documents the search found) → sequential wait for the result, then delegate with that result included. Spawning across separate turns when the work is independent wastes wall clock time. Exam traps trap \"Increase the subagent's context window / use a bigger model\" When a subagent lacks information the coordinator had, size and model are irrelevant — the information was never passed. Put it in the delegation prompt. trap Assuming subagents share memory or files with the coordinator They share the filesystem if tools allow it, but not the conversation. Don't rely on \"it saw it earlier\". trap Forgetting the Task tool in allowedTools A coordinator that \"never delegates\" may simply not be allowed to. Practice questions quiz a1 sub 01 quiz a1 sub 02 Build exercise exercise Parallel subagents with structured hand back 1. Reuse the coordinator from 1.2. Change the coordinator prompt to emit all independent delegations in one turn . 2. Measure wall clock time for three sequential vs. three parallel delegations. 3. Define a JSON contract for findings ( claim , source_url , published , excerpt , confidence ). 4. Add a synthesis subagent and pass it the full structured findings — verify every sentence in its report carries a citation. 5. Remove Task from allowed_tools and observe that the coordinator can no longer delegate. "},{"skill":"1.4","slug":"workflow-enforcement-handoff","title":"Workflow Enforcement & Handoff","summary":"When a step must never be skipped, enforce it in code — prerequisite gates instead of prompt instructions; decompose multi-concern requests; and hand off to humans with a structured summary.","minutes":5,"html":"<h2 id=\"what-you-need-to-know\">What you need to know</h2>\n<p>This task statement produces the single most common Architect-exam pattern:</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-key\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key concept</div><div class=\"callout-title\">Deterministic vs. probabilistic compliance</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>A <strong>prompt instruction</strong> (\"always verify the customer's identity before issuing a refund\") makes the behaviour <strong>likely</strong>. A <strong>programmatic gate</strong> makes it <strong>guaranteed</strong>. When the requirement involves money, identity, safety, legal or regulatory compliance — or a production incident shows the step being skipped even occasionally — the answer is enforcement in code.</p></div></aside>\n<p>Prompt guidance remains fine for preferences and style: tone, formatting, which of several acceptable approaches to try first.</p>\n<h2 id=\"prerequisite-gates\">Prerequisite gates</h2>\n<p>A prerequisite gate blocks a tool until required earlier steps have succeeded:</p>\n<div class=\"codeblock\"><div class=\"codeblock-head\"><span>prerequisite gate around process_refund</span><button class=\"copy-btn\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></div><pre><code class=\"lang-python\">class SupportSession:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.verified_customer_id = None\n\n    def get_customer(self, email, last4):\n        cust = crm.verify(email, last4)\n        if cust:\n            self.verified_customer_id = cust.id\n        return cust\n\n    def process_refund(self, customer_id, order_id, amount):\n        if self.verified_customer_id != customer_id:\n            return tool_error(\"permission\", retryable=False,\n                message=\"Identity not verified for this customer. Call get_customer first.\")\n        if amount &gt; REFUND_LIMIT:\n            return tool_error(\"business\", retryable=False,\n                message=f\"Refunds above ${REFUND_LIMIT} require a human. Use escalate_to_human.\")\n        return payments.refund(order_id, amount)</code></pre></div>\n<p>The same logic can live in a <strong>PreToolUse hook</strong> (1.5) when you can't change the tool itself — e.g. an MCP server you don't own. The error message tells Claude <em>what to do next</em>, so the agent recovers instead of looping.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-scenario\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Scenario</div><div class=\"callout-title\">S1 — 12% of refunds skip verification</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Despite a clear system prompt, logs show the agent occasionally calls <code>process_refund</code> before <code>get_customer</code> when customers sound confident. Adding few-shot examples or capital letters improves the rate but can't make it 100%. <strong>Answer:</strong> a programmatic prerequisite that blocks <code>process_refund</code> until verification has succeeded.</p></div></aside>\n<h2 id=\"multi-concern-requests\">Multi-concern requests</h2>\n<p>\"My order arrived broken, I was charged twice, and I want to change my address.\" Handling this as one blob leads to answering only the first issue. The pattern:</p>\n<ol><li><strong>Decompose</strong> into distinct concerns.</li><li><strong>Investigate</strong> each — in parallel where independent — sharing customer context.</li><li><strong>Synthesise</strong> a single, coherent reply that addresses every concern and states what happens next for each.</li></ol>\n<h2 id=\"structured-handoff-to-humans\">Structured handoff to humans</h2>\n<p>When the agent escalates (5.2), the human agent typically <strong>cannot see the transcript</strong> or doesn't have time to read it. Pass a structured summary:</p>\n<div class=\"codeblock\"><div class=\"codeblock-head\"><span>escalate_to_human payload</span><button class=\"copy-btn\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></div><pre><code class=\"lang-json\">{\n  \"customer_id\": \"C-20931\",\n  \"verified\": true,\n  \"issue\": \"Duplicate charge on order ORD-1182\",\n  \"evidence\": \"Two captures of $249.00 on 2026-03-02 (txn 88412, 88413)\",\n  \"attempted\": [\"lookup_order\", \"billing_history\"],\n  \"blocked_by\": \"Refund $249 exceeds agent limit $200\",\n  \"customer_request\": \"Refund the duplicate charge\",\n  \"recommended_action\": \"Approve refund of txn 88413\"\n}</code></pre></div>\n<p>Good handoffs are <strong>self-contained</strong>: the human can act without re-asking the customer.</p>\n<h2 id=\"exam-traps\">Exam traps</h2>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-trap\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Exam trap</div><div class=\"callout-title\">\"Strengthen the system prompt\" for a compliance failure</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Rewording, emphasis, few-shot examples and \"the model will get better\" all leave a non-zero failure rate. If the scenario demands it <em>always</em> happens, choose the gate or hook.</p></div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-trap\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Exam trap</div><div class=\"callout-title\">Gates that fail silently</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>A gate that blocks without a clear error leaves Claude retrying. Return a structured error explaining the missing prerequisite.</p></div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-trap\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Exam trap</div><div class=\"callout-title\">Handing off \"the conversation so far\"</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>A raw transcript dump is not a handoff. Humans need a concise, structured summary with IDs, amounts and the recommended action.</p></div></aside>\n<h2 id=\"practice-questions\">Practice questions</h2>\n<div class=\"quiz-embed\" data-qid=\"a1-gate-01\"></div>\n<div class=\"quiz-embed\" data-qid=\"a1-gate-02\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"build-exercise\">Build exercise</h2>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-exercise\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Build exercise</div><div class=\"callout-title\">Gate a refund tool and design the handoff</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><ol><li>Extend the agent from 1.1 with <code>process_refund</code> and <code>escalate_to_human</code>.</li><li>Implement the session-state gate above; verify with a test that <code>process_refund</code> is blocked before <code>get_customer</code> succeeds.</li><li>Add a refund limit; above it, return a business error pointing to <code>escalate_to_human</code>.</li><li>Define the handoff JSON schema and make <code>escalate_to_human</code> require it.</li><li>Feed a three-concern customer message and check the final reply addresses all three.</li><li>Compare with <code>gated_refund</code> in <code>architect/exercises/solutions.py</code>.</li></ol></div></aside>","headings":[{"id":"what-you-need-to-know","text":"What you need to know"},{"id":"prerequisite-gates","text":"Prerequisite gates"},{"id":"multi-concern-requests","text":"Multi-concern requests"},{"id":"structured-handoff-to-humans","text":"Structured handoff to humans"},{"id":"exam-traps","text":"Exam traps"},{"id":"practice-questions","text":"Practice questions"},{"id":"build-exercise","text":"Build exercise"}],"sources":["Building effective agents — Anthropic | https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/building-effective-agents","Agent SDK: Hooks | https://code.claude.com/docs/en/agent-sdk/hooks","Writing effective tools for agents — Anthropic | https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/writing-tools-for-agents","Strengthen guardrails: reduce hallucinations | https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/test-and-evaluate/strengthen-guardrails/reduce-hallucinations"],"text":" What you need to know This task statement produces the single most common Architect exam pattern key Deterministic vs. probabilistic compliance A prompt instruction (\"always verify the customer's identity before issuing a refund\") makes the behaviour likely . A programmatic gate makes it guaranteed . When the requirement involves money, identity, safety, legal or regulatory compliance — or a production incident shows the step being skipped even occasionally — the answer is enforcement in code. Prompt guidance remains fine for preferences and style tone, formatting, which of several acceptable approaches to try first. Prerequisite gates A prerequisite gate blocks a tool until required earlier steps have succeeded The same logic can live in a PreToolUse hook (1.5) when you can't change the tool itself — e.g. an MCP server you don't own. The error message tells Claude what to do next , so the agent recovers instead of looping. scenario S1 — 12% of refunds skip verification Despite a clear system prompt, logs show the agent occasionally calls process_refund before get_customer when customers sound confident. Adding few shot examples or capital letters improves the rate but can't make it 100%. Answer a programmatic prerequisite that blocks process_refund until verification has succeeded. Multi concern requests \"My order arrived broken, I was charged twice, and I want to change my address.\" Handling this as one blob leads to answering only the first issue. The pattern 1. Decompose into distinct concerns. 2. Investigate each — in parallel where independent — sharing customer context. 3. Synthesise a single, coherent reply that addresses every concern and states what happens next for each. Structured handoff to humans When the agent escalates (5.2), the human agent typically cannot see the transcript or doesn't have time to read it. Pass a structured summary Good handoffs are self contained the human can act without re asking the customer. Exam traps trap \"Strengthen the system prompt\" for a compliance failure Rewording, emphasis, few shot examples and \"the model will get better\" all leave a non zero failure rate. If the scenario demands it always happens, choose the gate or hook. trap Gates that fail silently A gate that blocks without a clear error leaves Claude retrying. Return a structured error explaining the missing prerequisite. trap Handing off \"the conversation so far\" A raw transcript dump is not a handoff. Humans need a concise, structured summary with IDs, amounts and the recommended action. Practice questions quiz a1 gate 01 quiz a1 gate 02 Build exercise exercise Gate a refund tool and design the handoff 1. Extend the agent from 1.1 with process_refund and escalate_to_human . 2. Implement the session state gate above; verify with a test that process_refund is blocked before get_customer succeeds. 3. Add a refund limit; above it, return a business error pointing to escalate_to_human . 4. Define the handoff JSON schema and make escalate_to_human require it. 5. Feed a three concern customer message and check the final reply addresses all three. 6. Compare with gated_refund in architect/exercises/solutions.py . "},{"skill":"1.5","slug":"agent-sdk-hooks","title":"Agent SDK Hooks","summary":"Intercepting tool calls with PreToolUse and PostToolUse hooks — blocking policy violations, redirecting to escalation, normalising heterogeneous tool data — and choosing hooks over prompts when guarantees are needed.","minutes":5,"html":"<h2 id=\"what-you-need-to-know\">What you need to know</h2>\n<p>Hooks are <strong>your code</strong>, invoked by the agent harness at defined lifecycle events. Because they run outside the model, they are <strong>deterministic</strong>: they fire every time, and their decision is final. The two events the exam focuses on:</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Event</th><th>Fires</th><th>Typical use</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>PreToolUse</strong></td><td>After Claude requests a tool, <strong>before</strong> it runs</td><td>Block or modify calls that violate policy; redirect to another path (escalation); require approval</td></tr><tr><td><strong>PostToolUse</strong></td><td><strong>After</strong> a tool returns, before Claude sees the result</td><td>Normalise formats, strip noise, enrich, redact, validate, log</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Other events exist (UserPromptSubmit, Stop, SubagentStop, PreCompact, SessionStart …), but tool interception is the core of this task statement.</p>\n<h2 id=\"blocking-and-redirecting-pretooluse\">Blocking and redirecting: PreToolUse</h2>\n<div class=\"codeblock\"><div class=\"codeblock-head\"><span>PreToolUse hook — refund policy</span><button class=\"copy-btn\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></div><pre><code class=\"lang-python\">async def refund_policy(input_data, tool_use_id, context):\n    if input_data[\"tool_name\"] != \"mcp__billing__process_refund\":\n        return {}\n    amount = input_data[\"tool_input\"][\"amount\"]\n    if amount &gt; 500:\n        return {\"hookSpecificOutput\": {\n            \"hookEventName\": \"PreToolUse\",\n            \"permissionDecision\": \"deny\",\n            \"permissionDecisionReason\":\n                \"Refunds over $500 need human approval. Call escalate_to_human \"\n                \"with the case summary instead.\"}}\n    return {}\n\noptions = ClaudeAgentOptions(hooks={\n    \"PreToolUse\": [HookMatcher(matcher=\"mcp__billing__process_refund\", hooks=[refund_policy])]})</code></pre></div>\n<p>The <strong>reason</strong> is returned to Claude, so the agent changes course (escalates) rather than retrying. That is the \"redirect\" part of the pattern.</p>\n<h2 id=\"normalising-data-posttooluse\">Normalising data: PostToolUse</h2>\n<p>Real systems are messy: one MCP server returns Unix timestamps, another ISO 8601, a legacy API <code>\"03/04/26\"</code>; status codes are numeric in one system and strings in another. Asking the model to interpret every variant wastes tokens and invites mistakes (is <code>03/04</code> March 4 or April 3?).</p>\n<div class=\"codeblock\"><div class=\"codeblock-head\"><span>PostToolUse hook — normalise dates and statuses</span><button class=\"copy-btn\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></div><pre><code class=\"lang-python\">async def normalize(input_data, tool_use_id, context):\n    result = input_data[\"tool_response\"]\n    result = convert_dates_to_iso(result)          # 1709337600 / \"03/04/26\" → \"2024-03-02\"\n    result = map_status_codes(result)              # 3 → \"shipped\"\n    return {\"hookSpecificOutput\": {\"hookEventName\": \"PostToolUse\",\n                                   \"additionalContext\": json.dumps(result)}}</code></pre></div>\n<p>Normalising in a hook gives the model <strong>one consistent representation</strong>, and the logic is testable code instead of prompt text. (<code>additionalContext</code> adds the normalised view next to the raw result; for MCP tools, Claude Code hooks can also replace the output outright with <code>updatedMCPToolOutput</code>. If you own the tool, normalising at the source is simplest of all.)</p>\n<h2 id=\"hooks-vs-prompts-vs-tool-changes\">Hooks vs. prompts vs. tool changes</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Requirement</th><th>Best mechanism</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Must hold every time (limits, compliance, identity)</td><td><strong>Hook</strong> or gate in the tool</td></tr><tr><td>Can't modify the tool (third-party MCP server)</td><td><strong>Hook</strong> around it</td></tr><tr><td>Consistent data format across tools</td><td><strong>PostToolUse</strong> hook (or fix at source if you own it)</td></tr><tr><td>Preference / style / approach</td><td>Prompt or CLAUDE.md</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-compare\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Comparison</div><div class=\"callout-title\">Claude Code hooks vs. Agent SDK hooks</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Same idea, different packaging. In <strong>Claude Code</strong> hooks are shell commands configured in <code>settings.json</code> (exit code 2 blocks a PreToolUse call). In the <strong>Agent SDK</strong> they are callback functions registered in <code>ClaudeAgentOptions(hooks=...)</code>. The exam expects you to pick \"hook\" as the mechanism; the syntax details matter less.</p></div></aside>\n<h2 id=\"exam-traps\">Exam traps</h2>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-trap\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Exam trap</div><div class=\"callout-title\">Adding \"never refund more than $500\" to the system prompt</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Probabilistic. A PreToolUse hook that denies the call is deterministic.</p></div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-trap\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Exam trap</div><div class=\"callout-title\">Asking the model to \"convert all dates to ISO before reasoning\"</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Costs tokens on every call and still fails sometimes. Normalise in a PostToolUse hook.</p></div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-trap\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Exam trap</div><div class=\"callout-title\">Blocking without guidance</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>A deny without a reason makes Claude retry the same call. Always say what to do instead.</p></div></aside>\n<h2 id=\"practice-questions\">Practice questions</h2>\n<div class=\"quiz-embed\" data-qid=\"a1-hook-01\"></div>\n<div class=\"quiz-embed\" data-qid=\"a1-hook-02\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"build-exercise\">Build exercise</h2>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-exercise\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Build exercise</div><div class=\"callout-title\">Two hooks for the support agent</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><ol><li>Wrap your billing tools in an MCP server (or SDK tools) and register a <strong>PreToolUse</strong> hook that denies refunds above a limit with a redirecting reason.</li><li>Make <code>lookup_order</code> return dates in three different formats; add a <strong>PostToolUse</strong> hook that normalises them to ISO 8601.</li><li>Write unit tests for both hook functions — they're plain code.</li><li>Run a conversation that asks for a $750 refund and confirm the agent escalates instead of retrying.</li></ol></div></aside>","headings":[{"id":"what-you-need-to-know","text":"What you need to know"},{"id":"blocking-and-redirecting-pretooluse","text":"Blocking and redirecting: PreToolUse"},{"id":"normalising-data-posttooluse","text":"Normalising data: PostToolUse"},{"id":"hooks-vs-prompts-vs-tool-changes","text":"Hooks vs. prompts vs. tool changes"},{"id":"exam-traps","text":"Exam traps"},{"id":"practice-questions","text":"Practice questions"},{"id":"build-exercise","text":"Build exercise"}],"sources":["Agent SDK: Hooks | https://code.claude.com/docs/en/agent-sdk/hooks","Claude Code hooks reference | https://code.claude.com/docs/en/hooks","Hooks guide | https://code.claude.com/docs/en/hooks-guide","Agent SDK Python reference | https://code.claude.com/docs/en/agent-sdk/python"],"text":" What you need to know Hooks are your code , invoked by the agent harness at defined lifecycle events. Because they run outside the model, they are deterministic they fire every time, and their decision is final. The two events the exam focuses on Event Fires Typical use PreToolUse After Claude requests a tool, before it runs Block or modify calls that violate policy; redirect to another path (escalation); require approval PostToolUse After a tool returns, before Claude sees the result Normalise formats, strip noise, enrich, redact, validate, log Other events exist (UserPromptSubmit, Stop, SubagentStop, PreCompact, SessionStart …), but tool interception is the core of this task statement. Blocking and redirecting PreToolUse The reason is returned to Claude, so the agent changes course (escalates) rather than retrying. That is the \"redirect\" part of the pattern. Normalising data PostToolUse Real systems are messy one MCP server returns Unix timestamps, another ISO 8601, a legacy API \"03/04/26\" ; status codes are numeric in one system and strings in another. Asking the model to interpret every variant wastes tokens and invites mistakes (is 03/04 March 4 or April 3?). Normalising in a hook gives the model one consistent representation , and the logic is testable code instead of prompt text. ( additionalContext adds the normalised view next to the raw result; for MCP tools, Claude Code hooks can also replace the output outright with updatedMCPToolOutput . If you own the tool, normalising at the source is simplest of all.) Hooks vs. prompts vs. tool changes Requirement Best mechanism Must hold every time (limits, compliance, identity) Hook or gate in the tool Can't modify the tool (third party MCP server) Hook around it Consistent data format across tools PostToolUse hook (or fix at source if you own it) Preference / style / approach Prompt or CLAUDE.md compare Claude Code hooks vs. Agent SDK hooks Same idea, different packaging. In Claude Code hooks are shell commands configured in settings.json (exit code 2 blocks a PreToolUse call). In the Agent SDK they are callback functions registered in ClaudeAgentOptions(hooks=...) . The exam expects you to pick \"hook\" as the mechanism; the syntax details matter less. Exam traps trap Adding \"never refund more than $500\" to the system prompt Probabilistic. A PreToolUse hook that denies the call is deterministic. trap Asking the model to \"convert all dates to ISO before reasoning\" Costs tokens on every call and still fails sometimes. Normalise in a PostToolUse hook. trap Blocking without guidance A deny without a reason makes Claude retry the same call. Always say what to do instead. Practice questions quiz a1 hook 01 quiz a1 hook 02 Build exercise exercise Two hooks for the support agent 1. Wrap your billing tools in an MCP server (or SDK tools) and register a PreToolUse hook that denies refunds above a limit with a redirecting reason. 2. Make lookup_order return dates in three different formats; add a PostToolUse hook that normalises them to ISO 8601. 3. Write unit tests for both hook functions — they're plain code. 4. Run a conversation that asks for a $750 refund and confirm the agent escalates instead of retrying. "},{"skill":"1.6","slug":"task-decomposition","title":"Task Decomposition Strategies","summary":"Fixed prompt chains versus dynamic, adaptive decomposition; per-file local passes plus a cross-file integration pass; and matching the decomposition to how predictable the work is.","minutes":5,"html":"<h2 id=\"what-you-need-to-know\">What you need to know</h2>\n<p>Decomposition is the decision of <strong>how to split a large task into steps</strong> — and <strong>who decides</strong> the steps. The exam contrasts two strategies and expects you to pick by how predictable the work is.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Strategy</th><th>Who decides the steps</th><th>Fits</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Prompt chaining</strong> (fixed sequential pipeline)</td><td>Your code, in advance</td><td>Predictable, multi-aspect work: \"check security, then performance, then style\" for every PR; extract → validate → summarise</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Dynamic / adaptive decomposition</strong></td><td>The model, at runtime, based on findings</td><td>Open-ended investigation: \"add tests to this legacy codebase\", \"find why checkout is slow\"</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-key\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key concept</div><div class=\"callout-title\">Rule of thumb</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>If you can write the steps down before seeing the input, <strong>chain</strong> them — it's cheaper, more predictable and easier to test. If the next step depends on what the previous one discovered, let the agent <strong>decompose adaptively</strong>, usually starting by mapping the problem (structure, dependencies, high-impact areas) and then prioritising.</p></div></aside>\n<h2 id=\"adaptive-decomposition-in-practice\">Adaptive decomposition in practice</h2>\n<p>For \"add comprehensive tests to a legacy codebase\", a good agent plan is not \"write tests for every file alphabetically\". It is:</p>\n<ol><li>Map the structure (entry points, modules, dependencies).</li><li>Identify <strong>high-impact</strong> areas (core business logic, frequently changed, bug-prone).</li><li>Create a prioritised plan; start with the highest value.</li><li><strong>Adapt</strong> as discoveries arrive (an untestable module needs refactoring first; shared fixtures emerge).</li></ol>\n<h2 id=\"splitting-large-reviews-local-integration-passes\">Splitting large reviews: local + integration passes</h2>\n<p>A single pass over a 40-file pull request produces <strong>attention dilution</strong>: detailed comments on the first files, superficial ones on the rest, and inconsistent standards (the same pattern flagged in one file, approved in another).</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-scenario\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Scenario</div><div class=\"callout-title\">S5 — inconsistent review depth on a large PR</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p><strong>Fix:</strong> split into <strong>per-file local passes</strong> (each file reviewed with the same criteria and full attention) and a separate <strong>cross-file integration pass</strong> that looks at data flow, interface contracts and duplicated logic across files. The passes can run in parallel; the integration pass receives the per-file summaries.</p></div></aside>\n<p>The same shape works for document sets (per-document extraction, then a reconciliation pass) and codebases (per-module analysis, then architecture synthesis).</p>\n<h2 id=\"choosing-the-granularity\">Choosing the granularity</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Too coarse</strong> — one giant prompt; dilution and missed items.</li><li><strong>Too fine</strong> — dozens of tiny calls; coordination overhead, lost cross-cutting context, and (for coordinators) coverage gaps between narrow subtasks (1.2).</li><li>Aim for steps that are <strong>independently verifiable</strong> — each has a clear output you could check.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"exam-traps\">Exam traps</h2>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-trap\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Exam trap</div><div class=\"callout-title\">Running an autonomous agent for a fixed checklist</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>If the steps are known and identical every time, a prompt chain beats an agent on cost, latency and reliability.</p></div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-trap\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Exam trap</div><div class=\"callout-title\">Forcing a fixed pipeline on open-ended investigation</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>A hard-coded sequence can't react to what it finds. Use an agent that plans and re-plans.</p></div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-trap\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Exam trap</div><div class=\"callout-title\">\"Use a larger context window\" for a shallow review</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>More context doesn't fix attention dilution; splitting the work does.</p></div></aside>\n<h2 id=\"practice-questions\">Practice questions</h2>\n<div class=\"quiz-embed\" data-qid=\"a1-decomp-01\"></div>\n<div class=\"quiz-embed\" data-qid=\"a1-decomp-02\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"build-exercise\">Build exercise</h2>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-exercise\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Build exercise</div><div class=\"callout-title\">Two decompositions of a PR review</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><ol><li>Collect a pull request with 15+ changed files (or synthesise one).</li><li>Review it in <strong>one</strong> prompt. Count findings per file.</li><li>Review it with a <strong>chain</strong>: per-file local passes using identical criteria, then an integration pass that receives only the per-file summaries plus the interface definitions.</li><li>Compare the distribution of findings, the consistency of verdicts on the same pattern, and total tokens.</li><li>Write down which tasks in your own work are \"chain\" tasks and which need adaptive decomposition.</li></ol></div></aside>","headings":[{"id":"what-you-need-to-know","text":"What you need to know"},{"id":"adaptive-decomposition-in-practice","text":"Adaptive decomposition in practice"},{"id":"splitting-large-reviews-local-integration-passes","text":"Splitting large reviews: local + integration passes"},{"id":"choosing-the-granularity","text":"Choosing the granularity"},{"id":"exam-traps","text":"Exam traps"},{"id":"practice-questions","text":"Practice questions"},{"id":"build-exercise","text":"Build exercise"}],"sources":["Building effective agents — Anthropic | https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/building-effective-agents","Chain complex prompts | https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/prompt-engineering/chain-prompts","Claude Code best practices | https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/claude-code-best-practices","How we built our multi-agent research system — Anthropic | https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/multi-agent-research-system"],"text":" What you need to know Decomposition is the decision of how to split a large task into steps — and who decides the steps. The exam contrasts two strategies and expects you to pick by how predictable the work is. Strategy Who decides the steps Fits Prompt chaining (fixed sequential pipeline) Your code, in advance Predictable, multi aspect work \"check security, then performance, then style\" for every PR; extract → validate → summarise Dynamic / adaptive decomposition The model, at runtime, based on findings Open ended investigation \"add tests to this legacy codebase\", \"find why checkout is slow\" key Rule of thumb If you can write the steps down before seeing the input, chain them — it's cheaper, more predictable and easier to test. If the next step depends on what the previous one discovered, let the agent decompose adaptively , usually starting by mapping the problem (structure, dependencies, high impact areas) and then prioritising. Adaptive decomposition in practice For \"add comprehensive tests to a legacy codebase\", a good agent plan is not \"write tests for every file alphabetically\". It is 1. Map the structure (entry points, modules, dependencies). 2. Identify high impact areas (core business logic, frequently changed, bug prone). 3. Create a prioritised plan; start with the highest value. 4. Adapt as discoveries arrive (an untestable module needs refactoring first; shared fixtures emerge). Splitting large reviews local + integration passes A single pass over a 40 file pull request produces attention dilution detailed comments on the first files, superficial ones on the rest, and inconsistent standards (the same pattern flagged in one file, approved in another). scenario S5 — inconsistent review depth on a large PR Fix split into per file local passes (each file reviewed with the same criteria and full attention) and a separate cross file integration pass that looks at data flow, interface contracts and duplicated logic across files. The passes can run in parallel; the integration pass receives the per file summaries. The same shape works for document sets (per document extraction, then a reconciliation pass) and codebases (per module analysis, then architecture synthesis). Choosing the granularity Too coarse — one giant prompt; dilution and missed items. Too fine — dozens of tiny calls; coordination overhead, lost cross cutting context, and (for coordinators) coverage gaps between narrow subtasks (1.2). Aim for steps that are independently verifiable — each has a clear output you could check. Exam traps trap Running an autonomous agent for a fixed checklist If the steps are known and identical every time, a prompt chain beats an agent on cost, latency and reliability. trap Forcing a fixed pipeline on open ended investigation A hard coded sequence can't react to what it finds. Use an agent that plans and re plans. trap \"Use a larger context window\" for a shallow review More context doesn't fix attention dilution; splitting the work does. Practice questions quiz a1 decomp 01 quiz a1 decomp 02 Build exercise exercise Two decompositions of a PR review 1. Collect a pull request with 15+ changed files (or synthesise one). 2. Review it in one prompt. Count findings per file. 3. Review it with a chain per file local passes using identical criteria, then an integration pass that receives only the per file summaries plus the interface definitions. 4. Compare the distribution of findings, the consistency of verdicts on the same pattern, and total tokens. 5. Write down which tasks in your own work are \"chain\" tasks and which need adaptive decomposition. "},{"skill":"1.7","slug":"session-state-resumption","title":"Session State, Resumption & Forking","summary":"Named sessions and --resume, fork_session for exploring alternatives from a shared baseline, telling a resumed session what changed, and when a fresh start with a summary beats resuming.","minutes":5,"html":"<h2 id=\"what-you-need-to-know\">What you need to know</h2>\n<p>Claude Code and the Agent SDK persist conversations as <strong>sessions</strong>. A session holds the message history — including every tool call and result. You can <strong>resume</strong> it, <strong>fork</strong> it, or deliberately <strong>start fresh</strong>. The exam tests which to choose.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Operation</th><th>How</th><th>Use when</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Resume</strong></td><td><code>claude --resume &lt;session-id or name&gt;</code> / <code>claude --continue</code> (most recent); SDK <code>resume=session_id</code></td><td>Continue the same line of work; the prior context is still valid</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Fork</strong></td><td>SDK <code>fork_session=True</code> with <code>resume=...</code>; Claude Code <code>--fork-session</code></td><td>Explore <strong>divergent approaches</strong> from the same baseline without them contaminating each other</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Fresh start + summary</strong></td><td>New session seeded with a structured summary of findings</td><td>Prior tool results are <strong>stale</strong> or the context is cluttered/degraded</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Naming sessions (e.g. <code>--resume auth-investigation</code>, or <code>/rename</code>) makes multi-day investigations easy to return to.</p>\n<h2 id=\"forking-for-alternatives\">Forking for alternatives</h2>\n<div class=\"codeblock\"><div class=\"codeblock-head\"><span>fork two approaches from one analysis</span><button class=\"copy-btn\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></div><pre><code class=\"lang-python\">base = await run(prompt=\"Analyse the payment module and its tests.\", options=opts)\n\nopt_a = ClaudeAgentOptions(resume=base.session_id, fork_session=True)\nopt_b = ClaudeAgentOptions(resume=base.session_id, fork_session=True)\nplan_a = await run(\"Propose a refactor using the strategy pattern.\", options=opt_a)\nplan_b = await run(\"Propose a refactor using a state machine.\", options=opt_b)</code></pre></div>\n<p>Both forks share the expensive analysis but evolve independently. Resuming the <em>same</em> session twice would interleave both explorations in one history.</p>\n<h2 id=\"resuming-after-things-changed\">Resuming after things changed</h2>\n<p>A resumed session \"remembers\" file contents and tool results from <strong>when it last ran</strong>. If teammates have since edited files, Claude may reason from outdated code.</p>\n<ul><li>If <strong>a few known files</strong> changed: resume and <strong>tell Claude precisely what changed</strong> (\"<code>billing.py</code> and <code>refunds.py</code> were modified since; re-read them before continuing\").</li><li>If <strong>much</strong> changed or the old context is cluttered: <strong>start a new session</strong> and seed it with a <strong>structured summary</strong> of what was learned (decisions, open questions, key file locations) — not the stale raw results.</li></ul>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-scenario\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Scenario</div><div class=\"callout-title\">S4 — resuming a two-day investigation</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Yesterday's session mapped a legacy module. Overnight, a refactor landed that touched 30 files. Resuming makes Claude cite functions that no longer exist. <strong>Better:</strong> fresh session + summary of yesterday's conclusions, then re-explore the changed areas.</p></div></aside>\n<h2 id=\"session-state-vs-application-state\">Session state vs. application state</h2>\n<p>Sessions persist the <em>conversation</em>. Business state that must be <strong>authoritative</strong> — which customer is verified, which refund was issued — belongs in your <strong>application</strong> (database, gate variables from 1.4), not only in the model's memory of the chat. Resuming a session must never be the mechanism that \"remembers\" a verification.</p>\n<h2 id=\"exam-traps\">Exam traps</h2>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-trap\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Exam trap</div><div class=\"callout-title\">Resuming the same session to compare two approaches</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>The approaches contaminate each other. Fork.</p></div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-trap\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Exam trap</div><div class=\"callout-title\">Resuming with stale results and hoping Claude notices</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>It usually won't. Tell it what changed, or start fresh with a summary.</p></div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-trap\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Exam trap</div><div class=\"callout-title\">Replaying the full old transcript into a new session</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>That brings the stale data back. Seed with a concise, structured summary instead.</p></div></aside>\n<h2 id=\"practice-questions\">Practice questions</h2>\n<div class=\"quiz-embed\" data-qid=\"a1-sess-01\"></div>\n<div class=\"quiz-embed\" data-qid=\"a1-sess-02\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"build-exercise\">Build exercise</h2>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-exercise\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Build exercise</div><div class=\"callout-title\">Resume, fork and fresh-start</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><ol><li>Run an analysis session on a small repo; note its session ID and give it a name.</li><li>Fork it twice with the Agent SDK and request two different refactoring approaches; confirm the histories are independent.</li><li>Edit two files by hand. Resume the original session without telling Claude; ask about those files and observe stale answers.</li><li>Resume again, this time stating which files changed; compare.</li><li>Write a 10-line structured summary and start a fresh session from it; compare quality and token usage.</li></ol></div></aside>","headings":[{"id":"what-you-need-to-know","text":"What you need to know"},{"id":"forking-for-alternatives","text":"Forking for alternatives"},{"id":"resuming-after-things-changed","text":"Resuming after things changed"},{"id":"session-state-vs-application-state","text":"Session state vs. application state"},{"id":"exam-traps","text":"Exam traps"},{"id":"practice-questions","text":"Practice questions"},{"id":"build-exercise","text":"Build exercise"}],"sources":["Agent SDK: Sessions | https://code.claude.com/docs/en/agent-sdk/sessions","CLI reference | https://code.claude.com/docs/en/cli-reference","Common workflows | https://code.claude.com/docs/en/common-workflows","Agent SDK Python reference | https://code.claude.com/docs/en/agent-sdk/python"],"text":" What you need to know Claude Code and the Agent SDK persist conversations as sessions . A session holds the message history — including every tool call and result. You can resume it, fork it, or deliberately start fresh . The exam tests which to choose. Operation How Use when Resume claude resume <session id or name / claude continue (most recent); SDK resume=session_id Continue the same line of work; the prior context is still valid Fork SDK fork_session=True with resume=... ; Claude Code fork session Explore divergent approaches from the same baseline without them contaminating each other Fresh start + summary New session seeded with a structured summary of findings Prior tool results are stale or the context is cluttered/degraded Naming sessions (e.g. resume auth investigation , or /rename ) makes multi day investigations easy to return to. Forking for alternatives Both forks share the expensive analysis but evolve independently. Resuming the same session twice would interleave both explorations in one history. Resuming after things changed A resumed session \"remembers\" file contents and tool results from when it last ran . If teammates have since edited files, Claude may reason from outdated code. If a few known files changed resume and tell Claude precisely what changed (\" billing.py and refunds.py were modified since; re read them before continuing\"). If much changed or the old context is cluttered start a new session and seed it with a structured summary of what was learned (decisions, open questions, key file locations) — not the stale raw results. scenario S4 — resuming a two day investigation Yesterday's session mapped a legacy module. Overnight, a refactor landed that touched 30 files. Resuming makes Claude cite functions that no longer exist. Better fresh session + summary of yesterday's conclusions, then re explore the changed areas. Session state vs. application state Sessions persist the conversation . Business state that must be authoritative — which customer is verified, which refund was issued — belongs in your application (database, gate variables from 1.4), not only in the model's memory of the chat. Resuming a session must never be the mechanism that \"remembers\" a verification. Exam traps trap Resuming the same session to compare two approaches The approaches contaminate each other. Fork. trap Resuming with stale results and hoping Claude notices It usually won't. Tell it what changed, or start fresh with a summary. trap Replaying the full old transcript into a new session That brings the stale data back. Seed with a concise, structured summary instead. Practice questions quiz a1 sess 01 quiz a1 sess 02 Build exercise exercise Resume, fork and fresh start 1. Run an analysis session on a small repo; note its session ID and give it a name. 2. Fork it twice with the Agent SDK and request two different refactoring approaches; confirm the histories are independent. 3. Edit two files by hand. Resume the original session without telling Claude; ask about those files and observe stale answers. 4. Resume again, this time stating which files changed; compare. 5. Write a 10 line structured summary and start a fresh session from it; compare quality and token usage. "},{"skill":"2.1","slug":"tool-interface-design","title":"Tool Interface Design","summary":"Tool descriptions are the primary signal Claude uses to choose tools — what a good description contains, how to resolve overlapping tools by renaming and splitting, and how system-prompt wording can accidentally override tool choice.","minutes":5,"html":"<h2 id=\"what-you-need-to-know\">What you need to know</h2>\n<p>Claude decides which tool to call from the <strong>tool name, description and input schema</strong>. Of these, the <strong>description</strong> carries the most weight. Anthropic's guidance on the agent–computer interface (ACI) is to invest in tools the way you would in a UI for a new colleague: clear, unambiguous, hard to misuse.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-key\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key concept</div><div class=\"callout-title\">What a good tool description contains</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><ul><li><strong>What it does</strong> and <strong>what it returns</strong> — in plain language.</li><li><strong>When to use it</strong> — and when to use a <em>different</em> tool instead.</li><li><strong>Input formats</strong> with examples (<code>order_id like \"ORD-12345\"</code>, ISO dates).</li><li><strong>Example queries</strong> it is meant for.</li><li><strong>Edge cases and limits</strong> (max results, what happens if nothing matches). Aim for several sentences, not a fragment.</li></ul></div></aside>\n<h2 id=\"the-classic-failure-overlapping-tools\">The classic failure: overlapping tools</h2>\n<div class=\"codeblock\"><div class=\"codeblock-head\"><span>before — minimal, overlapping descriptions</span><button class=\"copy-btn\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></div><pre><code class=\"lang-json\">{\"name\": \"get_customer\", \"description\": \"Retrieves customer information.\"}\n{\"name\": \"lookup_order\", \"description\": \"Retrieves order information.\"}</code></pre></div>\n<p>When a customer writes \"check my order #12345\", Claude sometimes calls <code>get_customer</code> — both descriptions are vague and both \"retrieve information\". The <strong>first fix</strong> is the descriptions:</p>\n<div class=\"codeblock\"><div class=\"codeblock-head\"><span>after — explicit purpose and boundaries</span><button class=\"copy-btn\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></div><pre><code class=\"lang-json\">{\"name\": \"get_customer\",\n \"description\": \"Find and verify a customer account by email or phone. Returns customer_id, name, tier and verification status. Use this FIRST when you need to know who the customer is. Do NOT use it to look up orders — use lookup_order.\"}\n{\"name\": \"lookup_order\",\n \"description\": \"Get one order's status, items, amounts and shipping events by order ID (format ORD-12345, also accepts #12345). Use when the customer mentions an order number or asks where an order is. Requires no customer lookup first.\"}</code></pre></div>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-compare\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Comparison</div><div class=\"callout-title\">Description fix vs. heavier alternatives</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Few-shot examples, a routing classifier or merging tools can also help — but they add cost and complexity and don't fix the <strong>root cause</strong> (Claude can't tell the tools apart). The exam's \"most effective first step\" is almost always: <strong>rewrite the descriptions</strong>.</p></div></aside>\n<h2 id=\"splitting-and-renaming\">Splitting and renaming</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Split generic tools.</strong> <code>analyze_document</code> that \"extracts, summarizes or verifies depending on parameters\" is hard to use correctly. Prefer <code>extract_data_points</code>, <code>summarize_content</code>, <code>verify_claim_against_source</code> — each with a precise contract.</li><li><strong>Rename to remove ambiguity.</strong> If a custom <code>analyze_dependencies</code> keeps being confused with the built-in Grep, rename it to <code>extract_web_results</code> / <code>build_import_graph</code> — names that say exactly what is different.</li><li><strong>Consolidate</strong> when tools are always called together in sequence (e.g. <code>get_user</code> → <code>get_user_orders</code>) — one well-described tool can reduce turns. Balance this against keeping contracts simple.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"return-values-are-part-of-the-interface\">Return values are part of the interface</h2>\n<p>Return <strong>meaningful, compact</strong> data: names over opaque IDs where possible, only the fields the agent needs, and clear errors (2.2). Huge raw payloads waste context (5.1).</p>\n<h2 id=\"system-prompts-can-override-tool-choice\">System prompts can override tool choice</h2>\n<p>A system prompt that says \"always <strong>analyze</strong> the customer's documents carefully\" may push Claude toward any tool with \"analyze\" in its name. When a tool is chosen for the wrong reason, check the <strong>system prompt for keyword-sensitive instructions</strong> that conflict with the tool descriptions.</p>\n<h2 id=\"exam-traps\">Exam traps</h2>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-trap\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Exam trap</div><div class=\"callout-title\">\"Add a routing classifier\" as the first fix</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Over-engineered for a problem caused by unclear descriptions.</p></div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-trap\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Exam trap</div><div class=\"callout-title\">\"Merge the two tools into one\"</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Sometimes valid, but it usually hides the ambiguity inside parameters instead of resolving it.</p></div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-trap\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Exam trap</div><div class=\"callout-title\">Few-shot examples as the primary fix for tool confusion</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>They help, cost tokens on every call, and don't scale to new cases. Fix the descriptions first.</p></div></aside>\n<h2 id=\"practice-questions\">Practice questions</h2>\n<div class=\"quiz-embed\" data-qid=\"a2-tool-01\"></div>\n<div class=\"quiz-embed\" data-qid=\"a2-tool-02\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"build-exercise\">Build exercise</h2>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-exercise\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Build exercise</div><div class=\"callout-title\">Measure description quality</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><ol><li>Create 30 test prompts for your support agent, labelled with the correct tool.</li><li>Run them with one-line descriptions; record tool-selection accuracy.</li><li>Rewrite descriptions with purpose, when-to-use, input format, examples and boundaries; rerun.</li><li>Add a system-prompt line \"Always analyze requests thoroughly\" plus a tool named <code>analyze_ticket</code>; observe the selection shift. Remove it.</li><li>Split one generic tool into two purpose-specific tools and measure again.</li></ol></div></aside>","headings":[{"id":"what-you-need-to-know","text":"What you need to know"},{"id":"the-classic-failure-overlapping-tools","text":"The classic failure: overlapping tools"},{"id":"splitting-and-renaming","text":"Splitting and renaming"},{"id":"return-values-are-part-of-the-interface","text":"Return values are part of the interface"},{"id":"system-prompts-can-override-tool-choice","text":"System prompts can override tool choice"},{"id":"exam-traps","text":"Exam traps"},{"id":"practice-questions","text":"Practice questions"},{"id":"build-exercise","text":"Build exercise"}],"sources":["Writing effective tools for agents — Anthropic | https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/writing-tools-for-agents","Tool use overview | https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/agents-and-tools/tool-use/overview","How to implement tool use | https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/agents-and-tools/tool-use/implement-tool-use","Building effective agents — Anthropic | https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/building-effective-agents"],"text":" What you need to know Claude decides which tool to call from the tool name, description and input schema . Of these, the description carries the most weight. Anthropic's guidance on the agent–computer interface (ACI) is to invest in tools the way you would in a UI for a new colleague clear, unambiguous, hard to misuse. key What a good tool description contains What it does and what it returns — in plain language. When to use it — and when to use a different tool instead. Input formats with examples ( order_id like \"ORD 12345\" , ISO dates). Example queries it is meant for. Edge cases and limits (max results, what happens if nothing matches). Aim for several sentences, not a fragment. The classic failure overlapping tools When a customer writes \"check my order 12345\", Claude sometimes calls get_customer — both descriptions are vague and both \"retrieve information\". The first fix is the descriptions compare Description fix vs. heavier alternatives Few shot examples, a routing classifier or merging tools can also help — but they add cost and complexity and don't fix the root cause (Claude can't tell the tools apart). The exam's \"most effective first step\" is almost always rewrite the descriptions . Splitting and renaming Split generic tools. analyze_document that \"extracts, summarizes or verifies depending on parameters\" is hard to use correctly. Prefer extract_data_points , summarize_content , verify_claim_against_source — each with a precise contract. Rename to remove ambiguity. If a custom analyze_dependencies keeps being confused with the built in Grep, rename it to extract_web_results / build_import_graph — names that say exactly what is different. Consolidate when tools are always called together in sequence (e.g. get_user → get_user_orders ) — one well described tool can reduce turns. Balance this against keeping contracts simple. Return values are part of the interface Return meaningful, compact data names over opaque IDs where possible, only the fields the agent needs, and clear errors (2.2). Huge raw payloads waste context (5.1). System prompts can override tool choice A system prompt that says \"always analyze the customer's documents carefully\" may push Claude toward any tool with \"analyze\" in its name. When a tool is chosen for the wrong reason, check the system prompt for keyword sensitive instructions that conflict with the tool descriptions. Exam traps trap \"Add a routing classifier\" as the first fix Over engineered for a problem caused by unclear descriptions. trap \"Merge the two tools into one\" Sometimes valid, but it usually hides the ambiguity inside parameters instead of resolving it. trap Few shot examples as the primary fix for tool confusion They help, cost tokens on every call, and don't scale to new cases. Fix the descriptions first. Practice questions quiz a2 tool 01 quiz a2 tool 02 Build exercise exercise Measure description quality 1. Create 30 test prompts for your support agent, labelled with the correct tool. 2. Run them with one line descriptions; record tool selection accuracy. 3. Rewrite descriptions with purpose, when to use, input format, examples and boundaries; rerun. 4. Add a system prompt line \"Always analyze requests thoroughly\" plus a tool named analyze_ticket ; observe the selection shift. Remove it. 5. Split one generic tool into two purpose specific tools and measure again. "},{"skill":"2.2","slug":"structured-tool-errors","title":"Structured Error Responses for MCP Tools","summary":"The isError flag, error categories that tell the agent whether to retry, distinguishing \"no results\" from \"lookup failed\", and recovering locally before propagating to the coordinator.","minutes":5,"html":"<h2 id=\"what-you-need-to-know\">What you need to know</h2>\n<p>Agents recover from errors only as well as the errors describe themselves. A tool that returns <code>\"Operation failed\"</code> gives Claude nothing to work with; one that says <strong>what kind</strong> of failure happened, <strong>whether retrying can help</strong>, and <strong>what to do instead</strong> lets the agent self-correct.</p>\n<p>In MCP, a tool result sets <strong><code>isError: true</code></strong> to signal failure (in the raw Messages API the equivalent is <code>is_error: true</code> on the <code>tool_result</code>). The content of the error is yours to design.</p>\n<h2 id=\"error-categories\">Error categories</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Category</th><th>Examples</th><th>Retryable?</th><th>Agent should</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Transient</strong></td><td>Timeout, 503, connection reset, rate limit</td><td>Yes</td><td>Retry with backoff (ideally inside the tool), then report</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Validation</strong></td><td>Malformed order ID, missing field, bad date</td><td>Yes, after fixing input</td><td>Correct the input or ask the user</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Business</strong></td><td>Refund exceeds policy, order already shipped</td><td>No</td><td>Explain to the user, offer alternatives, escalate</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Permission</strong></td><td>Not authorised, identity not verified</td><td>No</td><td>Escalate or complete the prerequisite</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"codeblock\"><div class=\"codeblock-head\"><span>structured error from an MCP tool</span><button class=\"copy-btn\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></div><pre><code class=\"lang-json\">{\n  \"isError\": true,\n  \"content\": [{\n    \"type\": \"text\",\n    \"text\": \"{\\\"errorCategory\\\": \\\"business\\\", \\\"isRetryable\\\": false, \\\"message\\\": \\\"Order ORD-1182 shipped on 2026-03-02 and can no longer be cancelled. A return can be started after delivery.\\\", \\\"suggestedAction\\\": \\\"offer_return\\\"}\"\n  }]\n}</code></pre></div>\n<p>The human-readable message matters as much as the category: Claude will often relay it to the customer.</p>\n<h2 id=\"empty-result-failure\">Empty result ≠ failure</h2>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-key\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key concept</div><div class=\"callout-title\">The most-tested distinction in this task statement</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><ul><li><code>search_orders(email)</code> <strong>succeeded</strong> and found nothing → return a normal result: <code>{\"orders\": [], \"searched\": \"email=…\"}</code>.</li><li><code>search_orders(email)</code> <strong>failed</strong> (database unreachable) → return an <strong>error</strong> with <code>isError: true</code>.</li></ul>\n<p>If a failure is returned as an empty list, the agent concludes \"this customer has no orders\" and acts on false information. If an empty result is returned as an error, the agent retries pointlessly or escalates.</p></div></aside>\n<h2 id=\"local-recovery-before-propagation\">Local recovery before propagation</h2>\n<p>In multi-agent systems, a subagent should handle what it can <strong>locally</strong> — retry transient failures, fix validation errors — and only propagate what it cannot resolve. When it does propagate, it returns <strong>structured context</strong>: failure type, what was attempted, partial results, alternatives (see 5.3). The coordinator then decides: retry elsewhere, continue with partial coverage, or escalate.</p>\n<h2 id=\"exam-traps\">Exam traps</h2>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-trap\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Exam trap</div><div class=\"callout-title\">Generic \"Operation failed\" errors</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>They prevent appropriate recovery. Categories + retryability + description.</p></div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-trap\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Exam trap</div><div class=\"callout-title\">Retrying business or permission errors</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>\"Refund exceeds limit\" will fail every time. Retrying wastes turns and annoys the customer.</p></div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-trap\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Exam trap</div><div class=\"callout-title\">Swallowing exceptions and returning <code>[]</code></div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Silent failure presented as success — the worst option on almost every exam item.</p></div></aside>\n<h2 id=\"practice-questions\">Practice questions</h2>\n<div class=\"quiz-embed\" data-qid=\"a2-err-01\"></div>\n<div class=\"quiz-embed\" data-qid=\"a2-err-02\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"build-exercise\">Build exercise</h2>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-exercise\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Build exercise</div><div class=\"callout-title\">Structured errors for an MCP server</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><ol><li>Build a small MCP server with <code>lookup_order</code> and <code>process_refund</code> (Python <code>mcp</code> SDK, stdio transport).</li><li>Implement a <code>tool_error(category, retryable, message, **extra)</code> helper returning <code>isError: true</code> with a JSON body.</li><li>Simulate each category; check Claude's behaviour: retry for transient, corrected input for validation, explanation for business, escalation for permission.</li><li>Make one lookup return an empty list and one raise a connection error; confirm the agent treats them differently.</li><li>Compare with <code>tool_error</code> and its tests in <code>architect/exercises/</code>.</li></ol></div></aside>","headings":[{"id":"what-you-need-to-know","text":"What you need to know"},{"id":"error-categories","text":"Error categories"},{"id":"empty-result-failure","text":"Empty result ≠ failure"},{"id":"local-recovery-before-propagation","text":"Local recovery before propagation"},{"id":"exam-traps","text":"Exam traps"},{"id":"practice-questions","text":"Practice questions"},{"id":"build-exercise","text":"Build exercise"}],"sources":["MCP — Server concepts | https://modelcontextprotocol.io/docs/learn/server-concepts","Writing effective tools for agents — Anthropic | https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/writing-tools-for-agents","How to implement tool use | https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/agents-and-tools/tool-use/implement-tool-use","Errors (Claude API) | https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/api/errors"],"text":" What you need to know Agents recover from errors only as well as the errors describe themselves. A tool that returns \"Operation failed\" gives Claude nothing to work with; one that says what kind of failure happened, whether retrying can help , and what to do instead lets the agent self correct. In MCP, a tool result sets isError true to signal failure (in the raw Messages API the equivalent is is_error true on the tool_result ). The content of the error is yours to design. Error categories Category Examples Retryable? Agent should Transient Timeout, 503, connection reset, rate limit Yes Retry with backoff (ideally inside the tool), then report Validation Malformed order ID, missing field, bad date Yes, after fixing input Correct the input or ask the user Business Refund exceeds policy, order already shipped No Explain to the user, offer alternatives, escalate Permission Not authorised, identity not verified No Escalate or complete the prerequisite The human readable message matters as much as the category Claude will often relay it to the customer. Empty result ≠ failure key The most tested distinction in this task statement search_orders(email) succeeded and found nothing → return a normal result \"orders\" , \"searched\" \"email=…\" . search_orders(email) failed (database unreachable) → return an error with isError true . If a failure is returned as an empty list, the agent concludes \"this customer has no orders\" and acts on false information. If an empty result is returned as an error, the agent retries pointlessly or escalates. Local recovery before propagation In multi agent systems, a subagent should handle what it can locally — retry transient failures, fix validation errors — and only propagate what it cannot resolve. When it does propagate, it returns structured context failure type, what was attempted, partial results, alternatives (see 5.3). The coordinator then decides retry elsewhere, continue with partial coverage, or escalate. Exam traps trap Generic \"Operation failed\" errors They prevent appropriate recovery. Categories + retryability + description. trap Retrying business or permission errors \"Refund exceeds limit\" will fail every time. Retrying wastes turns and annoys the customer. trap Swallowing exceptions and returning Silent failure presented as success — the worst option on almost every exam item. Practice questions quiz a2 err 01 quiz a2 err 02 Build exercise exercise Structured errors for an MCP server 1. Build a small MCP server with lookup_order and process_refund (Python mcp SDK, stdio transport). 2. Implement a tool_error(category, retryable, message, extra) helper returning isError true with a JSON body. 3. Simulate each category; check Claude's behaviour retry for transient, corrected input for validation, explanation for business, escalation for permission. 4. Make one lookup return an empty list and one raise a connection error; confirm the agent treats them differently. 5. Compare with tool_error and its tests in architect/exercises/ . "},{"skill":"2.3","slug":"tool-distribution-tool-choice","title":"Tool Distribution & tool_choice","summary":"Why too many tools degrade selection, how to scope tools per agent role, when a cross-role agent deserves one narrow extra tool, and how tool_choice auto, any and forced tool control behaviour.","minutes":5,"html":"<h2 id=\"what-you-need-to-know\">What you need to know</h2>\n<p>Two levers control <em>which</em> tools an agent uses: <strong>which tools it has</strong> (distribution) and <strong>whether it must use one</strong> (<code>tool_choice</code>).</p>\n<h2 id=\"distribution-fewer-role-specific-tools\">Distribution: fewer, role-specific tools</h2>\n<p>Giving one agent every tool in the system — 18 tools spanning search, documents, billing and reporting — <strong>degrades selection reliability</strong>: more similar options, longer tool lists to reason over, and agents wandering outside their role (a synthesis agent starting web searches itself).</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-key\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key concept</div><div class=\"callout-title\">Scope tools to the role</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><ul><li>Each agent gets the <strong>4–5 tools</strong> its role needs.</li><li>A web-search subagent gets search/fetch; a document subagent gets Read/Grep; a synthesis agent gets none or only verification.</li><li>Restricting tools is also <strong>least privilege</strong> — a review subagent without Write can't modify code.</li></ul></div></aside>\n<h3 id=\"the-cross-role-exception\">The cross-role exception</h3>\n<p>Sometimes an agent frequently needs one capability from another role. Example: the synthesis agent often needs to <strong>verify a single fact</strong>, and each round-trip through the coordinator to the search agent adds latency. Options:</p>\n<ul><li>Give it the <strong>full</strong> search toolset → it starts doing research itself (role creep).</li><li>Give it a <strong>narrow, scoped tool</strong> such as <code>verify_fact(claim, source_url)</code> → handles the frequent simple case; complex research still goes through the coordinator. ✔</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"tool-choice\">tool_choice</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Value</th><th>Behaviour</th><th>Use for</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>{\"type\": \"auto\"}</code> (default)</td><td>Claude may call a tool <strong>or</strong> answer in text</td><td>Normal agent turns</td></tr><tr><td><code>{\"type\": \"any\"}</code></td><td>Claude <strong>must</strong> call a tool — any of the provided ones</td><td>Guarantee structured output when several schemas/tools are valid</td></tr><tr><td><code>{\"type\": \"tool\", \"name\": \"extract_invoice\"}</code></td><td>Claude <strong>must</strong> call that tool</td><td>Force a specific step (e.g. always run <code>extract_metadata</code> first)</td></tr><tr><td><code>{\"type\": \"none\"}</code></td><td>No tool calls</td><td>Final summarisation turns</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"codeblock\"><div class=\"codeblock-head\"><span>guarantee structured output, unknown document type</span><button class=\"copy-btn\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></div><pre><code class=\"lang-python\">resp = client.messages.create(\n    model=MODEL, max_tokens=2048,\n    tools=[invoice_schema_tool, receipt_schema_tool, contract_schema_tool],\n    tool_choice={\"type\": \"any\"},          # never a plain-text reply\n    messages=[{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": doc}],\n)</code></pre></div>\n<p>With <code>auto</code>, Claude might reply \"This looks like an invoice from …\" in prose — a parse failure downstream. With <code>any</code>, it must pick one of the extraction tools. When you need a <em>specific</em> first step, force that tool, then continue with <code>auto</code> in later turns.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><div class=\"callout-title\">Forced tool use and extended thinking</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p><code>any</code> and forced-tool <code>tool_choice</code> are not compatible with extended thinking; use <code>auto</code> (with clear instructions) when thinking is enabled.</p></div></aside>\n<h2 id=\"exam-traps\">Exam traps</h2>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-trap\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Exam trap</div><div class=\"callout-title\">\"Give every agent all tools for flexibility\"</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Selection reliability drops and roles blur. Scope tools.</p></div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-trap\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Exam trap</div><div class=\"callout-title\">Using <code>auto</code> when the requirement is \"always return structured data\"</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p><code>auto</code> allows a text reply. Use <code>any</code> (or a forced tool).</p></div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-trap\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Exam trap</div><div class=\"callout-title\">Forcing a specific tool when the correct tool depends on the input</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Forced choice is for a known step. When the document type is unknown, <code>any</code> lets Claude choose among schemas.</p></div></aside>\n<h2 id=\"practice-questions\">Practice questions</h2>\n<div class=\"quiz-embed\" data-qid=\"a2-dist-01\"></div>\n<div class=\"quiz-embed\" data-qid=\"a2-dist-02\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"build-exercise\">Build exercise</h2>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-exercise\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Build exercise</div><div class=\"callout-title\">Tool scoping and tool_choice experiments</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><ol><li>Give one agent 15 tools; run 30 labelled prompts and record selection accuracy.</li><li>Split into three role agents with 4–5 tools each behind a coordinator; rerun.</li><li>For extraction, run 20 mixed documents with <code>auto</code> and count text replies; switch to <code>any</code>.</li><li>Force <code>extract_metadata</code> for the first turn, then switch to <code>auto</code>.</li><li>Add a narrow <code>verify_fact</code> tool to the synthesis agent and measure latency saved.</li></ol></div></aside>","headings":[{"id":"what-you-need-to-know","text":"What you need to know"},{"id":"distribution-fewer-role-specific-tools","text":"Distribution: fewer, role-specific tools"},{"id":"tool-choice","text":"tool_choice"},{"id":"exam-traps","text":"Exam traps"},{"id":"practice-questions","text":"Practice questions"},{"id":"build-exercise","text":"Build exercise"}],"sources":["How to implement tool use | https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/agents-and-tools/tool-use/implement-tool-use","Tool use overview | https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/agents-and-tools/tool-use/overview","Agent SDK: Subagents | https://code.claude.com/docs/en/agent-sdk/subagents","Writing effective tools for agents — Anthropic | https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/writing-tools-for-agents"],"text":" What you need to know Two levers control which tools an agent uses which tools it has (distribution) and whether it must use one ( tool_choice ). Distribution fewer, role specific tools Giving one agent every tool in the system — 18 tools spanning search, documents, billing and reporting — degrades selection reliability more similar options, longer tool lists to reason over, and agents wandering outside their role (a synthesis agent starting web searches itself). key Scope tools to the role Each agent gets the 4–5 tools its role needs. A web search subagent gets search/fetch; a document subagent gets Read/Grep; a synthesis agent gets none or only verification. Restricting tools is also least privilege — a review subagent without Write can't modify code. The cross role exception Sometimes an agent frequently needs one capability from another role. Example the synthesis agent often needs to verify a single fact , and each round trip through the coordinator to the search agent adds latency. Options Give it the full search toolset → it starts doing research itself (role creep). Give it a narrow, scoped tool such as verify_fact(claim, source_url) → handles the frequent simple case; complex research still goes through the coordinator. ✔ tool_choice Value Behaviour Use for \"type\" \"auto\" (default) Claude may call a tool or answer in text Normal agent turns \"type\" \"any\" Claude must call a tool — any of the provided ones Guarantee structured output when several schemas/tools are valid \"type\" \"tool\", \"name\" \"extract_invoice\" Claude must call that tool Force a specific step (e.g. always run extract_metadata first) \"type\" \"none\" No tool calls Final summarisation turns With auto , Claude might reply \"This looks like an invoice from …\" in prose — a parse failure downstream. With any , it must pick one of the extraction tools. When you need a specific first step, force that tool, then continue with auto in later turns. warn Forced tool use and extended thinking any and forced tool tool_choice are not compatible with extended thinking; use auto (with clear instructions) when thinking is enabled. Exam traps trap \"Give every agent all tools for flexibility\" Selection reliability drops and roles blur. Scope tools. trap Using auto when the requirement is \"always return structured data\" auto allows a text reply. Use any (or a forced tool). trap Forcing a specific tool when the correct tool depends on the input Forced choice is for a known step. When the document type is unknown, any lets Claude choose among schemas. Practice questions quiz a2 dist 01 quiz a2 dist 02 Build exercise exercise Tool scoping and tool_choice experiments 1. Give one agent 15 tools; run 30 labelled prompts and record selection accuracy. 2. Split into three role agents with 4–5 tools each behind a coordinator; rerun. 3. For extraction, run 20 mixed documents with auto and count text replies; switch to any . 4. Force extract_metadata for the first turn, then switch to auto . 5. Add a narrow verify_fact tool to the synthesis agent and measure latency saved. "},{"skill":"2.4","slug":"mcp-server-integration","title":"MCP Server Integration","summary":"Project-scoped .mcp.json versus user-scoped configuration, environment-variable expansion for secrets, tools from many servers at once, resources for catalogs, and when to build versus reuse a server.","minutes":5,"html":"<h2 id=\"what-you-need-to-know\">What you need to know</h2>\n<p>MCP (Model Context Protocol) lets Claude Code and Agent SDK agents use <strong>tools, resources and prompts</strong> exposed by external servers. As an architect you decide <strong>where</strong> a server is configured, <strong>how secrets get in</strong>, and <strong>what</strong> each server should expose.</p>\n<h2 id=\"scopes\">Scopes</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Scope</th><th>Stored in</th><th>Shared?</th><th>Use for</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Project</strong></td><td><code>.mcp.json</code> in the repo root</td><td>Yes — committed, everyone on the team gets it</td><td>Team integrations: GitHub, Jira, the internal docs server</td></tr><tr><td><strong>User</strong></td><td><code>~/.claude.json</code></td><td>No</td><td>Personal or experimental servers, available in all your projects</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Local</strong></td><td>Project entry in <code>~/.claude.json</code></td><td>No</td><td>Personal servers for one project only</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-key\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key concept</div><div class=\"callout-title\">Team vs. personal</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>If a new teammate \"doesn't have the Jira tools\", the server was configured at <strong>user</strong> scope on someone's machine. Team servers go in <strong><code>.mcp.json</code></strong>.</p></div></aside>\n<h2 id=\"secrets-environment-variable-expansion\">Secrets: environment-variable expansion</h2>\n<p>Never commit tokens. <code>.mcp.json</code> supports <code>${VAR}</code> (and <code>${VAR:-default}</code>) expansion:</p>\n<div class=\"codeblock\"><div class=\"codeblock-head\"><span>.mcp.json</span><button class=\"copy-btn\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></div><pre><code class=\"lang-json\">{\n  \"mcpServers\": {\n    \"github\": {\n      \"type\": \"http\",\n      \"url\": \"https://api.githubcopilot.com/mcp/\",\n      \"headers\": { \"Authorization\": \"Bearer ${GITHUB_TOKEN}\" }\n    },\n    \"inventory\": {\n      \"command\": \"python\",\n      \"args\": [\"tools/inventory_server.py\"],\n      \"env\": { \"DB_URL\": \"${INVENTORY_DB_URL}\" }\n    }\n  }\n}</code></pre></div>\n<p>Each developer (and the CI runner) provides the variables; the file stays safe to commit.</p>\n<h2 id=\"many-servers-one-tool-namespace\">Many servers, one tool namespace</h2>\n<p>Tools from <strong>all configured servers</strong> are discovered at connection time and available simultaneously, named <code>mcp__&lt;server&gt;__&lt;tool&gt;</code>. That makes <strong>descriptions</strong> (2.1) even more important: an MCP tool must out-compete built-ins like Grep. Enhance MCP tool descriptions so Claude knows when your specialised tool beats a generic built-in.</p>\n<h2 id=\"tools-vs-resources\">Tools vs. resources</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Tools</strong> perform actions or parameterised queries (<code>create_issue</code>, <code>search_orders</code>).</li><li><strong>Resources</strong> expose <strong>content</strong> the client can read: a catalog of 300 internal docs, database schemas, issue summaries.</li></ul>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-scenario\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Scenario</div><div class=\"callout-title\">S4 — agent wastes turns exploring docs</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>The agent calls <code>list_docs</code>, <code>search_docs</code>, <code>get_doc</code> repeatedly just to learn what exists. <strong>Better:</strong> expose a <strong>resource</strong> with the documentation catalog (titles, summaries, paths). Claude sees what's available up front and fetches only what it needs.</p></div></aside>\n<h2 id=\"build-or-reuse\">Build or reuse?</h2>\n<p>For <strong>standard integrations</strong> (GitHub, Jira, Slack, Postgres) use existing, maintained MCP servers. Build <strong>custom</strong> servers for team-specific workflows and internal systems. Writing your own GitHub server is rarely the right answer.</p>\n<h2 id=\"exam-traps\">Exam traps</h2>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-trap\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Exam trap</div><div class=\"callout-title\">Hard-coding tokens in <code>.mcp.json</code></div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Use <code>${ENV_VAR}</code> expansion.</p></div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-trap\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Exam trap</div><div class=\"callout-title\">Configuring team servers in <code>~/.claude.json</code></div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Teammates won't get them. Use project scope.</p></div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-trap\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Exam trap</div><div class=\"callout-title\">Exposing a static catalog only through search tools</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>A resource gives visibility without exploratory calls.</p></div></aside>\n<h2 id=\"practice-questions\">Practice questions</h2>\n<div class=\"quiz-embed\" data-qid=\"a2-mcp-01\"></div>\n<div class=\"quiz-embed\" data-qid=\"a2-mcp-02\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"build-exercise\">Build exercise</h2>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-exercise\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Build exercise</div><div class=\"callout-title\">Configure MCP for a team repository</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><ol><li>Add an existing GitHub MCP server to <code>.mcp.json</code> using <code>${GITHUB_TOKEN}</code>; commit the file without secrets.</li><li>Add a personal experimental server at user scope (<code>claude mcp add --scope user …</code>); confirm it doesn't appear in the repo.</li><li>Build a tiny server exposing your docs catalog as a <strong>resource</strong> and a <code>search_docs</code> <strong>tool</strong>; compare how many calls Claude needs to answer doc questions.</li><li>Run <code>/mcp</code> in Claude Code to inspect connected servers and tools.</li></ol></div></aside>","headings":[{"id":"what-you-need-to-know","text":"What you need to know"},{"id":"scopes","text":"Scopes"},{"id":"secrets-environment-variable-expansion","text":"Secrets: environment-variable expansion"},{"id":"many-servers-one-tool-namespace","text":"Many servers, one tool namespace"},{"id":"tools-vs-resources","text":"Tools vs. resources"},{"id":"build-or-reuse","text":"Build or reuse?"},{"id":"exam-traps","text":"Exam traps"},{"id":"practice-questions","text":"Practice questions"},{"id":"build-exercise","text":"Build exercise"}],"sources":["Claude Code: MCP | https://code.claude.com/docs/en/mcp","MCP architecture | https://modelcontextprotocol.io/docs/learn/architecture","MCP server concepts | https://modelcontextprotocol.io/docs/learn/server-concepts","Agent SDK overview | https://code.claude.com/docs/en/agent-sdk/overview"],"text":" What you need to know MCP (Model Context Protocol) lets Claude Code and Agent SDK agents use tools, resources and prompts exposed by external servers. As an architect you decide where a server is configured, how secrets get in , and what each server should expose. Scopes Scope Stored in Shared? Use for Project .mcp.json in the repo root Yes — committed, everyone on the team gets it Team integrations GitHub, Jira, the internal docs server User ~/.claude.json No Personal or experimental servers, available in all your projects Local Project entry in ~/.claude.json No Personal servers for one project only key Team vs. personal If a new teammate \"doesn't have the Jira tools\", the server was configured at user scope on someone's machine. Team servers go in .mcp.json . Secrets environment variable expansion Never commit tokens. .mcp.json supports $ VAR (and $ VAR default ) expansion Each developer (and the CI runner) provides the variables; the file stays safe to commit. Many servers, one tool namespace Tools from all configured servers are discovered at connection time and available simultaneously, named mcp__<server __<tool . That makes descriptions (2.1) even more important an MCP tool must out compete built ins like Grep. Enhance MCP tool descriptions so Claude knows when your specialised tool beats a generic built in. Tools vs. resources Tools perform actions or parameterised queries ( create_issue , search_orders ). Resources expose content the client can read a catalog of 300 internal docs, database schemas, issue summaries. scenario S4 — agent wastes turns exploring docs The agent calls list_docs , search_docs , get_doc repeatedly just to learn what exists. Better expose a resource with the documentation catalog (titles, summaries, paths). Claude sees what's available up front and fetches only what it needs. Build or reuse? For standard integrations (GitHub, Jira, Slack, Postgres) use existing, maintained MCP servers. Build custom servers for team specific workflows and internal systems. Writing your own GitHub server is rarely the right answer. Exam traps trap Hard coding tokens in .mcp.json Use $ ENV_VAR expansion. trap Configuring team servers in ~/.claude.json Teammates won't get them. Use project scope. trap Exposing a static catalog only through search tools A resource gives visibility without exploratory calls. Practice questions quiz a2 mcp 01 quiz a2 mcp 02 Build exercise exercise Configure MCP for a team repository 1. Add an existing GitHub MCP server to .mcp.json using $ GITHUB_TOKEN ; commit the file without secrets. 2. Add a personal experimental server at user scope ( claude mcp add scope user … ); confirm it doesn't appear in the repo. 3. Build a tiny server exposing your docs catalog as a resource and a search_docs tool ; compare how many calls Claude needs to answer doc questions. 4. Run /mcp in Claude Code to inspect connected servers and tools. "},{"skill":"2.5","slug":"built-in-tools","title":"Built-in Tools","summary":"Choosing between Read, Write, Edit, Bash, Grep and Glob — content search versus path patterns, unique anchors for Edit and the Read + Write fallback, and incremental exploration instead of reading everything.","minutes":5,"html":"<h2 id=\"what-you-need-to-know\">What you need to know</h2>\n<p>Claude Code and the Agent SDK ship with built-in tools. The exam tests picking the <strong>right one for the job</strong> — and building exploration strategies that don't flood the context.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Tool</th><th>Does</th><th>Use for</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Grep</strong></td><td>Searches file <strong>contents</strong> (regex)</td><td>All callers of <code>processRefund</code>, every place an error message is raised, imports of a module</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Glob</strong></td><td>Matches file <strong>paths</strong> by pattern</td><td>All <code>**/*.test.tsx</code>, every <code>migrations/*.sql</code>, config files by name</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Read</strong></td><td>Reads a file (or a range)</td><td>Inspect specific files found via Grep/Glob</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Write</strong></td><td>Writes a whole file</td><td>New files, or full rewrites</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Edit</strong></td><td>Replaces an exact, <strong>unique</strong> string</td><td>Targeted modifications</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Bash</strong></td><td>Runs shell commands</td><td>Tests, builds, git, project scripts</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-key\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key concept</div><div class=\"callout-title\">Grep vs. Glob</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Content → <strong>Grep</strong>. Name/path → <strong>Glob</strong>. \"Find all test files\" is Glob; \"find all tests that mock the payment client\" is Grep.</p></div></aside>\n<h2 id=\"edit-and-its-fallback\">Edit and its fallback</h2>\n<p>Edit replaces <code>old_string</code> with <code>new_string</code> and requires <code>old_string</code> to be <strong>unique</strong> in the file (unless replacing all occurrences). When the anchor occurs several times:</p>\n<ol><li>Include <strong>more surrounding context</strong> to make the anchor unique, or</li><li>Fall back to <strong>Read + Write</strong>: read the file, produce the full modified content, write it back.</li></ol>\n<p>Don't switch to <code>sed</code> through Bash to dodge the uniqueness check — that loses the safety the tool provides.</p>\n<h2 id=\"incremental-exploration\">Incremental exploration</h2>\n<p>The efficient way to understand unfamiliar code is <strong>targeted and incremental</strong>:</p>\n<ol><li><strong>Grep</strong> for entry points (route names, the function in the bug report).</li><li><strong>Read</strong> those files.</li><li>Follow <strong>imports</strong> and calls with further Grep/Read.</li><li>Stop when the question is answered.</li></ol>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-scenario\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Scenario</div><div class=\"callout-title\">S4 — tracing a function across wrappers</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>To find every caller of <code>charge_card</code>, the agent first Greps for the name, then discovers it's re-exported as <code>billing.charge</code> in a wrapper module, and Greps again for the wrapper name — tracing usage across wrapper modules rather than assuming one search found everything.</p></div></aside>\n<p>Reading every file \"to get the full picture\" fills the context with irrelevant code, degrades later answers (5.4) and costs tokens. For broad discovery, delegate to the <strong>Explore subagent</strong> (3.4).</p>\n<h2 id=\"exam-traps\">Exam traps</h2>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-trap\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Exam trap</div><div class=\"callout-title\">Glob to find function callers</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Glob matches paths only. Use Grep.</p></div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-trap\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Exam trap</div><div class=\"callout-title\">Reading the whole codebase up front</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Context degradation and cost. Explore incrementally or delegate exploration.</p></div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-trap\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Exam trap</div><div class=\"callout-title\">Retrying the same non-unique Edit</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>It will fail again. Add context or use Read + Write.</p></div></aside>\n<h2 id=\"practice-questions\">Practice questions</h2>\n<div class=\"quiz-embed\" data-qid=\"a2-bit-01\"></div>\n<div class=\"quiz-embed\" data-qid=\"a2-bit-02\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"build-exercise\">Build exercise</h2>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-exercise\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Build exercise</div><div class=\"callout-title\">Tool choice drills in a real repo</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><ol><li>In an open-source repo, ask Claude Code to \"find all test files\" and \"find all tests that use the HTTP client\"; check it uses Glob then Grep.</li><li>Pick a duplicated code line; ask for an Edit and watch it handle non-uniqueness.</li><li>Ask a \"how does X work?\" question and count files read; then ask again with \"explore incrementally, start from the entry point\" and compare.</li><li>Use the Explore subagent for the same question and compare the main-context size.</li></ol></div></aside>","headings":[{"id":"what-you-need-to-know","text":"What you need to know"},{"id":"edit-and-its-fallback","text":"Edit and its fallback"},{"id":"incremental-exploration","text":"Incremental exploration"},{"id":"exam-traps","text":"Exam traps"},{"id":"practice-questions","text":"Practice questions"},{"id":"build-exercise","text":"Build exercise"}],"sources":["Claude Code settings — tools available | https://code.claude.com/docs/en/settings","Claude Code best practices | https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/claude-code-best-practices","Agent SDK overview | https://code.claude.com/docs/en/agent-sdk/overview","Common workflows | https://code.claude.com/docs/en/common-workflows"],"text":" What you need to know Claude Code and the Agent SDK ship with built in tools. The exam tests picking the right one for the job — and building exploration strategies that don't flood the context. Tool Does Use for Grep Searches file contents (regex) All callers of processRefund , every place an error message is raised, imports of a module Glob Matches file paths by pattern All / .test.tsx , every migrations/ .sql , config files by name Read Reads a file (or a range) Inspect specific files found via Grep/Glob Write Writes a whole file New files, or full rewrites Edit Replaces an exact, unique string Targeted modifications Bash Runs shell commands Tests, builds, git, project scripts key Grep vs. Glob Content → Grep . Name/path → Glob . \"Find all test files\" is Glob; \"find all tests that mock the payment client\" is Grep. Edit and its fallback Edit replaces old_string with new_string and requires old_string to be unique in the file (unless replacing all occurrences). When the anchor occurs several times 1. Include more surrounding context to make the anchor unique, or 2. Fall back to Read + Write read the file, produce the full modified content, write it back. Don't switch to sed through Bash to dodge the uniqueness check — that loses the safety the tool provides. Incremental exploration The efficient way to understand unfamiliar code is targeted and incremental 1. Grep for entry points (route names, the function in the bug report). 2. Read those files. 3. Follow imports and calls with further Grep/Read. 4. Stop when the question is answered. scenario S4 — tracing a function across wrappers To find every caller of charge_card , the agent first Greps for the name, then discovers it's re exported as billing.charge in a wrapper module, and Greps again for the wrapper name — tracing usage across wrapper modules rather than assuming one search found everything. Reading every file \"to get the full picture\" fills the context with irrelevant code, degrades later answers (5.4) and costs tokens. For broad discovery, delegate to the Explore subagent (3.4). Exam traps trap Glob to find function callers Glob matches paths only. Use Grep. trap Reading the whole codebase up front Context degradation and cost. Explore incrementally or delegate exploration. trap Retrying the same non unique Edit It will fail again. Add context or use Read + Write. Practice questions quiz a2 bit 01 quiz a2 bit 02 Build exercise exercise Tool choice drills in a real repo 1. In an open source repo, ask Claude Code to \"find all test files\" and \"find all tests that use the HTTP client\"; check it uses Glob then Grep. 2. Pick a duplicated code line; ask for an Edit and watch it handle non uniqueness. 3. Ask a \"how does X work?\" question and count files read; then ask again with \"explore incrementally, start from the entry point\" and compare. 4. Use the Explore subagent for the same question and compare the main context size. "},{"skill":"3.1","slug":"claude-md-hierarchy","title":"CLAUDE.md Hierarchy & Organization","summary":"User, project and directory-level CLAUDE.md files, what is shared through version control, @import and .claude/rules/ for modular organisation, and /memory for diagnosing what is loaded.","minutes":5,"html":"<h2 id=\"what-you-need-to-know\">What you need to know</h2>\n<p><code>CLAUDE.md</code> files are Claude Code's <strong>memory</strong>: standing instructions loaded into context at the start of a session. Several levels combine; more specific levels add to (and can refine) broader ones.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Level</th><th>Location</th><th>Shared via git?</th><th>Use for</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>User</strong></td><td><code>~/.claude/CLAUDE.md</code></td><td><strong>No</strong> — only you</td><td>Personal preferences: your editor habits, how you like explanations</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Project</strong></td><td><code>./CLAUDE.md</code> or <code>./.claude/CLAUDE.md</code></td><td><strong>Yes</strong></td><td>Team conventions: build/test commands, architecture, coding standards</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Directory</strong></td><td><code>./packages/api/CLAUDE.md</code> etc.</td><td>Yes</td><td>Conventions specific to one area, loaded when Claude works there</td></tr><tr><td>(Local, personal)</td><td><code>./CLAUDE.local.md</code> (git-ignored)</td><td>No</td><td>Your personal notes for this project</td></tr><tr><td>(Enterprise)</td><td>Managed policy location</td><td>Org-wide</td><td>Organisation policies</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-key\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key concept</div><div class=\"callout-title\">The diagnostic pattern</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>\"A new team member's Claude doesn't follow our conventions\" → the conventions are in someone's <strong>user-level</strong> file, not the <strong>project</strong> file. Move them to the project CLAUDE.md and commit.</p></div></aside>\n<h2 id=\"modular-organisation\">Modular organisation</h2>\n<p>A single 1,500-line CLAUDE.md is hard to maintain and loads everything into every session. Two tools keep it modular:</p>\n<p><strong><code>@import</code></strong> — include other files:</p>\n<div class=\"codeblock\"><div class=\"codeblock-head\"><span>CLAUDE.md</span><button class=\"copy-btn\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></div><pre><code class=\"lang-markdown\"># Payments service\nBuild: `make build` · Test: `make test` · Never edit generated files in /gen.\n\n@docs/architecture.md\n@docs/api-conventions.md\n@~/.claude/my-shortcuts.md</code></pre></div>\n<p><strong><code>.claude/rules/</code></strong> — topic-specific rule files (e.g. <code>testing.md</code>, <code>api.md</code>, <code>security.md</code>). Without frontmatter they load like CLAUDE.md content; with <strong><code>paths:</code></strong> frontmatter they load only for matching files (3.3).</p>\n<p>Different packages can import only the standards relevant to them — the backend imports API and database conventions; the frontend imports component guidelines.</p>\n<h2 id=\"what-belongs-in-claude-md\">What belongs in CLAUDE.md</h2>\n<ul><li>Commands Claude can't guess (build, test, lint, deploy).</li><li>Architecture and where things live.</li><li>Conventions that <strong>always</strong> apply.</li><li>Gotchas (\"integration tests need <code>docker compose up</code> first\").</li></ul>\n<p>Task-specific procedures that only matter sometimes belong in <strong>skills</strong> (3.2); file-type conventions in <strong>path-scoped rules</strong> (3.3).</p>\n<h2 id=\"diagnosing\">Diagnosing</h2>\n<p>Run <strong><code>/memory</code></strong> to see which memory files are loaded and open them for editing. When Claude \"ignores\" an instruction, first check it's actually loaded — and at the expected level.</p>\n<h2 id=\"exam-traps\">Exam traps</h2>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-trap\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Exam trap</div><div class=\"callout-title\">Putting team conventions in <code>~/.claude/CLAUDE.md</code></div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Works on your machine only.</p></div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-trap\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Exam trap</div><div class=\"callout-title\">One giant CLAUDE.md for a monorepo</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Split with <code>@import</code>, directory-level files and <code>.claude/rules/</code>.</p></div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-trap\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Exam trap</div><div class=\"callout-title\">Debugging \"ignored instructions\" by rewording them first</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Check with <code>/memory</code> whether the file loads at all.</p></div></aside>\n<h2 id=\"practice-questions\">Practice questions</h2>\n<div class=\"quiz-embed\" data-qid=\"a3-md-01\"></div>\n<div class=\"quiz-embed\" data-qid=\"a3-md-02\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"build-exercise\">Build exercise</h2>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-exercise\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Build exercise</div><div class=\"callout-title\">Restructure a monorepo's memory</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><ol><li>Create a repo with <code>packages/api</code> and <code>packages/web</code>.</li><li>Write a project CLAUDE.md with build/test commands; import <code>docs/architecture.md</code>.</li><li>Add <code>packages/api/CLAUDE.md</code> with API conventions; confirm via <code>/memory</code> it loads when you work there.</li><li>Put a preference in <code>~/.claude/CLAUDE.md</code> and verify a teammate's clone doesn't get it.</li><li>Move testing guidance into <code>.claude/rules/testing.md</code>.</li></ol></div></aside>","headings":[{"id":"what-you-need-to-know","text":"What you need to know"},{"id":"modular-organisation","text":"Modular organisation"},{"id":"what-belongs-in-claude-md","text":"What belongs in CLAUDE.md"},{"id":"diagnosing","text":"Diagnosing"},{"id":"exam-traps","text":"Exam traps"},{"id":"practice-questions","text":"Practice questions"},{"id":"build-exercise","text":"Build exercise"}],"sources":["Claude Code: Memory (CLAUDE.md) | https://code.claude.com/docs/en/memory","Claude Code best practices | https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/claude-code-best-practices","Claude Code settings | https://code.claude.com/docs/en/settings","Features overview | https://code.claude.com/docs/en/features-overview"],"text":" What you need to know CLAUDE.md files are Claude Code's memory standing instructions loaded into context at the start of a session. Several levels combine; more specific levels add to (and can refine) broader ones. Level Location Shared via git? Use for User ~/.claude/CLAUDE.md No — only you Personal preferences your editor habits, how you like explanations Project ./CLAUDE.md or ./.claude/CLAUDE.md Yes Team conventions build/test commands, architecture, coding standards Directory ./packages/api/CLAUDE.md etc. Yes Conventions specific to one area, loaded when Claude works there (Local, personal) ./CLAUDE.local.md (git ignored) No Your personal notes for this project (Enterprise) Managed policy location Org wide Organisation policies key The diagnostic pattern \"A new team member's Claude doesn't follow our conventions\" → the conventions are in someone's user level file, not the project file. Move them to the project CLAUDE.md and commit. Modular organisation A single 1,500 line CLAUDE.md is hard to maintain and loads everything into every session. Two tools keep it modular @import — include other files .claude/rules/ — topic specific rule files (e.g. testing.md , api.md , security.md ). Without frontmatter they load like CLAUDE.md content; with paths frontmatter they load only for matching files (3.3). Different packages can import only the standards relevant to them — the backend imports API and database conventions; the frontend imports component guidelines. What belongs in CLAUDE.md Commands Claude can't guess (build, test, lint, deploy). Architecture and where things live. Conventions that always apply. Gotchas (\"integration tests need docker compose up first\"). Task specific procedures that only matter sometimes belong in skills (3.2); file type conventions in path scoped rules (3.3). Diagnosing Run /memory to see which memory files are loaded and open them for editing. When Claude \"ignores\" an instruction, first check it's actually loaded — and at the expected level. Exam traps trap Putting team conventions in ~/.claude/CLAUDE.md Works on your machine only. trap One giant CLAUDE.md for a monorepo Split with @import , directory level files and .claude/rules/ . trap Debugging \"ignored instructions\" by rewording them first Check with /memory whether the file loads at all. Practice questions quiz a3 md 01 quiz a3 md 02 Build exercise exercise Restructure a monorepo's memory 1. Create a repo with packages/api and packages/web . 2. Write a project CLAUDE.md with build/test commands; import docs/architecture.md . 3. Add packages/api/CLAUDE.md with API conventions; confirm via /memory it loads when you work there. 4. Put a preference in ~/.claude/CLAUDE.md and verify a teammate's clone doesn't get it. 5. Move testing guidance into .claude/rules/testing.md . "},{"skill":"3.2","slug":"slash-commands-skills","title":"Custom Slash Commands & Skills","summary":"Project versus personal commands and skills, SKILL.md frontmatter (context fork, allowed-tools, argument-hint), and when a workflow belongs in a skill rather than CLAUDE.md.","minutes":5,"html":"<h2 id=\"what-you-need-to-know\">What you need to know</h2>\n<p>Custom <strong>slash commands</strong> and <strong>skills</strong> package reusable workflows. Commands are Markdown prompt files invoked as <code>/name</code>; skills are folders with a <code>SKILL.md</code> (plus optional scripts and resources) that can be invoked as <code>/name</code> <strong>or</strong> loaded automatically when relevant. Current Claude Code treats commands as a simple form of skill; both keep working.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Scope</th><th>Commands</th><th>Skills</th><th>Shared?</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Project</strong></td><td><code>.claude/commands/review.md</code></td><td><code>.claude/skills/review/SKILL.md</code></td><td>Yes — version-controlled</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Personal</strong></td><td><code>~/.claude/commands/</code></td><td><code>~/.claude/skills/</code></td><td>No</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-key\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key concept</div><div class=\"callout-title\">Team availability</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>A <code>/review</code> command every developer should have on clone or pull → <strong><code>.claude/commands/</code></strong> (or <code>.claude/skills/</code>) in the repository. Personal variants go in <code>~/.claude/</code> — under a <strong>different name</strong> so they don't shadow or confuse the team version.</p></div></aside>\n<h2 id=\"skill-md-frontmatter\">SKILL.md frontmatter</h2>\n<div class=\"codeblock\"><div class=\"codeblock-head\"><span>.claude/skills/codebase-audit/SKILL.md</span><button class=\"copy-btn\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></div><pre><code class=\"lang-markdown\">---\nname: codebase-audit\ndescription: Audit a directory for dead code and risky patterns. Use when asked to audit or assess code health.\nargument-hint: \"[directory]\"\nallowed-tools: Read, Grep, Glob\ncontext: fork\n---\nAudit $ARGUMENTS. Report a table: file, issue, severity, suggested fix.\nDo not modify files.</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Effect</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>description</code></td><td>When Claude should use the skill (it's matched like a tool description)</td></tr><tr><td><code>argument-hint</code></td><td>Prompts the user for the right argument when invoking <code>/codebase-audit</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>allowed-tools</code></td><td>Restricts tools while the skill runs — e.g. read-only audits can't write</td></tr><tr><td><code>context: fork</code></td><td>Runs the skill in an <strong>isolated sub-agent context</strong>; verbose output stays out of the main conversation and only the result returns</td></tr><tr><td><code>$ARGUMENTS</code></td><td>Replaced by the text after the command</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><code>context: fork</code> is the answer whenever a skill produces <strong>lots of exploratory output</strong> (audits, brainstorming several alternatives) that would otherwise clutter the session.</p>\n<h2 id=\"skills-vs-claude-md\">Skills vs. CLAUDE.md</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Put it in…</th><th>When</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>CLAUDE.md</strong></td><td>The guidance should apply <strong>always</strong> — conventions, commands, architecture</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Skill</strong></td><td>A <strong>task-specific workflow</strong> used on demand — release checklist, migration procedure, review routine</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Putting every procedure into CLAUDE.md bloats every session's context with instructions that are irrelevant most of the time.</p>\n<h2 id=\"exam-traps\">Exam traps</h2>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-trap\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Exam trap</div><div class=\"callout-title\">Personal location for a team command</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p><code>~/.claude/commands/</code> isn't shared. Use <code>.claude/commands/</code> in the repo.</p></div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-trap\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Exam trap</div><div class=\"callout-title\">A verbose skill without <code>context: fork</code></div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>It floods the main context. Fork it.</p></div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-trap\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Exam trap</div><div class=\"callout-title\">Read-only workflow without <code>allowed-tools</code></div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Restrict tools to make \"audit, don't modify\" deterministic.</p></div></aside>\n<h2 id=\"practice-questions\">Practice questions</h2>\n<div class=\"quiz-embed\" data-qid=\"a3-skl-01\"></div>\n<div class=\"quiz-embed\" data-qid=\"a3-skl-02\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"build-exercise\">Build exercise</h2>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-exercise\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Build exercise</div><div class=\"callout-title\">Ship a team review skill</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><ol><li>Create <code>.claude/skills/review-pr/SKILL.md</code> with <code>argument-hint</code>, <code>allowed-tools: Read, Grep, Glob, Bash(git diff:*)</code> and <code>context: fork</code>.</li><li>Commit it; clone the repo elsewhere and confirm <code>/review-pr</code> is available.</li><li>Create a personal variant <code>~/.claude/skills/review-pr-strict/</code> with stricter criteria.</li><li>Run the skill with and without <code>context: fork</code> and compare the main session's context usage.</li></ol></div></aside>","headings":[{"id":"what-you-need-to-know","text":"What you need to know"},{"id":"skill-md-frontmatter","text":"SKILL.md frontmatter"},{"id":"skills-vs-claude-md","text":"Skills vs. CLAUDE.md"},{"id":"exam-traps","text":"Exam traps"},{"id":"practice-questions","text":"Practice questions"},{"id":"build-exercise","text":"Build exercise"}],"sources":["Claude Code: Skills | https://code.claude.com/docs/en/skills","Claude Code: Slash commands | https://code.claude.com/docs/en/commands","Equipping agents for the real world with Agent Skills — Anthropic | https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/equipping-agents-for-the-real-world-with-agent-skills","Agent Skills overview | https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/agents-and-tools/agent-skills/overview"],"text":" What you need to know Custom slash commands and skills package reusable workflows. Commands are Markdown prompt files invoked as /name ; skills are folders with a SKILL.md (plus optional scripts and resources) that can be invoked as /name or loaded automatically when relevant. Current Claude Code treats commands as a simple form of skill; both keep working. Scope Commands Skills Shared? Project .claude/commands/review.md .claude/skills/review/SKILL.md Yes — version controlled Personal ~/.claude/commands/ ~/.claude/skills/ No key Team availability A /review command every developer should have on clone or pull → .claude/commands/ (or .claude/skills/ ) in the repository. Personal variants go in ~/.claude/ — under a different name so they don't shadow or confuse the team version. SKILL.md frontmatter Field Effect description When Claude should use the skill (it's matched like a tool description) argument hint Prompts the user for the right argument when invoking /codebase audit allowed tools Restricts tools while the skill runs — e.g. read only audits can't write context fork Runs the skill in an isolated sub agent context ; verbose output stays out of the main conversation and only the result returns $ARGUMENTS Replaced by the text after the command context fork is the answer whenever a skill produces lots of exploratory output (audits, brainstorming several alternatives) that would otherwise clutter the session. Skills vs. CLAUDE.md Put it in… When CLAUDE.md The guidance should apply always — conventions, commands, architecture Skill A task specific workflow used on demand — release checklist, migration procedure, review routine Putting every procedure into CLAUDE.md bloats every session's context with instructions that are irrelevant most of the time. Exam traps trap Personal location for a team command ~/.claude/commands/ isn't shared. Use .claude/commands/ in the repo. trap A verbose skill without context fork It floods the main context. Fork it. trap Read only workflow without allowed tools Restrict tools to make \"audit, don't modify\" deterministic. Practice questions quiz a3 skl 01 quiz a3 skl 02 Build exercise exercise Ship a team review skill 1. Create .claude/skills/review pr/SKILL.md with argument hint , allowed tools Read, Grep, Glob, Bash(git diff ) and context fork . 2. Commit it; clone the repo elsewhere and confirm /review pr is available. 3. Create a personal variant ~/.claude/skills/review pr strict/ with stricter criteria. 4. Run the skill with and without context fork and compare the main session's context usage. "},{"skill":"3.3","slug":"path-specific-rules","title":"Path-Specific Rules","summary":".claude/rules/ files with paths glob frontmatter that load only when matching files are touched — and why they beat directory-level CLAUDE.md for conventions that span the tree.","minutes":5,"html":"<h2 id=\"what-you-need-to-know\">What you need to know</h2>\n<p>Some conventions apply to a <strong>kind of file</strong>, not a <strong>place</strong>: every test file, every Terraform module, every database migration. These files are often <strong>spread across many directories</strong>. Directory-level CLAUDE.md files can't express that without duplicating the rules in dozens of places.</p>\n<p><strong>Path-specific rules</strong> solve it: Markdown files in <strong><code>.claude/rules/</code></strong> with a <strong><code>paths:</code></strong> list of glob patterns in YAML frontmatter. Claude loads them <strong>only when working with files that match</strong>.</p>\n<div class=\"codeblock\"><div class=\"codeblock-head\"><span>.claude/rules/react-tests.md</span><button class=\"copy-btn\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></div><pre><code class=\"lang-markdown\">---\npaths:\n  - \"**/*.test.tsx\"\n  - \"**/*.test.ts\"\n---\n# Test conventions\n- Vitest + Testing Library; query by role, not test IDs.\n- One behaviour per test; name tests \"does X when Y\".\n- Mock network with MSW handlers from `test/handlers.ts`, never fetch directly.</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"codeblock\"><div class=\"codeblock-head\"><span>.claude/rules/terraform.md</span><button class=\"copy-btn\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></div><pre><code class=\"lang-markdown\">---\npaths:\n  - \"terraform/**/*\"\n  - \"**/*.tf\"\n---\nPin provider versions. Tag every resource with owner and cost-center. Never hard-code account IDs.</code></pre></div>\n<h2 id=\"why-path-scoping-is-better-here\">Why path scoping is better here</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Approach</th><th>Problem / benefit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Rules in root CLAUDE.md</td><td>Loaded in <strong>every</strong> session even when no tests are touched — wasted context, more instructions competing for attention</td></tr><tr><td>A CLAUDE.md in each test directory</td><td>Duplicated in 40 places, drifts out of sync, misses co-located tests next to source files</td></tr><tr><td><strong><code>.claude/rules/</code> with <code>paths:</code></strong></td><td>One source of truth; loads exactly when relevant, wherever the file lives ✔</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Directory-level CLAUDE.md remains right for conventions tied to <strong>one area</strong> of the tree (e.g. everything under <code>packages/billing/</code>).</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-scenario\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Scenario</div><div class=\"callout-title\">S2 — tests next to components</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>A React codebase co-locates <code>Button.test.tsx</code> beside <code>Button.tsx</code> in hundreds of folders. The team wants consistent test conventions applied automatically. <strong>Answer:</strong> a <code>.claude/rules/</code> file with <code>paths: [\"**/*.test.tsx\"]</code>.</p></div></aside>\n<h2 id=\"exam-traps\">Exam traps</h2>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-trap\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Exam trap</div><div class=\"callout-title\">Directory CLAUDE.md for cross-cutting file types</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Can't follow files spread across the tree without duplication.</p></div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-trap\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Exam trap</div><div class=\"callout-title\">Everything in root CLAUDE.md \"so it's always available\"</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Irrelevant context in every session. Scope it.</p></div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-trap\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Exam trap</div><div class=\"callout-title\">A skill for always-applicable conventions</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Skills are on-demand workflows; rules apply automatically when matching files are edited.</p></div></aside>\n<h2 id=\"practice-questions\">Practice questions</h2>\n<div class=\"quiz-embed\" data-qid=\"a3-rule-01\"></div>\n<div class=\"quiz-embed\" data-qid=\"a3-rule-02\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"build-exercise\">Build exercise</h2>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-exercise\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Build exercise</div><div class=\"callout-title\">Path-scoped conventions</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><ol><li>Create <code>.claude/rules/tests.md</code> with <code>paths: [\"**/*.test.ts\"]</code> and two distinctive conventions.</li><li>Ask Claude to edit a source file — confirm (<code>/memory</code> or by behaviour) the rule isn't applied.</li><li>Ask it to write a test anywhere in the tree — confirm the conventions are applied.</li><li>Add a second rule for <code>migrations/**/*.sql</code> and verify both scope correctly.</li></ol></div></aside>","headings":[{"id":"what-you-need-to-know","text":"What you need to know"},{"id":"why-path-scoping-is-better-here","text":"Why path scoping is better here"},{"id":"exam-traps","text":"Exam traps"},{"id":"practice-questions","text":"Practice questions"},{"id":"build-exercise","text":"Build exercise"}],"sources":["Claude Code: Memory (CLAUDE.md) | https://code.claude.com/docs/en/memory","Claude Code best practices | https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/claude-code-best-practices","Features overview | https://code.claude.com/docs/en/features-overview","Claude Code settings | https://code.claude.com/docs/en/settings"],"text":" What you need to know Some conventions apply to a kind of file , not a place every test file, every Terraform module, every database migration. These files are often spread across many directories . Directory level CLAUDE.md files can't express that without duplicating the rules in dozens of places. Path specific rules solve it Markdown files in .claude/rules/ with a paths list of glob patterns in YAML frontmatter. Claude loads them only when working with files that match . Why path scoping is better here Approach Problem / benefit Rules in root CLAUDE.md Loaded in every session even when no tests are touched — wasted context, more instructions competing for attention A CLAUDE.md in each test directory Duplicated in 40 places, drifts out of sync, misses co located tests next to source files .claude/rules/ with paths One source of truth; loads exactly when relevant, wherever the file lives ✔ Directory level CLAUDE.md remains right for conventions tied to one area of the tree (e.g. everything under packages/billing/ ). scenario S2 — tests next to components A React codebase co locates Button.test.tsx beside Button.tsx in hundreds of folders. The team wants consistent test conventions applied automatically. Answer a .claude/rules/ file with paths \" / .test.tsx\" . Exam traps trap Directory CLAUDE.md for cross cutting file types Can't follow files spread across the tree without duplication. trap Everything in root CLAUDE.md \"so it's always available\" Irrelevant context in every session. Scope it. trap A skill for always applicable conventions Skills are on demand workflows; rules apply automatically when matching files are edited. Practice questions quiz a3 rule 01 quiz a3 rule 02 Build exercise exercise Path scoped conventions 1. Create .claude/rules/tests.md with paths \" / .test.ts\" and two distinctive conventions. 2. Ask Claude to edit a source file — confirm ( /memory or by behaviour) the rule isn't applied. 3. Ask it to write a test anywhere in the tree — confirm the conventions are applied. 4. Add a second rule for migrations/ / .sql and verify both scope correctly. "},{"skill":"3.4","slug":"plan-mode-vs-direct","title":"Plan Mode vs Direct Execution","summary":"Plan mode for large, multi-file, architectural or ambiguous work; direct execution for small, clear changes; the Explore subagent for discovery; and combining planning with execution.","minutes":5,"html":"<h2 id=\"what-you-need-to-know\">What you need to know</h2>\n<p>In <strong>plan mode</strong> (Shift+Tab to cycle modes, or <code>--permission-mode plan</code>) Claude explores read-only and proposes a plan <strong>without editing files</strong>. You review, adjust, then let it execute. <strong>Direct execution</strong> skips the separate planning step.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-key\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key concept</div><div class=\"callout-title\">Decision rule</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Choose <strong>plan mode</strong> when…</th><th>Choose <strong>direct execution</strong> when…</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>The change spans <strong>many files</strong> or services</td><td>The change is small and <strong>well-scoped</strong></td></tr><tr><td>There are <strong>architectural decisions</strong> or several valid approaches</td><td>The fix is clear (e.g. a stack trace points to one line)</td></tr><tr><td>Requirements are <strong>ambiguous</strong></td><td>Requirements are precise</td></tr><tr><td>Mistakes are <strong>expensive</strong> to undo</td><td>A wrong attempt is cheap to revert</td></tr><tr><td>You need to understand an <strong>unfamiliar</strong> area first</td><td>You know the code</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div></aside>\n<p>Examples:</p>\n<ul><li>Migrate a monolith's logging library across 45 files → <strong>plan</strong>.</li><li>Restructure services into a new module layout → <strong>plan</strong>.</li><li>Add a null check where the stack trace points → <strong>direct</strong>.</li><li>Rename a variable in one function → <strong>direct</strong>.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"the-explore-subagent\">The Explore subagent</h2>\n<p>Discovery in a big codebase produces a lot of output: file listings, search hits, file contents. Doing it in the main session fills the context and degrades later work (5.4). The built-in <strong>Explore subagent</strong> runs that discovery in an <strong>isolated context</strong> (read-only tools) and returns a <strong>summary</strong>. Use it in plan mode and whenever you'd otherwise \"look around\" at length.</p>\n<h2 id=\"combining-the-two\">Combining the two</h2>\n<p>The typical high-quality flow is <strong>explore → plan → implement → verify</strong>:</p>\n<ol><li>Plan mode: explore (often via Explore), propose an approach, list affected files and risks.</li><li>Review and refine the plan (ask for alternatives if needed).</li><li>Switch to execution and implement step by step.</li><li>Verify with tests; commit.</li></ol>\n<p>Planning is not a separate tool you trade off against execution — it's a <strong>first phase</strong> for work that warrants it.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-scenario\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Scenario</div><div class=\"callout-title\">S2 — \"add a feature\" vs \"change our auth architecture\"</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Adding a field to a form and its validation: direct execution. Replacing session cookies with token-based auth across API, web and mobile clients: plan mode first, so the approach and touched components are agreed before edits start.</p></div></aside>\n<h2 id=\"exam-traps\">Exam traps</h2>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-trap\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Exam trap</div><div class=\"callout-title\">Plan mode for every change</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Overhead for trivial edits. Match the mode to complexity and risk.</p></div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-trap\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Exam trap</div><div class=\"callout-title\">Direct execution on a sweeping, ambiguous refactor</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Claude commits to an approach before alternatives were weighed; rework follows.</p></div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-trap\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Exam trap</div><div class=\"callout-title\">Doing verbose discovery in the main context</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Use the Explore subagent so only the summary lands in the session.</p></div></aside>\n<h2 id=\"practice-questions\">Practice questions</h2>\n<div class=\"quiz-embed\" data-qid=\"a3-plan-01\"></div>\n<div class=\"quiz-embed\" data-qid=\"a3-plan-02\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"build-exercise\">Build exercise</h2>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-exercise\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Build exercise</div><div class=\"callout-title\">Plan vs direct on the same repo</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><ol><li>Pick a cross-cutting change (swap a logging library). Run it in plan mode; review the plan; then execute.</li><li>Pick a one-line bug with a stack trace; fix it directly.</li><li>Repeat the cross-cutting change directly without a plan; compare rework and review effort.</li><li>Ask \"how is authentication wired?\" with and without the Explore subagent and compare context usage (<code>/context</code>).</li></ol></div></aside>","headings":[{"id":"what-you-need-to-know","text":"What you need to know"},{"id":"the-explore-subagent","text":"The Explore subagent"},{"id":"combining-the-two","text":"Combining the two"},{"id":"exam-traps","text":"Exam traps"},{"id":"practice-questions","text":"Practice questions"},{"id":"build-exercise","text":"Build exercise"}],"sources":["Claude Code: Permission modes | https://code.claude.com/docs/en/permission-modes","Common workflows | https://code.claude.com/docs/en/common-workflows","Claude Code subagents | https://code.claude.com/docs/en/sub-agents","Claude Code best practices | https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/claude-code-best-practices"],"text":" What you need to know In plan mode (Shift+Tab to cycle modes, or permission mode plan ) Claude explores read only and proposes a plan without editing files . You review, adjust, then let it execute. Direct execution skips the separate planning step. key Decision rule Choose plan mode when… Choose direct execution when… The change spans many files or services The change is small and well scoped There are architectural decisions or several valid approaches The fix is clear (e.g. a stack trace points to one line) Requirements are ambiguous Requirements are precise Mistakes are expensive to undo A wrong attempt is cheap to revert You need to understand an unfamiliar area first You know the code Examples Migrate a monolith's logging library across 45 files → plan . Restructure services into a new module layout → plan . Add a null check where the stack trace points → direct . Rename a variable in one function → direct . The Explore subagent Discovery in a big codebase produces a lot of output file listings, search hits, file contents. Doing it in the main session fills the context and degrades later work (5.4). The built in Explore subagent runs that discovery in an isolated context (read only tools) and returns a summary . Use it in plan mode and whenever you'd otherwise \"look around\" at length. Combining the two The typical high quality flow is explore → plan → implement → verify 1. Plan mode explore (often via Explore), propose an approach, list affected files and risks. 2. Review and refine the plan (ask for alternatives if needed). 3. Switch to execution and implement step by step. 4. Verify with tests; commit. Planning is not a separate tool you trade off against execution — it's a first phase for work that warrants it. scenario S2 — \"add a feature\" vs \"change our auth architecture\" Adding a field to a form and its validation direct execution. Replacing session cookies with token based auth across API, web and mobile clients plan mode first, so the approach and touched components are agreed before edits start. Exam traps trap Plan mode for every change Overhead for trivial edits. Match the mode to complexity and risk. trap Direct execution on a sweeping, ambiguous refactor Claude commits to an approach before alternatives were weighed; rework follows. trap Doing verbose discovery in the main context Use the Explore subagent so only the summary lands in the session. Practice questions quiz a3 plan 01 quiz a3 plan 02 Build exercise exercise Plan vs direct on the same repo 1. Pick a cross cutting change (swap a logging library). Run it in plan mode; review the plan; then execute. 2. Pick a one line bug with a stack trace; fix it directly. 3. Repeat the cross cutting change directly without a plan; compare rework and review effort. 4. Ask \"how is authentication wired?\" with and without the Explore subagent and compare context usage ( /context ). "},{"skill":"3.5","slug":"iterative-refinement","title":"Iterative Refinement","summary":"Getting from a first draft to the right result — concrete input/output examples, test-driven iteration, the interview pattern for unfamiliar domains, and batching interacting issues.","minutes":5,"html":"<h2 id=\"what-you-need-to-know\">What you need to know</h2>\n<p>The first result is rarely the final one. The exam tests <strong>how you give feedback</strong> so each iteration converges instead of oscillating.</p>\n<h2 id=\"1-concrete-input-output-examples\">1. Concrete input/output examples</h2>\n<p>Prose descriptions of a transformation are ambiguous (\"normalise the addresses\"). Two or three <strong>concrete examples</strong> of input → expected output remove the ambiguity faster than any amount of description.</p>\n<div class=\"codeblock\"><div class=\"codeblock-head\"><span>examples beat description</span><button class=\"copy-btn\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></div><pre><code class=\"lang-text\">Input:  \"123 main st., apt 4b, springfield IL\"\nOutput: {\"street\": \"123 Main St\", \"unit\": \"Apt 4B\", \"city\": \"Springfield\", \"state\": \"IL\"}\n\nInput:  \"PO Box 77, Shelbyville, Illinois 62565\"\nOutput: {\"street\": \"PO Box 77\", \"unit\": null, \"city\": \"Shelbyville\", \"state\": \"IL\", \"zip\": \"62565\"}</code></pre></div>\n<p>When Claude's output is inconsistent across attempts, add examples of the cases it gets wrong.</p>\n<h2 id=\"2-test-driven-iteration\">2. Test-driven iteration</h2>\n<p>Write (or have Claude write) <strong>tests first</strong>, confirm they fail, then let Claude implement and <strong>iterate until the tests pass</strong>. Tests give objective, repeatable feedback; Claude can run them itself and see exactly what's wrong. Share failing test output rather than describing the failure.</p>\n<h2 id=\"3-the-interview-pattern\">3. The interview pattern</h2>\n<p>In an unfamiliar domain you may not know which questions matter. Ask Claude to <strong>interview you first</strong>: \"Before implementing, ask me questions about requirements, edge cases and constraints.\" It surfaces considerations you hadn't anticipated (time zones, partial refunds, idempotency) before code is written.</p>\n<h2 id=\"4-batch-interacting-issues-sequence-independent-ones\">4. Batch interacting issues; sequence independent ones</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Issues are…</th><th>Give feedback…</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Interacting</strong> (fixing one affects another: validation + error messages + API contract)</td><td><strong>Together, in one message</strong></td><td>Claude can design a coherent fix; one-by-one fixes break each other</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Independent</strong> (typo in docs, slow query, missing log line)</td><td><strong>Sequentially</strong></td><td>Smaller, verifiable steps; easy to review</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-scenario\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Scenario</div><div class=\"callout-title\">S2 — the fix keeps breaking something else</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>You report three related validation problems one per message; each fix reverts part of the previous one. <strong>Better:</strong> describe all three in one message with the expected behaviour for each, plus tests.</p></div></aside>\n<h2 id=\"exam-traps\">Exam traps</h2>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-trap\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Exam trap</div><div class=\"callout-title\">Longer prose instructions for an ambiguous transformation</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Concrete input/output examples are more effective.</p></div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-trap\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Exam trap</div><div class=\"callout-title\">Describing a failure instead of sharing it</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Paste the failing test output or stack trace.</p></div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-trap\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Exam trap</div><div class=\"callout-title\">Diving into implementation in an unfamiliar domain</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Use the interview pattern first.</p></div></aside>\n<h2 id=\"practice-questions\">Practice questions</h2>\n<div class=\"quiz-embed\" data-qid=\"a3-iter-01\"></div>\n<div class=\"quiz-embed\" data-qid=\"a3-iter-02\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"build-exercise\">Build exercise</h2>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-exercise\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Build exercise</div><div class=\"callout-title\">Converge faster</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><ol><li>Ask Claude to implement an address normaliser from a prose description; score it on 20 test addresses.</li><li>Restart with three input/output examples; score again.</li><li>Restart test-first: write 10 failing tests, then let Claude iterate until green.</li><li>For a domain you don't know (e.g. tax rounding rules), use the interview pattern and list the questions it asked that you would have missed.</li></ol></div></aside>","headings":[{"id":"what-you-need-to-know","text":"What you need to know"},{"id":"1-concrete-input-output-examples","text":"1. Concrete input/output examples"},{"id":"2-test-driven-iteration","text":"2. Test-driven iteration"},{"id":"3-the-interview-pattern","text":"3. The interview pattern"},{"id":"4-batch-interacting-issues-sequence-independent-ones","text":"4. Batch interacting issues; sequence independent ones"},{"id":"exam-traps","text":"Exam traps"},{"id":"practice-questions","text":"Practice questions"},{"id":"build-exercise","text":"Build exercise"}],"sources":["Claude Code best practices | https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/claude-code-best-practices","Common workflows | https://code.claude.com/docs/en/common-workflows","Prompting best practices | https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/prompt-engineering/claude-prompting-best-practices","Multishot prompting | https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/prompt-engineering/multishot-prompting"],"text":" What you need to know The first result is rarely the final one. The exam tests how you give feedback so each iteration converges instead of oscillating. 1. Concrete input/output examples Prose descriptions of a transformation are ambiguous (\"normalise the addresses\"). Two or three concrete examples of input → expected output remove the ambiguity faster than any amount of description. When Claude's output is inconsistent across attempts, add examples of the cases it gets wrong. 2. Test driven iteration Write (or have Claude write) tests first , confirm they fail, then let Claude implement and iterate until the tests pass . Tests give objective, repeatable feedback; Claude can run them itself and see exactly what's wrong. Share failing test output rather than describing the failure. 3. The interview pattern In an unfamiliar domain you may not know which questions matter. Ask Claude to interview you first \"Before implementing, ask me questions about requirements, edge cases and constraints.\" It surfaces considerations you hadn't anticipated (time zones, partial refunds, idempotency) before code is written. 4. Batch interacting issues; sequence independent ones Issues are… Give feedback… Why Interacting (fixing one affects another validation + error messages + API contract) Together, in one message Claude can design a coherent fix; one by one fixes break each other Independent (typo in docs, slow query, missing log line) Sequentially Smaller, verifiable steps; easy to review scenario S2 — the fix keeps breaking something else You report three related validation problems one per message; each fix reverts part of the previous one. Better describe all three in one message with the expected behaviour for each, plus tests. Exam traps trap Longer prose instructions for an ambiguous transformation Concrete input/output examples are more effective. trap Describing a failure instead of sharing it Paste the failing test output or stack trace. trap Diving into implementation in an unfamiliar domain Use the interview pattern first. Practice questions quiz a3 iter 01 quiz a3 iter 02 Build exercise exercise Converge faster 1. Ask Claude to implement an address normaliser from a prose description; score it on 20 test addresses. 2. Restart with three input/output examples; score again. 3. Restart test first write 10 failing tests, then let Claude iterate until green. 4. For a domain you don't know (e.g. tax rounding rules), use the interview pattern and list the questions it asked that you would have missed. "},{"skill":"3.6","slug":"ci-cd-integration","title":"CI/CD Integration","summary":"Running Claude Code non-interactively with -p, machine-parseable output with --output-format json and --json-schema, CLAUDE.md as CI context, independent review instances and avoiding duplicate comments.","minutes":5,"html":"<h2 id=\"what-you-need-to-know\">What you need to know</h2>\n<p>In a pipeline there is no human at the keyboard. Claude Code must run <strong>non-interactively</strong>, produce <strong>parseable</strong> output, and work from the <strong>same project context</strong> developers use.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-key\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key concept</div><div class=\"callout-title\">The three CI essentials</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><ol><li><strong><code>-p</code> / <code>--print</code></strong> — run one prompt non-interactively and exit. Without it, the job waits for input and hangs.</li><li><strong><code>--output-format json</code></strong> (optionally <strong><code>--json-schema</code></strong>) — structured output your pipeline can parse into inline PR comments, status checks or tickets.</li><li><strong>CLAUDE.md</strong> in the repo — gives CI runs the testing standards, fixture conventions and review criteria.</li></ol></div></aside>\n<div class=\"codeblock\"><div class=\"codeblock-head\"><span>GitHub Actions — review step</span><button class=\"copy-btn\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></div><pre><code class=\"lang-yaml\">- name: Claude review\n  run: |\n    claude -p \"Review this PR's diff (git diff origin/main...HEAD). Report only\n    bugs and security issues per CLAUDE.md 'Review criteria'. Previous findings:\n    $(cat .review/previous.json)\" \\\n      --output-format json --json-schema \"$(cat .github/review-schema.json)\" \\\n      --allowedTools \"Read,Grep,Glob,Bash(git diff:*)\" &gt; review.json\n  env:\n    ANTHROPIC_API_KEY: ${{ secrets.ANTHROPIC_API_KEY }}</code></pre></div>\n<p>Anthropic also provides a ready-made <strong>Claude Code GitHub Action</strong> (<code>anthropics/claude-code-action</code>) that wires this up for <code>@claude</code> mentions and PR reviews.</p>\n<h2 id=\"context-for-ci\">Context for CI</h2>\n<p>CI runs start with <strong>no conversation history</strong>. Everything the reviewer or test generator must know goes into the repo's CLAUDE.md (or rules/skills): test framework and fixtures, what counts as a blocking issue, existing test files to avoid duplicating. When generated tests duplicate existing ones, the fix is <strong>context</strong> — list existing tests and conventions — not a bigger model.</p>\n<h2 id=\"independent-review\">Independent review</h2>\n<p>A Claude session that <strong>generated</strong> code is a weak reviewer of that code: it carries its own reasoning and tends to confirm it. In CI, run review as a <strong>separate invocation</strong> that sees the diff and the criteria, not the generator's reasoning (see 4.6).</p>\n<h2 id=\"avoiding-duplicate-comments\">Avoiding duplicate comments</h2>\n<p>Re-running review on each push re-reports the same issues. Pass <strong>prior findings</strong> into the prompt and ask for <strong>new or still-unresolved</strong> issues only; key findings by file + line + rule so the pipeline can de-duplicate.</p>\n<h2 id=\"synchronous-vs-batch-in-ci\">Synchronous vs. batch in CI</h2>\n<p>Pre-merge checks <strong>block developers</strong> → synchronous calls. Nightly audits or weekly technical-debt reports <strong>don't block anyone</strong> → the <strong>Message Batches API</strong> (50% cheaper, up to 24 h) is appropriate (4.5).</p>\n<h2 id=\"exam-traps\">Exam traps</h2>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-trap\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Exam trap</div><div class=\"callout-title\">Missing <code>-p</code></div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>The job hangs waiting for interactive input.</p></div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-trap\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Exam trap</div><div class=\"callout-title\">Parsing free-text output with regex</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Use <code>--output-format json</code> / <code>--json-schema</code>.</p></div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-trap\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Exam trap</div><div class=\"callout-title\">The generating session reviews its own work</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Use an independent instance.</p></div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-trap\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Exam trap</div><div class=\"callout-title\">Batch API for a blocking pre-merge check</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>No latency guarantee — developers could wait hours.</p></div></aside>\n<h2 id=\"practice-questions\">Practice questions</h2>\n<div class=\"quiz-embed\" data-qid=\"a3-ci-01\"></div>\n<div class=\"quiz-embed\" data-qid=\"a3-ci-02\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"build-exercise\">Build exercise</h2>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-exercise\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Build exercise</div><div class=\"callout-title\">CI review job</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><ol><li>Write a JSON schema for findings: <code>file</code>, <code>line</code>, <code>severity</code>, <code>category</code>, <code>message</code>, <code>suggestion</code>.</li><li>Run <code>claude -p … --output-format json --json-schema …</code> locally on a diff; validate the output.</li><li>Add the step to a workflow with <code>ANTHROPIC_API_KEY</code> from secrets and restricted <code>--allowedTools</code>.</li><li>Store findings as an artifact and feed them into the next run; verify no duplicates are posted.</li><li>Move review criteria into CLAUDE.md and confirm the CI run follows them.</li></ol></div></aside>","headings":[{"id":"what-you-need-to-know","text":"What you need to know"},{"id":"context-for-ci","text":"Context for CI"},{"id":"independent-review","text":"Independent review"},{"id":"avoiding-duplicate-comments","text":"Avoiding duplicate comments"},{"id":"synchronous-vs-batch-in-ci","text":"Synchronous vs. batch in CI"},{"id":"exam-traps","text":"Exam traps"},{"id":"practice-questions","text":"Practice questions"},{"id":"build-exercise","text":"Build exercise"}],"sources":["Claude Code headless / programmatic usage | https://code.claude.com/docs/en/headless","CLI reference | https://code.claude.com/docs/en/cli-reference","Claude Code GitHub Actions | https://code.claude.com/docs/en/github-actions","Claude Code best practices | https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/claude-code-best-practices"],"text":" What you need to know In a pipeline there is no human at the keyboard. Claude Code must run non interactively , produce parseable output, and work from the same project context developers use. key The three CI essentials 1. p / print — run one prompt non interactively and exit. Without it, the job waits for input and hangs. 2. output format json (optionally json schema ) — structured output your pipeline can parse into inline PR comments, status checks or tickets. 3. CLAUDE.md in the repo — gives CI runs the testing standards, fixture conventions and review criteria. Anthropic also provides a ready made Claude Code GitHub Action ( anthropics/claude code action ) that wires this up for @claude mentions and PR reviews. Context for CI CI runs start with no conversation history . Everything the reviewer or test generator must know goes into the repo's CLAUDE.md (or rules/skills) test framework and fixtures, what counts as a blocking issue, existing test files to avoid duplicating. When generated tests duplicate existing ones, the fix is context — list existing tests and conventions — not a bigger model. Independent review A Claude session that generated code is a weak reviewer of that code it carries its own reasoning and tends to confirm it. In CI, run review as a separate invocation that sees the diff and the criteria, not the generator's reasoning (see 4.6). Avoiding duplicate comments Re running review on each push re reports the same issues. Pass prior findings into the prompt and ask for new or still unresolved issues only; key findings by file + line + rule so the pipeline can de duplicate. Synchronous vs. batch in CI Pre merge checks block developers → synchronous calls. Nightly audits or weekly technical debt reports don't block anyone → the Message Batches API (50% cheaper, up to 24 h) is appropriate (4.5). Exam traps trap Missing p The job hangs waiting for interactive input. trap Parsing free text output with regex Use output format json / json schema . trap The generating session reviews its own work Use an independent instance. trap Batch API for a blocking pre merge check No latency guarantee — developers could wait hours. Practice questions quiz a3 ci 01 quiz a3 ci 02 Build exercise exercise CI review job 1. Write a JSON schema for findings file , line , severity , category , message , suggestion . 2. Run claude p … output format json json schema … locally on a diff; validate the output. 3. Add the step to a workflow with ANTHROPIC_API_KEY from secrets and restricted allowedTools . 4. Store findings as an artifact and feed them into the next run; verify no duplicates are posted. 5. Move review criteria into CLAUDE.md and confirm the CI run follows them. "},{"skill":"4.1","slug":"explicit-criteria","title":"Explicit Criteria & Precision","summary":"Replacing vague instructions with concrete, checkable criteria — what to report and what to skip, severity definitions with examples — and restoring trust when false positives have made a reviewer noise.","minutes":5,"html":"<h2 id=\"what-you-need-to-know\">What you need to know</h2>\n<p>Claude follows instructions precisely — which means <strong>vague instructions produce vague behaviour</strong>. \"Be conservative\", \"only report important issues\", \"flag anything suspicious\" give no decision boundary, so precision varies from run to run and between categories.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-key\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key concept</div><div class=\"callout-title\">Explicit criteria</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>State <strong>what to report</strong>, <strong>what to skip</strong>, and <strong>how to decide severity</strong> — with concrete examples.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Vague</th><th>Explicit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>\"Only report high-confidence issues\"</td><td>\"Report a finding only if you can name the input that triggers it and the incorrect result\"</td></tr><tr><td>\"Flag comments that may be inaccurate\"</td><td>\"Flag a comment only when it <strong>contradicts</strong> the code it describes (e.g. says 'returns null' but the function throws)\"</td></tr><tr><td>\"Be careful about style\"</td><td>\"Skip formatting, naming and import order — the linter handles them\"</td></tr><tr><td>\"Mark critical issues\"</td><td>\"<strong>Critical</strong> = data loss, security exposure or wrong money amounts; <strong>Major</strong> = incorrect behaviour in a supported path; <strong>Minor</strong> = everything else\"</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div></aside>\n<h2 id=\"why-false-positives-matter-so-much\">Why false positives matter so much</h2>\n<p>A review bot with a high false-positive rate in <strong>some</strong> categories trains developers to ignore <strong>all</strong> its comments — including the accurate ones. Trust is a system property.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-scenario\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Scenario</div><div class=\"callout-title\">S5 — developers dismiss everything</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Security and correctness findings are accurate, but \"possible performance issue\" and \"style\" findings are mostly wrong, and developers have started dismissing every comment. <strong>Fast, effective step:</strong> temporarily <strong>disable the high-false-positive categories</strong> so the accurate ones regain credibility, then improve those categories' criteria (with examples) before re-enabling them.</p></div></aside>\n<h2 id=\"writing-criteria-that-work\">Writing criteria that work</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Define the decision</strong>, not the attitude. Replace adjectives (\"important\", \"risky\") with conditions.</li><li><strong>Give examples per severity level</strong>, including borderline ones — code snippets for code review, clauses for contract review.</li><li><strong>Say what to ignore</strong>, especially things other tools already check, and local conventions the team accepts.</li><li><strong>Explain why</strong> a rule exists; Claude generalises better when it knows the purpose (\"we skip style because the linter enforces it\").</li><li>Make output <strong>structured</strong> (category, severity, evidence) so you can measure precision per category and catch drift.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"exam-traps\">Exam traps</h2>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-trap\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Exam trap</div><div class=\"callout-title\">\"Tell the model to be more conservative\"</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Doesn't define a boundary; precision barely moves. Explicit criteria do.</p></div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-trap\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Exam trap</div><div class=\"callout-title\">Confidence thresholds as the main fix</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>\"Only report findings with confidence &gt; 0.8\" relies on self-reported confidence, which is poorly calibrated without validation (4.6, 5.5). Criteria first.</p></div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-trap\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Exam trap</div><div class=\"callout-title\">Keeping noisy categories on while you tune</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Every false positive erodes trust in the accurate categories.</p></div></aside>\n<h2 id=\"practice-questions\">Practice questions</h2>\n<div class=\"quiz-embed\" data-qid=\"a4-crit-01\"></div>\n<div class=\"quiz-embed\" data-qid=\"a4-crit-02\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"build-exercise\">Build exercise</h2>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-exercise\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Build exercise</div><div class=\"callout-title\">Measure precision per category</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><ol><li>Run a review prompt (\"find issues, be conservative\") on 20 diffs and label every finding true/false positive by category.</li><li>Rewrite the prompt with explicit report/skip rules and severity definitions with code examples.</li><li>Re-run and compare precision per category.</li><li>Disable the worst category and re-measure how many findings developers would act on.</li></ol></div></aside>","headings":[{"id":"what-you-need-to-know","text":"What you need to know"},{"id":"why-false-positives-matter-so-much","text":"Why false positives matter so much"},{"id":"writing-criteria-that-work","text":"Writing criteria that work"},{"id":"exam-traps","text":"Exam traps"},{"id":"practice-questions","text":"Practice questions"},{"id":"build-exercise","text":"Build exercise"}],"sources":["Prompting best practices | https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/prompt-engineering/claude-prompting-best-practices","Prompt engineering overview | https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/prompt-engineering/overview","Define success criteria | https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/test-and-evaluate/define-success","Increase output consistency | https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/test-and-evaluate/strengthen-guardrails/increase-consistency"],"text":" What you need to know Claude follows instructions precisely — which means vague instructions produce vague behaviour . \"Be conservative\", \"only report important issues\", \"flag anything suspicious\" give no decision boundary, so precision varies from run to run and between categories. key Explicit criteria State what to report , what to skip , and how to decide severity — with concrete examples. Vague Explicit \"Only report high confidence issues\" \"Report a finding only if you can name the input that triggers it and the incorrect result\" \"Flag comments that may be inaccurate\" \"Flag a comment only when it contradicts the code it describes (e.g. says 'returns null' but the function throws)\" \"Be careful about style\" \"Skip formatting, naming and import order — the linter handles them\" \"Mark critical issues\" \" Critical = data loss, security exposure or wrong money amounts; Major = incorrect behaviour in a supported path; Minor = everything else\" Why false positives matter so much A review bot with a high false positive rate in some categories trains developers to ignore all its comments — including the accurate ones. Trust is a system property. scenario S5 — developers dismiss everything Security and correctness findings are accurate, but \"possible performance issue\" and \"style\" findings are mostly wrong, and developers have started dismissing every comment. Fast, effective step temporarily disable the high false positive categories so the accurate ones regain credibility, then improve those categories' criteria (with examples) before re enabling them. Writing criteria that work Define the decision , not the attitude. Replace adjectives (\"important\", \"risky\") with conditions. Give examples per severity level , including borderline ones — code snippets for code review, clauses for contract review. Say what to ignore , especially things other tools already check, and local conventions the team accepts. Explain why a rule exists; Claude generalises better when it knows the purpose (\"we skip style because the linter enforces it\"). Make output structured (category, severity, evidence) so you can measure precision per category and catch drift. Exam traps trap \"Tell the model to be more conservative\" Doesn't define a boundary; precision barely moves. Explicit criteria do. trap Confidence thresholds as the main fix \"Only report findings with confidence 0.8\" relies on self reported confidence, which is poorly calibrated without validation (4.6, 5.5). Criteria first. trap Keeping noisy categories on while you tune Every false positive erodes trust in the accurate categories. Practice questions quiz a4 crit 01 quiz a4 crit 02 Build exercise exercise Measure precision per category 1. Run a review prompt (\"find issues, be conservative\") on 20 diffs and label every finding true/false positive by category. 2. Rewrite the prompt with explicit report/skip rules and severity definitions with code examples. 3. Re run and compare precision per category. 4. Disable the worst category and re measure how many findings developers would act on. "},{"skill":"4.2","slug":"few-shot-prompting","title":"Few-Shot Prompting","summary":"Using 2–4 targeted examples — chosen for ambiguous cases and showing the reasoning — to get consistent format and judgment, generalise to new patterns and reduce hallucination in extraction.","minutes":5,"html":"<h2 id=\"what-you-need-to-know\">What you need to know</h2>\n<p>Few-shot prompting means including a handful of <strong>worked examples</strong> in the prompt. It is one of the most effective tools for <strong>consistency</strong> — of format and of judgment — when instructions alone leave room for interpretation.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-key\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key concept</div><div class=\"callout-title\">How to choose examples</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><ul><li><strong>2–4 examples</strong> is usually enough; more adds tokens with diminishing returns.</li><li>Choose <strong>ambiguous / borderline</strong> cases, not the obvious ones Claude already handles.</li><li><strong>Show the reasoning</strong>: why this action/category was chosen over the plausible alternative.</li><li><strong>Vary</strong> them (formats, lengths, edge cases) so Claude learns the pattern, not one surface form.</li><li>Wrap them in tags (<code>&lt;example&gt;</code>) so they're clearly separated from the real input.</li></ul></div></aside>\n<div class=\"codeblock\"><div class=\"codeblock-head\"><span>example for an ambiguous support decision</span><button class=\"copy-btn\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></div><pre><code class=\"lang-xml\">&lt;example&gt;\nCustomer: \"The blender I bought 40 days ago stopped working, I want my money back.\"\nDecision: offer_replacement_or_repair\nReasoning: Outside the 30-day refund window but within the 1-year warranty. A refund\nwould be a policy exception; warranty service is in policy. Escalate only if the\ncustomer rejects warranty options.\n&lt;/example&gt;</code></pre></div>\n<h2 id=\"what-few-shot-fixes\">What few-shot fixes</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Symptom</th><th>Few-shot helps because…</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Output format varies between runs</td><td>Examples demonstrate the exact structure</td></tr><tr><td>Inconsistent decisions on borderline cases</td><td>Examples with reasoning show where the boundary is</td></tr><tr><td>Extraction fails on unusual layouts (tables vs. prose, citations inline vs. bibliography)</td><td>Examples show how to handle each structure</td></tr><tr><td>Model invents values for missing fields</td><td>Examples show <code>null</code> returned when the source lacks the value</td></tr><tr><td>Review flags acceptable local patterns</td><td>Examples distinguish acceptable code from real issues</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Good examples <strong>generalise</strong>: Claude applies the demonstrated judgment to novel cases that resemble them, rather than matching only identical inputs.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-scenario\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Scenario</div><div class=\"callout-title\">S6 — varied document structures</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Research papers cite methodology inline in some, in a separate section in others, and in tables in a third group. Extraction misses fields in the less common layouts. <strong>Fix:</strong> add few-shot examples covering each structure, including one where a field is absent and returns null.</p></div></aside>\n<h2 id=\"few-shot-vs-other-tools\">Few-shot vs. other tools</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Explicit criteria</strong> (4.1) define the rule; <strong>examples</strong> show how to apply it at the boundary. Use both.</li><li>For <strong>tool selection</strong> problems, fix descriptions first (2.1); examples are a secondary aid.</li><li>For <strong>guaranteed</strong> structure, use a schema (4.3); examples improve the <em>content</em> within the structure.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"exam-traps\">Exam traps</h2>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-trap\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Exam trap</div><div class=\"callout-title\">Ten obvious examples</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>They cost tokens and don't teach the boundary. Choose ambiguous cases.</p></div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-trap\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Exam trap</div><div class=\"callout-title\">Examples without reasoning</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Claude copies the label but not the judgment; similar-but-different cases go wrong.</p></div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-trap\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Exam trap</div><div class=\"callout-title\">Few-shot as a substitute for a schema</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Examples make valid JSON likely; a schema makes it guaranteed.</p></div></aside>\n<h2 id=\"practice-questions\">Practice questions</h2>\n<div class=\"quiz-embed\" data-qid=\"a4-shot-01\"></div>\n<div class=\"quiz-embed\" data-qid=\"a4-shot-02\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"build-exercise\">Build exercise</h2>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-exercise\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Build exercise</div><div class=\"callout-title\">Targeted examples</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><ol><li>Take a classification task with 5 categories and 30 labelled tickets; run zero-shot and record per-category accuracy.</li><li>Add 3 examples of <strong>easy</strong> cases; re-measure.</li><li>Replace them with 3 <strong>borderline</strong> cases including reasoning; re-measure.</li><li>For an extraction task, add one example where a field is missing and returns <code>null</code>; count hallucinated values before and after.</li></ol></div></aside>","headings":[{"id":"what-you-need-to-know","text":"What you need to know"},{"id":"what-few-shot-fixes","text":"What few-shot fixes"},{"id":"few-shot-vs-other-tools","text":"Few-shot vs. other tools"},{"id":"exam-traps","text":"Exam traps"},{"id":"practice-questions","text":"Practice questions"},{"id":"build-exercise","text":"Build exercise"}],"sources":["Multishot prompting | https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/prompt-engineering/multishot-prompting","Use XML tags | https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/prompt-engineering/use-xml-tags","Reduce hallucinations | https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/test-and-evaluate/strengthen-guardrails/reduce-hallucinations","Prompting best practices | https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/prompt-engineering/claude-prompting-best-practices"],"text":" What you need to know Few shot prompting means including a handful of worked examples in the prompt. It is one of the most effective tools for consistency — of format and of judgment — when instructions alone leave room for interpretation. key How to choose examples 2–4 examples is usually enough; more adds tokens with diminishing returns. Choose ambiguous / borderline cases, not the obvious ones Claude already handles. Show the reasoning why this action/category was chosen over the plausible alternative. Vary them (formats, lengths, edge cases) so Claude learns the pattern, not one surface form. Wrap them in tags ( <example ) so they're clearly separated from the real input. What few shot fixes Symptom Few shot helps because… Output format varies between runs Examples demonstrate the exact structure Inconsistent decisions on borderline cases Examples with reasoning show where the boundary is Extraction fails on unusual layouts (tables vs. prose, citations inline vs. bibliography) Examples show how to handle each structure Model invents values for missing fields Examples show null returned when the source lacks the value Review flags acceptable local patterns Examples distinguish acceptable code from real issues Good examples generalise Claude applies the demonstrated judgment to novel cases that resemble them, rather than matching only identical inputs. scenario S6 — varied document structures Research papers cite methodology inline in some, in a separate section in others, and in tables in a third group. Extraction misses fields in the less common layouts. Fix add few shot examples covering each structure, including one where a field is absent and returns null. Few shot vs. other tools Explicit criteria (4.1) define the rule; examples show how to apply it at the boundary. Use both. For tool selection problems, fix descriptions first (2.1); examples are a secondary aid. For guaranteed structure, use a schema (4.3); examples improve the content within the structure. Exam traps trap Ten obvious examples They cost tokens and don't teach the boundary. Choose ambiguous cases. trap Examples without reasoning Claude copies the label but not the judgment; similar but different cases go wrong. trap Few shot as a substitute for a schema Examples make valid JSON likely; a schema makes it guaranteed. Practice questions quiz a4 shot 01 quiz a4 shot 02 Build exercise exercise Targeted examples 1. Take a classification task with 5 categories and 30 labelled tickets; run zero shot and record per category accuracy. 2. Add 3 examples of easy cases; re measure. 3. Replace them with 3 borderline cases including reasoning; re measure. 4. For an extraction task, add one example where a field is missing and returns null ; count hallucinated values before and after. "},{"skill":"4.3","slug":"structured-output-tool-use","title":"Structured Output via Tool Use & JSON Schemas","summary":"Enforcing output structure with tool definitions and JSON schemas — what schemas guarantee and what they don't, tool_choice for structure, nullable fields against fabrication, and enum patterns for ambiguity.","minutes":5,"html":"<h2 id=\"what-you-need-to-know\">What you need to know</h2>\n<p>Downstream systems need <strong>valid, predictable JSON</strong>. Asking for JSON in the prompt makes it likely; <strong>tool use with a JSON schema</strong> makes the structure reliable: you define a tool whose <code>input_schema</code> is the shape you want, and Claude \"calls\" it with the extracted data. (The API's <strong>structured outputs</strong> feature and <code>strict: true</code> tool definitions go further and constrain decoding to the schema.)</p>\n<div class=\"codeblock\"><div class=\"codeblock-head\"><span>extraction via tool use</span><button class=\"copy-btn\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></div><pre><code class=\"lang-python\">extract_invoice = {\n    \"name\": \"record_invoice\",\n    \"description\": \"Record the fields extracted from one invoice.\",\n    \"input_schema\": {\n        \"type\": \"object\",\n        \"properties\": {\n            \"vendor\":        {\"type\": \"string\"},\n            \"invoice_number\":{\"type\": \"string\"},\n            \"invoice_date\":  {\"type\": \"string\", \"description\": \"ISO 8601 (YYYY-MM-DD)\"},\n            \"due_date\":      {\"type\": [\"string\", \"null\"], \"description\": \"null if not stated\"},\n            \"currency\":      {\"type\": \"string\", \"enum\": [\"USD\", \"EUR\", \"GBP\", \"other\"]},\n            \"currency_other\":{\"type\": [\"string\", \"null\"]},\n            \"stated_total\":  {\"type\": \"number\"},\n            \"line_items\":    {\"type\": \"array\", \"items\": {\"type\": \"object\",\n                              \"properties\": {\"description\": {\"type\": \"string\"},\n                                             \"amount\": {\"type\": \"number\"}},\n                              \"required\": [\"description\", \"amount\"]}}\n        },\n        \"required\": [\"vendor\", \"invoice_number\", \"invoice_date\", \"due_date\",\n                     \"currency\", \"stated_total\", \"line_items\"]\n    },\n}\nresp = client.messages.create(model=MODEL, max_tokens=2048, tools=[extract_invoice],\n        tool_choice={\"type\": \"tool\", \"name\": \"record_invoice\"},\n        messages=[{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": invoice_text}])\ndata = next(b.input for b in resp.content if b.type == \"tool_use\")</code></pre></div>\n<h2 id=\"what-schemas-guarantee-and-what-they-don-t\">What schemas guarantee — and what they don't</h2>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-key\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key concept</div><div class=\"callout-title\">Syntax vs. semantics</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>A schema eliminates <strong>syntax and shape errors</strong>: missing braces, wrong types, missing required keys. It does <strong>not</strong> guarantee <strong>correct values</strong>: line items that don't sum to the total, a date in the wrong field, a value copied from the wrong section. Those need <strong>semantic validation</strong> in code (4.4).</p></div></aside>\n<h2 id=\"designing-schemas-that-prevent-fabrication\">Designing schemas that prevent fabrication</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Nullable / optional fields</strong> for information that may be absent. If <code>due_date</code> is required and non-nullable, Claude must put <em>something</em> there — and will invent a plausible date. Make it <code>[\"string\",\"null\"]</code> and say \"null if not stated\".</li><li><strong>Enums with an escape hatch</strong>: add <code>\"other\"</code> plus a detail field (<code>currency_other</code>) so unusual values aren't forced into a wrong category.</li><li><strong><code>\"unclear\"</code></strong> values where the source is genuinely ambiguous — better than a confident guess.</li><li><strong>Format instructions</strong> in descriptions (ISO dates, units, normalised names) so values are consistent.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"tool-choice-for-structure\">tool_choice for structure</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Situation</th><th><code>tool_choice</code></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>One known schema, must always be used</td><td>Forced: <code>{\"type\":\"tool\",\"name\":\"record_invoice\"}</code></td></tr><tr><td>Several schemas, document type unknown</td><td><code>{\"type\":\"any\"}</code> — must call one of them</td></tr><tr><td>Extraction is optional (some inputs aren't documents)</td><td><code>auto</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"exam-traps\">Exam traps</h2>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-trap\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Exam trap</div><div class=\"callout-title\">\"The schema guarantees correct data\"</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>It guarantees structure. Validate semantics.</p></div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-trap\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Exam trap</div><div class=\"callout-title\">Required non-nullable fields for optional information</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Causes fabrication. Use nullable fields.</p></div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-trap\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Exam trap</div><div class=\"callout-title\">Prompt-only \"respond in JSON\" for a downstream integration</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Use tool use/structured outputs for reliability.</p></div></aside>\n<h2 id=\"practice-questions\">Practice questions</h2>\n<div class=\"quiz-embed\" data-qid=\"a4-schema-01\"></div>\n<div class=\"quiz-embed\" data-qid=\"a4-schema-02\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"build-exercise\">Build exercise</h2>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-exercise\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Build exercise</div><div class=\"callout-title\">Schema-first extraction</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><ol><li>Implement <code>record_invoice</code> as above and extract 20 invoices with a forced tool choice.</li><li>Make <code>due_date</code> required and non-nullable; count invented dates on invoices without one. Then make it nullable and recount.</li><li>Add <code>\"other\"</code> + detail to the currency enum; feed a CHF invoice.</li><li>Validate <code>sum(line_items) == stated_total</code> in code and count semantic errors the schema didn't catch.</li></ol></div></aside>","headings":[{"id":"what-you-need-to-know","text":"What you need to know"},{"id":"what-schemas-guarantee-and-what-they-don-t","text":"What schemas guarantee — and what they don't"},{"id":"designing-schemas-that-prevent-fabrication","text":"Designing schemas that prevent fabrication"},{"id":"tool-choice-for-structure","text":"tool_choice for structure"},{"id":"exam-traps","text":"Exam traps"},{"id":"practice-questions","text":"Practice questions"},{"id":"build-exercise","text":"Build exercise"}],"sources":["Structured outputs | https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/structured-outputs","How to implement tool use | https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/agents-and-tools/tool-use/implement-tool-use","Increase output consistency | https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/test-and-evaluate/strengthen-guardrails/increase-consistency","Reduce hallucinations | https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/test-and-evaluate/strengthen-guardrails/reduce-hallucinations"],"text":" What you need to know Downstream systems need valid, predictable JSON . Asking for JSON in the prompt makes it likely; tool use with a JSON schema makes the structure reliable you define a tool whose input_schema is the shape you want, and Claude \"calls\" it with the extracted data. (The API's structured outputs feature and strict true tool definitions go further and constrain decoding to the schema.) What schemas guarantee — and what they don't key Syntax vs. semantics A schema eliminates syntax and shape errors missing braces, wrong types, missing required keys. It does not guarantee correct values line items that don't sum to the total, a date in the wrong field, a value copied from the wrong section. Those need semantic validation in code (4.4). Designing schemas that prevent fabrication Nullable / optional fields for information that may be absent. If due_date is required and non nullable, Claude must put something there — and will invent a plausible date. Make it \"string\",\"null\" and say \"null if not stated\". Enums with an escape hatch add \"other\" plus a detail field ( currency_other ) so unusual values aren't forced into a wrong category. \"unclear\" values where the source is genuinely ambiguous — better than a confident guess. Format instructions in descriptions (ISO dates, units, normalised names) so values are consistent. tool_choice for structure Situation tool_choice One known schema, must always be used Forced \"type\" \"tool\",\"name\" \"record_invoice\" Several schemas, document type unknown \"type\" \"any\" — must call one of them Extraction is optional (some inputs aren't documents) auto Exam traps trap \"The schema guarantees correct data\" It guarantees structure. Validate semantics. trap Required non nullable fields for optional information Causes fabrication. Use nullable fields. trap Prompt only \"respond in JSON\" for a downstream integration Use tool use/structured outputs for reliability. Practice questions quiz a4 schema 01 quiz a4 schema 02 Build exercise exercise Schema first extraction 1. Implement record_invoice as above and extract 20 invoices with a forced tool choice. 2. Make due_date required and non nullable; count invented dates on invoices without one. Then make it nullable and recount. 3. Add \"other\" + detail to the currency enum; feed a CHF invoice. 4. Validate sum(line_items) == stated_total in code and count semantic errors the schema didn't catch. "},{"skill":"4.4","slug":"validation-retry-loops","title":"Validation, Retry & Feedback Loops","summary":"Validating extractions in code and retrying with the specific error, recognising when retries can't help, self-correction fields that expose inconsistencies, and detected_pattern fields for learning from dismissals.","minutes":5,"html":"<h2 id=\"what-you-need-to-know\">What you need to know</h2>\n<p>Schemas catch shape errors; <strong>validation code</strong> catches semantic errors. What you do <em>after</em> validation fails decides the system's accuracy.</p>\n<h2 id=\"retry-with-error-feedback\">Retry with error feedback</h2>\n<div class=\"codeblock\"><div class=\"codeblock-head\"><span>validation–retry loop</span><button class=\"copy-btn\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></div><pre><code class=\"lang-python\">def extract(doc, max_retries=2):\n    messages = [{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": doc}]\n    for attempt in range(max_retries + 1):\n        data = call_extraction(messages)\n        errors = validate(data)              # e.g. totals, date order, required semantics\n        if not errors:\n            return data, \"ok\"\n        messages += [\n            {\"role\": \"assistant\", \"content\": as_tool_use(data)},\n            {\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": tool_result(\n                f\"Validation failed: {errors}. Re-check the document and correct \"\n                f\"only these fields. If the value is not in the document, return null.\")},\n        ]\n    return data, \"needs_review\"</code></pre></div>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-key\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key concept</div><div class=\"callout-title\">A good retry includes</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><ol><li>The <strong>original document</strong> (still in the conversation),</li><li>The <strong>failed output</strong>, and</li><li>The <strong>specific validation errors</strong> (\"line items sum to 1,240.00 but stated_total is 1,420.00\").</li></ol>\n<p>A bare \"try again\" gives Claude nothing to correct against.</p></div></aside>\n<h2 id=\"when-retries-won-t-help\">When retries won't help</h2>\n<p>Retries fix <strong>format and reading mistakes</strong> — a misread digit, a value placed in the wrong field. They cannot produce information that <strong>isn't in the source</strong>. If the contract doesn't state a renewal date, or the value lives in an <strong>external document</strong> not provided, retrying just invites fabrication. Detect these cases (same error after retry, or the field is marked absent) and route them to <code>null</code> / <strong>human review</strong> instead of looping.</p>\n<h2 id=\"self-correction-fields\">Self-correction fields</h2>\n<p>Design the schema so inconsistencies become <strong>visible to code</strong>:</p>\n<ul><li><code>calculated_total</code> (sum of extracted line items) next to <code>stated_total</code> (as printed) — a mismatch flags either a misread or a document error.</li><li><code>conflict_detected: true</code> + <code>conflict_note</code> when the document contradicts itself (two different dates for the same event).</li><li><code>source_excerpt</code> for key fields so reviewers can verify quickly.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"learning-from-dismissals-detected-pattern\">Learning from dismissals: detected_pattern</h2>\n<p>In review systems, add a <code>detected_pattern</code> (or rule ID) field to every finding. When developers dismiss findings, you can aggregate <strong>which patterns</strong> get dismissed and fix those criteria (4.1) — instead of guessing why trust is dropping.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-scenario\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Scenario</div><div class=\"callout-title\">S6 — persistent total mismatches</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Retries fix most mismatches. A residual 3% keep failing; inspection shows those invoices carry a discount on a separate credit note not included in the input. <strong>Answer:</strong> stop retrying those — flag <code>conflict_detected</code>, route to review, and consider supplying the credit note as input.</p></div></aside>\n<h2 id=\"exam-traps\">Exam traps</h2>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-trap\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Exam trap</div><div class=\"callout-title\">Unlimited retries</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Cost without benefit when information is missing. Cap and route to review.</p></div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-trap\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Exam trap</div><div class=\"callout-title\">Retrying without the specific error</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Same mistake, again.</p></div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-trap\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Exam trap</div><div class=\"callout-title\">Silently \"fixing\" values in code</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Overwriting <code>stated_total</code> with the calculated value hides real document discrepancies. Flag instead.</p></div></aside>\n<h2 id=\"practice-questions\">Practice questions</h2>\n<div class=\"quiz-embed\" data-qid=\"a4-retry-01\"></div>\n<div class=\"quiz-embed\" data-qid=\"a4-retry-02\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"build-exercise\">Build exercise</h2>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-exercise\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Build exercise</div><div class=\"callout-title\">Validation–retry extraction</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><ol><li>Add semantic validators to your invoice extractor (sum check, due ≥ invoice date).</li><li>Implement the retry loop with specific errors and a cap of 2.</li><li>Add <code>calculated_total</code> and <code>conflict_detected</code> to the schema.</li><li>Feed invoices where the total is genuinely inconsistent; confirm they end as <code>needs_review</code> instead of looping.</li><li>Compare with <code>extract_with_retry</code> in <code>architect/exercises/solutions.py</code>.</li></ol></div></aside>","headings":[{"id":"what-you-need-to-know","text":"What you need to know"},{"id":"retry-with-error-feedback","text":"Retry with error feedback"},{"id":"when-retries-won-t-help","text":"When retries won't help"},{"id":"self-correction-fields","text":"Self-correction fields"},{"id":"learning-from-dismissals-detected-pattern","text":"Learning from dismissals: detected_pattern"},{"id":"exam-traps","text":"Exam traps"},{"id":"practice-questions","text":"Practice questions"},{"id":"build-exercise","text":"Build exercise"}],"sources":["Increase output consistency | https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/test-and-evaluate/strengthen-guardrails/increase-consistency","Reduce hallucinations | https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/test-and-evaluate/strengthen-guardrails/reduce-hallucinations","Structured outputs | https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/structured-outputs","Building effective agents — Anthropic | https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/building-effective-agents"],"text":" What you need to know Schemas catch shape errors; validation code catches semantic errors. What you do after validation fails decides the system's accuracy. Retry with error feedback key A good retry includes 1. The original document (still in the conversation), 2. The failed output , and 3. The specific validation errors (\"line items sum to 1,240.00 but stated_total is 1,420.00\"). A bare \"try again\" gives Claude nothing to correct against. When retries won't help Retries fix format and reading mistakes — a misread digit, a value placed in the wrong field. They cannot produce information that isn't in the source . If the contract doesn't state a renewal date, or the value lives in an external document not provided, retrying just invites fabrication. Detect these cases (same error after retry, or the field is marked absent) and route them to null / human review instead of looping. Self correction fields Design the schema so inconsistencies become visible to code calculated_total (sum of extracted line items) next to stated_total (as printed) — a mismatch flags either a misread or a document error. conflict_detected true + conflict_note when the document contradicts itself (two different dates for the same event). source_excerpt for key fields so reviewers can verify quickly. Learning from dismissals detected_pattern In review systems, add a detected_pattern (or rule ID) field to every finding. When developers dismiss findings, you can aggregate which patterns get dismissed and fix those criteria (4.1) — instead of guessing why trust is dropping. scenario S6 — persistent total mismatches Retries fix most mismatches. A residual 3% keep failing; inspection shows those invoices carry a discount on a separate credit note not included in the input. Answer stop retrying those — flag conflict_detected , route to review, and consider supplying the credit note as input. Exam traps trap Unlimited retries Cost without benefit when information is missing. Cap and route to review. trap Retrying without the specific error Same mistake, again. trap Silently \"fixing\" values in code Overwriting stated_total with the calculated value hides real document discrepancies. Flag instead. Practice questions quiz a4 retry 01 quiz a4 retry 02 Build exercise exercise Validation–retry extraction 1. Add semantic validators to your invoice extractor (sum check, due ≥ invoice date). 2. Implement the retry loop with specific errors and a cap of 2. 3. Add calculated_total and conflict_detected to the schema. 4. Feed invoices where the total is genuinely inconsistent; confirm they end as needs_review instead of looping. 5. Compare with extract_with_retry in architect/exercises/solutions.py . "},{"skill":"4.5","slug":"batch-processing","title":"Batch Processing Strategy","summary":"When the Message Batches API is the right choice — 50% cost, up to 24 hours, no latency guarantee — and how to design batch jobs with custom_id correlation, failure resubmission and prompt refinement on a sample.","minutes":5,"html":"<h2 id=\"what-you-need-to-know\">What you need to know</h2>\n<p>The <strong>Message Batches API</strong> processes many Messages requests asynchronously.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-key\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key concept</div><div class=\"callout-title\">Facts to know cold</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><ul><li><strong>50% cost</strong> of standard API pricing.</li><li>Most batches finish within an hour, but processing can take <strong>up to 24 hours</strong>; there is <strong>no latency SLA</strong>.</li><li>Up to <strong>100,000 requests or 256 MB</strong> per batch; results are available for <strong>29 days</strong>.</li><li>Each request has a <strong><code>custom_id</code></strong>; results may come back <strong>in any order</strong> — correlate by <code>custom_id</code>.</li><li>Each request is a single Messages call: there is <strong>no multi-turn tool loop inside one batch request</strong> (Claude can't call a tool and continue within the batch).</li></ul></div></aside>\n<h2 id=\"matching-workloads\">Matching workloads</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Workload</th><th>Batch?</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Nightly audit of yesterday's commits</td><td>✔</td><td>Nobody waits for it</td></tr><tr><td>Weekly technical-debt report</td><td>✔</td><td>Latency-tolerant</td></tr><tr><td>Back-filling extraction for 200,000 archived documents</td><td>✔</td><td>Large, cost-sensitive, not urgent</td></tr><tr><td>Pre-merge check that blocks the PR</td><td>✘</td><td>Developers would wait up to hours</td></tr><tr><td>Interactive support agent</td><td>✘</td><td>Real-time, multi-turn tool use</td></tr><tr><td>Agentic workflow needing several tool calls per item</td><td>✘ (or redesign)</td><td>No tool loop inside a request</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-scenario\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Scenario</div><div class=\"callout-title\">S5 — two CI workloads, one budget</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>The team wants to cut cost on (a) blocking pre-merge reviews and (b) a nightly security audit. <strong>Answer:</strong> move only (b) to batches; keep (a) synchronous.</p></div></aside>\n<h2 id=\"designing-a-batch-job\">Designing a batch job</h2>\n<ol><li><strong>Refine the prompt on a sample</strong> (e.g. 50 documents) with synchronous calls before submitting 50,000 — fixing a prompt after a full batch run means paying twice.</li><li>Give every request a meaningful <strong><code>custom_id</code></strong> (document ID).</li><li><strong>Plan around the window</strong>: if results are needed by 9:00 and processing can take 24 h, submit early enough — or split into smaller batches on a schedule.</li><li>On completion, collect results by <code>custom_id</code>; for <strong>failed</strong> items, fix the cause (e.g. split documents that exceeded the context limit) and <strong>resubmit only those <code>custom_id</code>s</strong>.</li><li>Combine with <strong>prompt caching</strong> for a shared system prompt to save further.</li></ol>\n<div class=\"codeblock\"><div class=\"codeblock-head\"><span>resubmit only failures</span><button class=\"copy-btn\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></div><pre><code class=\"lang-python\">failed = [r.custom_id for r in results if r.result.type != \"succeeded\"]\nretry_requests = [build_request(doc_id, chunk_if_too_large(docs[doc_id])) for doc_id in failed]\nclient.messages.batches.create(requests=retry_requests)</code></pre></div>\n<h2 id=\"exam-traps\">Exam traps</h2>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-trap\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Exam trap</div><div class=\"callout-title\">Batch for anything \"to save cost\"</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Only latency-tolerant, non-blocking work.</p></div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-trap\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Exam trap</div><div class=\"callout-title\">Resubmitting the whole batch after partial failure</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Pay twice. Resubmit failed <code>custom_id</code>s only.</p></div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-trap\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Exam trap</div><div class=\"callout-title\">Expecting results in submission order</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Correlate by <code>custom_id</code>.</p></div></aside>\n<h2 id=\"practice-questions\">Practice questions</h2>\n<div class=\"quiz-embed\" data-qid=\"a4-batch-01\"></div>\n<div class=\"quiz-embed\" data-qid=\"a4-batch-02\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"build-exercise\">Build exercise</h2>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-exercise\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Build exercise</div><div class=\"callout-title\">Batch extraction with failure handling</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><ol><li>Tune your extraction prompt on 20 documents synchronously.</li><li>Submit 500 documents as a batch with <code>custom_id</code> = document ID.</li><li>Include a few oversized documents; when they fail, chunk them and resubmit only those.</li><li>Compare cost with the synchronous run.</li><li>Compare with <code>resubmit_failed</code> in <code>architect/exercises/solutions.py</code>.</li></ol></div></aside>","headings":[{"id":"what-you-need-to-know","text":"What you need to know"},{"id":"matching-workloads","text":"Matching workloads"},{"id":"designing-a-batch-job","text":"Designing a batch job"},{"id":"exam-traps","text":"Exam traps"},{"id":"practice-questions","text":"Practice questions"},{"id":"build-exercise","text":"Build exercise"}],"sources":["Batch processing | https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/batch-processing","Pricing | https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/about-claude/pricing","Rate limits | https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/api/rate-limits","Prompt caching | https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/prompt-caching"],"text":" What you need to know The Message Batches API processes many Messages requests asynchronously. key Facts to know cold 50% cost of standard API pricing. Most batches finish within an hour, but processing can take up to 24 hours ; there is no latency SLA . Up to 100,000 requests or 256 MB per batch; results are available for 29 days . Each request has a custom_id ; results may come back in any order — correlate by custom_id . Each request is a single Messages call there is no multi turn tool loop inside one batch request (Claude can't call a tool and continue within the batch). Matching workloads Workload Batch? Why Nightly audit of yesterday's commits ✔ Nobody waits for it Weekly technical debt report ✔ Latency tolerant Back filling extraction for 200,000 archived documents ✔ Large, cost sensitive, not urgent Pre merge check that blocks the PR ✘ Developers would wait up to hours Interactive support agent ✘ Real time, multi turn tool use Agentic workflow needing several tool calls per item ✘ (or redesign) No tool loop inside a request scenario S5 — two CI workloads, one budget The team wants to cut cost on (a) blocking pre merge reviews and (b) a nightly security audit. Answer move only (b) to batches; keep (a) synchronous. Designing a batch job 1. Refine the prompt on a sample (e.g. 50 documents) with synchronous calls before submitting 50,000 — fixing a prompt after a full batch run means paying twice. 2. Give every request a meaningful custom_id (document ID). 3. Plan around the window if results are needed by 9 00 and processing can take 24 h, submit early enough — or split into smaller batches on a schedule. 4. On completion, collect results by custom_id ; for failed items, fix the cause (e.g. split documents that exceeded the context limit) and resubmit only those custom_id s . 5. Combine with prompt caching for a shared system prompt to save further. Exam traps trap Batch for anything \"to save cost\" Only latency tolerant, non blocking work. trap Resubmitting the whole batch after partial failure Pay twice. Resubmit failed custom_id s only. trap Expecting results in submission order Correlate by custom_id . Practice questions quiz a4 batch 01 quiz a4 batch 02 Build exercise exercise Batch extraction with failure handling 1. Tune your extraction prompt on 20 documents synchronously. 2. Submit 500 documents as a batch with custom_id = document ID. 3. Include a few oversized documents; when they fail, chunk them and resubmit only those. 4. Compare cost with the synchronous run. 5. Compare with resubmit_failed in architect/exercises/solutions.py . "},{"skill":"4.6","slug":"multi-pass-review","title":"Multi-Instance & Multi-Pass Review","summary":"Why self-review in the same session underperforms, independent reviewer instances, per-file plus cross-file passes, and using calibrated confidence to route findings to humans.","minutes":5,"html":"<h2 id=\"what-you-need-to-know\">What you need to know</h2>\n<p>Review quality depends as much on <strong>who reviews and how the work is split</strong> as on the prompt.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-self-review-limitation\">The self-review limitation</h2>\n<p>A model asked to review output it generated <strong>in the same session</strong> retains the reasoning that produced it. It tends to re-justify its choices and miss its own errors — even when told to be critical. Instructions like \"review your work carefully\" or extended thinking in the same session help only marginally.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-key\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key concept</div><div class=\"callout-title\">Independent review instance</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Run the review in a <strong>separate instance/session</strong> that receives the artifact (code, extraction, report) and the <strong>criteria</strong> — but <strong>not</strong> the generator's reasoning. It evaluates the output on its merits, like a human reviewer seeing a PR for the first time.</p></div></aside>\n<p>This is the evaluator-optimizer pattern with proper separation: generator and evaluator are different contexts.</p>\n<h2 id=\"multi-pass-review\">Multi-pass review</h2>\n<p>For large inputs, one pass suffers attention dilution (1.6). Split by focus:</p>\n<ol><li><strong>Per-file (local) passes</strong> — each file with the same criteria and full attention: bugs, error handling, security within the file.</li><li><strong>Cross-file (integration) pass</strong> — interactions: API contracts, data flow, consistency of shared types, duplicated logic.</li></ol>\n<p>Passes can also be split by <strong>concern</strong> (security pass, correctness pass) when categories need different expertise and criteria.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-scenario\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Scenario</div><div class=\"callout-title\">S5 — reviewer misses a broken contract</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Per-file comments are good, but a renamed field breaks a consumer in another package and nobody notices. <strong>Add</strong> a cross-file integration pass that receives the per-file summaries plus interface definitions.</p></div></aside>\n<h2 id=\"confidence-for-routing\">Confidence for routing</h2>\n<p>Asking reviewers (or extractors) to output a <strong>confidence</strong> per finding enables routing: high-confidence findings auto-post, low-confidence ones go to a human or a second pass. But self-reported confidence is only useful once <strong>calibrated</strong> against labelled data (5.5) — treat raw scores as a routing hint, not truth.</p>\n<h2 id=\"exam-traps\">Exam traps</h2>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-trap\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Exam trap</div><div class=\"callout-title\">\"Ask the same session to double-check its work\"</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Self-confirmation. Use an independent instance.</p></div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-trap\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Exam trap</div><div class=\"callout-title\">One mega-pass over 40 files</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Inconsistent depth. Per-file + integration.</p></div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-trap\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Exam trap</div><div class=\"callout-title\">Uncalibrated confidence as an auto-approve gate</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Validate thresholds on labelled data first.</p></div></aside>\n<h2 id=\"practice-questions\">Practice questions</h2>\n<div class=\"quiz-embed\" data-qid=\"a4-review-01\"></div>\n<div class=\"quiz-embed\" data-qid=\"a4-review-02\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"build-exercise\">Build exercise</h2>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-exercise\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Build exercise</div><div class=\"callout-title\">Generator vs. independent reviewer</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><ol><li>Have Claude implement a function with a subtle bug seeded in the spec.</li><li>Ask the <strong>same session</strong> to review; record what it finds.</li><li>Start a <strong>new session</strong>, give it the code and criteria only; record what it finds.</li><li>Add a cross-file pass for a two-module change and check it catches a contract mismatch the per-file passes miss.</li></ol></div></aside>","headings":[{"id":"what-you-need-to-know","text":"What you need to know"},{"id":"the-self-review-limitation","text":"The self-review limitation"},{"id":"multi-pass-review","text":"Multi-pass review"},{"id":"confidence-for-routing","text":"Confidence for routing"},{"id":"exam-traps","text":"Exam traps"},{"id":"practice-questions","text":"Practice questions"},{"id":"build-exercise","text":"Build exercise"}],"sources":["Building effective agents — Anthropic | https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/building-effective-agents","Claude Code best practices | https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/claude-code-best-practices","Claude Code GitHub Actions | https://code.claude.com/docs/en/github-actions","Develop tests | https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/test-and-evaluate/develop-tests"],"text":" What you need to know Review quality depends as much on who reviews and how the work is split as on the prompt. The self review limitation A model asked to review output it generated in the same session retains the reasoning that produced it. It tends to re justify its choices and miss its own errors — even when told to be critical. Instructions like \"review your work carefully\" or extended thinking in the same session help only marginally. key Independent review instance Run the review in a separate instance/session that receives the artifact (code, extraction, report) and the criteria — but not the generator's reasoning. It evaluates the output on its merits, like a human reviewer seeing a PR for the first time. This is the evaluator optimizer pattern with proper separation generator and evaluator are different contexts. Multi pass review For large inputs, one pass suffers attention dilution (1.6). Split by focus 1. Per file (local) passes — each file with the same criteria and full attention bugs, error handling, security within the file. 2. Cross file (integration) pass — interactions API contracts, data flow, consistency of shared types, duplicated logic. Passes can also be split by concern (security pass, correctness pass) when categories need different expertise and criteria. scenario S5 — reviewer misses a broken contract Per file comments are good, but a renamed field breaks a consumer in another package and nobody notices. Add a cross file integration pass that receives the per file summaries plus interface definitions. Confidence for routing Asking reviewers (or extractors) to output a confidence per finding enables routing high confidence findings auto post, low confidence ones go to a human or a second pass. But self reported confidence is only useful once calibrated against labelled data (5.5) — treat raw scores as a routing hint, not truth. Exam traps trap \"Ask the same session to double check its work\" Self confirmation. Use an independent instance. trap One mega pass over 40 files Inconsistent depth. Per file + integration. trap Uncalibrated confidence as an auto approve gate Validate thresholds on labelled data first. Practice questions quiz a4 review 01 quiz a4 review 02 Build exercise exercise Generator vs. independent reviewer 1. Have Claude implement a function with a subtle bug seeded in the spec. 2. Ask the same session to review; record what it finds. 3. Start a new session , give it the code and criteria only; record what it finds. 4. Add a cross file pass for a two module change and check it catches a contract mismatch the per file passes miss. "},{"skill":"5.1","slug":"conversation-context","title":"Preserving Critical Context","summary":"Keeping exact numbers, dates and commitments alive across long interactions — case-facts blocks instead of lossy summaries, the lost-in-the-middle effect, trimming verbose tool output and positioning key findings.","minutes":5,"html":"<h2 id=\"what-you-need-to-know\">What you need to know</h2>\n<p>Long interactions — a 40-turn support conversation, a multi-hour research run — eventually need context management. The common technique, <strong>progressive summarisation</strong>, saves tokens but is <strong>lossy in exactly the wrong places</strong>: \"the customer was offered a partial refund\" survives; \"$137.50 on order ORD-1182, approved on 3 March, to be processed within 5 days\" does not.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-key\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key concept</div><div class=\"callout-title\">Case facts block</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Keep a <strong>persistent, structured block</strong> of critical facts — amounts, dates, IDs, order numbers, promises made, verification status — <strong>outside</strong> the summarised history and include it verbatim in every request (e.g. at the top of the system prompt or first user turn). Summaries may compress the narrative; they must never be the only home of a number.</p></div></aside>\n<div class=\"codeblock\"><div class=\"codeblock-head\"><span>case facts block (re-sent every turn)</span><button class=\"copy-btn\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></div><pre><code class=\"lang-xml\">&lt;case_facts&gt;\ncustomer_id: C-20931 (verified 2026-03-03 via email + last4)\norder: ORD-1182 · delivered 2026-02-27 · item: Blender X200\nissue: motor failure; customer requests refund\nagreed: partial refund $137.50 to original card, within 5 business days\nopen: confirm return label sent\n&lt;/case_facts&gt;</code></pre></div>\n<h2 id=\"lost-in-the-middle\">Lost in the middle</h2>\n<p>Models attend most reliably to the <strong>beginning and end</strong> of long inputs; facts buried in the middle of a large context are more likely to be missed. Mitigations:</p>\n<ul><li>Put <strong>key findings and instructions at the start</strong> (and restate the task at the end for very long inputs).</li><li>Use <strong>clear section headers / XML tags</strong> so content is navigable.</li><li>For aggregated research, place a <strong>summary of key findings first</strong>, details after.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"trim-tool-output\">Trim tool output</h2>\n<p>An order-lookup tool that returns 40 fields (warehouse codes, internal flags, audit timestamps) for a task that needs 5 fills the context quickly and pushes important facts toward the middle. <strong>Trim</strong> results to relevant fields before they accumulate — in the tool itself, or in a PostToolUse hook (1.5).</p>\n<h2 id=\"structured-state-from-subagents\">Structured state from subagents</h2>\n<p>In multi-agent systems, ask subagents to return <strong>structured results with metadata</strong> rather than prose (1.3). Downstream agents get compact, unambiguous facts, and the coordinator can keep a running facts table instead of a growing transcript.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-scenario\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Scenario</div><div class=\"callout-title\">S1 — the refund amount drifts</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Late in a long conversation the agent confirms \"$130\" though \"$137.50\" was agreed earlier; the history had been summarised twice. <strong>Fix:</strong> case facts block maintained by the application and re-sent each turn.</p></div></aside>\n<h2 id=\"exam-traps\">Exam traps</h2>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-trap\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Exam trap</div><div class=\"callout-title\">\"Use a model with a bigger context window\"</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Delays the problem; doesn't stop summaries from dropping numbers or the middle from being under-attended.</p></div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-trap\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Exam trap</div><div class=\"callout-title\">Relying on progressive summaries for amounts and dates</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Keep them verbatim in a facts block.</p></div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-trap\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Exam trap</div><div class=\"callout-title\">Passing full raw tool payloads forward \"just in case\"</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Trim to what the task needs.</p></div></aside>\n<h2 id=\"practice-questions\">Practice questions</h2>\n<div class=\"quiz-embed\" data-qid=\"a5-ctx-01\"></div>\n<div class=\"quiz-embed\" data-qid=\"a5-ctx-02\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"build-exercise\">Build exercise</h2>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-exercise\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Build exercise</div><div class=\"callout-title\">Case facts vs. summaries</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><ol><li>Script a 30-turn support conversation with several numbers and commitments.</li><li>Run it with progressive summarisation every 10 turns; ask at the end for the agreed amount, date and order.</li><li>Add an application-maintained <code>&lt;case_facts&gt;</code> block re-sent every turn; repeat.</li><li>Trim <code>lookup_order</code> output to five fields and compare total tokens.</li></ol></div></aside>","headings":[{"id":"what-you-need-to-know","text":"What you need to know"},{"id":"lost-in-the-middle","text":"Lost in the middle"},{"id":"trim-tool-output","text":"Trim tool output"},{"id":"structured-state-from-subagents","text":"Structured state from subagents"},{"id":"exam-traps","text":"Exam traps"},{"id":"practice-questions","text":"Practice questions"},{"id":"build-exercise","text":"Build exercise"}],"sources":["Effective context engineering for AI agents | https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/effective-context-engineering-for-ai-agents","Long context prompting tips | https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/prompt-engineering/long-context-tips","Context windows | https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/context-windows","Compaction | https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/compaction"],"text":" What you need to know Long interactions — a 40 turn support conversation, a multi hour research run — eventually need context management. The common technique, progressive summarisation , saves tokens but is lossy in exactly the wrong places \"the customer was offered a partial refund\" survives; \"$137.50 on order ORD 1182, approved on 3 March, to be processed within 5 days\" does not. key Case facts block Keep a persistent, structured block of critical facts — amounts, dates, IDs, order numbers, promises made, verification status — outside the summarised history and include it verbatim in every request (e.g. at the top of the system prompt or first user turn). Summaries may compress the narrative; they must never be the only home of a number. Lost in the middle Models attend most reliably to the beginning and end of long inputs; facts buried in the middle of a large context are more likely to be missed. Mitigations Put key findings and instructions at the start (and restate the task at the end for very long inputs). Use clear section headers / XML tags so content is navigable. For aggregated research, place a summary of key findings first , details after. Trim tool output An order lookup tool that returns 40 fields (warehouse codes, internal flags, audit timestamps) for a task that needs 5 fills the context quickly and pushes important facts toward the middle. Trim results to relevant fields before they accumulate — in the tool itself, or in a PostToolUse hook (1.5). Structured state from subagents In multi agent systems, ask subagents to return structured results with metadata rather than prose (1.3). Downstream agents get compact, unambiguous facts, and the coordinator can keep a running facts table instead of a growing transcript. scenario S1 — the refund amount drifts Late in a long conversation the agent confirms \"$130\" though \"$137.50\" was agreed earlier; the history had been summarised twice. Fix case facts block maintained by the application and re sent each turn. Exam traps trap \"Use a model with a bigger context window\" Delays the problem; doesn't stop summaries from dropping numbers or the middle from being under attended. trap Relying on progressive summaries for amounts and dates Keep them verbatim in a facts block. trap Passing full raw tool payloads forward \"just in case\" Trim to what the task needs. Practice questions quiz a5 ctx 01 quiz a5 ctx 02 Build exercise exercise Case facts vs. summaries 1. Script a 30 turn support conversation with several numbers and commitments. 2. Run it with progressive summarisation every 10 turns; ask at the end for the agreed amount, date and order. 3. Add an application maintained <case_facts block re sent every turn; repeat. 4. Trim lookup_order output to five fields and compare total tokens. "},{"skill":"5.2","slug":"escalation-ambiguity","title":"Escalation & Ambiguity Resolution","summary":"When an agent should hand over to a human — explicit requests, policy gaps, lack of progress — which triggers are unreliable, and how to resolve ambiguous matches by asking rather than guessing.","minutes":5,"html":"<h2 id=\"what-you-need-to-know\">What you need to know</h2>\n<p>The support scenario's target — high first-contact resolution — is only valuable if the agent <strong>escalates the right cases</strong>. Too eager, and humans drown in simple tickets; too reluctant, and customers get stuck or receive out-of-policy decisions.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-key\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key concept</div><div class=\"callout-title\">Reliable escalation triggers</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><ol><li><strong>The customer explicitly asks for a human</strong> → escalate <strong>immediately</strong>. Don't try to resolve first, don't argue.</li><li><strong>Policy gap or exception</strong> — the request isn't covered by policy, or needs an exception (refund beyond the window, competitor price match the policy doesn't mention).</li><li><strong>No meaningful progress</strong> — repeated failed attempts, missing information that can't be obtained.</li><li><strong>Authority limits</strong> — actions above a threshold (amount limits enforced by hooks, 1.5).</li></ol></div></aside>\n<p>Write these as <strong>explicit criteria</strong> in the system prompt, ideally with a few examples of borderline cases (4.2) — \"escalate when…/resolve yourself when…\".</p>\n<h2 id=\"unreliable-triggers\">Unreliable triggers</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Trigger</th><th>Why it's unreliable</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Sentiment</strong> (\"escalate angry customers\")</td><td>Anger doesn't measure complexity. An angry customer with a simple, in-policy issue is best served by resolving it now; a calm one asking for a policy exception needs a human</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Self-reported confidence</strong> (\"escalate if your confidence &lt; 0.7\")</td><td>Poorly calibrated; the model can be confidently wrong on exactly the cases that need escalation</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Turn count alone</strong></td><td>Long conversations aren't necessarily stuck</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Frustration still matters: acknowledge it, and offer a human if the customer <strong>reiterates</strong> they want one — but base the decision on the criteria above.</p>\n<h2 id=\"ambiguity-resolution\">Ambiguity resolution</h2>\n<p>When a lookup returns <strong>multiple matches</strong> (two \"Jordan Lee\" accounts) the agent must <strong>ask for an additional identifier</strong> (email, order number, postcode) — never pick one heuristically (\"most recent\", \"first result\"). Acting on the wrong customer's account is worse than an extra question.</p>\n<p>Similarly, when a request is ambiguous (\"cancel it\" with two open orders), <strong>clarify before acting</strong>.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-scenario\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Scenario</div><div class=\"callout-title\">S1 — competitor price match</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>A customer asks to match a competitor's lower price. Policy covers price adjustments for drops in your own store's price but says nothing about competitors. <strong>Correct:</strong> escalate — it's a policy gap — with a structured handoff (1.4), not a refusal and not an improvised discount.</p></div></aside>\n<h2 id=\"exam-traps\">Exam traps</h2>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-trap\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Exam trap</div><div class=\"callout-title\">Trying to resolve after an explicit request for a human</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Honor the request immediately.</p></div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-trap\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Exam trap</div><div class=\"callout-title\">Sentiment analysis as the escalation router</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Measures mood, not need.</p></div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-trap\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Exam trap</div><div class=\"callout-title\">Choosing the most likely match among several customers</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Ask for an identifier.</p></div></aside>\n<h2 id=\"practice-questions\">Practice questions</h2>\n<div class=\"quiz-embed\" data-qid=\"a5-esc-01\"></div>\n<div class=\"quiz-embed\" data-qid=\"a5-esc-02\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"build-exercise\">Build exercise</h2>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-exercise\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Build exercise</div><div class=\"callout-title\">Escalation policy as code and prompt</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><ol><li>Write explicit escalation criteria and three borderline examples into the system prompt.</li><li>Make <code>get_customer</code> return two matches for a common name; verify the agent asks for an identifier.</li><li>Test 15 scripted conversations (angry-but-simple, calm-policy-gap, explicit human request…) and record escalation precision/recall.</li><li>Compare with a sentiment-based router on the same set.</li><li>Compare with <code>should_escalate</code> in <code>architect/exercises/solutions.py</code>.</li></ol></div></aside>","headings":[{"id":"what-you-need-to-know","text":"What you need to know"},{"id":"unreliable-triggers","text":"Unreliable triggers"},{"id":"ambiguity-resolution","text":"Ambiguity resolution"},{"id":"exam-traps","text":"Exam traps"},{"id":"practice-questions","text":"Practice questions"},{"id":"build-exercise","text":"Build exercise"}],"sources":["Building effective agents — Anthropic | https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/building-effective-agents","Reduce hallucinations | https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/test-and-evaluate/strengthen-guardrails/reduce-hallucinations","Content moderation use case guide | https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/about-claude/use-case-guides/content-moderation","Writing effective tools for agents — Anthropic | https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/writing-tools-for-agents"],"text":" What you need to know The support scenario's target — high first contact resolution — is only valuable if the agent escalates the right cases . Too eager, and humans drown in simple tickets; too reluctant, and customers get stuck or receive out of policy decisions. key Reliable escalation triggers 1. The customer explicitly asks for a human → escalate immediately . Don't try to resolve first, don't argue. 2. Policy gap or exception — the request isn't covered by policy, or needs an exception (refund beyond the window, competitor price match the policy doesn't mention). 3. No meaningful progress — repeated failed attempts, missing information that can't be obtained. 4. Authority limits — actions above a threshold (amount limits enforced by hooks, 1.5). Write these as explicit criteria in the system prompt, ideally with a few examples of borderline cases (4.2) — \"escalate when…/resolve yourself when…\". Unreliable triggers Trigger Why it's unreliable Sentiment (\"escalate angry customers\") Anger doesn't measure complexity. An angry customer with a simple, in policy issue is best served by resolving it now; a calm one asking for a policy exception needs a human Self reported confidence (\"escalate if your confidence < 0.7\") Poorly calibrated; the model can be confidently wrong on exactly the cases that need escalation Turn count alone Long conversations aren't necessarily stuck Frustration still matters acknowledge it, and offer a human if the customer reiterates they want one — but base the decision on the criteria above. Ambiguity resolution When a lookup returns multiple matches (two \"Jordan Lee\" accounts) the agent must ask for an additional identifier (email, order number, postcode) — never pick one heuristically (\"most recent\", \"first result\"). Acting on the wrong customer's account is worse than an extra question. Similarly, when a request is ambiguous (\"cancel it\" with two open orders), clarify before acting . scenario S1 — competitor price match A customer asks to match a competitor's lower price. Policy covers price adjustments for drops in your own store's price but says nothing about competitors. Correct escalate — it's a policy gap — with a structured handoff (1.4), not a refusal and not an improvised discount. Exam traps trap Trying to resolve after an explicit request for a human Honor the request immediately. trap Sentiment analysis as the escalation router Measures mood, not need. trap Choosing the most likely match among several customers Ask for an identifier. Practice questions quiz a5 esc 01 quiz a5 esc 02 Build exercise exercise Escalation policy as code and prompt 1. Write explicit escalation criteria and three borderline examples into the system prompt. 2. Make get_customer return two matches for a common name; verify the agent asks for an identifier. 3. Test 15 scripted conversations (angry but simple, calm policy gap, explicit human request…) and record escalation precision/recall. 4. Compare with a sentiment based router on the same set. 5. Compare with should_escalate in architect/exercises/solutions.py . "},{"skill":"5.3","slug":"error-propagation","title":"Error Propagation in Multi-Agent Systems","summary":"How failures should travel from subagents to the coordinator — structured context instead of silence or total abort — local recovery first, and coverage annotations in the final output.","minutes":5,"html":"<h2 id=\"what-you-need-to-know\">What you need to know</h2>\n<p>In a multi-agent system, a failure in one subagent is information the <strong>coordinator</strong> needs to decide what to do. Two opposite mistakes dominate exam distractors:</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-key\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key concept</div><div class=\"callout-title\">Two anti-patterns</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><ol><li><strong>Silent suppression</strong> — the subagent catches the error and returns an empty result <strong>as if successful</strong>. The coordinator concludes \"no sources exist\" and the final report is confidently incomplete.</li><li><strong>Total abort</strong> — one subagent's timeout terminates the entire workflow, discarding everything the other subagents found.</li></ol></div></aside>\n<p>The correct pattern sits between them: <strong>recover locally where possible; otherwise propagate structured error context and let the coordinator decide.</strong></p>\n<h2 id=\"local-recovery-first\">Local recovery first</h2>\n<p>Subagents should handle <strong>transient</strong> failures themselves (retry with backoff), fix <strong>validation</strong> problems (reformulate the query), and only propagate what they can't resolve. This keeps the coordinator's context free of noise.</p>\n<h2 id=\"what-to-propagate\">What to propagate</h2>\n<div class=\"codeblock\"><div class=\"codeblock-head\"><span>subagent result with a partial failure</span><button class=\"copy-btn\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></div><pre><code class=\"lang-json\">{\n  \"status\": \"partial\",\n  \"results\": [{\"claim\": \"...\", \"source\": \"...\", \"date\": \"2025-11-02\"}],\n  \"error\": {\n    \"type\": \"timeout\",\n    \"attempted\": \"search: 'EU AI Act enforcement fines 2026' (3 attempts)\",\n    \"partial\": \"2 of 5 planned sources retrieved\",\n    \"alternatives\": [\"query official EU press releases\", \"search national regulator sites\"]\n  }\n}</code></pre></div>\n<p>With <strong>failure type, attempted query, partial results and alternatives</strong>, the coordinator can retry with a different query, delegate to another subagent, proceed with partial coverage, or escalate.</p>\n<h2 id=\"coverage-annotations\">Coverage annotations</h2>\n<p>When the system proceeds despite gaps, the output must <strong>say so</strong>: \"Regulatory enforcement data for 2026 could not be retrieved (search timeout); figures in section 3 cover 2025 only.\" This is graceful degradation — useful partial results with honest boundaries.</p>\n<h2 id=\"distinguish-access-failures-from-empty-results\">Distinguish access failures from empty results</h2>\n<p>As in 2.2: \"searched, found nothing\" is valid evidence of absence; \"couldn't search\" is not. The structured status (<code>ok</code> / <code>empty</code> / <code>partial</code> / <code>failed</code>) keeps that distinction through the pipeline.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-scenario\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Scenario</div><div class=\"callout-title\">S3 — the web subagent times out</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p><strong>Wrong:</strong> return <code>[]</code> (suppression) or raise and stop the run (abort). <strong>Right:</strong> retry locally; then return partial results with the structured error; the coordinator re-delegates with an alternative query or annotates the gap in the report.</p></div></aside>\n<h2 id=\"exam-traps\">Exam traps</h2>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-trap\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Exam trap</div><div class=\"callout-title\">Returning empty success on failure</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>The most damaging option: wrong conclusions with no signal.</p></div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-trap\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Exam trap</div><div class=\"callout-title\">Aborting everything on one failure</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Throws away valid work.</p></div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-trap\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Exam trap</div><div class=\"callout-title\">Propagating raw stack traces</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Unstructured noise; the coordinator needs type, attempt, partial results and alternatives.</p></div></aside>\n<h2 id=\"practice-questions\">Practice questions</h2>\n<div class=\"quiz-embed\" data-qid=\"a5-prop-01\"></div>\n<div class=\"quiz-embed\" data-qid=\"a5-prop-02\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"build-exercise\">Build exercise</h2>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-exercise\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Build exercise</div><div class=\"callout-title\">Propagation in a research pipeline</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><ol><li>Make one search subagent fail randomly with timeouts.</li><li>Version A: swallow errors and return <code>[]</code>. Version B: raise and abort. Version C: retry locally, then return structured partial results.</li><li>Compare final reports for accuracy and honesty about coverage.</li><li>Add coverage annotations to the synthesis prompt for any <code>partial</code>/<code>failed</code> inputs.</li></ol></div></aside>","headings":[{"id":"what-you-need-to-know","text":"What you need to know"},{"id":"local-recovery-first","text":"Local recovery first"},{"id":"what-to-propagate","text":"What to propagate"},{"id":"coverage-annotations","text":"Coverage annotations"},{"id":"distinguish-access-failures-from-empty-results","text":"Distinguish access failures from empty results"},{"id":"exam-traps","text":"Exam traps"},{"id":"practice-questions","text":"Practice questions"},{"id":"build-exercise","text":"Build exercise"}],"sources":["How we built our multi-agent research system — Anthropic | https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/multi-agent-research-system","Agent SDK: Subagents | https://code.claude.com/docs/en/agent-sdk/subagents","Writing effective tools for agents — Anthropic | https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/writing-tools-for-agents","Errors (Claude API) | https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/api/errors"],"text":" What you need to know In a multi agent system, a failure in one subagent is information the coordinator needs to decide what to do. Two opposite mistakes dominate exam distractors key Two anti patterns 1. Silent suppression — the subagent catches the error and returns an empty result as if successful . The coordinator concludes \"no sources exist\" and the final report is confidently incomplete. 2. Total abort — one subagent's timeout terminates the entire workflow, discarding everything the other subagents found. The correct pattern sits between them recover locally where possible; otherwise propagate structured error context and let the coordinator decide. Local recovery first Subagents should handle transient failures themselves (retry with backoff), fix validation problems (reformulate the query), and only propagate what they can't resolve. This keeps the coordinator's context free of noise. What to propagate With failure type, attempted query, partial results and alternatives , the coordinator can retry with a different query, delegate to another subagent, proceed with partial coverage, or escalate. Coverage annotations When the system proceeds despite gaps, the output must say so \"Regulatory enforcement data for 2026 could not be retrieved (search timeout); figures in section 3 cover 2025 only.\" This is graceful degradation — useful partial results with honest boundaries. Distinguish access failures from empty results As in 2.2 \"searched, found nothing\" is valid evidence of absence; \"couldn't search\" is not. The structured status ( ok / empty / partial / failed ) keeps that distinction through the pipeline. scenario S3 — the web subagent times out Wrong return (suppression) or raise and stop the run (abort). Right retry locally; then return partial results with the structured error; the coordinator re delegates with an alternative query or annotates the gap in the report. Exam traps trap Returning empty success on failure The most damaging option wrong conclusions with no signal. trap Aborting everything on one failure Throws away valid work. trap Propagating raw stack traces Unstructured noise; the coordinator needs type, attempt, partial results and alternatives. Practice questions quiz a5 prop 01 quiz a5 prop 02 Build exercise exercise Propagation in a research pipeline 1. Make one search subagent fail randomly with timeouts. 2. Version A swallow errors and return . Version B raise and abort. Version C retry locally, then return structured partial results. 3. Compare final reports for accuracy and honesty about coverage. 4. Add coverage annotations to the synthesis prompt for any partial / failed inputs. "},{"skill":"5.4","slug":"large-codebase-context","title":"Context in Large Codebase Exploration","summary":"Recognising context degradation in long sessions and countering it with scratchpad files, subagent delegation, phase summaries, deliberate /compact and crash-recovery manifests.","minutes":5,"html":"<h2 id=\"what-you-need-to-know\">What you need to know</h2>\n<p>Exploring a large, unfamiliar codebase is the Developer Productivity scenario (S4) in a nutshell. The longer a session runs, the more its context fills with file contents, search results and dead ends.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-key\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key concept</div><div class=\"callout-title\">Symptom of context degradation</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Early in the session Claude cites specific classes and functions it read. Later it starts saying things like \"this probably follows the typical repository pattern\" — generic statements instead of the concrete code it saw an hour ago. Answers become inconsistent with earlier findings.</p></div></aside>\n<h2 id=\"countermeasures\">Countermeasures</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Technique</th><th>How it helps</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Scratchpad / findings file</strong></td><td>Claude writes key findings (entry points, data flow, decisions) to a file such as <code>NOTES.md</code> as it goes; later questions reference the file instead of relying on old context</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Subagent delegation</strong></td><td>Verbose searches run in subagents (or the Explore subagent); only summaries return, keeping the main context focused</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Summarise between phases</strong></td><td>Before moving from \"map the system\" to \"plan the change\", write a structured summary; start the next phase from it</td></tr><tr><td><strong><code>/compact</code></strong> (with focus instructions)</td><td>Compresses history deliberately at a good moment — \"compact, keep the list of call sites and the migration decisions\"</td></tr><tr><td><strong><code>/clear</code> + summary</strong></td><td>When a phase is done, clear and continue from the written summary</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Crash-recovery manifest</strong></td><td>For long multi-step jobs, a manifest of completed steps, outputs and next steps lets work resume after a crash or reset without redoing everything</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"codeblock\"><div class=\"codeblock-head\"><span>NOTES.md — scratchpad maintained during exploration</span><button class=\"copy-btn\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></div><pre><code class=\"lang-markdown\">## Findings (updated 14:20)\n- Payment entry: api/routes/checkout.py::create_payment → services/billing.py::charge\n- charge() re-exported as billing.charge_card in legacy/wrappers.py (3 callers)\n- Retries: tenacity, 3 attempts, no idempotency key  ← risk\n## Decisions\n- Migrate callers to services.billing.charge, keep wrapper deprecated\n## Next\n- Check webhooks/ for direct Stripe calls</code></pre></div>\n<h2 id=\"combine-with-incremental-exploration\">Combine with incremental exploration</h2>\n<p>Context management complements good exploration habits (2.5): Grep and targeted Reads instead of reading everything, and the Explore subagent for broad questions.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-scenario\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Scenario</div><div class=\"callout-title\">S4 — three-hour legacy investigation</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>After two hours, answers contradict earlier ones. <strong>Better approach:</strong> findings file from the start, delegate module surveys to subagents, summarise and <code>/compact</code> at phase boundaries; if a fresh session is needed, start it from the findings file (1.7).</p></div></aside>\n<h2 id=\"exam-traps\">Exam traps</h2>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-trap\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Exam trap</div><div class=\"callout-title\">\"Use a model with a larger context window\"</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Doesn't remove noise; degradation still occurs.</p></div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-trap\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Exam trap</div><div class=\"callout-title\">Reading the whole repository into context first</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Accelerates degradation.</p></div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-trap\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Exam trap</div><div class=\"callout-title\">Compacting without guidance at a random moment</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Compact at phase boundaries with instructions about what to keep.</p></div></aside>\n<h2 id=\"practice-questions\">Practice questions</h2>\n<div class=\"quiz-embed\" data-qid=\"a5-code-01\"></div>\n<div class=\"quiz-embed\" data-qid=\"a5-code-02\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"build-exercise\">Build exercise</h2>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-exercise\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Build exercise</div><div class=\"callout-title\">Long exploration with a findings file</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><ol><li>Pick a large open-source repo and a cross-cutting question.</li><li>Session A: explore freely for an hour; ask a detail question about something found early.</li><li>Session B: instruct Claude to maintain <code>NOTES.md</code>, use the Explore subagent for surveys and <code>/compact</code> at phase boundaries; ask the same question.</li><li>Kill session B midway and resume from <code>NOTES.md</code> in a fresh session.</li></ol></div></aside>","headings":[{"id":"what-you-need-to-know","text":"What you need to know"},{"id":"countermeasures","text":"Countermeasures"},{"id":"combine-with-incremental-exploration","text":"Combine with incremental exploration"},{"id":"exam-traps","text":"Exam traps"},{"id":"practice-questions","text":"Practice questions"},{"id":"build-exercise","text":"Build exercise"}],"sources":["Effective context engineering for AI agents | https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/effective-context-engineering-for-ai-agents","Claude Code best practices | https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/claude-code-best-practices","Claude Code subagents | https://code.claude.com/docs/en/sub-agents","Claude Code slash commands | https://code.claude.com/docs/en/commands"],"text":" What you need to know Exploring a large, unfamiliar codebase is the Developer Productivity scenario (S4) in a nutshell. The longer a session runs, the more its context fills with file contents, search results and dead ends. key Symptom of context degradation Early in the session Claude cites specific classes and functions it read. Later it starts saying things like \"this probably follows the typical repository pattern\" — generic statements instead of the concrete code it saw an hour ago. Answers become inconsistent with earlier findings. Countermeasures Technique How it helps Scratchpad / findings file Claude writes key findings (entry points, data flow, decisions) to a file such as NOTES.md as it goes; later questions reference the file instead of relying on old context Subagent delegation Verbose searches run in subagents (or the Explore subagent); only summaries return, keeping the main context focused Summarise between phases Before moving from \"map the system\" to \"plan the change\", write a structured summary; start the next phase from it /compact (with focus instructions) Compresses history deliberately at a good moment — \"compact, keep the list of call sites and the migration decisions\" /clear + summary When a phase is done, clear and continue from the written summary Crash recovery manifest For long multi step jobs, a manifest of completed steps, outputs and next steps lets work resume after a crash or reset without redoing everything Combine with incremental exploration Context management complements good exploration habits (2.5) Grep and targeted Reads instead of reading everything, and the Explore subagent for broad questions. scenario S4 — three hour legacy investigation After two hours, answers contradict earlier ones. Better approach findings file from the start, delegate module surveys to subagents, summarise and /compact at phase boundaries; if a fresh session is needed, start it from the findings file (1.7). Exam traps trap \"Use a model with a larger context window\" Doesn't remove noise; degradation still occurs. trap Reading the whole repository into context first Accelerates degradation. trap Compacting without guidance at a random moment Compact at phase boundaries with instructions about what to keep. Practice questions quiz a5 code 01 quiz a5 code 02 Build exercise exercise Long exploration with a findings file 1. Pick a large open source repo and a cross cutting question. 2. Session A explore freely for an hour; ask a detail question about something found early. 3. Session B instruct Claude to maintain NOTES.md , use the Explore subagent for surveys and /compact at phase boundaries; ask the same question. 4. Kill session B midway and resume from NOTES.md in a fresh session. "},{"skill":"5.5","slug":"human-review-calibration","title":"Human Review & Confidence Calibration","summary":"Why aggregate accuracy hides weak segments, measuring per document type and field, stratified random sampling of automated output, and calibrating field-level confidence thresholds on labelled data.","minutes":5,"html":"<h2 id=\"what-you-need-to-know\">What you need to know</h2>\n<p>Automation pays off when humans review <strong>fewer</strong> outputs — but only if the system knows <strong>which</strong> outputs are safe to auto-accept. That requires measuring accuracy at the right granularity and calibrating confidence.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-key\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key concept</div><div class=\"callout-title\">Aggregate accuracy hides failure</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>\"97% accurate overall\" can mean 99.5% on standard invoices (90% of volume) and 75% on handwritten forms (3% of volume). Reducing human review based on the aggregate would silently pass a quarter of the handwritten forms with errors. Measure <strong>per document type</strong> and <strong>per field</strong> before changing review policy.</p></div></aside>\n<h2 id=\"stratified-random-sampling\">Stratified random sampling</h2>\n<p>Even after automation, keep a <strong>sample</strong> of auto-accepted results flowing to humans to detect drift and novel failure patterns. Sample <strong>stratified</strong> by document type, source and confidence band — simple random sampling would almost never pick the rare types where errors concentrate.</p>\n<h2 id=\"field-level-confidence-and-calibration\">Field-level confidence and calibration</h2>\n<p>Ask the extractor to output a <strong>confidence per field</strong> (or derive one from validation signals). Then:</p>\n<ol><li>Build a <strong>labelled validation set</strong> (human-verified ground truth).</li><li>Compare predicted confidence with actual correctness per field/type — e.g. of fields marked 0.9, are ~90% correct?</li><li>Set <strong>thresholds</strong> per field: above → auto-accept; below → human review.</li><li>Recalibrate when inputs or prompts change.</li></ol>\n<p>Uncalibrated self-reported confidence is not a safe gate; calibrated confidence is a powerful router.</p>\n<h2 id=\"routing-design\">Routing design</h2>\n<div class=\"codeblock\"><div class=\"codeblock-head\"><span>review routing</span><button class=\"copy-btn\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></div><pre><code class=\"lang-text\">if validation_failed or conflict_detected:          → human review (always)\nelif doc_type in LOW_ACCURACY_TYPES:                → human review\nelif min(field_confidence) &lt; calibrated_threshold:  → human review (only low fields highlighted)\nelse:                                                → auto-accept, eligible for stratified sample</code></pre></div>\n<p>Show reviewers the <strong>low-confidence fields and source excerpts</strong>, not the whole document from scratch.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-scenario\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Scenario</div><div class=\"callout-title\">S6 — \"can we cut review by 80%?\"</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Overall accuracy is 96%. <strong>First step:</strong> break accuracy down by document type and field on a labelled sample; only segments that meet the bar move to auto-accept, with stratified sampling continuing.</p></div></aside>\n<h2 id=\"exam-traps\">Exam traps</h2>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-trap\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Exam trap</div><div class=\"callout-title\">Reducing review based on aggregate accuracy</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Check segments first.</p></div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-trap\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Exam trap</div><div class=\"callout-title\">Simple random sampling for monitoring</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Misses rare, error-prone segments. Stratify.</p></div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-trap\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Exam trap</div><div class=\"callout-title\">Raw model confidence as an auto-approve threshold</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Calibrate on labelled data.</p></div></aside>\n<h2 id=\"practice-questions\">Practice questions</h2>\n<div class=\"quiz-embed\" data-qid=\"a5-hrev-01\"></div>\n<div class=\"quiz-embed\" data-qid=\"a5-hrev-02\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"build-exercise\">Build exercise</h2>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-exercise\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Build exercise</div><div class=\"callout-title\">Calibrate an extraction pipeline</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><ol><li>Label 200 extracted documents across 4 types.</li><li>Compute accuracy overall, per type and per field.</li><li>Add per-field confidence output; plot confidence vs. correctness.</li><li>Choose thresholds per field and estimate the review workload and residual error.</li><li>Implement stratified sampling of auto-accepted results.</li></ol></div></aside>","headings":[{"id":"what-you-need-to-know","text":"What you need to know"},{"id":"stratified-random-sampling","text":"Stratified random sampling"},{"id":"field-level-confidence-and-calibration","text":"Field-level confidence and calibration"},{"id":"routing-design","text":"Routing design"},{"id":"exam-traps","text":"Exam traps"},{"id":"practice-questions","text":"Practice questions"},{"id":"build-exercise","text":"Build exercise"}],"sources":["Define success criteria | https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/test-and-evaluate/define-success","Develop tests | https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/test-and-evaluate/develop-tests","Reduce hallucinations | https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/test-and-evaluate/strengthen-guardrails/reduce-hallucinations","Building effective agents — Anthropic | https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/building-effective-agents"],"text":" What you need to know Automation pays off when humans review fewer outputs — but only if the system knows which outputs are safe to auto accept. That requires measuring accuracy at the right granularity and calibrating confidence. key Aggregate accuracy hides failure \"97% accurate overall\" can mean 99.5% on standard invoices (90% of volume) and 75% on handwritten forms (3% of volume). Reducing human review based on the aggregate would silently pass a quarter of the handwritten forms with errors. Measure per document type and per field before changing review policy. Stratified random sampling Even after automation, keep a sample of auto accepted results flowing to humans to detect drift and novel failure patterns. Sample stratified by document type, source and confidence band — simple random sampling would almost never pick the rare types where errors concentrate. Field level confidence and calibration Ask the extractor to output a confidence per field (or derive one from validation signals). Then 1. Build a labelled validation set (human verified ground truth). 2. Compare predicted confidence with actual correctness per field/type — e.g. of fields marked 0.9, are ~90% correct? 3. Set thresholds per field above → auto accept; below → human review. 4. Recalibrate when inputs or prompts change. Uncalibrated self reported confidence is not a safe gate; calibrated confidence is a powerful router. Routing design Show reviewers the low confidence fields and source excerpts , not the whole document from scratch. scenario S6 — \"can we cut review by 80%?\" Overall accuracy is 96%. First step break accuracy down by document type and field on a labelled sample; only segments that meet the bar move to auto accept, with stratified sampling continuing. Exam traps trap Reducing review based on aggregate accuracy Check segments first. trap Simple random sampling for monitoring Misses rare, error prone segments. Stratify. trap Raw model confidence as an auto approve threshold Calibrate on labelled data. Practice questions quiz a5 hrev 01 quiz a5 hrev 02 Build exercise exercise Calibrate an extraction pipeline 1. Label 200 extracted documents across 4 types. 2. Compute accuracy overall, per type and per field. 3. Add per field confidence output; plot confidence vs. correctness. 4. Choose thresholds per field and estimate the review workload and residual error. 5. Implement stratified sampling of auto accepted results. "},{"skill":"5.6","slug":"information-provenance","title":"Information Provenance & Uncertainty","summary":"Keeping claim–source mappings through multi-agent synthesis, annotating conflicting values instead of silently choosing, carrying dates so time differences aren't mistaken for contradictions, and rendering content types appropriately.","minutes":5,"html":"<h2 id=\"what-you-need-to-know\">What you need to know</h2>\n<p>In multi-source synthesis — the research system (S3) above all — the value of the output depends on whether readers can <strong>trace each claim</strong> and <strong>see where sources disagree</strong>. Provenance is easily lost: each summarisation step drops URLs, dates and qualifiers.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-key\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key concept</div><div class=\"callout-title\">Claim–source mapping</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Every finding travels as a structured record — <strong>claim, source (URL or document + page), excerpt, publication/collection date</strong> — from the subagent that found it, through the coordinator, into the synthesis. The synthesis agent is instructed to <strong>preserve</strong> these mappings and cite them, not to re-summarise them away.</p></div></aside>\n<div class=\"codeblock\"><div class=\"codeblock-head\"><span>finding record</span><button class=\"copy-btn\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></div><pre><code class=\"lang-json\">{\"claim\": \"Global market reached $4.2B in 2025\",\n \"source\": \"https://example.org/market-report-2026\", \"title\": \"Market Report 2026\",\n \"excerpt\": \"…revenues of USD 4.2 billion in calendar 2025…\",\n \"published\": \"2026-01-15\", \"method\": \"vendor revenue survey\"}</code></pre></div>\n<h2 id=\"conflicting-values\">Conflicting values</h2>\n<p>Two credible sources give different numbers (4.2B vs. 3.6B). The wrong move is to <strong>pick one</strong> arbitrarily (or average them) without saying so.</p>\n<ul><li>Report <strong>both values with attribution</strong>.</li><li>Look for explanations in <strong>metadata</strong>: different years, definitions, methodologies, regions.</li><li>Mark the conflict explicitly; let the coordinator decide whether to investigate further.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"dates-prevent-false-contradictions\">Dates prevent false contradictions</h2>\n<p>Many apparent conflicts are <strong>temporal</strong>: a 2022 figure and a 2025 figure aren't contradictory. Requiring <code>published</code> / <code>data_as_of</code> fields lets the synthesis present a trend instead of a conflict.</p>\n<h2 id=\"uncertainty-and-structure\">Uncertainty and structure</h2>\n<ul><li>Distinguish <strong>well-established</strong> findings (multiple independent sources) from <strong>contested</strong> or single-source ones; reports can have separate sections.</li><li><strong>Render content appropriately</strong>: financial data as tables, narrative news as prose, technical findings as structured lists — forcing everything into one format obscures what kind of evidence it is.</li><li>Built-in <strong>citations</strong> (API Citations feature for documents) help when synthesising from supplied documents.</li></ul>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-scenario\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Scenario</div><div class=\"callout-title\">S3 — the report cites nothing for its key number</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Subagents found the figure with its source, but the coordinator passed only prose summaries to the synthesis agent. <strong>Fix:</strong> pass structured finding records and require claim-level citations in the report format.</p></div></aside>\n<h2 id=\"exam-traps\">Exam traps</h2>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-trap\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Exam trap</div><div class=\"callout-title\">Choosing the \"more reliable\" source silently</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Present both, attributed; explain the difference if possible.</p></div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-trap\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Exam trap</div><div class=\"callout-title\">Dropping dates during summarisation</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Creates false conflicts and stale conclusions.</p></div></aside>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-trap\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Exam trap</div><div class=\"callout-title\">Asking the synthesis agent to \"add citations\" afterwards</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>It can't cite what it no longer has. Preserve mappings throughout.</p></div></aside>\n<h2 id=\"practice-questions\">Practice questions</h2>\n<div class=\"quiz-embed\" data-qid=\"a5-prov-01\"></div>\n<div class=\"quiz-embed\" data-qid=\"a5-prov-02\"></div>\n<h2 id=\"build-exercise\">Build exercise</h2>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-exercise\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Build exercise</div><div class=\"callout-title\">Provenance-preserving synthesis</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><ol><li>Have two subagents research the same statistic from different sources.</li><li>Pass prose summaries to the synthesis agent; check citations.</li><li>Pass structured finding records; require each sentence to cite a record ID.</li><li>Seed a conflict (different years) and confirm the report explains it as a trend; seed a true conflict and confirm both values are attributed.</li></ol></div></aside>","headings":[{"id":"what-you-need-to-know","text":"What you need to know"},{"id":"conflicting-values","text":"Conflicting values"},{"id":"dates-prevent-false-contradictions","text":"Dates prevent false contradictions"},{"id":"uncertainty-and-structure","text":"Uncertainty and structure"},{"id":"exam-traps","text":"Exam traps"},{"id":"practice-questions","text":"Practice questions"},{"id":"build-exercise","text":"Build exercise"}],"sources":["Citations | https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/citations","How we built our multi-agent research system — Anthropic | https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/multi-agent-research-system","Reduce hallucinations | https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/test-and-evaluate/strengthen-guardrails/reduce-hallucinations","Long context prompting tips | https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/prompt-engineering/long-context-tips"],"text":" What you need to know In multi source synthesis — the research system (S3) above all — the value of the output depends on whether readers can trace each claim and see where sources disagree . Provenance is easily lost each summarisation step drops URLs, dates and qualifiers. key Claim–source mapping Every finding travels as a structured record — claim, source (URL or document + page), excerpt, publication/collection date — from the subagent that found it, through the coordinator, into the synthesis. The synthesis agent is instructed to preserve these mappings and cite them, not to re summarise them away. Conflicting values Two credible sources give different numbers (4.2B vs. 3.6B). The wrong move is to pick one arbitrarily (or average them) without saying so. Report both values with attribution . Look for explanations in metadata different years, definitions, methodologies, regions. Mark the conflict explicitly; let the coordinator decide whether to investigate further. Dates prevent false contradictions Many apparent conflicts are temporal a 2022 figure and a 2025 figure aren't contradictory. Requiring published / data_as_of fields lets the synthesis present a trend instead of a conflict. Uncertainty and structure Distinguish well established findings (multiple independent sources) from contested or single source ones; reports can have separate sections. Render content appropriately financial data as tables, narrative news as prose, technical findings as structured lists — forcing everything into one format obscures what kind of evidence it is. Built in citations (API Citations feature for documents) help when synthesising from supplied documents. scenario S3 — the report cites nothing for its key number Subagents found the figure with its source, but the coordinator passed only prose summaries to the synthesis agent. Fix pass structured finding records and require claim level citations in the report format. Exam traps trap Choosing the \"more reliable\" source silently Present both, attributed; explain the difference if possible. trap Dropping dates during summarisation Creates false conflicts and stale conclusions. trap Asking the synthesis agent to \"add citations\" afterwards It can't cite what it no longer has. Preserve mappings throughout. Practice questions quiz a5 prov 01 quiz a5 prov 02 Build exercise exercise Provenance preserving synthesis 1. Have two subagents research the same statistic from different sources. 2. Pass prose summaries to the synthesis agent; check citations. 3. Pass structured finding records; require each sentence to cite a record ID. 4. Seed a conflict (different years) and confirm the report explains it as a trend; seed a true conflict and confirm both values are attributed. "}],"questions":[{"id":"a1-loop-01","skill":"1.1","type":"single","stem":"<p>Your support agent sometimes replies \"Let me look up your order now.\" and the conversation ends without any lookup. The API response for that turn has <code>stop_reason: \"tool_use\"</code> and contains a text block followed by a <code>tool_use</code> block. Your loop exits as soon as the response contains a text block. What is the correct fix?</p>","options":["Add a system-prompt instruction telling Claude never to write text in the same turn as a tool call","Drive the loop by <code>stop_reason</code>: execute tools and continue on <code>tool_use</code>, exit only on <code>end_turn</code>","Raise the loop's iteration limit so that the agent has more turns available to finish its work","Parse the text block for phrases like \"let me\" and treat those as a signal to keep looping"],"correct":[1],"explanation":"<p><code>stop_reason</code> is the contract for loop control. Claude often explains its next step in text alongside a <code>tool_use</code> block; exiting on text skips the requested call. Prompting Claude not to write text is fragile and unnecessary, the iteration limit is irrelevant (the loop exits after one turn), and phrase parsing is exactly the natural-language anti-pattern to avoid.</p>","scenario":"S1","domain":1},{"id":"a1-loop-02","skill":"1.1","type":"single","stem":"<p>A developer-productivity agent repeats the same Grep calls and contradicts conclusions it reached earlier. The loop sends <code>[original task, latest tool result]</code> on each iteration to save tokens. What is the root cause?</p>","options":["The model's temperature is too high, so its tool choices vary randomly from one iteration to the next","The Grep tool's description is too vague, so the model cannot tell when a search has already been done","Earlier assistant turns and tool results are dropped, so Claude has no record of what it already did","The agent lacks a scratchpad tool, which is required for any agent that runs more than a few iterations"],"correct":[2],"explanation":"<p>Each iteration must append the full assistant turn and the tool results to the history; that is how Claude knows what it has done. Rebuilding the prompt from the task plus the latest result throws that away, causing repeated calls and contradictions. Context size should be managed deliberately (trimming, summaries of facts), not by discarding the loop's own history.</p>","scenario":"S4","domain":1},{"id":"a1-loop-03","skill":"1.1","type":"single","stem":"<p>Your research agent's loop stops when it reaches 10 iterations, and reports the current text as the final answer. Reviewers notice reports that end mid-investigation. What is the best change?</p>","options":["Increase the limit to 25 iterations so that complex research requests have enough turns to finish","Ask Claude in the system prompt to always wrap up its research within ten tool calls at the most","Remove the limit entirely, since <code>stop_reason</code> already guarantees that the loop will eventually terminate","Keep a cap only as a safety net that flags the run as incomplete; treat <code>end_turn</code> as the completion signal"],"correct":[3],"explanation":"<p>An iteration cap is a guard against runaway loops and cost, not a completion signal. Reaching it means the run was cut off, which should be surfaced (raise, flag, log) rather than presented as a finished report. Raising the number just moves the problem; prompting for brevity degrades quality; removing the cap entirely loses protection against loops that never reach <code>end_turn</code>.</p>","scenario":"S3","domain":1},{"id":"a1-loop-04","skill":"1.1","type":"single","stem":"<p>An extraction agent occasionally returns a JSON object that is cut off mid-string, and your loop passes it downstream as the final answer. The response for those cases shows <code>stop_reason: \"max_tokens\"</code>. What should the loop do?</p>","options":["Treat <code>max_tokens</code> as an incomplete response: raise the token limit or continue generation, never accept it as final","Treat <code>max_tokens</code> like <code>end_turn</code>, but repair the truncated JSON with a lenient parser before passing it on","Retry the identical request immediately, since the truncation is a transient failure of the API service","Switch to a larger-context model, because the context window was exceeded during the extraction call"],"correct":[0],"explanation":"<p><code>max_tokens</code> means output hit the configured output limit, so the result is truncated. The loop must handle it explicitly by increasing <code>max_tokens</code> or continuing. Repairing truncated JSON silently loses data; retrying the same request reproduces the truncation; the context window is a different limit (that would be <code>model_context_window_exceeded</code>).</p>","scenario":"S6","domain":1},{"id":"a1-loop-05","skill":"1.1","type":"single","stem":"<p>In one turn Claude requests <code>get_customer</code> and <code>lookup_order</code> together. Your code executes <code>get_customer</code>, appends its result, and calls the API again, planning to run <code>lookup_order</code> later. The API returns an error. Why?</p>","options":["The two tools must be declared as parallel-safe in their definitions before Claude may call them together","Claude may only request one tool per turn, so the second tool call violated the API's tool-use contract","Every <code>tool_use</code> block in the assistant turn needs a matching <code>tool_result</code> in the next user message","The <code>get_customer</code> result must be sent as an assistant message instead of being placed in a user message"],"correct":[2],"explanation":"<p>When Claude requests several tools in one turn, all of them must be answered — each <code>tool_result</code> with the matching <code>tool_use_id</code> — in the next user message. Claude is allowed to call multiple tools per turn; no \"parallel-safe\" declaration is required, and tool results go in user messages.</p>","scenario":"S1","domain":1},{"id":"a1-loop-06","skill":"1.1","type":"single","stem":"<p>A tool call fails because a file path does not exist. Which handling gives the agent the best chance to recover?</p>","options":["Raise an exception to stop the loop so that a developer can investigate the failure and restart the run","Return a <code>tool_result</code> with <code>is_error: true</code> and a message such as \"File not found; similar: src/api/app.py\"","Return an empty string as the tool result, so the model notices that nothing useful came back from it","Silently retry the same call three times before returning the last error to the developer's error logs"],"correct":[1],"explanation":"<p>Errors are information for the model: returned as an error result with a helpful message, Claude can correct its input or choose another approach. Aborting the loop gives up immediately; an empty string hides the failure; retrying a deterministic \"not found\" error wastes calls.</p>","scenario":"S4","domain":1},{"id":"a1-loop-07","skill":"1.1","type":"single","stem":"<p>Which statement best describes how decisions are made inside a well-designed agentic loop compared with a workflow?</p>","options":["In both designs the developer pre-configures the order of tool calls, and the model only fills in their parameters","In the loop the model picks tools at random, while a workflow uses the model's judgement to pick each next step","In a loop the developer must specify a decision tree in the system prompt that the model follows step by step","In the loop Claude chooses the next tool from the current context; a workflow follows steps fixed in code"],"correct":[3],"explanation":"<p>The defining property of an agent loop is model-driven decision making: Claude decides which tool to call next based on what it has observed. Workflows encode the sequence in code. Decision trees in prompts recreate a workflow less reliably; nothing about loops is random.</p>","scenario":"S3","domain":1},{"id":"a1-loop-08","skill":"1.1","type":"single","stem":"<p>A CI agent that uses a server-side web-fetch tool sometimes returns a response with <code>stop_reason: \"pause_turn\"</code>. Your loop treats any stop reason other than <code>tool_use</code> as completion. What happens, and what is the fix?</p>","options":["Nothing harmful happens; <code>pause_turn</code> is just an informational label that indicates a long but complete turn","The run ends unfinished; send the assistant content back unchanged in a new request so Claude can continue","The tool failed permanently; return an error result for the fetch tool and let Claude choose another approach","The rate limit was hit; wait for the time given in the retry-after header and then resend the original request"],"correct":[1],"explanation":"<p><code>pause_turn</code> indicates a long-running server-tool turn was paused; it is not completion. Continue by sending the response back so Claude resumes. Treating it as done truncates the work. It isn't a tool failure or a rate limit (those surface as errors or HTTP 429).</p>","scenario":"S5","domain":1},{"id":"a1-loop-09","skill":"1.1","type":"single","stem":"<p>A teammate proposes ending the agent loop when Claude's text contains \"Is there anything else I can help you with?\". What is the main problem with this approach?</p>","options":["The phrase is too long to match efficiently, so a shorter keyword such as \"done\" should be used instead","Customers would see the phrase repeated in every message, which hurts the support experience for them","It requires streaming the response, which is not supported when tools are attached to the request","Natural-language markers are unreliable; wording varies and Claude may say it before finishing tool work"],"correct":[3],"explanation":"<p>Parsing text for completion markers is a classic anti-pattern: phrasing varies, and Claude can produce such sentences in a turn that also requests tools. <code>stop_reason</code> is the reliable signal. The other options misidentify the issue.</p>","scenario":"S1","domain":1},{"id":"a1-orch-01","skill":"1.2","type":"single","stem":"<p>A multi-agent research system produced a well-sourced report on \"the impact of AI on creative industries\" that only covers visual art. Logs show the coordinator created subtasks for digital art, graphic design and photography; each subagent completed its task thoroughly. What is the most likely root cause?</p>","options":["The web-search subagent's queries were too narrow, so it never found sources about music or writing","The coordinator decomposed the topic too narrowly, so most creative industries were never delegated","The synthesis subagent dropped findings on other industries because its context window filled up","The document-analysis subagent filtered out sources that did not focus specifically on visual art"],"correct":[1],"explanation":"<p>Every subagent did what it was asked; the gap lies in what was asked. The coordinator's decomposition omitted music, writing, film and other industries, so the fix belongs in the coordinator's decomposition and coverage check, not in the subagents.</p>","scenario":"S3","domain":1},{"id":"a1-orch-02","skill":"1.2","type":"single","stem":"<p>To reduce latency, an engineer proposes letting the search subagent send its results directly to the synthesis subagent instead of returning them to the coordinator. What is the strongest argument against this?</p>","options":["Subagents cannot technically send messages to each other because the SDK lacks a messaging feature","Direct messages between subagents always cost more tokens than routing everything via the coordinator","It removes the coordinator's central view, so errors, conflicts and coverage gaps are no longer handled in one place","The synthesis subagent would need the same tools as the search subagent in order to read its results"],"correct":[2],"explanation":"<p>Hub-and-spoke routing gives observability, consistent error handling, conflict resolution and coverage checks. Direct subagent-to-subagent flows lose these. The other claims are not the core issue (or not true).</p>","scenario":"S3","domain":1},{"id":"a1-orch-03","skill":"1.2","type":"single","stem":"<p>Which responsibility belongs to the coordinator rather than to the subagents in a hub-and-spoke research system?</p>","options":["Running the individual web searches and reading the full text of every source that the searches return","Deciding which subagents to invoke for a request and checking whether the combined results cover the question","Formatting each individual finding as JSON with its source URL, publication date and a supporting excerpt","Retrying transient search timeouts with exponential backoff before giving up on a specific source request"],"correct":[1],"explanation":"<p>The coordinator decomposes, selects subagents, routes communication, aggregates and checks coverage. Searching, formatting individual findings and retrying transient failures are local work for subagents.</p>","scenario":"S3","domain":1},{"id":"a1-orch-04","skill":"1.2","type":"single","stem":"<p>Users report that simple factual questions (\"When was the EU AI Act adopted?\") take as long and cost as much as broad research questions. What change best addresses this?</p>","options":["Replace all subagents with a cheaper model so that every request costs less regardless of its complexity","Cache every final report so that repeated questions are answered directly from the stored report text","Add a separate classifier model that routes simple questions to a fully independent question-answering system","Instruct the coordinator to scale effort to the query, using one subagent or none for simple fact lookups"],"correct":[3],"explanation":"<p>Coordinators should scale the number of subagents to query complexity; a simple fact needs at most one. A separate classifier and system is heavier than needed, caching helps only exact repeats, and downgrading every subagent lowers quality for complex questions.</p>","scenario":"S3","domain":1},{"id":"a1-orch-05","skill":"1.2","type":"single","stem":"<p>After subagents return, the coordinator notices two findings that contradict each other and one dimension of the question with no sources. What should it do next?</p>","options":["Synthesize immediately and let the reader resolve the contradiction and the missing dimension themselves","Discard both contradictory findings and the missing dimension so that the report only contains agreed facts","Delegate targeted follow-up tasks for the gap and the conflict, then synthesize with the new results","Restart the entire research process from scratch with a more detailed decomposition of the original question"],"correct":[2],"explanation":"<p>Coordinators should evaluate results and iterate with targeted follow-ups — the orchestrator-workers pattern with an evaluation step. Ignoring gaps produces incomplete reports; discarding findings loses information; restarting everything wastes the valid work.</p>","scenario":"S3","domain":1},{"id":"a1-orch-06","skill":"1.2","type":"single","stem":"<p>A team considers a multi-agent setup for a refactoring task where every step needs the full, shared understanding of a tightly coupled module. Which recommendation is most appropriate?</p>","options":["Use a single agent with full context, since tightly coupled work gains little from splitting across subagents","Use one subagent per file, because parallel subagents always finish refactoring tasks faster than one agent","Use a coordinator with three subagents so that each subagent can double-check the work of the other two","Use a mesh of peer agents that exchange context directly so that every agent shares the full module context"],"correct":[0],"explanation":"<p>Multi-agent systems pay off for broad, parallelisable work. Tightly coupled tasks need shared context; splitting them adds coordination cost and loses information. Per-file parallelism and peer meshes ignore the coupling; triple-checking is not what the task needs.</p>","scenario":"S2","domain":1},{"id":"a1-orch-07","skill":"1.2","type":"single","stem":"<p>A support platform wants separate specialists for billing, shipping and account security, with consistent logging and policy checks for every customer interaction. Which topology fits best?</p>","options":["Three independent agents behind a keyword router, each logging to its own store with its own policy checks","A single agent with all tools from every specialist and a very long system prompt describing all policies","A coordinator that delegates to the three specialist subagents and applies logging and policy checks centrally","Specialists that hand conversations directly to each other whenever a request falls outside their own area"],"correct":[2],"explanation":"<p>A coordinator gives one place for logging, policy enforcement and aggregation while specialists keep focused tools and prompts. Independent agents duplicate controls; one mega-agent suffers tool overload; direct handoffs between peers lose central control.</p>","scenario":"S1","domain":1},{"id":"a1-orch-08","skill":"1.2","type":"single","stem":"<p>The coordinator's prompt says \"research the topic using your subagents\". Reports frequently duplicate work across subagents and leave gaps. Which prompt change is most effective?</p>","options":["Add \"be thorough and avoid duplication\" to the prompt of every subagent in the research system","Have the coordinator list dimensions, assign each with clear boundaries, and check coverage","Increase the number of subagents spawned per request so that more of the topic is covered by chance","Ask each subagent to read the other subagents' outputs before starting its own assigned research work"],"correct":[1],"explanation":"<p>Duplication and gaps are decomposition problems. Explicit dimensions, one owner per dimension with boundaries, and a coverage check fix them. Generic exhortations don't define boundaries; more subagents add cost and overlap; cross-reading breaks isolation.</p>","scenario":"S3","domain":1},{"id":"a1-orch-09","skill":"1.2","type":"single","stem":"<p>Why does delegating a broad codebase survey to a subagent help the main agent that will later implement a change?</p>","options":["Subagents run on faster hardware, so the codebase survey completes sooner than it would in the main agent","The subagent can modify files in parallel with the main agent, which halves the total implementation time","The main agent automatically receives the subagent's full transcript, giving it more detailed information","The verbose exploration stays in the subagent's context; the main agent receives only a condensed summary"],"correct":[3],"explanation":"<p>Context isolation is the main benefit: noisy exploration doesn't accumulate in the main context. The main agent receives the subagent's result, not its transcript. Hardware and parallel editing are not the point.</p>","scenario":"S4","domain":1},{"id":"a1-sub-01","skill":"1.3","type":"single","stem":"<p>The synthesis subagent produces reports without citations, although the search subagent captured URLs and dates for every finding. The coordinator delegates synthesis with the prompt \"Write a report on the findings so far.\" What is the fix?</p>","options":["Give the synthesis subagent web-search tools so that it can look up the sources for each claim on its own","Switch the synthesis subagent to a larger model, which will be more careful about including citations","Pass the findings with their source metadata in the delegation prompt and require citations in the output format","Configure the synthesis subagent to inherit the coordinator's conversation history so it sees the sources"],"correct":[2],"explanation":"<p>Subagents don't inherit the parent's context; they only see their delegation prompt. The coordinator must pass the structured findings with metadata explicitly. Search tools cause redundant re-research; a bigger model can't cite data it never received; inheritance isn't how subagent context works.</p>","scenario":"S3","domain":1},{"id":"a1-sub-02","skill":"1.3","type":"single","stem":"<p>A coordinator built with the Agent SDK never spawns its configured subagents; it tries to do all the research itself. The <code>agents</code> dictionary is correctly defined. What is the most likely cause?</p>","options":["The subagent descriptions are written in the second person instead of describing when to use each one","The <code>Task</code> tool is missing from the coordinator's <code>allowedTools</code>, so it cannot invoke subagents at all","Subagents can only be invoked when they are defined as Markdown files in the <code>.claude/agents/</code> folder","The coordinator needs <code>tool_choice</code> set to \"any\" before it is permitted to delegate to its subagents"],"correct":[1],"explanation":"<p>Delegation happens through the Task (Agent) tool; without it in <code>allowedTools</code>, the coordinator cannot spawn subagents. Programmatic <code>AgentDefinition</code>s are fully supported, and neither description style nor <code>tool_choice</code> is the gate.</p>","scenario":"S3","domain":1},{"id":"a1-sub-03","skill":"1.3","type":"single","stem":"<p>The coordinator needs results from three independent subagents (market data, regulation, competitors). Currently it delegates one, waits, then delegates the next. How can it reduce wall-clock time?</p>","options":["Merge the three subagents into one subagent that has all of their tools and instructions combined","Run the three subagents in separate sessions and resume the coordinator once all of them have finished","Set a lower <code>max_tokens</code> on each subagent so that every one of them finishes its research more quickly","Emit all three Task tool calls in a single coordinator response so that the subagents run in parallel"],"correct":[3],"explanation":"<p>Multiple Task calls in one response run concurrently. Merging subagents loses specialisation and isolation; separate sessions add orchestration complexity without being the SDK pattern; lowering <code>max_tokens</code> truncates results.</p>","scenario":"S3","domain":1},{"id":"a1-sub-04","skill":"1.3","type":"single","stem":"<p>Which delegation prompt gives a regulation-research subagent the best chance of producing useful, mergeable output?</p>","options":["\"Research regulation. Be thorough and accurate, and make sure that you cover everything that is relevant.\"","\"Continue the research we discussed earlier, focusing on the regulatory side of the analysis this time.\"","\"Research EU and US AI regulation 2024–2026 for medical devices; skip market data; return JSON with claim, source, date.\"","\"You are an expert regulatory analyst. Use all of your knowledge to write a detailed essay about AI regulation.\""],"correct":[2],"explanation":"<p>A good delegation prompt is self-contained: objective, scope boundaries (what to skip), and a structured output format with metadata. \"Continue what we discussed\" assumes inherited context; vague or essay-style prompts produce unmergeable prose and overlap.</p>","scenario":"S3","domain":1},{"id":"a1-sub-05","skill":"1.3","type":"single","stem":"<p>In an <code>AgentDefinition</code>, what is the purpose of the <code>description</code> field?</p>","options":["It is the system prompt that defines the subagent's role, method and output format","It restricts which tools the subagent may use while it is working on a delegated task","It tells the parent agent when this subagent is the right choice for a delegated task","It is shown to the end user as an explanation whenever the subagent is called upon"],"correct":[2],"explanation":"<p><code>description</code> is what the parent uses to decide when to delegate to this subagent — like a tool description. <code>prompt</code> is the subagent's system prompt, and <code>tools</code> restricts its toolset.</p>","scenario":"S4","domain":1},{"id":"a1-sub-06","skill":"1.3","type":"single","stem":"<p>A document-analysis subagent must analyse only the documents that the search subagent finds. How should the coordinator sequence these two subagents?</p>","options":["Spawn both in the same response so that they run in parallel and the analyst picks up documents as they arrive","Run search first, then delegate analysis with the found documents and their metadata in the analyst's prompt","Spawn the analyst first so that it is ready, then spawn the search subagent and let it forward the documents","Give the analyst the search tools as well, so a single subagent performs both search and analysis in one go"],"correct":[1],"explanation":"<p>Dependent subtasks run sequentially, with the earlier result passed explicitly to the next subagent. Parallel spawning only fits independent work; subagents don't forward to each other; merging roles loses focus.</p>","scenario":"S3","domain":1},{"id":"a1-sub-07","skill":"1.3","type":"single","stem":"<p>How should subagents return their results to make aggregation by the coordinator reliable?</p>","options":["As structured records that separate each claim from its metadata such as source, date and confidence","As polished prose paragraphs, because the coordinator will include them directly in the final report","As the full transcript of the subagent's work, so the coordinator can review every step it took","As a single summary sentence, to keep the coordinator's context window as small as it possibly can"],"correct":[0],"explanation":"<p>Structured records with metadata let the coordinator merge, de-duplicate, detect conflicts and keep provenance. Prose must be re-parsed and loses detail; transcripts flood the coordinator's context; one sentence loses the evidence.</p>","scenario":"S3","domain":1},{"id":"a1-sub-08","skill":"1.3","type":"single","stem":"<p>A code-review subagent should be unable to modify files. Where is this best enforced?</p>","options":["In the delegation prompt, by telling the subagent clearly that it must never write or edit any file","In the coordinator's system prompt, by asking it to check that subagents did not modify anything","In the subagent's <code>AgentDefinition</code>, by listing only read-only tools such as Read, Grep and Glob","In a weekly audit of the git history that reverts any changes the review subagent might have made"],"correct":[2],"explanation":"<p>Restricting the subagent's tools makes \"read-only\" deterministic and also improves tool selection. Prompt instructions are probabilistic; after-the-fact audits don't prevent changes.</p>","scenario":"S4","domain":1},{"id":"a1-sub-09","skill":"1.3","type":"single","stem":"<p>The coordinator delegates \"analyse the documents we found\" to a subagent, which replies that it has no documents. The search results are in the coordinator's conversation. What is the underlying misconception in this design?</p>","options":["That subagents run asynchronously, so the coordinator must wait before it can delegate any further work","That a subagent can see the coordinator's conversation; it only receives what the delegation prompt contains","That subagents need a file system to work with documents, so the results must be saved to disk first","That the coordinator's context window is shared, so large search results must be summarised beforehand"],"correct":[1],"explanation":"<p>Subagents start with a fresh context and see only their own system prompt and the delegation prompt. The results must be passed explicitly (or via a file the subagent is told to read).</p>","scenario":"S3","domain":1},{"id":"a1-gate-01","skill":"1.4","type":"single","stem":"<p>Production logs show that in 12% of cases your support agent calls <code>process_refund</code> before <code>get_customer</code> has verified the customer's identity, despite a system prompt that says identity must always be verified first. Company policy requires verification for every refund. What should you do?</p>","options":["Add few-shot examples that show the agent verifying identity first in several typical refund conversations","Rewrite the system prompt with the verification requirement in capital letters at the very top of the prompt","Add a programmatic prerequisite that blocks <code>process_refund</code> until <code>get_customer</code> has returned a verified ID","Add a routing classifier that detects refund requests and sends them to a dedicated refund-only agent"],"correct":[2],"explanation":"<p>When a rule must hold every time, enforce it deterministically. A prerequisite gate (in the tool or a PreToolUse hook) guarantees the order; prompt wording and few-shot examples only make compliance more likely. A classifier addresses routing, not the missing verification.</p>","scenario":"S1","domain":1},{"id":"a1-gate-02","skill":"1.4","type":"single","stem":"<p>Your agent escalates a billing dispute to a human. Human agents complain that they have to re-read long transcripts and re-ask customers for details. What is the best improvement?</p>","options":["Attach the complete conversation transcript to every escalation so that the human agent sees everything","Pass a structured handoff summary with customer ID, issue, evidence, steps tried and the recommended action","Ask the customer to re-explain their issue in one message right before the escalation takes place","Escalate earlier in the conversation so that transcripts are shorter by the time a human picks them up"],"correct":[1],"explanation":"<p>A structured, self-contained handoff lets the human act without re-reading or re-asking. Full transcripts are what they're complaining about; making customers repeat themselves hurts the experience; escalating earlier doesn't address the information format.</p>","scenario":"S1","domain":1},{"id":"a1-gate-03","skill":"1.4","type":"single","stem":"<p>A customer writes: \"My blender arrived broken, I was charged twice, and I need to update my shipping address.\" The agent's reply only addresses the broken blender. Which approach fixes this most effectively?</p>","options":["Ask the customer to send each issue in a separate message so that the agent handles one at a time","Increase <code>max_tokens</code> so that the agent's reply has room to address every issue the customer raises","Route the whole message to a human whenever it contains more than one distinct customer request","Decompose the message into separate concerns, handle each with shared context, then reply covering all three"],"correct":[3],"explanation":"<p>Multi-concern requests should be decomposed, investigated (in parallel where independent) and synthesised into one reply. Making customers split messages or escalating every multi-part message is poor service; output length wasn't the cause.</p>","scenario":"S1","domain":1},{"id":"a1-gate-04","skill":"1.4","type":"single","stem":"<p>Which requirement is most appropriately handled by prompt guidance rather than programmatic enforcement?</p>","options":["Refunds above $500 must never be issued without approval from a human supervisor","Account deletions must only happen after the customer has passed two-factor verification","Replies should use a friendly tone and offer one relevant help-center article when helpful","Customer card numbers must never be stored in conversation logs or in any tool output"],"correct":[2],"explanation":"<p>Tone and helpful suggestions are preferences where occasional variation is acceptable — prompt guidance fits. Approval limits, verification and data-protection rules require deterministic enforcement.</p>","scenario":"S1","domain":1},{"id":"a1-gate-05","skill":"1.4","type":"single","stem":"<p>Your prerequisite gate blocks <code>process_refund</code> when identity isn't verified, but it returns the bare string \"Error\". The agent then calls <code>process_refund</code> repeatedly. What should the gate return?</p>","options":["Nothing at all, so that the agent assumes the refund went through and moves on with the conversation","A success message, while logging the blocked attempt for later review by the support team lead","A structured error stating that identity is not verified and that <code>get_customer</code> must be called first","A generic timeout error, which encourages the agent to wait before attempting the refund again later"],"correct":[2],"explanation":"<p>A blocked action should explain why and what to do next, so Claude recovers by verifying first. Silence or fake success misleads the agent and customer; a fake timeout invites pointless retries.</p>","scenario":"S1","domain":1},{"id":"a1-gate-06","skill":"1.4","type":"single","stem":"<p>An extraction pipeline must never write records to the finance system unless validation has passed. Currently the agent is told \"only call <code>write_record</code> after validating\". Occasionally invalid records arrive. What is the best design?</p>","options":["Add a second prompt instruction that reminds the agent to validate before writing at the end of every turn","Make <code>write_record</code> reject input that lacks a passing validation result, so the order is enforced in code","Ask the agent to output a confidence score and only allow writes when the confidence is above 0.9","Review the written records each morning and delete the invalid ones before the finance team sees them"],"correct":[1],"explanation":"<p>Enforcing the prerequisite in the tool (or a hook) guarantees the rule. More reminders remain probabilistic; self-reported confidence is not validation; cleaning up afterwards lets bad data reach the system.</p>","scenario":"S6","domain":1},{"id":"a1-gate-07","skill":"1.4","type":"single","stem":"<p>Why is a gate enforced in application code preferable to relying on the model remembering earlier verification in the conversation?</p>","options":["Application code is cheaper to run than additional model calls that would otherwise check verification","Application state is authoritative and survives summarisation or resumption; the model's memory may not","Models cannot read earlier tool results, so they have no way of knowing whether verification happened","Gates make the agent respond faster because the model no longer needs to consider verification at all"],"correct":[1],"explanation":"<p>Business facts like \"this customer is verified\" must live in authoritative application state; conversation memory can be summarised, truncated or resumed incorrectly. Models can read earlier results, and speed or cost aren't the main reason.</p>","scenario":"S1","domain":1},{"id":"a1-gate-08","skill":"1.4","type":"single","stem":"<p>Which handoff payload is most useful for a human taking over a refund escalation?</p>","options":["{\"note\": \"Customer is unhappy about a charge, please help them out as soon as possible.\"}","{\"transcript\": \"&lt;the full 48-turn conversation between the agent and the customer&gt;\"}","{\"sentiment\": \"negative\", \"confidence\": 0.62, \"topic\": \"billing\", \"turns\": 48}","{\"customer_id\": \"C-2093\", \"issue\": \"duplicate charge $249\", \"recommended\": \"refund txn 88413\"}"],"correct":[3],"explanation":"<p>A useful handoff carries identifiers, the specific issue with amounts, what was tried, and a recommended action. Vague notes, raw transcripts and sentiment metadata don't let the human act immediately.</p>","scenario":"S1","domain":1},{"id":"a1-gate-09","skill":"1.4","type":"single","stem":"<p>A CI agent may merge pull requests only when all required checks have passed. The current system prompt states this rule, and once the agent merged a PR with a failing check. What is the most reliable fix?</p>","options":["Add an example to the system prompt that shows the agent refusing to merge a PR with a failing check","Lower the model's temperature so that the agent follows the merge instructions more consistently","Ask the agent to summarise all check statuses in its reply before it decides whether to merge","Have the merge tool verify check status itself and refuse the merge when any required check has failed"],"correct":[3],"explanation":"<p>The rule must hold every time, so enforce it in the tool (or a PreToolUse hook). Examples, temperature and self-summaries make compliance more likely but not guaranteed.</p>","scenario":"S5","domain":1},{"id":"a1-hook-01","skill":"1.5","type":"single","stem":"<p>Policy says refunds over $500 require a human. You use a third-party billing MCP server whose <code>process_refund</code> tool you cannot modify. How do you enforce the policy?</p>","options":["Add the policy to the tool's description through a wrapper prompt that is shown to the model","Register a PreToolUse hook that denies refund calls above $500 and tells Claude to escalate instead","Add a PostToolUse hook that reverses any refund above $500 right after the billing tool has run","Ask the billing vendor to add an approval step and rely on the system prompt until they deliver it"],"correct":[1],"explanation":"<p>A PreToolUse hook intercepts the call before it executes, denies it deterministically and redirects Claude to escalation — without changing the server. Descriptions and prompts are probabilistic; reversing after the fact means the refund already happened.</p>","scenario":"S1","domain":1},{"id":"a1-hook-02","skill":"1.5","type":"single","stem":"<p>Three MCP servers return dates as Unix timestamps, ISO 8601 strings and \"MM/DD/YY\". The agent occasionally misreads dates. What is the best solution?</p>","options":["Add a system-prompt section that explains each of the three date formats and how to interpret them","Add few-shot examples that convert each of the three date formats into a single canonical format","Use a PostToolUse hook to normalise all dates to ISO 8601 before the model sees the tool results","Ask Claude to call a separate date-conversion tool every time it encounters a date in a tool result"],"correct":[2],"explanation":"<p>Normalising in a PostToolUse hook gives the model one consistent format, deterministically and without extra tokens or tool calls. Prompt explanations and examples still leave room for misreading; an extra conversion tool adds turns.</p>","scenario":"S1","domain":1},{"id":"a1-hook-03","skill":"1.5","type":"single","stem":"<p>A PreToolUse hook denies a refund call but returns no reason. What behaviour should you expect from the agent?</p>","options":["The agent will automatically escalate, because a denied call always triggers the escalation path","The agent may retry the same call or give up unhelpfully, because it doesn't know what to do instead","The agent will stop the whole conversation, because denied tool calls end the agentic loop at once","The agent will continue as if the refund succeeded, since denials are hidden from the model entirely"],"correct":[1],"explanation":"<p>Without a reason, Claude lacks guidance and may retry or respond poorly. Include a reason that redirects (\"refunds over $500 need a human; call escalate_to_human\"). Denials don't automatically escalate or end the loop, and they are visible to the model.</p>","scenario":"S1","domain":1},{"id":"a1-hook-04","skill":"1.5","type":"single","stem":"<p>Which event is the right place to strip internal fields (audit IDs, warehouse codes) from a large tool result before Claude reasons over it?</p>","options":["PreToolUse, because it runs before the tool and can remove the fields from the request","UserPromptSubmit, because it runs on every user message and can clean the conversation history","SessionStart, because it can configure the tool to omit those fields for the whole session","PostToolUse, because it runs after the tool returns and before the model sees the result"],"correct":[3],"explanation":"<p>PostToolUse runs after a tool returns, so it can trim or transform the result before it enters the model's context. PreToolUse acts on the request; the other events don't see tool results.</p>","scenario":"S4","domain":1},{"id":"a1-hook-05","skill":"1.5","type":"single","stem":"<p>What is the key difference between enforcing a rule with a hook and stating it in CLAUDE.md?</p>","options":["A hook runs as code on every matching event, so it applies deterministically; CLAUDE.md is guidance the model follows","CLAUDE.md is enforced by the harness before every tool call, whereas a hook is only advisory text for the model","Hooks only apply in the Agent SDK, whereas CLAUDE.md rules apply in both the SDK and in Claude Code sessions","Hooks are loaded into the model's context like CLAUDE.md, but they are placed at a higher priority position"],"correct":[0],"explanation":"<p>Hooks are code executed by the harness at lifecycle events — deterministic. CLAUDE.md is context the model follows with high but not perfect reliability. Hooks exist in Claude Code (shell commands in settings) and the SDK (callbacks).</p>","scenario":"S2","domain":1},{"id":"a1-hook-06","skill":"1.5","type":"single","stem":"<p>A hook needs to block account-deletion calls unless the session has completed two-factor verification. Where should the verification status come from?</p>","options":["From asking the model, inside the hook, whether the customer has completed two-factor verification","From the customer's last message, by checking whether it mentions that a code has been entered","From application state set by the verification tool when it succeeded, which the hook can read","From the model's system prompt, where the agent records verification status as part of its notes"],"correct":[2],"explanation":"<p>Deterministic gates must rely on authoritative state — set by the verification tool on success. Asking the model or parsing messages reintroduces probabilistic behaviour and is open to manipulation.</p>","scenario":"S1","domain":1},{"id":"a1-hook-07","skill":"1.5","type":"single","stem":"<p>Extraction tools return currency amounts as \"1.234,50 €\", \"$1,234.50\" and \"1234.5 USD\". A downstream validator rejects many records. What is the most robust approach?</p>","options":["Ask Claude in the system prompt to always rewrite currency amounts as plain decimal numbers","Relax the validator so that it accepts all three formats and leaves conversion to finance staff","Add few-shot examples that show how each format should be normalised in the extracted output","Normalise amounts and currency codes in a PostToolUse hook so all results share one representation"],"correct":[3],"explanation":"<p>Deterministic normalisation in a hook (or at the source) yields consistent data every time. Prompts and examples improve but don't guarantee consistency; relaxing the validator pushes the problem downstream.</p>","scenario":"S6","domain":1},{"id":"a1-hook-08","skill":"1.5","type":"single","stem":"<p>Which outcome can a PreToolUse hook achieve that a system-prompt instruction cannot?</p>","options":["Making Claude prefer one tool over another when both tools could satisfy the customer's request","Guaranteeing that a specific tool call is never executed when its arguments violate a policy","Teaching Claude the reasoning behind a company policy so that it applies it to novel situations","Reducing the number of tokens Claude spends deciding which tool to call for a given request"],"correct":[1],"explanation":"<p>Only deterministic interception can guarantee a call is never executed. Preferences and reasoning are prompt territory; hooks don't reduce model reasoning tokens.</p>","scenario":"S1","domain":1},{"id":"a1-hook-09","skill":"1.5","type":"single","stem":"<p>A team wants every Bash command run by their productivity agent to be logged with its exit code for auditing. Which mechanism is most appropriate?</p>","options":["A system-prompt instruction asking the agent to report each command and its exit code in its reply","A weekly review of the agent's transcripts to reconstruct which commands were run and their results","A PostToolUse hook on the Bash tool that writes the command and result to the audit log","A separate logging subagent that the main agent is told to call after every Bash command it runs"],"correct":[2],"explanation":"<p>A PostToolUse hook on Bash runs after every execution and logs deterministically. Prompted self-reporting and logging subagents can be skipped; transcript review is after the fact and incomplete.</p>","scenario":"S4","domain":1},{"id":"a1-decomp-01","skill":"1.6","type":"single","stem":"<p>A CI review covers 40 changed files in a single prompt. Comments are detailed for the first files and superficial for the rest, and the same pattern is flagged in one file but accepted in another. What is the best redesign?</p>","options":["Use a model with a larger context window so that all 40 files receive the same amount of attention","Ask the reviewer in the prompt to give every file equal attention and apply the same standards everywhere","Run per-file review passes with identical criteria plus a separate cross-file pass for interactions","Only review the ten files with the most changed lines and skip review entirely for the remaining files"],"correct":[2],"explanation":"<p>Splitting into local passes gives consistent depth; an integration pass catches cross-file issues. A bigger context window doesn't fix attention dilution; prompting for equal attention has limited effect; skipping files misses issues.</p>","scenario":"S5","domain":1},{"id":"a1-decomp-02","skill":"1.6","type":"single","stem":"<p>An agent must \"add comprehensive tests to a legacy codebase\" with no documentation. Which decomposition is most appropriate?</p>","options":["A fixed pipeline that writes tests for every file in alphabetical order, one file per model call","Map the structure first, identify high-impact areas, prioritise, and adapt the plan as findings arrive","Ask the agent to write all the tests in one long response so that it can see the entire codebase at once","Generate tests for the files that changed most recently, because those are most likely to contain bugs"],"correct":[1],"explanation":"<p>Open-ended work with unknown structure needs adaptive decomposition: understand, prioritise, then adapt. A fixed alphabetical pipeline ignores impact; one response can't cover a codebase; recent changes are a heuristic, not a plan.</p>","scenario":"S4","domain":1},{"id":"a1-decomp-03","skill":"1.6","type":"single","stem":"<p>Every incoming contract must go through the same steps: extract parties and dates, check them against the CRM, then draft a summary. Which approach fits best?</p>","options":["A prompt chain that runs the three steps in a fixed order, with checks between the steps","An autonomous agent that decides for each contract which of the steps to run and in what order","A coordinator that spawns a new team of subagents for each contract to discuss how to proceed","A single prompt that performs all three steps at once and returns one combined text answer"],"correct":[0],"explanation":"<p>When steps are known and identical every time, a fixed prompt chain is simpler, cheaper and more predictable, with gates between steps. Agents and dynamic teams add cost and nondeterminism; one giant prompt loses per-step checks.</p>","scenario":"S6","domain":1},{"id":"a1-decomp-04","skill":"1.6","type":"single","stem":"<p>After per-file review passes, a renamed field still breaks a consumer in another package. What was missing from the decomposition?</p>","options":["A larger number of per-file passes so that each file is reviewed twice by separate instances","A style-focused pass that checks naming conventions consistently across all changed files","A final pass by the same instance that generated the per-file comments to double-check them","A cross-file integration pass that examines contracts and data flow between the changed files"],"correct":[3],"explanation":"<p>Per-file passes can't see interactions between files. An integration pass with the interfaces and per-file summaries catches broken contracts. More per-file passes or style passes don't look across files.</p>","scenario":"S5","domain":1},{"id":"a1-decomp-05","skill":"1.6","type":"single","stem":"<p>Which signal most strongly suggests a task should use dynamic decomposition rather than a fixed prompt chain?</p>","options":["The task involves more than three steps that all need to be completed for the output to be useful","The task processes a large volume of similar documents that each follow the same overall structure","The sub-questions to investigate depend on what earlier steps discover and cannot be listed in advance","The output must be returned as structured JSON for a downstream system to consume reliably"],"correct":[2],"explanation":"<p>Dynamic decomposition is for work whose next steps depend on findings. Step count, volume and output format don't decide it; many long, high-volume, JSON-producing tasks are perfect fixed chains.</p>","scenario":"S3","domain":1},{"id":"a1-decomp-06","skill":"1.6","type":"single","stem":"<p>A developer asks Claude Code to migrate 200 API endpoints to a new error-handling convention. What decomposition is most effective?</p>","options":["Ask Claude to convert all 200 endpoints in one prompt and review the entire resulting diff at once","Agree on the pattern with a few endpoints first, then apply it in verifiable batches with tests per batch","Spawn 200 parallel subagents, one per endpoint, each deciding independently how to apply the convention","Convert endpoints in random order so that the review effort is spread evenly across the whole team"],"correct":[1],"explanation":"<p>Establishing the pattern on a sample, then applying it in independently verifiable batches, balances consistency and reviewability. One giant change is hard to verify; 200 independent agents produce inconsistent interpretations.</p>","scenario":"S2","domain":1},{"id":"a1-decomp-07","skill":"1.6","type":"single","stem":"<p>Why are independently verifiable steps a good target granularity when decomposing an extraction pipeline?</p>","options":["They guarantee that the pipeline uses fewer tokens than a single prompt performing the whole task","They allow every step to use a different model, which is required for reliable extraction pipelines","They remove the need for a final validation step, since each part has already been checked once","Each step's output can be checked on its own, so errors are caught early and localised to that step"],"correct":[3],"explanation":"<p>Verifiable steps localise errors and allow gates between steps. They don't necessarily save tokens, don't require different models, and don't remove end-to-end validation.</p>","scenario":"S6","domain":1},{"id":"a1-decomp-08","skill":"1.6","type":"single","stem":"<p>A coordinator splits a research question into 25 tiny subtasks. Costs are high and cross-cutting insights are missing. What went wrong?</p>","options":["The decomposition is too fine-grained, adding coordination overhead and splitting connected topics apart","The decomposition is too coarse, so each subagent receives far too much work for a single context","The subagents should share one context window so that they can see each other's partial findings","The coordinator should have used a fixed prompt chain because research questions are always predictable"],"correct":[0],"explanation":"<p>Over-fine decomposition multiplies overhead and fragments related topics, losing cross-cutting insight. Shared context breaks isolation, and research is not reliably predictable enough for a fixed chain.</p>","scenario":"S3","domain":1},{"id":"a1-decomp-09","skill":"1.6","type":"single","stem":"<p>An agent investigating \"why is checkout slow?\" first measures endpoint timings, then decides to inspect database queries because one endpoint dominates. Which pattern is this?</p>","options":["Prompt chaining, because each step's output is passed to the next step in a predefined sequence","Parallelisation by sectioning, because the investigation is split into independent sections","Routing, because the request is classified up front and sent to a specialised handler for databases","Adaptive decomposition, because the next subtask is chosen based on what the previous step found"],"correct":[3],"explanation":"<p>The agent chooses its next step from findings — adaptive decomposition. Prompt chains fix steps in advance; sectioning runs predefined independent parts; routing classifies once up front.</p>","scenario":"S4","domain":1},{"id":"a1-sess-01","skill":"1.7","type":"single","stem":"<p>After a session that analysed a payment module, you want to compare two refactoring strategies (strategy pattern vs. state machine) without them influencing each other. What should you do?</p>","options":["Resume the same session twice in a row and ask for each strategy in turn within the one history","Fork the session twice from the analysis baseline and explore one strategy in each fork","Start two completely new sessions and redo the full analysis of the payment module in each of them","Ask for both strategies in a single prompt and let Claude compare them inside one long response"],"correct":[1],"explanation":"<p>Forking reuses the shared analysis while keeping explorations independent. Resuming the same session interleaves them; fresh sessions repeat expensive analysis; a single response can't explore each strategy in depth.</p>","scenario":"S2","domain":1},{"id":"a1-sess-02","skill":"1.7","type":"single","stem":"<p>You resume yesterday's investigation session, but overnight a refactor changed 30 files the session had read. Claude now refers to functions that no longer exist. What is the best course of action?</p>","options":["Keep resuming the session and correct Claude manually every time it cites a function that was removed","Resume the session and ask Claude to \"be careful about outdated information\" from the previous day","Start a new session seeded with a structured summary of yesterday's conclusions, then re-explore changes","Copy yesterday's entire transcript into a new session so that Claude has all of the earlier context"],"correct":[2],"explanation":"<p>When much has changed, prior tool results are stale; a fresh session seeded with a concise summary avoids reasoning from outdated code. Manual corrections are endless; vague warnings don't identify what's stale; replaying the transcript reintroduces stale data.</p>","scenario":"S4","domain":1},{"id":"a1-sess-03","skill":"1.7","type":"single","stem":"<p>You resume a session after a teammate edited two specific files (<code>billing.py</code>, <code>refunds.py</code>). Everything else is unchanged. What is the most efficient approach?</p>","options":["Start a fresh session and redo the full analysis so that no outdated information remains anywhere","Resume and tell Claude exactly which two files changed so that it re-reads them before continuing","Resume without comment, since Claude automatically detects changed files and refreshes its context","Fork the session so that the changes made by your teammate don't affect the original conversation"],"correct":[1],"explanation":"<p>For a few known changes, resuming and naming the changed files is efficient and accurate. A fresh start wastes valid context; Claude doesn't automatically know files changed; forking doesn't refresh stale content.</p>","scenario":"S2","domain":1},{"id":"a1-sess-04","skill":"1.7","type":"single","stem":"<p>Why should a support agent's \"customer is verified\" status not depend solely on the resumed conversation history?</p>","options":["Authoritative state belongs in the application; history can be summarised, truncated or resumed incorrectly","Resumed sessions always discard tool results, so the verification would be lost in every case","The model cannot read tool results from earlier turns once a session has been resumed by the user","Verification expires after each turn, so it must be repeated whenever the conversation continues"],"correct":[0],"explanation":"<p>Business-critical state must be authoritative in your system. Conversation history is not a reliable store for guarantees. Resumed sessions do keep tool results, and verification doesn't expire per turn.</p>","scenario":"S1","domain":1},{"id":"a1-sess-05","skill":"1.7","type":"single","stem":"<p>A multi-day codebase investigation is hard to return to because there are many unnamed sessions. What practice helps most?</p>","options":["Always use <code>--continue</code>, which reopens the most recent session regardless of which task it covered","Delete old sessions daily so that only one session exists for the whole team at any point in time","Give sessions task-based names and resume them by name, e.g. <code>--resume auth-investigation</code>","Paste the previous day's final answer into a new session every morning to continue from there"],"correct":[2],"explanation":"<p>Named sessions make it easy to resume the right line of work. <code>--continue</code> picks only the most recent; deleting sessions loses history; pasting final answers drops the working context.</p>","scenario":"S4","domain":1},{"id":"a1-sess-06","skill":"1.7","type":"single","stem":"<p>A research run crashed after four of seven subagent tasks completed. What design would let it resume without redoing the finished work?</p>","options":["Re-run the whole research request with a lower temperature so that it produces the same results again","Rely on the session history, since the coordinator automatically skips subagent tasks it has done before","Increase the timeout so that runs no longer crash and all seven tasks complete in a single attempt","Persist completed task results and a manifest of pending tasks, and resume from that state"],"correct":[3],"explanation":"<p>A manifest of completed and pending work lets the system resume precisely. Re-running repeats cost and may differ; implicit skipping isn't guaranteed; longer timeouts don't prevent other crashes.</p>","scenario":"S3","domain":1},{"id":"a1-sess-07","skill":"1.7","type":"single","stem":"<p>What does <code>fork_session</code> change compared with a plain resume?</p>","options":["It deletes the original session after copying its history into a new session","It resets the conversation but keeps the original session's tool permissions","It continues from the same history but writes further turns to a new session ID","It merges two existing sessions into a single combined history for comparison"],"correct":[2],"explanation":"<p>Forking branches from the existing history into a new session, leaving the original untouched. It doesn't delete, reset or merge sessions.</p>","scenario":"S2","domain":1},{"id":"a1-sess-08","skill":"1.7","type":"single","stem":"<p>A session has become cluttered with dead ends and verbose output, but its conclusions are valuable. You want to continue the work with a clean context. What should you do?</p>","options":["Write a structured summary of the conclusions and continue in a new session seeded with that summary","Fork the session, because forks start with an empty context while keeping the previous conclusions","Resume the session and ask Claude to ignore everything except its final conclusions from now on","Copy the full transcript into a file and ask Claude to read the file at the start of a new session"],"correct":[0],"explanation":"<p>A curated summary carries forward the value without the clutter. Forks keep the full history; asking Claude to ignore context doesn't remove it; re-reading the whole transcript brings the clutter back.</p>","scenario":"S4","domain":1},{"id":"a1-sess-09","skill":"1.7","type":"single","stem":"<p>A nightly CI job uses the Agent SDK. Each night's run should start clean, but the job sometimes needs to continue a run that was interrupted by a runner restart. What is the best approach?</p>","options":["Always resume the previous night's session so that the agent can build on everything it learned before","Start a new session per night; persist the session ID and resume it only to finish an interrupted run","Fork the first-ever session every night so that all runs share the same original analysis baseline","Disable session persistence and restart interrupted runs from scratch whenever the runner restarts"],"correct":[1],"explanation":"<p>New sessions keep nights independent; persisting the ID allows resuming only when needed. Always resuming accumulates stale context; forking an old baseline carries outdated data; restarting from scratch wastes completed work.</p>","scenario":"S5","domain":1},{"id":"a2-tool-01","skill":"2.1","type":"single","stem":"<p>Your support agent frequently calls <code>get_customer</code> when customers ask about an order (\"check my order #12345\"). Both tools have one-line descriptions (\"Retrieves customer information\" / \"Retrieves order information\"). What is the most effective first step?</p>","options":["Add five few-shot examples to the system prompt showing order questions routed to <code>lookup_order</code>","Add a routing classifier that inspects each message and decides which tool should be offered to Claude","Expand both descriptions with purpose, input formats, example queries and when to use each versus the other","Merge the two tools into a single <code>lookup</code> tool that accepts either a customer email or an order number"],"correct":[2],"explanation":"<p>Tool descriptions are the primary signal for tool selection; minimal, overlapping descriptions are the root cause. Rewriting them is low-effort and high-impact. Few-shot examples add tokens and don't fix the ambiguity; a classifier is over-engineered as a first step; merging tools hides the ambiguity in parameters.</p>","scenario":"S1","domain":2},{"id":"a2-tool-02","skill":"2.1","type":"single","stem":"<p>A research agent has one tool, <code>analyze_document(doc, mode)</code>, where <code>mode</code> is \"extract\", \"summarize\" or \"verify\". It often picks the wrong mode. What is the best redesign?</p>","options":["Keep the tool and list the three modes in the system prompt with a short explanation of each of them","Split it into <code>extract_data_points</code>, <code>summarize_content</code> and <code>verify_claim_against_source</code> with clear contracts","Make <code>mode</code> optional and let the tool decide automatically which operation is the most appropriate one","Add a fourth mode, \"auto\", that runs all three operations and returns every result to the agent at once"],"correct":[1],"explanation":"<p>Purpose-specific tools with precise inputs and outputs are selected and used more reliably than one generic tool with a mode switch. Prompt explanations don't fix the interface; letting the tool guess or running everything shifts the ambiguity and wastes work.</p>","scenario":"S3","domain":2},{"id":"a2-tool-03","skill":"2.1","type":"single","stem":"<p>A custom MCP tool <code>analyze_dependencies</code> is frequently ignored in favour of the built-in Grep, even when it would give better results. What is the best fix?</p>","options":["Rename and re-describe it so its distinct value is clear, e.g. <code>build_import_graph</code>","Remove Grep from the agent's tools so that it has no choice but to use the custom dependency tool","Add a system-prompt instruction saying the custom tool must always be preferred over built-in tools","Set <code>tool_choice</code> to force the custom tool on every turn of every session that uses the agent"],"correct":[0],"explanation":"<p>Claude chooses based on names and descriptions; making the custom tool's distinct value explicit lets Claude pick it when appropriate. Removing Grep breaks other tasks; blanket \"always prefer\" instructions misfire; forcing the tool every turn breaks normal operation.</p>","scenario":"S4","domain":2},{"id":"a2-tool-04","skill":"2.1","type":"single","stem":"<p>Which tool description best supports reliable selection?</p>","options":["\"Order tool. Returns everything the system knows about orders, for any customer request.\"","\"Use this tool for orders and any other questions the customer might ask about their purchases.\"","\"Get one order's status, items and shipping events by order ID (ORD-12345 or #12345). Use when the customer cites an order; use get_customer to identify the customer.\"","\"This tool is very important. ALWAYS use it first, before any other tool, whenever a customer writes.\""],"correct":[2],"explanation":"<p>A good description states what the tool does, its input format, when to use it, and how it differs from similar tools. Vague, over-broad or shouting descriptions cause mis-selection.</p>","scenario":"S1","domain":2},{"id":"a2-tool-05","skill":"2.1","type":"single","stem":"<p>A system prompt says \"Always analyze sources critically.\" Afterwards, the agent starts calling <code>analyze_sentiment</code> on research sources, which is irrelevant. What is the likely cause?</p>","options":["The sentiment tool's input schema is too permissive, so it accepts research sources as valid input","The model's temperature is too high, causing it to explore tools that it would not normally use","The keyword \"analyze\" in the system prompt steers Claude toward a tool whose name shares that word","The sentiment tool returns results too quickly, so Claude prefers it over slower research tools"],"correct":[2],"explanation":"<p>Keyword-sensitive instructions in system prompts can override well-written tool descriptions. Check and reword prompt instructions that conflict with tool selection. Schema permissiveness, temperature and speed aren't the mechanism.</p>","scenario":"S3","domain":2},{"id":"a2-tool-06","skill":"2.1","type":"single","stem":"<p><code>get_user</code> and <code>get_user_orders</code> are almost always called together in sequence, adding a turn to most conversations. What is a reasonable design improvement?</p>","options":["Add a system-prompt instruction to always call both tools in the same turn whenever possible","Consider a consolidated tool that returns the user and their recent orders in one call","Remove <code>get_user_orders</code> and ask customers to provide the order details themselves","Rename <code>get_user_orders</code> so that it sorts alphabetically before <code>get_user</code> in the list"],"correct":[1],"explanation":"<p>Consolidating tools that are always used together reduces turns, provided the combined contract stays clear. Prompt instructions don't remove the extra hop; shifting work to customers is poor design; ordering in the list is irrelevant.</p>","scenario":"S1","domain":2},{"id":"a2-tool-07","skill":"2.1","type":"single","stem":"<p>Why should tool results return names and relevant fields rather than raw internal IDs and full database rows?</p>","options":["Because the API rejects tool results that contain more than a few dozen fields per object","Because internal IDs are always confidential and must never be shown to the model at all","Because tool results are not counted toward the context window when they are kept short","Because compact, meaningful results are easier for Claude to use and consume less context"],"correct":[3],"explanation":"<p>Return values are part of the tool interface. Meaningful, compact results improve reasoning and save context. There is no small field limit, IDs are not inherently forbidden, and tool results do count toward context.</p>","scenario":"S4","domain":2},{"id":"a2-tool-08","skill":"2.1","type":"single","stem":"<p>Two extraction tools — <code>extract_invoice</code> and <code>extract_receipt</code> — are confused on documents that look similar. Which addition to the descriptions helps most?</p>","options":["The distinguishing features of each document type and an explicit \"use the other tool when…\" boundary","A longer list of all the fields each tool extracts, including the data type of every single field","An instruction that the tool is the default choice whenever the model is unsure which one to use","The internal implementation details of each tool, such as the parser library it relies on"],"correct":[0],"explanation":"<p>Descriptions should help Claude decide between similar tools: what distinguishes the document types and when to choose the other tool. Field lists, defaults and implementation details don't resolve the boundary.</p>","scenario":"S6","domain":2},{"id":"a2-err-01","skill":"2.2","type":"single","stem":"<p>Your <code>lookup_order</code> MCP tool returns <code>{\"orders\": []}</code> both when no orders exist and when the order database is unreachable. What is the main risk, and the fix?</p>","options":["The agent will make too many tool calls; the fix is to cache empty results for a few minutes","The agent's responses will be too slow; the fix is to add a shorter timeout to the database call","The agent treats an outage as \"no orders\"; return <code>isError: true</code> with a categorised error when lookup fails","The model will hallucinate orders; the fix is to always return at least one placeholder order record"],"correct":[2],"explanation":"<p>Conflating failure with an empty result makes the agent act on false information. A failed lookup must be an error (with category and retryability), distinct from a successful empty result. The other options don't address the misrepresentation.</p>","scenario":"S1","domain":2},{"id":"a2-err-02","skill":"2.2","type":"single","stem":"<p><code>process_refund</code> fails because the refund exceeds policy. The tool returns <code>{\"isError\": true, \"message\": \"Operation failed\"}</code> and the agent retries three times. What should the error contain instead?</p>","options":["A shorter message such as \"Fail\" so the model wastes fewer tokens on reading the error text","A business-error category, <code>isRetryable: false</code>, and a message explaining the limit and next step","A transient-error category so that the agent waits a moment before trying the refund once again","The full stack trace of the payment service so that the model can diagnose the underlying problem"],"correct":[1],"explanation":"<p>Structured errors with category and retryability let the agent respond correctly: business errors shouldn't be retried; explain or escalate. A generic message causes useless retries; mislabelling as transient makes it worse; stack traces are noise.</p>","scenario":"S1","domain":2},{"id":"a2-err-03","skill":"2.2","type":"single","stem":"<p>Which error category should an agent normally retry without changing anything?</p>","options":["Transient errors such as timeouts or temporary service unavailability","Validation errors such as a malformed date in the tool's input","Business errors such as a request that violates a company policy","Permission errors such as a caller not authorised for the resource"],"correct":[0],"explanation":"<p>Transient failures may succeed on retry (with backoff). Validation errors need corrected input; business and permission errors will fail again and need explanation or escalation.</p>","scenario":"S3","domain":2},{"id":"a2-err-04","skill":"2.2","type":"single","stem":"<p>A schema-validation MCP tool rejects an extraction because <code>invoice_date</code> is \"31/02/2026\". What makes the error response most useful?</p>","options":["A permission-error category, since the extraction was not authorised to write invalid data","A transient-error category, so the agent retries the identical validation call a few times","An empty success response, so the pipeline continues and a human fixes the date later on","A validation-error category naming the field and problem, so Claude can re-check and correct it"],"correct":[3],"explanation":"<p>Validation errors are recoverable by correcting the input; naming the field and problem enables precise self-correction. Other categories trigger wrong behaviour; empty success hides the failure.</p>","scenario":"S6","domain":2},{"id":"a2-err-05","skill":"2.2","type":"single","stem":"<p>Where should transient errors from a web-search API preferably be handled in a multi-agent research system?</p>","options":["Only in the coordinator, which retries the whole subagent task whenever any search call fails","Locally in the search subagent or tool, with retries, before propagating anything it cannot resolve","Nowhere — transient errors should be ignored because they usually resolve themselves over time","In the synthesis agent, which should note any missing searches when it writes the final report"],"correct":[1],"explanation":"<p>Local recovery keeps transient noise out of the coordinator; only unresolved failures propagate with context. Retrying whole tasks is wasteful; ignoring errors loses data; synthesis is too late.</p>","scenario":"S3","domain":2},{"id":"a2-err-06","skill":"2.2","type":"single","stem":"<p><code>get_customer</code> returns <code>isError: true</code> with category \"permission\" because the agent's service account lacks access to VIP accounts. What should the agent do?</p>","options":["Retry the call with exponential backoff until the permission check eventually succeeds","Ask the customer for their password so that it can authenticate as the customer instead","Tell the customer their account does not exist, because the lookup returned no data","Not retry; explain it can't access the account and escalate or hand off to a human with access"],"correct":[3],"explanation":"<p>Permission errors are not retryable and must not be misrepresented as \"not found\". Escalating is appropriate; requesting passwords is unacceptable.</p>","scenario":"S1","domain":2},{"id":"a2-err-07","skill":"2.2","type":"single","stem":"<p>A file-search MCP tool returns \"No matches for pattern 'chargeCard' in 1,284 files.\" Should this be marked <code>isError: true</code>?</p>","options":["Yes, because the user expected matches and the search did not produce any of them","No, it is a successful search with an empty result, which is valid information for the agent","Yes, because MCP requires errors for every response that contains no data whatsoever","No, but the tool should instead return a guess about which files probably contain matches"],"correct":[1],"explanation":"<p>A completed search with no matches is a valid result and evidence of absence; marking it an error would mislead the agent. Guessing results would be fabrication.</p>","scenario":"S4","domain":2},{"id":"a2-err-08","skill":"2.2","type":"single","stem":"<p>Why should a structured tool error include a human-readable message in addition to a machine-readable category?</p>","options":["Because MCP clients reject error results that contain only structured fields and no free text","Because the message is logged by the server while the category is discarded by the client","Because Claude often relays the explanation to the user and uses it to decide on next steps","Because a readable message allows the agent to skip checking the category field entirely"],"correct":[2],"explanation":"<p>The category drives programmatic recovery; the message gives Claude context to explain the situation and choose the next action. The other claims are incorrect.</p>","scenario":"S1","domain":2},{"id":"a2-dist-01","skill":"2.3","type":"single","stem":"<p>A synthesis agent was given all 18 tools in the research system \"for flexibility\". It now runs its own web searches instead of synthesising, and tool-selection errors have increased. What is the best change?</p>","options":["Restrict it to the few tools its role needs and route further research requests through the coordinator","Keep all tools but add a system-prompt rule telling the synthesis agent never to search the web itself","Give all agents all tools so that each of them can independently complete any part of the work","Remove tools from the coordinator instead, so that the synthesis agent has fewer competing options"],"correct":[0],"explanation":"<p>Too many tools degrade selection and invite role creep. Scope each agent to its role. Prompt-only restrictions are weaker; universal tool access worsens the problem; the coordinator isn't the issue.</p>","scenario":"S3","domain":2},{"id":"a2-dist-02","skill":"2.3","type":"single","stem":"<p>An extraction service sends documents of unknown type and offers three extraction tools (invoice, receipt, contract). Sometimes Claude replies in prose instead of calling a tool. Which <code>tool_choice</code> setting guarantees a structured result?</p>","options":["<code>{\"type\": \"auto\"}</code>, because it lets Claude decide whether a tool call is appropriate for the input","<code>{\"type\": \"any\"}</code>, because Claude must call one of the provided tools but may choose which one","<code>{\"type\": \"tool\", \"name\": \"extract_invoice\"}</code>, because invoices are the most common type of document","<code>{\"type\": \"none\"}</code>, combined with a prompt that asks for JSON output in the reply text instead"],"correct":[1],"explanation":"<p><code>any</code> forces a tool call while letting Claude pick the right schema. <code>auto</code> allows prose; forcing the invoice tool misclassifies other documents; <code>none</code> disables tools entirely.</p>","scenario":"S6","domain":2},{"id":"a2-dist-03","skill":"2.3","type":"single","stem":"<p>The synthesis agent frequently needs to verify a single fact, and each round-trip through the coordinator to the search agent adds 40% latency. What is the best design?</p>","options":["Give the synthesis agent the full search toolset so that it never needs the coordinator for research","Remove fact verification from the system and accept a lower accuracy in exchange for lower latency","Give the synthesis agent a narrow <code>verify_fact</code> tool for simple checks; keep complex research with the coordinator","Run every possible verification in advance before synthesis starts, whether or not it will be needed"],"correct":[2],"explanation":"<p>A scoped cross-role tool handles the frequent simple case without turning the synthesis agent into a researcher. Full toolsets cause role creep; dropping verification lowers quality; pre-verifying everything is wasteful.</p>","scenario":"S3","domain":2},{"id":"a2-dist-04","skill":"2.3","type":"single","stem":"<p>An extraction pipeline must always run <code>extract_metadata</code> before any other processing, then let Claude choose freely. How should <code>tool_choice</code> be configured?</p>","options":["Set <code>{\"type\": \"any\"}</code> on every turn, so Claude always calls some tool, which includes the metadata tool","Set <code>{\"type\": \"auto\"}</code> and explain in the system prompt that metadata extraction should come first","Set <code>{\"type\": \"none\"}</code> on the first turn so Claude plans first, then enable all tools afterwards","Force <code>extract_metadata</code> on the first turn, then switch to <code>{\"type\": \"auto\"}</code> for later turns"],"correct":[3],"explanation":"<p>Forcing a specific tool guarantees the first step; switching back to <code>auto</code> restores flexibility. <code>any</code> doesn't guarantee which tool; prompt guidance is probabilistic; <code>none</code> doesn't run the step.</p>","scenario":"S6","domain":2},{"id":"a2-dist-05","skill":"2.3","type":"single","stem":"<p>Why does restricting a code-review subagent to Read, Grep and Glob improve the system beyond selection accuracy?</p>","options":["It makes the subagent run on a cheaper model tier that is reserved for read-only tool sets","It applies least privilege, so the reviewer cannot modify code even if it is misled or makes a mistake","It disables the subagent's ability to reason about code, which keeps its review output shorter","It allows the subagent to share its context with the main agent because it cannot cause side effects"],"correct":[1],"explanation":"<p>Scoped tools also enforce least privilege, limiting damage from mistakes or injected instructions. Tool restrictions don't change model tier, reasoning ability or context sharing.</p>","scenario":"S4","domain":2},{"id":"a2-dist-06","skill":"2.3","type":"single","stem":"<p>A CI summarisation step must produce plain text only, even though the agent has tools configured for earlier steps. Which setting prevents tool calls in that final turn?</p>","options":["<code>tool_choice: {\"type\": \"none\"}</code> for the final summarisation request","<code>tool_choice: {\"type\": \"any\"}</code> for the final summarisation request","Removing the tools from CLAUDE.md before the summarisation step runs","Setting <code>max_tokens</code> very low so that Claude cannot fit a tool call"],"correct":[0],"explanation":"<p><code>none</code> prevents tool use while keeping tool definitions available. <code>any</code> does the opposite; CLAUDE.md doesn't configure API tools; a low <code>max_tokens</code> truncates output.</p>","scenario":"S5","domain":2},{"id":"a2-dist-07","skill":"2.3","type":"single","stem":"<p>With extended thinking enabled, an engineer sets <code>tool_choice: {\"type\": \"tool\", \"name\": \"record_invoice\"}</code> and receives an error. What is the explanation and alternative?</p>","options":["Forced tool use needs <code>max_tokens</code> above 64,000; raise the limit and keep the forced tool choice","Tools cannot be used together with extended thinking at all, so thinking must be disabled entirely","Forced and <code>any</code> tool choice aren't compatible with extended thinking; use <code>auto</code> with clear instructions","The tool name must be prefixed with <code>mcp__</code> when thinking is enabled; rename the tool accordingly"],"correct":[2],"explanation":"<p>Extended thinking supports <code>auto</code> (and <code>none</code>) tool choice, not <code>any</code> or a forced tool. Tools themselves work with thinking; token limits and prefixes are unrelated.</p>","scenario":"S6","domain":2},{"id":"a2-dist-08","skill":"2.3","type":"single","stem":"<p>A support platform has 15 tools across billing, shipping and account management. Selection errors are frequent. Which architecture change helps most?</p>","options":["Add more examples of every tool to the system prompt so that Claude learns all 15 tools thoroughly","Shorten every tool description to one line so that the list of 15 tools takes up less context","Set <code>tool_choice</code> to <code>any</code> so that Claude always picks some tool instead of answering in text","Split into specialist agents with 4–5 relevant tools each, coordinated by a routing coordinator"],"correct":[3],"explanation":"<p>Fewer, role-specific tools per agent improve selection. More examples and shorter descriptions don't address overload (shorter descriptions make it worse); forcing tool use doesn't improve which tool is chosen.</p>","scenario":"S1","domain":2},{"id":"a2-mcp-01","skill":"2.4","type":"single","stem":"<p>A new developer clones the repository, but Claude Code doesn't have the team's Jira MCP tools that everyone else uses. What is the most likely cause and fix?</p>","options":["The developer's API key lacks MCP permissions; request an upgraded key from the administrator","The server was configured at user scope on other machines; add it to the project's <code>.mcp.json</code> and commit it","MCP servers only load after the first session; ask the developer to restart Claude Code twice","Jira tools require plan mode; ask the developer to switch permission modes before using them"],"correct":[1],"explanation":"<p>Team servers belong in project-scoped <code>.mcp.json</code>, which is version-controlled. User-scoped configuration exists only on each person's machine. The other explanations aren't how MCP configuration works.</p>","scenario":"S2","domain":2},{"id":"a2-mcp-02","skill":"2.4","type":"single","stem":"<p>The team's <code>.mcp.json</code> must configure a GitHub MCP server that needs a personal access token. How should the token be provided?</p>","options":["Commit the token in <code>.mcp.json</code> but restrict the repository's visibility to the team members only","Store the token in CLAUDE.md so that Claude can pass it to the MCP server when it is needed","Reference <code>${GITHUB_TOKEN}</code> in <code>.mcp.json</code> and let each developer and CI provide the environment variable","Ask Claude to prompt each developer for the token at the start of every session that uses GitHub"],"correct":[2],"explanation":"<p>Environment-variable expansion keeps secrets out of version control while sharing the configuration. Committing tokens or putting them in CLAUDE.md leaks them; prompting through Claude exposes secrets in conversation.</p>","scenario":"S2","domain":2},{"id":"a2-mcp-03","skill":"2.4","type":"single","stem":"<p>An agent spends many turns calling <code>list_docs</code> and <code>search_docs</code> just to discover which of 300 internal documents exist. What MCP design improvement helps most?</p>","options":["Expose the document catalog as an MCP resource so Claude can see what exists without exploratory calls","Add a <code>list_all_docs_with_full_text</code> tool that returns the complete content of all 300 documents","Increase the agent's iteration limit so that the exploratory calls no longer exhaust its turn budget","Remove <code>search_docs</code> so the agent is forced to read the catalog page by page using <code>list_docs</code>"],"correct":[0],"explanation":"<p>Resources expose content such as catalogs for the client to read, reducing exploratory tool calls. Dumping all full text floods context; more iterations treat the symptom; removing search makes things worse.</p>","scenario":"S4","domain":2},{"id":"a2-mcp-04","skill":"2.4","type":"single","stem":"<p>A developer wants to try an experimental MCP server across all their projects without affecting teammates. Where should it be configured?</p>","options":["In the project's <code>.mcp.json</code>, marked as optional so that teammates can disable it if they want","In the repository's CLAUDE.md, where personal tools are listed for the developer's own sessions","In a <code>.claude/rules/</code> file with a path glob that matches only the developer's own working files","At user scope (in <code>~/.claude.json</code>), which applies to that developer only, across all projects"],"correct":[3],"explanation":"<p>User scope is personal and cross-project. Project <code>.mcp.json</code> is shared; CLAUDE.md and rules don't configure MCP servers.</p>","scenario":"S4","domain":2},{"id":"a2-mcp-05","skill":"2.4","type":"single","stem":"<p>The team needs Claude Code to work with GitHub issues and pull requests. What is the recommended approach?</p>","options":["Build a custom MCP server for GitHub so that the team controls exactly which endpoints are exposed","Use an existing maintained GitHub MCP server and reserve custom servers for team-specific workflows","Give Claude raw <code>curl</code> access through Bash and let it call the GitHub REST API directly as needed","Copy the GitHub API documentation into CLAUDE.md so that Claude knows how to call each endpoint"],"correct":[1],"explanation":"<p>For standard integrations, reuse existing servers; build custom ones for internal or team-specific needs. Raw curl and pasted docs are less reliable and harder to secure.</p>","scenario":"S2","domain":2},{"id":"a2-mcp-06","skill":"2.4","type":"single","stem":"<p>Claude Code is connected to three MCP servers. How are their tools made available to Claude?</p>","options":["Only the tools of the first server listed in the configuration are available during a session","Claude must call a <code>switch_server</code> tool before it can use tools from a different MCP server","Tools are available only after the user approves each server again at the start of every turn","Tools from all configured servers are discovered at connection and available together, named by server"],"correct":[3],"explanation":"<p>All configured servers' tools are available simultaneously (namespaced as <code>mcp__server__tool</code>). There's no single-server limit or switching tool, and approval isn't per turn.</p>","scenario":"S4","domain":2},{"id":"a2-mcp-07","skill":"2.4","type":"single","stem":"<p>A specialised MCP code-search tool is available, but Claude keeps using built-in Grep for tasks where the MCP tool is better. What should you change first?</p>","options":["Disable all built-in tools so that only MCP tools are available to Claude in this project","Improve the MCP tool's description to explain what it does better than Grep and when to use it","Move the MCP server from project scope to user scope so that it takes precedence over Grep","Ask developers to mention the MCP tool by name in every request where it should be used"],"correct":[1],"explanation":"<p>Descriptions drive selection; MCP tools must make their advantage over built-ins explicit. Disabling built-ins is heavy-handed; scope doesn't affect priority; relying on users to name tools doesn't scale.</p>","scenario":"S4","domain":2},{"id":"a2-mcp-08","skill":"2.4","type":"single","stem":"<p>A CI job uses the project's <code>.mcp.json</code>, which references <code>${JIRA_TOKEN}</code>. The job fails to connect to the Jira server. What is the most likely fix?</p>","options":["Provide <code>JIRA_TOKEN</code> as an environment variable (from the CI secret store) in the job","Replace <code>${JIRA_TOKEN}</code> with the literal token value in the committed <code>.mcp.json</code> file","Move the Jira server to user scope on the CI runner so it reads the developer's settings","Add <code>--verbose</code> to the Claude Code command so that the server connection is retried"],"correct":[0],"explanation":"<p>The configuration expands the variable at runtime; CI must supply it from its secret store. Committing the literal token leaks it; user scope on runners and verbose output don't provide the credential.</p>","scenario":"S5","domain":2},{"id":"a2-bit-01","skill":"2.5","type":"single","stem":"<p>An engineer asks the agent to find every place that calls <code>processRefund</code>. Which built-in tool is the right first choice?</p>","options":["Glob, because it finds all files whose names match the pattern <code>processRefund</code>","Grep, because it searches file contents for the function name across the codebase","Read, because reading each file in turn is the most reliable way to find every call","Write, because it can add a marker comment wherever the function happens to be used"],"correct":[1],"explanation":"<p>Grep searches contents; it's the tool for finding callers. Glob matches file paths, Read on every file is slow and floods context, and Write modifies files.</p>","scenario":"S4","domain":2},{"id":"a2-bit-02","skill":"2.5","type":"single","stem":"<p>An Edit fails because the anchor text <code>return None</code> appears seven times in the file. What is the best next step?</p>","options":["Retry the same Edit several times, because the tool picks a random occurrence on each attempt","Use <code>sed</code> through Bash to replace every occurrence of the string in the file at once","Include enough surrounding lines to make the anchor unique, or Read the file and Write it back","Delete the file and recreate it from memory with the intended change already applied"],"correct":[2],"explanation":"<p>Edit needs a unique anchor; add context or fall back to Read + Write. Retrying fails identically; blanket <code>sed</code> changes all occurrences and bypasses safety; recreating from memory risks losing content.</p>","scenario":"S2","domain":2},{"id":"a2-bit-03","skill":"2.5","type":"single","stem":"<p>Which task is best served by Glob?</p>","options":["Finding every test file in the repository that matches <code>**/*.test.tsx</code>","Finding every test that mocks the payment client inside its test body","Finding the line where an error message \"invalid currency\" is raised","Finding every module that imports the deprecated <code>legacy/billing</code> path"],"correct":[0],"explanation":"<p>Glob matches paths. The other tasks require searching file contents, which is Grep.</p>","scenario":"S2","domain":2},{"id":"a2-bit-04","skill":"2.5","type":"single","stem":"<p>To understand how checkout works in an unfamiliar codebase, which exploration strategy is most effective?</p>","options":["Read every source file first so that the agent has the complete picture before answering anything","Ask the user to explain the architecture in detail and avoid reading code until they have done so","Use Glob to list all files and then answer based on the file names without reading any contents","Grep for checkout entry points, Read those files, and follow imports and calls incrementally"],"correct":[3],"explanation":"<p>Incremental, targeted exploration builds understanding without flooding the context. Reading everything degrades context and costs tokens; file names alone are insufficient; deferring entirely to the user ignores available tools.</p>","scenario":"S4","domain":2},{"id":"a2-bit-05","skill":"2.5","type":"single","stem":"<p>Grep finds <code>charge_card</code> called in only one place, but the bug report mentions it being called from several services. What should the agent do next?</p>","options":["Conclude that the bug report is wrong, since Grep has already searched the entire codebase","Look for wrappers or re-exports of <code>charge_card</code> and Grep for those names to trace indirect callers","Use Glob for <code>*charge_card*</code> to find files named after the function in other service folders","Read every file in all services to check manually whether they call the function somewhere"],"correct":[1],"explanation":"<p>Functions are often re-exported or wrapped under other names; tracing those names finds indirect callers. Dismissing the report is premature; Glob matches paths; reading everything is wasteful.</p>","scenario":"S4","domain":2},{"id":"a2-bit-06","skill":"2.5","type":"single","stem":"<p>When is the Write tool preferable to Edit?</p>","options":["When changing a single line in a large file, because Write is faster for small changes","When the file is under version control, because Edit does not work with tracked files","When the anchor text is unique, because Write avoids the overhead of matching the anchor","When creating a brand-new file or rewriting most of an existing file's contents"],"correct":[3],"explanation":"<p>Write replaces a whole file — ideal for new files or full rewrites. Edit is for targeted changes with unique anchors and works fine with version-controlled files.</p>","scenario":"S2","domain":2},{"id":"a2-bit-07","skill":"2.5","type":"single","stem":"<p>A CI agent needs to run the project's test suite and report failures. Which tool does it need, and how should it be restricted?</p>","options":["Read, restricted to test files, so that it can read the test results from the source files","Glob, restricted to <code>**/*.test.*</code>, so that it can find and execute the test files directly","Bash, restricted by permission rules to the test command (e.g. <code>Bash(npm test:*)</code>)","Write, restricted to the test directory, so that it can write and then run new test files"],"correct":[2],"explanation":"<p>Running commands requires Bash; permission rules can limit it to the specific command. Read and Glob don't execute anything; Write doesn't run tests.</p>","scenario":"S5","domain":2},{"id":"a2-bit-08","skill":"2.5","type":"single","stem":"<p>Why is delegating a broad \"where is X used across the monorepo?\" survey to the Explore subagent better than running dozens of Grep and Read calls in the main session?</p>","options":["The Explore subagent has access to a faster search index that the main session cannot use","The Explore subagent can edit files while it searches, which saves time later in the session","The main session cannot run more than ten Grep calls per conversation without an error","The search output stays in the subagent's context; the main session receives only the summary"],"correct":[3],"explanation":"<p>Delegating keeps verbose search results out of the main context. There's no special index, the Explore subagent is read-only, and there's no ten-call limit.</p>","scenario":"S4","domain":2},{"id":"a3-md-01","skill":"3.1","type":"single","stem":"<p>A new team member reports that Claude Code doesn't follow the team's API naming conventions, while it does for everyone else. The conventions were added months ago by the tech lead. Where are they most likely stored?</p>","options":["In the project's <code>CLAUDE.md</code>, which new clones only load after the first commit by that developer","In the tech lead's <code>~/.claude/CLAUDE.md</code>, which is personal and not shared through version control","In a <code>.claude/rules/</code> file, which requires each developer to activate it with a command first","In the repository's README, which Claude Code reads only when it is explicitly mentioned in a prompt"],"correct":[1],"explanation":"<p>User-level memory applies only to that user. Team conventions belong in the project CLAUDE.md (or <code>.claude/rules/</code>), committed to the repository. Project files load for every clone, and rules don't need activation.</p>","scenario":"S2","domain":3},{"id":"a3-md-02","skill":"3.1","type":"single","stem":"<p>A monorepo's root CLAUDE.md has grown to 1,400 lines covering backend, frontend, data and infrastructure conventions. Sessions load it all, and maintainers struggle to keep it current. What is the best restructuring?</p>","options":["Shorten it by deleting older conventions so that it fits within a few hundred lines again","Move all conventions into a single skill that developers invoke at the start of each session","Split it into focused files via <code>@import</code>, <code>.claude/rules/</code> and directory-level CLAUDE.md files","Convert it into a JSON settings file, which Claude Code parses more efficiently than Markdown"],"correct":[2],"explanation":"<p>Modular organisation keeps each part maintainable and lets areas load only what they need. Deleting conventions loses knowledge; a skill isn't for always-on conventions; settings.json doesn't hold memory instructions.</p>","scenario":"S2","domain":3},{"id":"a3-md-03","skill":"3.1","type":"single","stem":"<p>Claude seems to ignore an instruction in CLAUDE.md. What is the most sensible first diagnostic step?</p>","options":["Run <code>/memory</code> to check which memory files are actually loaded in the current session","Rewrite the instruction in capital letters and move it to the very top of the CLAUDE.md file","Switch to a more capable model, because smaller models often ignore memory instructions","Repeat the instruction in every prompt until Claude starts following it consistently again"],"correct":[0],"explanation":"<p>First confirm the file is loaded at all (and at the expected level). Rewording, switching models or repeating the instruction are premature if the file isn't loaded.</p>","scenario":"S2","domain":3},{"id":"a3-md-04","skill":"3.1","type":"single","stem":"<p>Where should the build and test commands that every developer and CI run need be documented for Claude Code?</p>","options":["In each developer's <code>~/.claude/CLAUDE.md</code>, so that personal environments can be customised freely","In <code>CLAUDE.local.md</code>, so that the commands stay out of version control and remain private","In a personal skill, so that developers invoke it whenever they need to run the build or tests","In the project-level <code>CLAUDE.md</code>, committed to the repository for everyone who clones it"],"correct":[3],"explanation":"<p>Shared, always-relevant project facts belong in the project CLAUDE.md. User-level and local files aren't shared; skills are on-demand workflows, not core project facts.</p>","scenario":"S2","domain":3},{"id":"a3-md-05","skill":"3.1","type":"single","stem":"<p>The <code>packages/billing</code> folder has conventions that apply only to code in that package. What is the most appropriate place for them?</p>","options":["The root CLAUDE.md, in a section titled \"Billing\" that all sessions load regardless of the folder","A <code>packages/billing/CLAUDE.md</code> file, which applies when Claude works in that part of the tree","A <code>.claude/rules/</code> file with <code>paths: [\"**/*\"]</code> so that it loads for every file in the repository","The billing team's personal <code>~/.claude/CLAUDE.md</code> files, which only those developers would load"],"correct":[1],"explanation":"<p>Directory-level CLAUDE.md suits conventions tied to one area. Root placement loads them everywhere; a <code>**/*</code> glob is equivalent to always-on; personal files exclude other contributors.</p>","scenario":"S2","domain":3},{"id":"a3-md-06","skill":"3.1","type":"single","stem":"<p>What does <code>@docs/api-conventions.md</code> inside a CLAUDE.md file do?</p>","options":["It creates a hyperlink that Claude follows only if the user explicitly asks about API conventions","It registers the file as a skill that Claude may load when it decides the file is relevant","It imports the referenced file's content into memory along with the CLAUDE.md that contains it","It marks the file as read-only so that Claude does not modify the API conventions document"],"correct":[2],"explanation":"<p><code>@path</code> imports another file into memory, enabling modular CLAUDE.md organisation. It isn't a lazy link, a skill registration or a permission setting.</p>","scenario":"S2","domain":3},{"id":"a3-md-07","skill":"3.1","type":"single","stem":"<p>A developer wants Claude to always explain changes in German for them personally, without affecting teammates. Where should this preference go?</p>","options":["In the project CLAUDE.md, under a \"personal preferences\" heading with the developer's name","In the developer's <code>~/.claude/CLAUDE.md</code>, which applies only to that developer's sessions","In <code>.mcp.json</code>, as an environment variable that sets the response language for the project","In a <code>.claude/rules/</code> file scoped to the developer's own feature branch using a path glob"],"correct":[1],"explanation":"<p>Personal preferences belong in user-level memory. The project file affects everyone; <code>.mcp.json</code> configures MCP servers; path globs scope by file path, not by branch or person.</p>","scenario":"S2","domain":3},{"id":"a3-md-08","skill":"3.1","type":"single","stem":"<p>CI runs of Claude Code generate tests that ignore the team's fixture conventions, while local runs follow them. Local developers have the conventions in their user-level memory. What should change?</p>","options":["Add the conventions to the CI job's command-line prompt as a long string on every invocation","Copy each developer's <code>~/.claude/CLAUDE.md</code> onto the CI runner so it behaves like a developer","Switch the CI job to a larger model that infers the conventions from the existing test files","Move the conventions into the project CLAUDE.md (or a rule file) so CI and every developer load them"],"correct":[3],"explanation":"<p>CI only gets project-level context. Putting conventions in the repository makes them available everywhere. Prompt strings duplicate and drift; copying personal files is fragile; a larger model doesn't know unwritten conventions.</p>","scenario":"S5","domain":3},{"id":"a3-skl-01","skill":"3.2","type":"single","stem":"<p>The team wants a <code>/review</code> command that every developer has automatically when they clone or pull the repository. Where should it be created?</p>","options":["In <code>.claude/commands/</code> (or <code>.claude/skills/</code>) inside the repository, committed to version control","In <code>~/.claude/commands/</code> on each developer's machine, installed by a setup script once","In the project's CLAUDE.md, described as a workflow that Claude follows when asked to review","In <code>.claude/settings.local.json</code>, which registers custom commands for the current project"],"correct":[0],"explanation":"<p>Project-scoped commands/skills in the repository are shared via version control. Personal directories need manual setup; CLAUDE.md isn't a command; <code>settings.local.json</code> is personal and doesn't define commands.</p>","scenario":"S2","domain":3},{"id":"a3-skl-02","skill":"3.2","type":"single","stem":"<p>A <code>/audit</code> skill explores the codebase and produces long intermediate output, which crowds the main conversation. Which SKILL.md frontmatter setting addresses this?</p>","options":["<code>allowed-tools: Read, Grep</code> so that the skill can only produce a smaller amount of output","<code>argument-hint: \"[directory]\"</code> so that developers narrow the audit to a single directory","<code>context: fork</code> so the skill runs in an isolated sub-agent context and only its result returns","<code>description: short</code> so that Claude keeps the skill's output brief when it is invoked"],"correct":[2],"explanation":"<p><code>context: fork</code> isolates verbose work from the main session. <code>allowed-tools</code> restricts tools, not output volume; <code>argument-hint</code> guides arguments; there is no \"short\" description mode.</p>","scenario":"S2","domain":3},{"id":"a3-skl-03","skill":"3.2","type":"single","stem":"<p>A skill should audit code but must never modify files, even if its instructions are misread. What should its frontmatter include?</p>","options":["<code>context: fork</code>, because forked skills cannot write to the file system of the main session","<code>allowed-tools</code> restricted to read-only tools such as Read, Grep and Glob only","<code>argument-hint: \"read-only\"</code> so that the skill is always invoked in a read-only manner","A line in the description stating that the skill must not modify any files at all"],"correct":[1],"explanation":"<p><code>allowed-tools</code> deterministically restricts what the skill can do. Forked contexts can still use write tools if allowed; argument hints and descriptions don't enforce anything.</p>","scenario":"S2","domain":3},{"id":"a3-skl-04","skill":"3.2","type":"single","stem":"<p>A team's release checklist (tag, changelog, version bump, announcement) is needed about twice a month. Where should it live?</p>","options":["In the root CLAUDE.md, so that Claude always knows the release procedure in every session","In <code>.mcp.json</code>, as a release server that exposes each checklist step as a separate tool","In each developer's personal notes, so that only release managers can see the procedure","In a project skill (e.g. <code>.claude/skills/release/SKILL.md</code>) that is invoked when releasing"],"correct":[3],"explanation":"<p>Task-specific, occasional workflows fit skills; they load on demand. Putting them in CLAUDE.md wastes context in every session; an MCP server is unnecessary; personal notes aren't shared.</p>","scenario":"S2","domain":3},{"id":"a3-skl-05","skill":"3.2","type":"single","stem":"<p>What does <code>argument-hint</code> in a skill's frontmatter do?</p>","options":["It validates the argument's format and rejects invocations with invalid arguments","It sets a default argument that is used whenever the user does not provide one","It shows users what argument the command expects, such as <code>[path to file]</code>","It tells Claude which tools to use for processing the argument once given"],"correct":[2],"explanation":"<p><code>argument-hint</code> is a usage hint displayed to the user. It doesn't validate, default or select tools.</p>","scenario":"S2","domain":3},{"id":"a3-skl-06","skill":"3.2","type":"single","stem":"<p>A developer wants a stricter personal version of the team's <code>/review</code> skill without affecting the team. What is the recommended approach?</p>","options":["Edit the team's <code>.claude/skills/review/SKILL.md</code> locally and avoid committing the change","Create a personal skill in <code>~/.claude/skills/</code> under a different name, e.g. <code>/review-strict</code>","Create a personal skill named <code>review</code> that overrides the team skill for everyone on the team","Add the stricter criteria to the project CLAUDE.md so they apply to every <code>/review</code> invocation"],"correct":[1],"explanation":"<p>A personal variant with a distinct name avoids confusing or shadowing the team skill. Uncommitted local edits are fragile; same-name personal skills cause confusion; project CLAUDE.md affects everyone.</p>","scenario":"S2","domain":3},{"id":"a3-skl-07","skill":"3.2","type":"single","stem":"<p>In a command file, the body says \"Explain the module $ARGUMENTS in plain language.\" What happens when a user types <code>/explain src/billing</code>?</p>","options":["Claude asks the user which arguments they meant, since <code>$ARGUMENTS</code> is a reserved keyword","The command fails because arguments must be declared in the frontmatter before use","<code>$ARGUMENTS</code> is replaced with <code>src/billing</code>, and the resulting prompt is sent to Claude","<code>$ARGUMENTS</code> is replaced with a list of all files in the repository by default"],"correct":[2],"explanation":"<p><code>$ARGUMENTS</code> is substituted with the text typed after the command. No declaration is required, and it's not replaced with file listings.</p>","scenario":"S2","domain":3},{"id":"a3-skl-08","skill":"3.2","type":"single","stem":"<p>When is a skill a better choice than adding instructions to CLAUDE.md?</p>","options":["When the guidance is a task-specific workflow needed only sometimes, so it shouldn't load every session","When the guidance must apply to every request, because skills are loaded with a higher priority","When the guidance is short, because skills have a smaller context cost than memory instructions","When the guidance needs to be shared with the team, because CLAUDE.md is always kept personal"],"correct":[0],"explanation":"<p>Skills load on demand — ideal for occasional workflows. Always-on conventions belong in CLAUDE.md; project CLAUDE.md is shared; length isn't the deciding factor.</p>","scenario":"S4","domain":3},{"id":"a3-rule-01","skill":"3.3","type":"single","stem":"<p>A React codebase co-locates test files (<code>*.test.tsx</code>) next to components in hundreds of folders. The team wants test conventions applied automatically whenever Claude writes or edits a test. What is the best mechanism?</p>","options":["A CLAUDE.md file in every folder that contains test files, each with the same test conventions","A section in the root CLAUDE.md, so the conventions are loaded for every session in the repo","A <code>.claude/rules/</code> file with <code>paths: [\"**/*.test.tsx\"]</code> frontmatter containing the conventions","A <code>/test-conventions</code> skill that developers invoke before asking Claude to work on tests"],"correct":[2],"explanation":"<p>Path-scoped rules load exactly when matching files are touched, wherever they live. Hundreds of directory files duplicate and drift; root CLAUDE.md loads everywhere; a manually invoked skill can be forgotten.</p>","scenario":"S2","domain":3},{"id":"a3-rule-02","skill":"3.3","type":"single","stem":"<p>What is the main context benefit of path-specific rules compared with putting the same rules in the root CLAUDE.md?</p>","options":["Path-specific rules are compressed, so they use fewer tokens than the same text in CLAUDE.md","They load only when Claude works on matching files, instead of occupying context in every session","They are cached across sessions, so they never count toward the context window after first use","They are loaded into the system prompt, which does not count toward the context window"],"correct":[1],"explanation":"<p>Scoped loading keeps irrelevant instructions out of context. Rules aren't compressed or free of context cost; caching and system-prompt placement don't remove token cost.</p>","scenario":"S2","domain":3},{"id":"a3-rule-03","skill":"3.3","type":"single","stem":"<p>Terraform files live under <code>infra/</code> and also in <code>services/*/deploy/</code>. Which configuration applies Terraform conventions to all of them?</p>","options":["A CLAUDE.md in <code>infra/</code> only, since Terraform conventions primarily concern infrastructure code","A rule file with <code>paths: [\"infra/*\"]</code>, which also matches nested folders across the repository","A skill named <code>terraform</code>, which Claude loads automatically whenever any file is opened","A rule file with <code>paths: [\"**/*.tf\"]</code>, which matches Terraform files anywhere in the tree"],"correct":[3],"explanation":"<p>A glob on the file type catches all Terraform files wherever they are. A CLAUDE.md in <code>infra/</code> misses services; <code>infra/*</code> doesn't match <code>services/*/deploy</code>; a skill isn't loaded for every file.</p>","scenario":"S2","domain":3},{"id":"a3-rule-04","skill":"3.3","type":"single","stem":"<p>Which situation is better served by a directory-level CLAUDE.md than by a path-scoped rule?</p>","options":["Conventions for all SQL migration files spread across several service directories","Conventions for all code in the <code>packages/payments</code> package, which lives in one folder","Conventions for every Storybook story file located next to its component in the tree","Conventions for all Dockerfiles placed in the root folders of dozens of microservices"],"correct":[1],"explanation":"<p>Directory-level CLAUDE.md fits an area of the tree. File types spread across directories (migrations, stories, Dockerfiles) are better targeted with path globs.</p>","scenario":"S2","domain":3},{"id":"a3-rule-05","skill":"3.3","type":"single","stem":"<p>A rule file <code>.claude/rules/api.md</code> has no <code>paths</code> frontmatter. How is it loaded?</p>","options":["It is never loaded until a <code>paths</code> field is added to the frontmatter of the rule file","It is loaded only when a developer explicitly invokes it as a slash command by name","It loads unconditionally, like CLAUDE.md content, because it isn't scoped to any paths","It is loaded only for files in the same directory as the rule file, <code>.claude/rules/</code>"],"correct":[2],"explanation":"<p>Rules without <code>paths</code> apply generally, like project memory; <code>paths</code> frontmatter makes them conditional. Rules aren't slash commands.</p>","scenario":"S2","domain":3},{"id":"a3-rule-06","skill":"3.3","type":"single","stem":"<p>CI-generated tests ignore the conventions for test files. The conventions sit in a personal path-scoped rule in one developer's <code>~/.claude/rules/</code>. What should change?</p>","options":["Copy the rule into the CI job's prompt string so that every pipeline run includes it","Rename the rule file so that it matches the CI runner's username on the build machine","Switch the CI job to plan mode so that it reads all available rule files before acting","Move the rule into the project's <code>.claude/rules/</code> so that CI and every developer get it"],"correct":[3],"explanation":"<p>Project-level rules are shared through the repository and apply in CI. Personal rules don't reach CI; prompt strings drift; naming and plan mode don't load personal rules.</p>","scenario":"S5","domain":3},{"id":"a3-rule-07","skill":"3.3","type":"single","stem":"<p>A team writes a rule with <code>paths: [\"src/**/*.ts\"]</code> for TypeScript style, but it also needs to apply to <code>.tsx</code> files. What is the correct change?</p>","options":["Add <code>\"src/**/*.tsx\"</code> to the <code>paths</code> list so both extensions match","Rename all <code>.tsx</code> files to <code>.ts</code> so the existing pattern matches them","Remove <code>paths</code> so the rule loads for every file in the repository","Copy the rule into a second file with the same content and a <code>.tsx</code> name"],"correct":[0],"explanation":"<p>Add the additional glob; <code>paths</code> accepts a list. Renaming files is destructive; removing <code>paths</code> over-applies the rule; duplicating content drifts.</p>","scenario":"S2","domain":3},{"id":"a3-rule-08","skill":"3.3","type":"single","stem":"<p>Why might a team choose a path-scoped rule over a skill for \"how to write database migrations\"?</p>","options":["Rules can execute scripts that validate migrations, whereas skills can only contain text","Skills are not shared through version control, while rules are always committed to the repo","Rules run in a forked context, so they do not consume any space in the main conversation","Rules apply automatically when migration files are edited, without anyone needing to invoke them"],"correct":[3],"explanation":"<p>Path-scoped rules activate automatically for matching files, guaranteeing conventions are present. Skills can be shared and contain scripts; rules don't run in forked contexts.</p>","scenario":"S2","domain":3},{"id":"a3-plan-01","skill":"3.4","type":"single","stem":"<p>A developer must migrate a monolith's logging library across 45 files in several services, with a choice between two migration strategies. Which approach is most appropriate?</p>","options":["Direct execution, starting with the first file, and adjusting the approach as problems appear","Plan mode first to explore, compare strategies and list affected files, then execute the agreed plan","Direct execution with the instruction to \"be careful\", followed by a full review of the diff","Ask Claude to rewrite the logging library itself so that the existing calls no longer need changes"],"correct":[1],"explanation":"<p>Multi-file, architectural changes with alternative approaches benefit from planning before edits. Direct execution commits to an approach early; \"be careful\" doesn't replace planning; rewriting the library is out of scope.</p>","scenario":"S2","domain":3},{"id":"a3-plan-02","skill":"3.4","type":"single","stem":"<p>A stack trace points to a missing null check on one line of one function. Which approach is most appropriate?</p>","options":["Direct execution: make the fix, run the relevant tests, and review the small diff","Plan mode: explore the whole codebase first to understand where else null checks are needed","The Explore subagent: survey the module before deciding whether a change is necessary at all","A multi-agent review: generate the fix and have two independent reviewers approve it"],"correct":[0],"explanation":"<p>Small, well-understood changes are best done directly. Planning or exploration adds overhead without benefit here.</p>","scenario":"S2","domain":3},{"id":"a3-plan-03","skill":"3.4","type":"single","stem":"<p>During planning, Claude needs to survey how authentication is wired across 12 services, which produces a lot of search output. How should this be done?</p>","options":["Run the searches in the main session and use <code>/clear</code> once the survey is complete","Ask the developer to perform the searches and paste a summary of what they found","Skip the survey and plan based on the naming conventions of the services instead","Use the Explore subagent so discovery happens in an isolated context and a summary returns"],"correct":[3],"explanation":"<p>The Explore subagent keeps verbose discovery out of the main context. <code>/clear</code> would also discard useful context; offloading to the developer or skipping discovery reduces plan quality.</p>","scenario":"S4","domain":3},{"id":"a3-plan-04","skill":"3.4","type":"single","stem":"<p>Which statement best describes how plan mode and direct execution relate in a complex change?</p>","options":["They are alternatives; once a task starts in plan mode it must be completed in plan mode","Plan mode is only for documentation tasks, while direct execution is for every code change","Planning is a first phase: explore and agree a plan, then switch to execution to implement it","Direct execution should always come first, with plan mode used afterwards to document it"],"correct":[2],"explanation":"<p>The typical flow is explore → plan → implement → verify. Plan mode doesn't edit files, so implementation happens after switching modes.</p>","scenario":"S2","domain":3},{"id":"a3-plan-05","skill":"3.4","type":"single","stem":"<p>Which change most clearly warrants plan mode?</p>","options":["Fixing a typo in an error message that a user reported in a single template file","Adding a log statement at the start of one function to help debug an issue","Replacing session cookies with token-based auth across the API, web app and mobile client","Renaming a local variable inside one function for clarity during a routine refactor"],"correct":[2],"explanation":"<p>Cross-cutting architectural changes with risk and multiple components warrant planning. The others are small, clear, single-location edits.</p>","scenario":"S2","domain":3},{"id":"a3-plan-06","skill":"3.4","type":"single","stem":"<p>Requirements for a feature are ambiguous (\"make exports faster and more flexible\"). What is the best way to start?</p>","options":["Plan mode, clarifying the goals and options before committing to an implementation","Direct execution with the most likely interpretation, then iterate if it's wrong","Generate three full implementations in parallel and let the team pick their favourite","Increase <code>max_tokens</code> so Claude can write a more complete implementation on the first try"],"correct":[0],"explanation":"<p>Ambiguity calls for planning and clarification first. Guessing wastes effort; three full implementations are expensive; output length is irrelevant.</p>","scenario":"S2","domain":3},{"id":"a3-plan-07","skill":"3.4","type":"single","stem":"<p>What does plan mode prevent that makes it safe for exploring unfamiliar, critical code?</p>","options":["It prevents Claude from reading files outside the current working directory of the session","It prevents file edits and other modifying actions while Claude explores and proposes a plan","It prevents Claude from using more than a fixed number of tokens for the planning phase","It prevents Claude from calling MCP tools, limiting it to the built-in tools only"],"correct":[1],"explanation":"<p>Plan mode is read-only analysis: Claude can explore but not modify. It doesn't restrict directories, token counts or MCP tools in that way.</p>","scenario":"S4","domain":3},{"id":"a3-plan-08","skill":"3.4","type":"single","stem":"<p>A developer always uses plan mode, even for one-line fixes, and complains about slow progress. What advice fits best?</p>","options":["Keep using plan mode for everything, because it always improves the quality of every change","Never use plan mode, because direct execution is always faster and the quality is the same","Use plan mode only on Fridays, when there is more time available for reviewing the plans","Match the mode to the task: plan for complex or ambiguous work, direct for small edits"],"correct":[3],"explanation":"<p>Plan mode is valuable for complex or risky work and overhead for trivial edits. Choose by complexity and risk.</p>","scenario":"S2","domain":3},{"id":"a3-iter-01","skill":"3.5","type":"single","stem":"<p>Claude's address-normalisation function produces inconsistent output despite a detailed prose description of the rules. What is the most effective next step?</p>","options":["Rewrite the description at greater length, covering more rules and exceptions in detail","Provide two or three concrete input → expected-output examples, including the tricky cases","Ask Claude to try again with the same description until the output becomes consistent","Switch to a different model that is known to follow long prose instructions more closely"],"correct":[1],"explanation":"<p>Concrete examples remove ambiguity faster than more prose. Repeating or switching models doesn't fix the underspecified transformation.</p>","scenario":"S2","domain":3},{"id":"a3-iter-02","skill":"3.5","type":"single","stem":"<p>You report three related validation bugs to Claude one at a time. Each fix breaks part of the previous one. What should you do instead?</p>","options":["Continue one at a time but ask Claude to be more careful not to break earlier fixes","Revert all changes and implement the validation logic yourself without Claude's help","Describe all three interacting issues in one message with expected behaviour and tests for each","Open three separate sessions so that each bug is fixed in isolation from the others"],"correct":[2],"explanation":"<p>Interacting issues should be addressed together so Claude can design a coherent fix. Asking for care doesn't change the information available; separate sessions make the interference worse.</p>","scenario":"S2","domain":3},{"id":"a3-iter-03","skill":"3.5","type":"single","stem":"<p>Which workflow gives Claude the most objective feedback while implementing a parsing function?</p>","options":["Describe the desired behaviour and review the output manually after each attempt","Ask Claude to self-assess its implementation and rate its correctness from 1 to 10","Write failing tests first, then let Claude implement and iterate until the tests pass","Ask Claude to explain its implementation and check whether the explanation sounds right"],"correct":[2],"explanation":"<p>Tests provide repeatable, objective feedback Claude can run itself. Manual review and self-assessment are subjective and slower.</p>","scenario":"S2","domain":3},{"id":"a3-iter-04","skill":"3.5","type":"single","stem":"<p>A developer must implement tax rounding for a country whose rules they don't know well. Which technique helps most before implementation?</p>","options":["The interview pattern: ask Claude to question you about requirements and edge cases first","Start implementing immediately and fix rounding errors as users report them later on","Ask Claude for the most common rounding rule worldwide and implement that one","Copy rounding code from another country's module and adjust the constants"],"correct":[0],"explanation":"<p>The interview pattern surfaces requirements and edge cases you might not anticipate in an unfamiliar domain. The other options risk building the wrong thing.</p>","scenario":"S2","domain":3},{"id":"a3-iter-05","skill":"3.5","type":"single","stem":"<p>A test fails in CI after Claude's change. What is the most useful feedback to give Claude?</p>","options":["\"The tests are failing, please fix it.\"","\"Something is wrong with the date handling, I think.\"","\"Please review all of your changes again more carefully.\"","The failing test's name and its full assertion output"],"correct":[3],"explanation":"<p>Exact failure output lets Claude locate and fix the issue. Vague descriptions force guessing.</p>","scenario":"S5","domain":3},{"id":"a3-iter-06","skill":"3.5","type":"single","stem":"<p>You have four unrelated small issues: a doc typo, a missing log line, a slow query, and an outdated dependency. How should you give the feedback?</p>","options":["All in one message, since batching every issue together is always the most efficient choice","Sequentially, one at a time, so that each change stays small and easy to verify","Only the slow query, since the other issues are too minor to bring to Claude at all","In four parallel sessions, each with a full copy of the repository and conversation"],"correct":[1],"explanation":"<p>Independent issues are best handled sequentially for small, verifiable changes. Batching is for interacting issues; ignoring issues isn't a strategy; parallel sessions add overhead.</p>","scenario":"S2","domain":3},{"id":"a3-iter-07","skill":"3.5","type":"single","stem":"<p>Claude's extraction output format varies between attempts for the same document type. Which refinement is most effective?</p>","options":["Tell Claude the format \"must be consistent\" and repeat this in every subsequent prompt","Increase the temperature so that Claude explores different formats and chooses the best","Show a concrete example of the exact expected output format alongside the instructions","Accept the variation and normalise the different formats manually after extraction"],"correct":[2],"explanation":"<p>A concrete output example (or a schema) anchors format. Exhortation and higher temperature don't; manual normalisation hides the problem.</p>","scenario":"S6","domain":3},{"id":"a3-iter-08","skill":"3.5","type":"single","stem":"<p>Why is test-driven iteration particularly effective with Claude Code?</p>","options":["Because Claude Code refuses to write code until tests exist for the target function","Because Claude can run the tests itself, see precise failures, and iterate until they pass","Because tests reduce the number of tokens Claude needs to generate the implementation","Because tests are automatically converted into CLAUDE.md rules for later sessions"],"correct":[1],"explanation":"<p>Claude can execute tests and use their output as a feedback loop. It doesn't refuse without tests, tests don't reduce generation tokens, and they're not converted into rules.</p>","scenario":"S2","domain":3},{"id":"a3-ci-01","skill":"3.6","type":"single","stem":"<p>A pipeline step runs <code>claude \"Review this PR for bugs\"</code> and the job hangs until it times out. What is the fix?</p>","options":["Add <code>--verbose</code> so that Claude Code prints progress and the CI runner sees activity","Run in non-interactive print mode with <code>-p</code> so the command returns its result and exits","Increase the CI job's timeout so that Claude has enough time to finish the review","Set <code>ANTHROPIC_MODEL</code> to a faster model so the review completes before the timeout"],"correct":[1],"explanation":"<p>Without <code>-p</code>/<code>--print</code>, Claude Code starts an interactive session and waits for input. Verbosity, longer timeouts or faster models don't change interactivity.</p>","scenario":"S5","domain":3},{"id":"a3-ci-02","skill":"3.6","type":"single","stem":"<p>Your pipeline needs to post Claude's review findings as inline PR comments. What is the most reliable way to get findings your script can process?</p>","options":["Ask Claude to format findings as a Markdown table and parse the table with regular expressions","Ask Claude to list one finding per line starting with \"FILE:\" and split the text on newlines","Use <code>--output-format json</code> with <code>--json-schema</code> describing file, line, severity and message","Have Claude post comments directly through the GitHub API using a token in the prompt"],"correct":[2],"explanation":"<p>Structured JSON output constrained by a schema is machine-parseable and robust. Parsing free text is brittle; putting tokens in prompts is insecure.</p>","scenario":"S5","domain":3},{"id":"a3-ci-03","skill":"3.6","type":"single","stem":"<p>Claude generated code in a CI step and is then asked, in the same session, to review that code. Reviews rarely find problems that humans later catch. What improves review quality?</p>","options":["Run the review in a separate, independent Claude invocation without the generator's reasoning","Ask the same session to \"review critically\" and list at least five possible issues each time","Enable extended thinking for the same session so that it reflects more deeply on its code","Repeat the review in the same session three times and merge the findings from all runs"],"correct":[0],"explanation":"<p>A model reviewing its own work in the same context tends to confirm its reasoning. An independent instance reviews on the merits. Prompting, thinking or repetition in the same session help only marginally.</p>","scenario":"S5","domain":3},{"id":"a3-ci-04","skill":"3.6","type":"single","stem":"<p>Each push to a PR re-runs the Claude review, and developers complain about duplicate comments on issues already reported. What is the best fix?</p>","options":["Run the review only once per PR, on the first push, and skip it for all later pushes","Delete all previous comments before posting the new findings on every review run","Include the previous findings in the prompt and ask for only new or still-unresolved issues","Lower the review's sensitivity so that it reports fewer issues on each subsequent run"],"correct":[2],"explanation":"<p>Passing prior findings lets Claude report only what's new or unresolved. Reviewing once misses new problems; deleting comments loses discussion; lowering sensitivity misses real issues.</p>","scenario":"S5","domain":3},{"id":"a3-ci-05","skill":"3.6","type":"single","stem":"<p>A team wants to cut costs for (a) a blocking pre-merge review and (b) a nightly audit of all repositories. Which plan is best?</p>","options":["Move both to the Message Batches API, since it is 50% cheaper for every type of workload","Keep (a) synchronous and move only (b) to the Message Batches API instead","Move (a) to batches and keep (b) synchronous, since nightly jobs need fast results","Keep both synchronous, because batches cannot be used in any CI environment"],"correct":[1],"explanation":"<p>Batches suit latency-tolerant, non-blocking jobs; they can take up to 24 hours. Blocking checks must stay synchronous.</p>","scenario":"S5","domain":3},{"id":"a3-ci-06","skill":"3.6","type":"single","stem":"<p>CI-generated tests duplicate tests that already exist and ignore fixture conventions. What is the most effective fix?</p>","options":["Use a larger model in CI that will infer conventions and existing coverage on its own","Add a post-processing step that deletes generated tests whose names match existing ones","Run test generation twice and keep only the tests that appear in both of the runs","Document fixture conventions and existing test locations in the project CLAUDE.md for CI to load"],"correct":[3],"explanation":"<p>CI runs depend on project context; documenting conventions and existing tests addresses the cause. Model size, name-based deletion and double generation don't provide missing context.</p>","scenario":"S5","domain":3},{"id":"a3-ci-07","skill":"3.6","type":"single","stem":"<p>Which practice best secures a Claude Code step in a GitHub Actions workflow?</p>","options":["Hard-code the API key in the workflow file so that the job always has valid credentials","Supply the API key from repository secrets and restrict tools with <code>--allowedTools</code>","Use <code>--dangerously-skip-permissions</code> so the job never blocks on a permission prompt","Give the job admin rights to the repository so that it never fails on missing access"],"correct":[1],"explanation":"<p>Secrets keep credentials out of code; restricting tools limits what the job can do. Hard-coded keys leak; skipping permissions and admin rights expand risk unnecessarily.</p>","scenario":"S5","domain":3},{"id":"a3-ci-08","skill":"3.6","type":"single","stem":"<p>What advantage does <code>--json-schema</code> add on top of <code>--output-format json</code>?</p>","options":["It makes the output faster to generate because the schema reduces the number of tokens","It converts any Markdown in Claude's answer into HTML for display inside the pull request","It constrains the result to a defined structure, so findings always have the fields the script expects","It validates the code under review against the schema and fails the build on mismatches"],"correct":[2],"explanation":"<p><code>--json-schema</code> constrains the structured result to your schema. <code>--output-format json</code> alone wraps the response in a JSON envelope without guaranteeing the inner structure.</p>","scenario":"S5","domain":3},{"id":"a4-crit-01","skill":"4.1","type":"single","stem":"<p>Your CI review prompt says \"Be conservative and only report high-confidence issues.\" False positives remain high, especially for \"possible performance problems\". What change is most effective?</p>","options":["Add \"be even more conservative\" and ask Claude to double-check each finding before reporting it","Define explicit criteria for what to report, what to skip, and how to rate severity","Require a confidence score on each finding and post only those with a score above 0.8","Switch to a larger model, which will better understand what \"high-confidence\" means for the team"],"correct":[1],"explanation":"<p>Vague instructions give no decision boundary; explicit criteria do. Self-reported confidence is poorly calibrated; a bigger model still faces an undefined boundary.</p>","scenario":"S5","domain":4},{"id":"a4-crit-02","skill":"4.1","type":"single","stem":"<p>Developers have started dismissing all review comments, including accurate security findings, because style and performance comments are mostly wrong. What is the best immediate step?</p>","options":["Add a disclaimer to every comment explaining that some findings may be false positives","Post all findings in a single summary comment so that they are less intrusive for developers","Keep all categories but lower the number of comments per PR to a maximum of three","Temporarily disable the high-false-positive categories while improving their criteria"],"correct":[3],"explanation":"<p>Removing noisy categories restores trust in the accurate ones while you fix the criteria. Disclaimers and summaries don't fix precision; capping comments may drop the most important ones.</p>","scenario":"S5","domain":4},{"id":"a4-crit-03","skill":"4.1","type":"single","stem":"<p>Which instruction is the best example of an explicit criterion for a review agent?</p>","options":["\"Flag comments that might be inaccurate or misleading in any way.\"","\"Flag a code comment only when it contradicts what the code does.\"","\"Use your judgement to decide which comments deserve attention.\"","\"Be strict about comments, because documentation quality matters.\""],"correct":[1],"explanation":"<p>An explicit criterion defines a checkable condition. The others rely on undefined judgement.</p>","scenario":"S5","domain":4},{"id":"a4-crit-04","skill":"4.1","type":"single","stem":"<p>A contract-review agent marks too many clauses as \"high risk\". Which prompt improvement helps most?</p>","options":["Ask the agent to mark fewer clauses as high risk so that the results look more balanced","Remove the \"high risk\" category so that the agent only uses \"medium\" and \"low\" labels","Define each risk level with concrete conditions and example clauses for each level","Ask the agent to explain its reasoning for each label in a long paragraph of text"],"correct":[2],"explanation":"<p>Concrete definitions and examples per level set the boundary. Asking for \"fewer\" is arbitrary; removing the category loses information; explanations don't define criteria.</p>","scenario":"S6","domain":4},{"id":"a4-crit-05","skill":"4.1","type":"single","stem":"<p>Why include a \"skip\" list (e.g. formatting, import order, naming per our linter) in a review prompt?</p>","options":["Because Claude cannot recognise formatting problems unless they are listed explicitly","Because it removes categories handled elsewhere, reducing noise and false positives","Because skip lists reduce the review's token cost by shortening the reviewed code","Because the review API requires an explicit list of ignored categories in the prompt"],"correct":[1],"explanation":"<p>Telling Claude what not to report removes noise from categories other tools already enforce. Claude can see formatting; skip lists don't shorten input; no API requirement exists.</p>","scenario":"S5","domain":4},{"id":"a4-crit-06","skill":"4.1","type":"single","stem":"<p>A support agent is told \"be careful with refunds\". It now refuses many legitimate refunds. What is the best fix?</p>","options":["Replace the instruction with explicit refund criteria: eligibility windows, amount limits and required checks","Remove the instruction entirely, so that the agent processes every refund that customers request","Add \"but don't be too careful\" so that the agent balances caution against customer satisfaction","Ask the agent to escalate every refund to a human so that no refund decision is ever wrong"],"correct":[0],"explanation":"<p>Vague caution produces over-refusal; explicit conditions define when refunds are allowed. Removing guidance or adding vague counterweights doesn't define the boundary; escalating everything defeats automation.</p>","scenario":"S1","domain":4},{"id":"a4-crit-07","skill":"4.1","type":"single","stem":"<p>How can you measure whether new explicit criteria improved a review agent?</p>","options":["Count how many findings it produces per PR and aim for as few findings as possible","Ask the agent to rate the quality of its own findings before and after the change","Track precision per category (and recall on known issues) on a labelled set of diffs","Survey developers once a year about whether the review comments feel useful"],"correct":[2],"explanation":"<p>Per-category precision and recall on labelled data show whether criteria work. Fewer findings may mean missed bugs; self-ratings are unreliable; annual surveys are too slow.</p>","scenario":"S5","domain":4},{"id":"a4-crit-08","skill":"4.1","type":"single","stem":"<p>Why does explaining <em>why</em> a rule exists (\"we skip style because the linter enforces it\") help Claude?</p>","options":["Because Claude ignores any rule that is stated without a justification attached to it","Because explanations make the prompt longer, which increases the model's attention to it","Because explanations are required for rules to be cached by prompt caching mechanisms","Because knowing the purpose helps Claude generalise the rule correctly to unlisted cases"],"correct":[3],"explanation":"<p>Context about purpose helps Claude apply rules sensibly to new cases. Claude doesn't ignore unexplained rules; length isn't the mechanism; caching doesn't require explanations.</p>","scenario":"S6","domain":4},{"id":"a4-shot-01","skill":"4.2","type":"single","stem":"<p>Your support agent handles clear cases well but is inconsistent on borderline ones (e.g. a product 40 days old when the refund window is 30 days but warranty is one year). What prompt change helps most?</p>","options":["Add ten examples of clear-cut refund approvals and denials to the system prompt","Add 2–4 examples of borderline cases that show the decision and the reasoning behind it","Add a rule that all borderline cases must be escalated to a human supervisor","Increase the temperature so that the agent considers more options on borderline cases"],"correct":[1],"explanation":"<p>Targeted examples of ambiguous cases with reasoning show where the boundary lies. Clear-cut examples don't teach the boundary; escalating everything defeats automation; higher temperature adds variance.</p>","scenario":"S1","domain":4},{"id":"a4-shot-02","skill":"4.2","type":"single","stem":"<p>Extraction from research papers misses methodology details when they appear in tables rather than in prose. What is the best fix?</p>","options":["Add an instruction to \"read the whole document carefully, including all tables\"","Convert every paper to plain text with tables removed before running extraction","Add few-shot examples covering papers with methodology in prose, in a section and in tables","Increase <code>max_tokens</code> so that the extraction output has room for more methodology detail"],"correct":[2],"explanation":"<p>Examples that cover varied structures teach Claude how to handle each layout. Generic exhortations are weak; removing tables removes the information; output length isn't the issue.</p>","scenario":"S6","domain":4},{"id":"a4-shot-03","skill":"4.2","type":"single","stem":"<p>An extraction prompt causes Claude to invent publication dates when none are present. Which few-shot example is most valuable to add?</p>","options":["An example with a clearly visible publication date extracted correctly in ISO format","An example where the date appears in two different formats on the first page of the paper","An example where the date is in the footer instead of the header of the document","An example where no date is present and the output correctly returns <code>null</code> for it"],"correct":[3],"explanation":"<p>Demonstrating the \"absent → null\" behaviour directly targets fabrication. Examples with dates present don't teach what to do when they're missing.</p>","scenario":"S6","domain":4},{"id":"a4-shot-04","skill":"4.2","type":"single","stem":"<p>A code-review agent flags an internal error-handling pattern that the team intentionally uses. How can few-shot examples help?</p>","options":["Show an example of the accepted pattern labelled as acceptable, alongside a genuinely problematic variant","Show ten examples of real bugs so that the agent focuses only on problems that are severe","Show the pattern in a negative example so that the agent learns to flag it even more consistently","Few-shot examples cannot help; the pattern must be removed from the codebase entirely instead"],"correct":[0],"explanation":"<p>Contrasting acceptable local patterns with genuine issues teaches the distinction. Unrelated bug examples or negative framing don't address this; changing the codebase is unnecessary.</p>","scenario":"S5","domain":4},{"id":"a4-shot-05","skill":"4.2","type":"single","stem":"<p>How many few-shot examples are typically recommended for steering a specific judgement?</p>","options":["Exactly one, since more examples always cause Claude to copy their surface form","A small, targeted set — roughly two to four — chosen for the ambiguous cases","At least twenty, so that every possible input variation is represented once","As many as fit into the context window, to maximise the guidance available"],"correct":[1],"explanation":"<p>A few well-chosen examples usually suffice; more add tokens with diminishing returns. One may not cover variation; dozens are costly and unnecessary.</p>","scenario":"S6","domain":4},{"id":"a4-shot-06","skill":"4.2","type":"single","stem":"<p>Why do few-shot examples that include reasoning generalise better than examples that only show labels?</p>","options":["Because reasoning text is cached, so later requests with similar inputs are processed faster","Because labels alone are ignored by Claude unless they are wrapped in XML example tags","Because the reasoning shows which features drove the decision, so Claude applies them to new cases","Because reasoning makes the examples longer, which gives them more weight in the prompt"],"correct":[2],"explanation":"<p>Reasoning communicates the decision criteria, enabling transfer to novel cases. The other mechanisms are not how few-shot works.</p>","scenario":"S1","domain":4},{"id":"a4-shot-07","skill":"4.2","type":"single","stem":"<p>A research agent's summaries vary widely in structure. Which technique most directly improves format consistency?</p>","options":["Ask the agent to use \"a professional and consistent format\" in every summary it writes","Lower <code>max_tokens</code> so that the summaries cannot vary much in their overall length","Add a post-processing step that reformats each summary into the desired structure","Include one or two example summaries in exactly the desired structure"],"correct":[3],"explanation":"<p>Concrete examples demonstrate the target format. Vague style requests are weak; shorter outputs aren't more consistent; post-processing hides the problem.</p>","scenario":"S3","domain":4},{"id":"a4-shot-08","skill":"4.2","type":"single","stem":"<p>When is fixing a tool's description preferable to adding few-shot examples?</p>","options":["When the agent's output format is inconsistent between runs for the same tool","When the agent makes inconsistent judgements on borderline business cases","When the agent fabricates values for fields that are missing in documents","When the agent confuses two tools because their purposes are described ambiguously"],"correct":[3],"explanation":"<p>Tool confusion is a description problem; fix the root cause first. The other symptoms are classic few-shot (or schema) use cases.</p>","scenario":"S2","domain":4},{"id":"a4-schema-01","skill":"4.3","type":"single","stem":"<p>Your extraction pipeline uses a tool with a JSON schema and a forced <code>tool_choice</code>. Outputs always parse, but some invoices have line items that don't sum to the stated total. What does this show?</p>","options":["The schema is misconfigured, because a correct schema would prevent arithmetic inconsistencies","The forced <code>tool_choice</code> causes these errors; switching to <code>auto</code> would improve accuracy","Schemas eliminate syntax and shape errors but not semantic errors, which need validation in code","The model's temperature is too high, which causes random arithmetic errors in extraction"],"correct":[2],"explanation":"<p>Schema enforcement guarantees structure, not correctness of values. Semantic checks (sums, date order) belong in validation code. <code>tool_choice</code> and temperature aren't the cause.</p>","scenario":"S6","domain":4},{"id":"a4-schema-02","skill":"4.3","type":"single","stem":"<p>Contracts sometimes lack a renewal date, but your schema marks <code>renewal_date</code> as a required string. Extractions contain plausible but invented dates. What is the best fix?</p>","options":["Add \"never invent dates\" to the system prompt, in capital letters near the schema","Remove <code>renewal_date</code> from the schema and extract it in a separate free-text step","Make <code>renewal_date</code> nullable and describe it as \"null if the contract does not state one\"","Add a default value of \"1970-01-01\" so that missing dates are easy to detect later"],"correct":[2],"explanation":"<p>A required non-nullable field forces a value; nullable fields let the model express absence. Prompt warnings are weaker; free text loses structure; sentinel dates are error-prone.</p>","scenario":"S6","domain":4},{"id":"a4-schema-03","skill":"4.3","type":"single","stem":"<p>A <code>document_type</code> enum has values [\"invoice\", \"receipt\", \"contract\"]. Occasionally purchase orders arrive and are labelled \"invoice\". What schema change helps most?</p>","options":["Remove the enum and let Claude write any document type as a free-text string","Add a confidence field so that downstream systems can decide whether to trust the label","Add an \"other\" value with a <code>document_type_detail</code> string, plus \"unclear\" where needed","Keep the enum and add an instruction that purchase orders should be labelled \"receipt\""],"correct":[2],"explanation":"<p>An escape hatch prevents forcing unexpected inputs into wrong categories while keeping structure. Free text loses consistency; confidence doesn't fix the missing category; mislabelling instructions are wrong.</p>","scenario":"S6","domain":4},{"id":"a4-schema-04","skill":"4.3","type":"single","stem":"<p>Why is tool use with an <code>input_schema</code> more reliable for structured output than asking for JSON in the prompt?</p>","options":["Because tool use runs on a different model that is specially trained for JSON output","Because the schema constrains the call's shape, so required fields and types are respected","Because tool calls are free of charge, so the output can be retried until it parses","Because prompts are not allowed to contain JSON examples when tools are configured"],"correct":[1],"explanation":"<p>Tool schemas (and structured outputs / strict mode) constrain structure. It's the same model, tool calls cost tokens, and prompts may contain JSON.</p>","scenario":"S6","domain":4},{"id":"a4-schema-05","skill":"4.3","type":"single","stem":"<p>Extracted dates come back as \"March 3, 2026\", \"03/03/26\" and \"2026-03-03\". How can the schema design improve consistency?</p>","options":["Use a separate tool for each date format and let Claude choose the matching tool","Add more date fields so that each format has its own dedicated field in the output","Use type \"number\" for dates so that Claude must output Unix timestamps every time","Specify the format in the field description, e.g. \"ISO 8601 (YYYY-MM-DD)\", and validate it"],"correct":[3],"explanation":"<p>Format instructions in field descriptions plus validation produce consistent values. Multiple tools or fields complicate the schema; numeric timestamps are error-prone for models.</p>","scenario":"S6","domain":4},{"id":"a4-schema-06","skill":"4.3","type":"single","stem":"<p>Which <code>tool_choice</code> setting ensures a specific extraction tool is used when there is exactly one schema and every input is a document?</p>","options":["<code>{\"type\": \"auto\"}</code>","<code>{\"type\": \"none\"}</code>","<code>{\"type\": \"tool\", \"name\": \"record_invoice\"}</code>","No <code>tool_choice</code>; just list the tool first"],"correct":[2],"explanation":"<p>Forcing the specific tool guarantees it is called. <code>auto</code> allows text replies, <code>none</code> disables tools, and tool order doesn't force anything.</p>","scenario":"S6","domain":4},{"id":"a4-schema-07","skill":"4.3","type":"single","stem":"<p>A field <code>vendor_vat_id</code> is often absent. The schema marks it optional (not in <code>required</code>). Some outputs omit the key, others return an empty string. Which design is clearer for downstream systems?</p>","options":["Keep it optional and let downstream systems treat missing keys and empty strings the same way","Remove the field, since it is usually absent and therefore not worth extracting at all","Make it required as a string and instruct Claude to write \"N/A\" when it isn't present","Make it required but nullable, and describe it as \"null when no VAT ID is shown\""],"correct":[3],"explanation":"<p>Required-but-nullable makes absence explicit and consistent (<code>null</code>). Mixed missing/empty values complicate consumers; \"N/A\" strings are ambiguous; dropping the field loses data.</p>","scenario":"S6","domain":4},{"id":"a4-schema-08","skill":"4.3","type":"single","stem":"<p>After moving to schema-based extraction, a team proposes removing all validation code because \"the schema guarantees correct output\". What is the right response?</p>","options":["Keep semantic validation; schemas guarantee structure, not that values are correct or consistent","Agree, because the schema also checks that values match the source document exactly","Agree, but keep a small sample of manual reviews purely for compliance documentation","Disagree, because schemas often produce malformed JSON that needs repairing"],"correct":[0],"explanation":"<p>Schemas constrain format; values can still be wrong. Validation remains necessary. Schemas don't check against the source and don't routinely produce malformed JSON.</p>","scenario":"S6","domain":4},{"id":"a4-retry-01","skill":"4.4","type":"single","stem":"<p>Validation detects that extracted line items sum to $1,240 but <code>stated_total</code> is $1,420. What should the retry request contain?</p>","options":["Only the instruction \"Try again and be more careful with the numbers this time.\"","The document, the failed extraction and the specific error describing the mismatch","A fresh request with the document only, so that the model isn't biased by its previous answer","The document with all numbers removed except the total, to simplify the extraction task"],"correct":[1],"explanation":"<p>Specific error feedback with the original and failed output lets Claude target the correction. Generic retries repeat mistakes; fresh requests lose the diagnostic; removing numbers destroys data.</p>","scenario":"S6","domain":4},{"id":"a4-retry-02","skill":"4.4","type":"single","stem":"<p>A small share of extractions keep failing validation after two retries because the required value appears only in an external document that is not provided. What should the pipeline do?</p>","options":["Retry up to ten times, since the model may eventually find the missing value somewhere","Fill the field with the most common value seen in other documents of the same type","Stop retrying, set the field to null or flag it, and route the document to human review","Ask Claude to estimate the value based on similar documents it has processed before"],"correct":[2],"explanation":"<p>Retries can't recover information absent from the input. Route such cases to review rather than looping or fabricating.</p>","scenario":"S6","domain":4},{"id":"a4-retry-03","skill":"4.4","type":"single","stem":"<p>Which schema addition helps code detect arithmetic inconsistencies in invoices?</p>","options":["A <code>confidence</code> field for the total, reported by Claude between 0 and 1","A <code>calculated_total</code> field next to <code>stated_total</code>, compared in validation","A <code>notes</code> free-text field where Claude can mention any concerns it has","A <code>currency_symbol</code> field so that totals are always interpreted correctly"],"correct":[1],"explanation":"<p>Self-correction fields make inconsistencies programmatically detectable. Confidence and notes are unstructured or uncalibrated; currency symbols don't reveal arithmetic issues.</p>","scenario":"S6","domain":4},{"id":"a4-retry-04","skill":"4.4","type":"single","stem":"<p>Developers dismiss many review findings, but you don't know which kinds. Which output field helps you improve the prompt systematically?</p>","options":["A <code>detected_pattern</code> (rule ID) on each finding, so dismissals can be aggregated by pattern","A <code>severity</code> field, so that developers can filter out low-severity findings themselves","A <code>timestamp</code> field, so that you can see when each finding was generated by the job","An <code>explanation</code> field with several paragraphs of reasoning for each finding reported"],"correct":[0],"explanation":"<p>Tagging findings with the pattern that produced them lets you analyse dismissals by pattern and fix the worst criteria. The other fields don't reveal which rules misfire.</p>","scenario":"S5","domain":4},{"id":"a4-retry-05","skill":"4.4","type":"single","stem":"<p>A document genuinely contains two different due dates (one in the header, one in the payment terms). How should the extraction handle this?</p>","options":["Pick the later date, since payment terms are usually more authoritative than headers","Average the two dates so that the result lies between the conflicting values","Retry until the model returns the same date twice in a row for the document","Set <code>conflict_detected: true</code>, capture both values with their locations, and route to review"],"correct":[3],"explanation":"<p>Genuine source conflicts should be surfaced, not silently resolved. Picking, averaging or retrying until consistent hides a real discrepancy.</p>","scenario":"S6","domain":4},{"id":"a4-retry-06","skill":"4.4","type":"single","stem":"<p>Your validation code finds a mismatch and \"fixes\" it by overwriting <code>stated_total</code> with the calculated sum. Why is this problematic?</p>","options":["It slows down the pipeline because every invoice must be recalculated twice","It hides real discrepancies in documents that may need human attention","It violates the JSON schema, because <code>stated_total</code> must be a string","It causes the model to ignore line items in future extractions"],"correct":[1],"explanation":"<p>Silently correcting values masks document errors (e.g. missing credit notes). Flag and review instead. The other effects aren't accurate.</p>","scenario":"S6","domain":4},{"id":"a4-retry-07","skill":"4.4","type":"single","stem":"<p>Which error type is most likely to be resolved by a retry with specific feedback?</p>","options":["A value that is not present anywhere in the provided document text","A value that is only available in a related document the system does not have","A digit misread from a table that is clearly legible in the document text","A value that the document intentionally leaves blank for the customer to fill"],"correct":[2],"explanation":"<p>Reading or placement errors are correctable when the information is present. Absent or intentionally blank values can't be recovered by retrying.</p>","scenario":"S6","domain":4},{"id":"a4-retry-08","skill":"4.4","type":"single","stem":"<p>How many retries should a validation–retry loop typically allow?</p>","options":["Unlimited retries, because every extraction will eventually pass validation","No retries; any validation failure should go directly to human review","A small cap (e.g. one or two), after which the item is routed to review","Exactly ten retries, the number required by the API for structured outputs"],"correct":[2],"explanation":"<p>A small cap captures most correctable errors without wasting cost on unrecoverable ones. Unlimited retries waste money; zero retries forgo cheap fixes; there's no API-mandated number.</p>","scenario":"S6","domain":4},{"id":"a4-batch-01","skill":"4.5","type":"single","stem":"<p>Which workload is the best fit for the Message Batches API?</p>","options":["A pre-merge check that must pass before developers can merge their pull request","An interactive support chat where customers expect replies within a few seconds","A nightly audit of all repositories whose report is read the next morning","An agent that needs several tool calls per item to gather data before answering"],"correct":[2],"explanation":"<p>Batches suit latency-tolerant, non-blocking work. Blocking checks and live chats need synchronous responses; multi-turn tool loops don't run inside a single batch request.</p>","scenario":"S5","domain":4},{"id":"a4-batch-02","skill":"4.5","type":"single","stem":"<p>A batch of 10,000 document extractions completes with 180 failures caused by documents exceeding the context limit. What is the most efficient next step?</p>","options":["Resubmit the entire batch of 10,000 requests with a larger <code>max_tokens</code> value","Chunk the oversized documents and resubmit only the failed <code>custom_id</code>s","Process the 180 documents manually, since batches cannot be submitted twice","Wait 24 hours and resubmit the same 180 requests without any changes"],"correct":[1],"explanation":"<p>Fix the cause and resubmit only failed items, correlated by <code>custom_id</code>. Resubmitting everything pays twice; <code>max_tokens</code> is the output limit, not the input size; unchanged resubmission fails again.</p>","scenario":"S6","domain":4},{"id":"a4-batch-03","skill":"4.5","type":"single","stem":"<p>What are the cost and latency characteristics of the Message Batches API?</p>","options":["90% cheaper than standard calls, with results guaranteed within one hour","50% cheaper than standard calls, processed within up to 24 hours, without a latency SLA","The same price as standard calls, but with higher rate limits and priority processing","50% cheaper than standard calls, with results guaranteed within five minutes"],"correct":[1],"explanation":"<p>Batches cost 50% of standard pricing and complete within up to 24 hours, with no latency guarantee.</p>","scenario":"S6","domain":4},{"id":"a4-batch-04","skill":"4.5","type":"single","stem":"<p>Before submitting 50,000 documents to a batch, what should you do first?</p>","options":["Submit all 50,000 immediately and refine the prompt afterwards based on the results","Split the job into 50,000 single-request batches to isolate any individual failures","Disable validation for the batch, since batch results are always schema-compliant","Refine the prompt on a representative sample with synchronous calls, then submit"],"correct":[3],"explanation":"<p>Iterating on a sample avoids paying for 50,000 poor results. Single-request batches add overhead; batch results still need validation.</p>","scenario":"S6","domain":4},{"id":"a4-batch-05","skill":"4.5","type":"single","stem":"<p>Batch results come back in a different order than the requests were submitted. How should your system match results to documents?</p>","options":["By position in the results file, since batches preserve submission order","By comparing the extracted content to each document and choosing the best match","By the <code>custom_id</code> set on each request, which is returned with its result","By timestamp, since results are returned in the order they were processed"],"correct":[2],"explanation":"<p><code>custom_id</code> is the correlation key; result order is not guaranteed. Content matching and timestamps are unreliable.</p>","scenario":"S6","domain":4},{"id":"a4-batch-06","skill":"4.5","type":"single","stem":"<p>A report built from batch extraction must be ready by 08:00 each day. What scheduling consideration matters most?</p>","options":["Batches always finish within an hour, so submitting at 07:00 is sufficient every day","Processing can take up to 24 hours, so submit early enough to meet the deadline","Batches run only overnight, so submit before midnight and results arrive by morning","Batches process in submission order, so put the report's documents at the front"],"correct":[1],"explanation":"<p>Without a latency SLA, plan around the 24-hour window. Most batches finish quickly, but that isn't guaranteed; there's no overnight-only schedule or ordering guarantee.</p>","scenario":"S6","domain":4},{"id":"a4-batch-07","skill":"4.5","type":"single","stem":"<p>A research workflow requires Claude to call search tools several times per question and then answer. Why is it a poor fit for a single batch request per question?</p>","options":["Batches don't support system prompts, which the research workflow requires","Batches can contain at most 100 requests, too few for a research workload","Each request is a single call; tools can't be run and continued within it","Batches reject requests that define tools, so the research tools can't be used"],"correct":[2],"explanation":"<p>A batch request is a single call; multi-turn tool loops require your code to run tools and send follow-up requests. System prompts and tool definitions are allowed; batches hold up to 100,000 requests.</p>","scenario":"S3","domain":4},{"id":"a4-batch-08","skill":"4.5","type":"single","stem":"<p>What can further reduce cost for a large batch whose requests all share the same long system prompt?</p>","options":["Combine the batch with prompt caching of the shared prefix","Remove the system prompt and repeat it in every user message","Split the batch into many small batches of one request each","Increase <code>max_tokens</code> so that fewer requests are needed overall"],"correct":[0],"explanation":"<p>Prompt caching of the shared prefix stacks with batch discounts. The other options increase cost or don't affect it.</p>","scenario":"S5","domain":4},{"id":"a4-review-01","skill":"4.6","type":"single","stem":"<p>Claude generated a feature and is then asked, in the same session, to review it. The review rarely finds issues that later surface in production. What is the best improvement?</p>","options":["Ask the same session to review the code three times and combine the findings","Enable extended thinking for the same session's review to make it deeper","Use an independent instance that lacks the generator's reasoning","Ask the same session to assume the role of a senior engineer before reviewing"],"correct":[2],"explanation":"<p>Self-review in the same context tends to confirm earlier reasoning. An independent reviewer evaluates on the merits. Repetition, thinking and role prompts in the same context help only marginally.</p>","scenario":"S5","domain":4},{"id":"a4-review-02","skill":"4.6","type":"single","stem":"<p>An extraction system outputs a confidence score per field. The team wants to auto-accept fields above 0.9. What must happen first?</p>","options":["Nothing; model confidence scores are calibrated probabilities by design","Ask Claude to be more honest about its confidence in the system prompt","Round all scores to one decimal place so that the threshold applies cleanly","Validate the scores against a labelled set to check that 0.9 really means ~90% correct"],"correct":[3],"explanation":"<p>Self-reported confidence must be calibrated against ground truth before it gates decisions. It isn't calibrated by default; prompting for honesty doesn't calibrate it.</p>","scenario":"S6","domain":4},{"id":"a4-review-03","skill":"4.6","type":"single","stem":"<p>Per-file review passes are thorough, but a change to a shared type breaks another module unnoticed. Which addition solves this?</p>","options":["A second per-file pass with stricter criteria for every changed file","A cross-file integration pass focused on contracts and data flow between files","A style pass that checks consistent naming across all changed files","A pass by the generating session to confirm its changes are consistent"],"correct":[1],"explanation":"<p>Integration passes examine interactions per-file passes can't see. Stricter per-file passes, style passes or self-confirmation don't look across modules.</p>","scenario":"S5","domain":4},{"id":"a4-review-04","skill":"4.6","type":"single","stem":"<p>Why does an independent reviewer instance catch more issues than the generating session?</p>","options":["It lacks the generator's reasoning, so it does not simply confirm that reasoning","It uses a newer model version than the generating session by default in Claude Code","It has access to more tools than the generating session, including web search","It runs with a higher temperature, which makes it explore more possible issues"],"correct":[0],"explanation":"<p>Separation from the generator's context avoids confirmation of its own reasoning. Model version, tools and temperature are not the mechanism.</p>","scenario":"S5","domain":4},{"id":"a4-review-05","skill":"4.6","type":"single","stem":"<p>A security-sensitive codebase needs both a correctness review and a security review. Which design is most effective?</p>","options":["One pass with a long prompt combining correctness and security criteria together","A security pass only, since correctness issues are caught by the test suite anyway","Separate focused passes with their own criteria for correctness and for security","Two identical general passes whose findings are merged and de-duplicated afterwards"],"correct":[2],"explanation":"<p>Focus-specific passes with tailored criteria reduce dilution and improve depth. Combined prompts dilute; tests don't catch everything; identical passes add little.</p>","scenario":"S5","domain":4},{"id":"a4-review-06","skill":"4.6","type":"single","stem":"<p>How can calibrated field-level confidence be used in a review architecture?</p>","options":["To decide which model to use for the next document in the batch","To replace semantic validation, since confident fields are always correct","To shorten prompts by removing instructions for high-confidence fields","To route low-confidence fields to human review and auto-accept the rest"],"correct":[3],"explanation":"<p>Calibrated confidence is a router for human attention. It doesn't replace validation, choose models or shorten prompts.</p>","scenario":"S6","domain":4},{"id":"a4-review-07","skill":"4.6","type":"single","stem":"<p>A research report is written by one agent. Which review setup best catches unsupported claims?</p>","options":["Ask the writing agent to append \"all claims verified\" after re-reading the report","Have an independent agent check each claim against the cited sources","Ask the writing agent to rate its confidence in each paragraph from 1 to 5","Have the coordinator count the number of citations per section of the report"],"correct":[1],"explanation":"<p>Independent claim-by-claim verification against sources catches unsupported statements. Self-attestation, self-rating and citation counts don't verify content.</p>","scenario":"S3","domain":4},{"id":"a4-review-08","skill":"4.6","type":"single","stem":"<p>A team runs the same review prompt on the same session twice and merges results, hoping to improve recall. Why is this less effective than an independent instance?</p>","options":["Because merging findings from two runs always creates duplicates that confuse developers","Because running a review twice doubles the cost without changing the findings at all","Because both runs share the same context and reasoning, so they tend to miss the same issues","Because the API rejects identical prompts sent twice to the same conversation history"],"correct":[2],"explanation":"<p>Runs in the same context share blind spots. An independent instance offers a genuinely different perspective. The other claims are inaccurate.</p>","scenario":"S5","domain":4},{"id":"a5-ctx-01","skill":"5.1","type":"single","stem":"<p>In long support conversations, your agent summarises the history every 10 turns. Late in conversations it sometimes confirms the wrong refund amount or date that was agreed earlier. What is the best fix?</p>","options":["Summarise less often, e.g. every 20 turns, so that fewer details are lost per summary","Switch to a model with a larger context window so that summarisation is rarely needed","Keep a persistent case-facts block with exact amounts, dates and IDs, re-sent every turn","Ask the summariser to \"preserve all important details\" when it condenses the history"],"correct":[2],"explanation":"<p>Progressive summaries drop exact values; a persistent, verbatim facts block keeps them. Less frequent summaries and bigger windows delay but don't prevent the loss; asking the summariser is unreliable.</p>","scenario":"S1","domain":5},{"id":"a5-ctx-02","skill":"5.1","type":"single","stem":"<p>An aggregated research input of 80,000 tokens contains a key finding in the middle that the synthesis agent often overlooks. What change helps most?</p>","options":["Put a summary of key findings at the start and organise the rest under clear section headers","Repeat the full input twice so that every finding appears in more than one position","Remove the beginning and end of the input so that the middle becomes more prominent","Ask the synthesis agent to read the input more carefully before writing its report"],"correct":[0],"explanation":"<p>The lost-in-the-middle effect is mitigated by placing key information at the start and structuring the input. Duplicating doubles cost; cutting content loses information; exhortation is weak.</p>","scenario":"S3","domain":5},{"id":"a5-ctx-03","skill":"5.1","type":"single","stem":"<p><code>lookup_order</code> returns 40 fields per order, but the agent needs only status, items, amount, delivery date and tracking. Conversations hit context limits. What should you do?</p>","options":["Ask the agent to ignore irrelevant fields when it reads the order lookup results","Trim the result to the needed fields in the tool or a PostToolUse hook","Summarise the conversation more often so that the order data takes up less space","Call <code>lookup_order</code> less frequently by caching the full result in the conversation"],"correct":[1],"explanation":"<p>Trimming tool output prevents context bloat at the source. Asking the model to ignore fields still costs tokens; summarising more often loses facts; caching full results keeps the bloat.</p>","scenario":"S1","domain":5},{"id":"a5-ctx-04","skill":"5.1","type":"single","stem":"<p>Subagents return long prose summaries, and the coordinator's context fills quickly, causing later results to be handled poorly. Which change helps most?</p>","options":["Let subagents write directly into the final report so the coordinator never sees them","Increase the number of subagents so each one returns a shorter summary on average","Have subagents return compact structured records with key facts and source metadata","Ask the coordinator to forget earlier results once it has read the newer results"],"correct":[2],"explanation":"<p>Structured, compact results preserve facts while saving context. Bypassing the coordinator loses control; more subagents add overhead; models can't \"forget\" on request.</p>","scenario":"S3","domain":5},{"id":"a5-ctx-05","skill":"5.1","type":"single","stem":"<p>Where should a support agent's case-facts block be maintained?</p>","options":["Inside the model's own summary, which it updates whenever it notices a new fact","In application code that updates it from tool results and includes it in each request","In the customer's messages, by asking them to restate key facts in every message","In CLAUDE.md, so that the facts are loaded for every conversation with the agent"],"correct":[1],"explanation":"<p>Application-maintained state is reliable and verbatim. Model-maintained summaries can drift; customers shouldn't repeat facts; CLAUDE.md is for standing project instructions, not per-case data.</p>","scenario":"S1","domain":5},{"id":"a5-ctx-06","skill":"5.1","type":"single","stem":"<p>A long code-review session must keep track of the list of confirmed issues and their file locations. What preserves them best across context compaction?</p>","options":["Recording them in a findings file or facts block kept outside the summarised history","Relying on <code>/compact</code> to keep every issue, since compaction never drops specific details","Asking Claude to repeat the full issue list at the end of every one of its responses","Restarting the session after each issue so that the context never needs to be compacted"],"correct":[0],"explanation":"<p>Persisting findings outside the history protects them from lossy compression. Compaction can drop details; repeating lists bloats context; restarting loses working state.</p>","scenario":"S4","domain":5},{"id":"a5-ctx-07","skill":"5.1","type":"single","stem":"<p>Why is progressive summarisation especially risky for research involving statistics?</p>","options":["Summaries are always longer than the original text, increasing token costs","Summarisation removes all sources, so no citations can ever be recovered","Summarisation is only supported for prose, not for numbers or tables","Summaries tend to compress exact figures, dates and qualifiers into vague statements"],"correct":[3],"explanation":"<p>Numbers, dates and qualifiers are exactly what summaries lose. Summaries are usually shorter; sources may survive if structured; summarisation isn't restricted to prose.</p>","scenario":"S3","domain":5},{"id":"a5-esc-01","skill":"5.2","type":"single","stem":"<p>A customer writes: \"I don't want to talk to a bot. Get me a human now.\" The issue is a simple address change the agent could complete. What should the agent do?</p>","options":["Complete the address change quickly first, then offer to connect a human if still needed","Explain that the agent can handle this request and ask the customer to give it a try","Escalate to a human immediately, passing a structured summary of the request","Ask the customer to rate their frustration from 1 to 10 before deciding on escalation"],"correct":[2],"explanation":"<p>Explicit requests for a human should be honoured immediately. Attempting to resolve first or persuading the customer ignores the request; rating frustration is irrelevant.</p>","scenario":"S1","domain":5},{"id":"a5-esc-02","skill":"5.2","type":"single","stem":"<p><code>get_customer</code> returns two accounts named \"Jordan Lee\". What should the agent do?</p>","options":["Choose the account with the most recent order, since that is most likely to be the customer","Choose the first account in the results, since the search ranks the best match first","Ask the customer for an additional identifier such as email or order number before acting","Proceed with both accounts and apply any change to each of them to be safe"],"correct":[2],"explanation":"<p>Ambiguous matches require clarification; acting on the wrong account is harmful. Heuristic selection or changing both accounts risks errors.</p>","scenario":"S1","domain":5},{"id":"a5-esc-03","skill":"5.2","type":"single","stem":"<p>A team proposes routing conversations to humans whenever sentiment analysis detects strong negative emotion. What is the main problem?</p>","options":["Sentiment analysis requires a separate model, which adds latency to every conversation","Sentiment doesn't measure complexity or policy fit, so escalations get misrouted","Customers with negative sentiment should never be escalated because humans are expensive","Sentiment analysis cannot run on text that contains order numbers or product names"],"correct":[1],"explanation":"<p>Escalation should follow need — explicit requests, policy gaps, lack of progress — not mood. Latency isn't the main issue; the other claims are wrong.</p>","scenario":"S1","domain":5},{"id":"a5-esc-04","skill":"5.2","type":"single","stem":"<p>A customer asks the agent to match a competitor's lower price. Policy covers price adjustments for your own price drops but says nothing about competitors. What should the agent do?</p>","options":["Refuse, because the policy does not mention competitor price matching at all","Offer a discount equal to the price difference, since customer satisfaction is the goal","Ask the customer to provide proof of the price and then apply the match automatically","Escalate as a policy gap with a structured handoff, without promising an outcome"],"correct":[3],"explanation":"<p>Situations the policy doesn't cover are policy gaps — escalate. Refusing or improvising a discount both exceed the agent's mandate.</p>","scenario":"S1","domain":5},{"id":"a5-esc-05","skill":"5.2","type":"single","stem":"<p>Which escalation trigger is the least reliable?</p>","options":["The customer explicitly asks to speak with a human agent","The request requires an exception that the policy does not cover","The agent has tried the available tools and cannot make progress","The agent's self-reported confidence falls below a fixed threshold"],"correct":[3],"explanation":"<p>Self-reported confidence is poorly calibrated and can be high exactly when the agent is wrong. The other triggers are reliable signals.</p>","scenario":"S1","domain":5},{"id":"a5-esc-06","skill":"5.2","type":"single","stem":"<p>A customer writes \"cancel it\". They have two open orders. What is the correct behaviour?</p>","options":["Cancel the most recent order, since customers usually refer to their latest purchase","Ask the customer which of the two orders they mean before cancelling anything","Cancel both orders, since the customer clearly wants to cancel their purchase","Escalate to a human, because cancellation requests always need human approval"],"correct":[1],"explanation":"<p>Ambiguous requests with consequential actions require clarification. Guessing or cancelling both risks harm; escalating every cancellation is unnecessary.</p>","scenario":"S1","domain":5},{"id":"a5-esc-07","skill":"5.2","type":"single","stem":"<p>How should escalation criteria be communicated to the support agent?</p>","options":["As explicit conditions in the system prompt, with a few examples of borderline cases","As a single instruction to \"escalate when appropriate, using your best judgement\"","As a sentiment threshold that the agent evaluates on every incoming message","As a list of keywords that trigger escalation whenever they appear in a message"],"correct":[0],"explanation":"<p>Explicit criteria plus borderline examples give consistent behaviour. Vague judgement is inconsistent; sentiment and keywords are poor proxies.</p>","scenario":"S1","domain":5},{"id":"a5-prop-01","skill":"5.3","type":"single","stem":"<p>A web-search subagent times out. It currently catches the exception and returns an empty result list marked as success. What is the main risk?</p>","options":["The coordinator will retry too often, increasing the overall cost of the research run","The coordinator concludes no sources exist and produces a confidently incomplete report","The synthesis agent will crash because it cannot process an empty list of findings","The search API will block the account because of repeated timeouts in a short period"],"correct":[1],"explanation":"<p>Silent suppression makes failures look like valid empty results, leading to wrong conclusions. The other consequences are not the main risk.</p>","scenario":"S3","domain":5},{"id":"a5-prop-02","skill":"5.3","type":"single","stem":"<p>One of five subagents fails after retries. Which coordinator behaviour is best?</p>","options":["Abort the entire research run and report an error, since the result would be incomplete","Ignore the failure and synthesise from the four successful results without mentioning it","Continue with the available results, try an alternative if possible, and annotate the coverage gap","Restart all five subagents from the beginning so that the results are consistent"],"correct":[2],"explanation":"<p>Graceful degradation keeps valid work and states its limits. Aborting discards work; ignoring hides gaps; restarting everything wastes cost.</p>","scenario":"S3","domain":5},{"id":"a5-prop-03","skill":"5.3","type":"single","stem":"<p>What should a subagent include when it propagates an unresolved failure to the coordinator?</p>","options":["Only the exception class name, so that the coordinator's context stays as small as possible","The failure type, the attempted query, any partial results and possible alternative approaches","The full stack trace and raw HTTP response, so the coordinator can debug the failure itself","A recommendation to abort, since subagents know best whether the run can still succeed"],"correct":[1],"explanation":"<p>Structured context lets the coordinator decide on recovery. Bare names are too little; raw traces are noise; the coordinator decides, not the subagent.</p>","scenario":"S3","domain":5},{"id":"a5-prop-04","skill":"5.3","type":"single","stem":"<p>A file-analysis subagent hits a permission error on one directory. Where should the decision about what to do next be made?</p>","options":["In the subagent, which should silently skip the directory and continue with the others","In the subagent, which should stop the whole system because permissions are critical","In the tool, which should automatically elevate its permissions and retry the read","In the coordinator, after the subagent reports the error with context and partial results"],"correct":[3],"explanation":"<p>Non-recoverable errors propagate with context so the coordinator can choose (skip and annotate, escalate, request access). Silent skipping hides gaps; stopping everything overreacts; auto-elevation is unsafe.</p>","scenario":"S4","domain":5},{"id":"a5-prop-05","skill":"5.3","type":"single","stem":"<p>Which errors should a subagent typically resolve locally before propagating?</p>","options":["Transient failures such as timeouts, which may succeed on retry with backoff","Permission failures, by trying alternative credentials stored in its environment","Business-rule violations, by adjusting the request until the rule no longer applies","All errors, so that the coordinator never has to handle any failures at all"],"correct":[0],"explanation":"<p>Transient errors are good candidates for local retry. Permission and business errors should propagate; hiding all errors from the coordinator removes its ability to decide.</p>","scenario":"S3","domain":5},{"id":"a5-prop-06","skill":"5.3","type":"single","stem":"<p>A final research report is produced although regulatory data for one region could not be retrieved. What should the report include?</p>","options":["Nothing about the gap, since mentioning it would reduce readers' trust in the report","Estimated figures for the missing region based on neighbouring regions' data","A coverage note stating which data is missing and why, so readers can judge the limits","A recommendation to discard the report and wait for the data to become available"],"correct":[2],"explanation":"<p>Coverage annotations make limitations transparent. Hiding gaps or inventing figures misleads; discarding valid work is unnecessary.</p>","scenario":"S3","domain":5},{"id":"a5-prop-07","skill":"5.3","type":"single","stem":"<p>In a support system, the shipping-status subagent fails while billing and account subagents succeed. What is the best response to the customer?</p>","options":["Apologise that the system is down and ask the customer to try again later for everything","Answer billing and account, and explain that shipping status is unavailable for now","Answer the billing and account parts and state that the shipment is on its way as expected","Escalate the whole conversation to a human because one part of the request failed"],"correct":[1],"explanation":"<p>Partial results with honest communication about the failure serve the customer best. Failing everything wastes valid answers; guessing shipping status is fabrication; full escalation is unnecessary.</p>","scenario":"S1","domain":5},{"id":"a5-code-01","skill":"5.4","type":"single","stem":"<p>After two hours of exploring a legacy codebase, Claude starts describing \"typical repository patterns\" instead of the specific classes it read earlier, and contradicts earlier findings. What is happening, and what helps most?</p>","options":["The model is overloaded by the request rate; pause for a few minutes and then continue","The codebase is too complex for Claude; switch to manual exploration for the remaining work","Context degradation; keep a findings file, delegate searches and summarise per phase","The session's tools have expired; restart Claude Code and repeat the last question again"],"correct":[2],"explanation":"<p>Long sessions accumulate noise and degrade. Scratchpad files, subagent delegation and phase summaries counter it. Pausing, giving up or restarting without preserving findings don't address it.</p>","scenario":"S4","domain":5},{"id":"a5-code-02","skill":"5.4","type":"single","stem":"<p>A multi-hour migration analysis might be interrupted by a crash or a forced context reset. Which practice lets the work resume efficiently?</p>","options":["Rely on the session history, which always restores every finding after a crash","Keep all findings in the conversation and avoid writing anything to the disk","Run the analysis in a single very long response so that it cannot be interrupted","Maintain a manifest of completed steps, findings and next steps as you go"],"correct":[3],"explanation":"<p>A recovery manifest captures state outside the context, enabling resumption. Session history may be incomplete or stale; single responses can't contain multi-hour work.</p>","scenario":"S4","domain":5},{"id":"a5-code-03","skill":"5.4","type":"single","stem":"<p>When is the best moment to run <code>/compact</code> during a long exploration?</p>","options":["At random intervals, so that the context never grows beyond a fixed size","At a phase boundary, with instructions about which findings must be kept","Never; compaction always destroys too much information to be useful","After every tool call, so that the context only contains the latest result"],"correct":[1],"explanation":"<p>Deliberate compaction at phase boundaries, guided by what to keep, preserves important information. Random or constant compaction loses context; avoiding it entirely lets degradation build.</p>","scenario":"S4","domain":5},{"id":"a5-code-04","skill":"5.4","type":"single","stem":"<p>Which approach keeps the main session's context focused while surveying how 15 services handle authentication?</p>","options":["Read all authentication-related files from all 15 services into the main session at once","Delegate the survey to subagents (or the Explore subagent) that return concise summaries","Ask Claude to keep its answers shorter so that each service uses less context","Skip the survey and infer authentication patterns from the service names alone"],"correct":[1],"explanation":"<p>Delegation isolates verbose exploration and returns summaries. Reading everything floods context; shorter answers don't reduce tool output; skipping the survey loses information.</p>","scenario":"S4","domain":5},{"id":"a5-code-05","skill":"5.4","type":"single","stem":"<p>Why should Claude write key findings to a file such as <code>NOTES.md</code> during a long refactoring investigation?</p>","options":["So findings survive compaction, resets and new sessions, and can be referenced instead of old context","Because Claude Code automatically loads <code>NOTES.md</code> into the system prompt of every session","Because writing to files is cheaper in tokens than keeping the findings in the conversation","Because files are the only place where Claude can store code snippets during a session"],"correct":[0],"explanation":"<p>A findings file persists outside the context and can be re-read as needed. It isn't auto-loaded like CLAUDE.md (unless imported), and storing in files isn't about token price.</p>","scenario":"S2","domain":5},{"id":"a5-code-06","skill":"5.4","type":"single","stem":"<p>A developer starts a fresh session to continue yesterday's investigation. What is the best way to provide continuity?</p>","options":["Paste the entire previous transcript into the new session as the first message","Ask Claude to remember yesterday's session, since Claude Code keeps long-term memory","Start from scratch and repeat the whole investigation to avoid stale information","Point Claude to the findings file or structured summary written during the previous session"],"correct":[3],"explanation":"<p>A curated summary or findings file gives continuity without stale clutter. Full transcripts bring back noise; Claude doesn't remember past sessions implicitly; redoing everything wastes effort.</p>","scenario":"S4","domain":5},{"id":"a5-code-07","skill":"5.4","type":"single","stem":"<p>Which symptom most directly indicates context degradation rather than a knowledge gap?</p>","options":["Claude doesn't know an internal library that has never appeared in the session","Claude refuses to run a command that is blocked by the permission settings","Claude contradicts specific findings it reported earlier in the same session","Claude asks a clarifying question about an ambiguous requirement in the task"],"correct":[2],"explanation":"<p>Contradicting its own earlier findings signals that earlier context is no longer used well. The other behaviours have different causes.</p>","scenario":"S4","domain":5},{"id":"a5-hrev-01","skill":"5.5","type":"single","stem":"<p>An extraction system reports 97% overall accuracy, and management wants to remove human review. What should you check first?</p>","options":["Whether the 97% figure was computed on the most recent week's documents only","Accuracy per document type and per field, since the aggregate may hide weak segments","Whether reviewers agree with each other, since inter-rater reliability affects accuracy","Whether the model's self-reported confidence is above 0.9 on average across all fields"],"correct":[1],"explanation":"<p>Aggregate accuracy can mask poor performance on specific types or fields. Segment-level validation must come before removing review. The other checks are secondary or misleading.</p>","scenario":"S6","domain":5},{"id":"a5-hrev-02","skill":"5.5","type":"single","stem":"<p>After reducing human review, how should you sample auto-accepted results for ongoing quality monitoring?</p>","options":["Simple random sampling of 1% of all results, regardless of their document type","Only the results with the lowest confidence, since those are the most likely errors","Stratified random sampling across document types and confidence bands","Only results that customers complain about, since these are the real errors"],"correct":[2],"explanation":"<p>Stratified sampling ensures rare and risky segments are monitored. Simple random sampling rarely hits rare types; low-confidence-only sampling misses confident errors; complaints are late and incomplete.</p>","scenario":"S6","domain":5},{"id":"a5-hrev-03","skill":"5.5","type":"single","stem":"<p>How should confidence thresholds for auto-accepting extracted fields be set?</p>","options":["Calibrated per field against a labelled validation set, checking predicted against actual accuracy","Set to 0.9 for every field, since that is the industry standard for automated extraction","Set by asking Claude what threshold it would recommend for each of the fields","Set to the average confidence of the last 100 documents processed by the system"],"correct":[0],"explanation":"<p>Thresholds must reflect measured accuracy, field by field. Fixed or self-recommended thresholds are arbitrary; averages say nothing about correctness.</p>","scenario":"S6","domain":5},{"id":"a5-hrev-04","skill":"5.5","type":"single","stem":"<p>Handwritten forms are 3% of volume but have 75% field accuracy; typed invoices are 90% of volume with 99.5% accuracy. What is the best review policy?</p>","options":["Remove review for everything, since overall accuracy is above 98% when weighted by volume","Keep full review for everything, since any error rate above zero is unacceptable","Remove review for handwritten forms, since they are only a small share of volume","Keep human review for handwritten forms; auto-accept typed invoices with stratified sampling"],"correct":[3],"explanation":"<p>Segment-based policy focuses human effort where errors concentrate. Removing all review lets a quarter of handwritten fields through wrong; full review wastes effort on the reliable segment.</p>","scenario":"S6","domain":5},{"id":"a5-hrev-05","skill":"5.5","type":"single","stem":"<p>What should a human reviewer be shown to review an extraction efficiently?</p>","options":["The full document only, so that the reviewer re-extracts every field independently","The low-confidence or flagged fields with their source excerpts highlighted","The model's complete reasoning transcript for the extraction of the document","Only the final extracted values, without any reference to the source document"],"correct":[1],"explanation":"<p>Highlighting uncertain fields with source evidence focuses reviewer effort. Re-extracting everything is wasteful; transcripts are noise; values without sources can't be verified.</p>","scenario":"S6","domain":5},{"id":"a5-hrev-06","skill":"5.5","type":"single","stem":"<p>A review bot's findings will be auto-posted if the model reports high confidence. Before trusting this, what is needed?</p>","options":["A prompt instruction telling the model to report confidence honestly and conservatively","A rule that confidence must be reported with two decimal places for consistency","A larger model, since larger models always produce calibrated confidence scores","Evidence from labelled data that high-confidence findings really are mostly correct"],"correct":[3],"explanation":"<p>Confidence must be validated against ground truth before it gates actions. Prompting, formatting and model size don't establish calibration.</p>","scenario":"S5","domain":5},{"id":"a5-hrev-07","skill":"5.5","type":"single","stem":"<p>After a prompt change, extraction accuracy on a key field drops, but overall accuracy looks stable. Which monitoring practice would have caught this?</p>","options":["Tracking per-field and per-type accuracy on a labelled sample after changes","Tracking the total number of documents processed per day by the pipeline","Tracking the average response latency of the extraction requests over time","Tracking the overall accuracy figure, since it reflects all fields equally"],"correct":[0],"explanation":"<p>Field-level metrics reveal regressions that aggregates hide. Volume and latency don't measure accuracy.</p>","scenario":"S6","domain":5},{"id":"a5-prov-01","skill":"5.6","type":"single","stem":"<p>Two credible sources report different market sizes for the same year ($4.2B and $3.6B). How should the synthesis agent handle this?</p>","options":["Use the higher figure, since larger reported markets are usually more up to date","Average the two figures and report $3.9B as the best available estimate","Report both values with attribution and note any differences in definition or methodology","Omit the market size entirely, since the sources disagree and neither can be trusted"],"correct":[2],"explanation":"<p>Conflicts should be surfaced with attribution and context, not silently resolved. Picking, averaging or omitting loses information or misleads.</p>","scenario":"S3","domain":5},{"id":"a5-prov-02","skill":"5.6","type":"single","stem":"<p>A report shows the 2022 adoption rate as 35% from one source and 61% from another. Investigation shows the second figure is from 2025. What prevented this from being presented correctly?</p>","options":["The search subagent used too few sources to estimate the adoption rate reliably","Publication and data dates were not preserved, so a trend looked like a contradiction","The synthesis agent's context was too small to hold both figures at the same time","The coordinator should have used a single source to avoid conflicting statistics"],"correct":[1],"explanation":"<p>Carrying dates lets the synthesis present a trend instead of a false conflict. Source count, context size or single-sourcing aren't the issue.</p>","scenario":"S3","domain":5},{"id":"a5-prov-03","skill":"5.6","type":"single","stem":"<p>How should findings be passed from subagents to the synthesis agent to preserve provenance?</p>","options":["As structured records with claim, source, excerpt and date, which the synthesis must cite","As a single prose summary, with a list of all URLs appended at the end of the text","As raw HTML pages, so that the synthesis agent can re-extract everything it needs","As short bullet points without sources, to minimise the synthesis agent's context"],"correct":[0],"explanation":"<p>Claim-source mappings let each statement be traced. Prose with an appended URL list loses which claim came from where; raw pages bloat context; dropping sources loses provenance.</p>","scenario":"S3","domain":5},{"id":"a5-prov-04","skill":"5.6","type":"single","stem":"<p>A report contains a key statistic without a citation. The search subagent had captured its source, but the coordinator passed prose summaries to the synthesis agent. What is the fix?</p>","options":["Ask the synthesis agent to add citations afterwards by searching for the statistic again","Remove all statistics from reports, since they are difficult to cite consistently","Add a final review step that deletes any sentence without a citation from the report","Pass structured findings with source metadata through to synthesis and require claim-level citations"],"correct":[3],"explanation":"<p>Provenance must be preserved end to end. Re-searching may find a different source; removing statistics or sentences loses content.</p>","scenario":"S3","domain":5},{"id":"a5-prov-05","skill":"5.6","type":"single","stem":"<p>A research report mixes quarterly financial figures, news developments and technical findings. How should content be presented?</p>","options":["Everything as prose paragraphs, so the report reads consistently from start to finish","Everything as a single large table, so that all findings can be compared directly","Financial data in tables, news as prose, technical findings as structured lists","Everything as bullet points, so that the report is as short as possible overall"],"correct":[2],"explanation":"<p>Rendering each content type appropriately keeps evidence clear. Forcing one format obscures the nature of the information.</p>","scenario":"S3","domain":5},{"id":"a5-prov-06","skill":"5.6","type":"single","stem":"<p>An extraction pipeline merges data from a contract and its amendment, which disagree on the termination notice period. What should the output contain?</p>","options":["The value from the original contract, since amendments are often not legally binding","Both values with their source documents and dates, flagged as a conflict for review","The shorter notice period, since that is the more conservative choice for the business","No value at all, since conflicting data cannot be extracted reliably by a model"],"correct":[1],"explanation":"<p>Conflicting sources should be attributed and flagged. Choosing by heuristic or dropping the field hides important information.</p>","scenario":"S6","domain":5},{"id":"a5-prov-07","skill":"5.6","type":"single","stem":"<p>Why should reports distinguish well-established findings from contested ones?</p>","options":["Because contested findings must always be removed before a report is published","Because established findings do not need citations, while contested ones do","Because separating them reduces the number of tokens in the final report","Because readers can then weigh conclusions according to the strength of the evidence"],"correct":[3],"explanation":"<p>Signalling evidence strength communicates uncertainty honestly. Contested findings can be valuable, all findings need citations, and token count isn't the point.</p>","scenario":"S3","domain":5}],"glossary":[{"term":"@import","skill":"3.1","html":"CLAUDE.md syntax (<code>@path/to/file.md</code>) that pulls another file into memory, so large instruction sets can be split into focused modules.","text":"CLAUDE.md syntax (`@path/to/file.md`) that pulls another file into memory, so large instruction sets can be split into focused modules."},{"term":"Adaptive decomposition","skill":"1.6","html":"Breaking a task into subtasks at runtime based on what earlier steps discover — suited to open-ended investigation where the subtasks can't be known in advance.","text":"Breaking a task into subtasks at runtime based on what earlier steps discover — suited to open-ended investigation where the subtasks can't be known in advance."},{"term":"AgentDefinition","skill":"1.3","html":"Agent SDK object that defines a subagent: <code>description</code> (when to use it), <code>prompt</code> (its system instructions), <code>tools</code> (its allowed tools) and optionally <code>model</code>.","text":"Agent SDK object that defines a subagent: `description` (when to use it), `prompt` (its system instructions), `tools` (its allowed tools) and optionally `model`."},{"term":"Agent SDK (Claude Agent SDK)","skill":"1.1","html":"Library that exposes Claude Code's agent harness — tools, permissions, hooks, sessions, subagents, MCP — for building your own agents in Python or TypeScript.","text":"Library that exposes Claude Code's agent harness — tools, permissions, hooks, sessions, subagents, MCP — for building your own agents in Python or TypeScript."},{"term":"Agentic loop","skill":"1.1","html":"Request → inspect <code>stop_reason</code> → execute requested tools → append <code>tool_result</code> blocks → repeat until <code>end_turn</code>.","text":"Request → inspect `stop_reason` → execute requested tools → append `tool_result` blocks → repeat until `end_turn`."},{"term":"Aggregate accuracy","skill":"5.5","html":"A single accuracy number across all documents; can hide poor accuracy on specific document types or fields.","text":"A single accuracy number across all documents; can hide poor accuracy on specific document types or fields."},{"term":"allowed-tools","skill":"3.2","html":"Skill/command frontmatter that restricts which tools Claude may use while the skill runs.","text":"Skill/command frontmatter that restricts which tools Claude may use while the skill runs."},{"term":"allowedTools / allowed_tools","skill":"1.3","html":"Agent SDK and Claude Code option listing auto-approved tools; a coordinator needs <code>Task</code> (the Agent tool) in it to spawn subagents.","text":"Agent SDK and Claude Code option listing auto-approved tools; a coordinator needs `Task` (the Agent tool) in it to spawn subagents."},{"term":"Anchor text","skill":"2.5","html":"The exact, unique string the Edit tool replaces; if it occurs more than once the edit fails or needs more surrounding context.","text":"The exact, unique string the Edit tool replaces; if it occurs more than once the edit fails or needs more surrounding context."},{"term":"argument-hint","skill":"3.2","html":"Skill/command frontmatter that tells users what argument to pass, e.g. <code>[path to file]</code>.","text":"Skill/command frontmatter that tells users what argument to pass, e.g. `[path to file]`."},{"term":"$ARGUMENTS","skill":"3.2","html":"Placeholder in a command or skill body that is replaced by the text typed after the slash command.","text":"Placeholder in a command or skill body that is replaced by the text typed after the slash command."},{"term":"Batch interacting issues","skill":"3.5","html":"Reporting related problems in one message so Claude can fix them together, instead of one at a time where each fix may break another.","text":"Reporting related problems in one message so Claude can fix them together, instead of one at a time where each fix may break another."},{"term":"Built-in tools","skill":"2.5","html":"Claude Code / Agent SDK tools such as Read, Write, Edit, Bash, Grep, Glob, WebSearch and WebFetch.","text":"Claude Code / Agent SDK tools such as Read, Write, Edit, Bash, Grep, Glob, WebSearch and WebFetch."},{"term":"Business error","skill":"2.2","html":"A tool failure caused by a policy or rule (e.g. refund exceeds limit); not retryable — explain or escalate.","text":"A tool failure caused by a policy or rule (e.g. refund exceeds limit); not retryable — explain or escalate."},{"term":"Case facts block","skill":"5.1","html":"A persistent, verbatim section of the context holding critical values (amounts, dates, IDs, commitments) that is never summarized away.","text":"A persistent, verbatim section of the context holding critical values (amounts, dates, IDs, commitments) that is never summarized away."},{"term":"Claim–source mapping","skill":"5.6","html":"Structured link from each claim to its source (URL or document, excerpt, date) that survives summarization and synthesis.","text":"Structured link from each claim to its source (URL or document, excerpt, date) that survives summarization and synthesis."},{"term":"CLAUDE.md","skill":"3.1","html":"Markdown memory file that Claude Code loads as standing instructions; exists at user, project and directory level.","text":"Markdown memory file that Claude Code loads as standing instructions; exists at user, project and directory level."},{"term":"Context degradation","skill":"5.4","html":"Declining answer quality in long sessions as the context fills with stale or verbose material; the model starts citing \"typical patterns\" instead of the specific code it read.","text":"Declining answer quality in long sessions as the context fills with stale or verbose material; the model starts citing \"typical patterns\" instead of the specific code it read."},{"term":"context: fork","skill":"3.2","html":"Skill frontmatter that runs the skill in an isolated sub-agent context so its verbose work doesn't pollute the main conversation.","text":"Skill frontmatter that runs the skill in an isolated sub-agent context so its verbose work doesn't pollute the main conversation."},{"term":"Coordinator (lead agent)","skill":"1.2","html":"The agent in a hub-and-spoke system that decomposes the task, delegates to subagents, routes all communication and synthesizes results.","text":"The agent in a hub-and-spoke system that decomposes the task, delegates to subagents, routes all communication and synthesizes results."},{"term":"Coverage annotation","skill":"5.3","html":"A note in a synthesized output stating which parts are incomplete because a source or subagent failed.","text":"A note in a synthesized output stating which parts are incomplete because a source or subagent failed."},{"term":"Coverage gap","skill":"1.2","html":"Part of the question no subagent investigated — usually caused by an overly narrow decomposition by the coordinator.","text":"Part of the question no subagent investigated — usually caused by an overly narrow decomposition by the coordinator."},{"term":"Crash-recovery manifest","skill":"5.4","html":"A file recording completed steps, findings and next steps so a long task can resume after a crash or context reset.","text":"A file recording completed steps, findings and next steps so a long task can resume after a crash or context reset."},{"term":"Cross-file integration pass","skill":"1.6","html":"A review pass that looks at interactions between files (data flow, API contracts) after per-file local passes.","text":"A review pass that looks at interactions between files (data flow, API contracts) after per-file local passes."},{"term":"custom_id","skill":"4.5","html":"Caller-defined identifier on each Message Batches request used to match results (which can arrive in any order) and to resubmit only failed items.","text":"Caller-defined identifier on each Message Batches request used to match results (which can arrive in any order) and to resubmit only failed items."},{"term":"Data normalization hook","skill":"1.5","html":"A PostToolUse hook that converts heterogeneous tool outputs (dates, currencies, status codes) into one format before the model sees them.","text":"A PostToolUse hook that converts heterogeneous tool outputs (dates, currencies, status codes) into one format before the model sees them."},{"term":"Delegation prompt","skill":"1.3","html":"The prompt a coordinator writes for a subagent; must contain all context the subagent needs, since it doesn't inherit the parent conversation.","text":"The prompt a coordinator writes for a subagent; must contain all context the subagent needs, since it doesn't inherit the parent conversation."},{"term":"detected_pattern","skill":"4.4","html":"A field recording which rule or pattern triggered a finding, so dismissed findings can be analyzed and the prompt improved.","text":"A field recording which rule or pattern triggered a finding, so dismissed findings can be analyzed and the prompt improved."},{"term":"Deterministic enforcement","skill":"1.4","html":"Guaranteeing a rule in code (hooks, gates, permissions, schemas) instead of relying on the model to follow prompt instructions.","text":"Guaranteeing a rule in code (hooks, gates, permissions, schemas) instead of relying on the model to follow prompt instructions."},{"term":"Directory-level CLAUDE.md","skill":"3.1","html":"A CLAUDE.md inside a subdirectory that applies when Claude works in that part of the tree.","text":"A CLAUDE.md inside a subdirectory that applies when Claude works in that part of the tree."},{"term":"end_turn","skill":"1.1","html":"<code>stop_reason</code> meaning Claude finished its response; the loop ends.","text":"`stop_reason` meaning Claude finished its response; the loop ends."},{"term":"Enum with \"other\"","skill":"4.3","html":"Schema pattern pairing a fixed category list with an <code>\"other\"</code> value plus a free-text detail field, so unforeseen cases aren't forced into a wrong category.","text":"Schema pattern pairing a fixed category list with an `\"other\"` value plus a free-text detail field, so unforeseen cases aren't forced into a wrong category."},{"term":"Error category","skill":"2.2","html":"Classification of a tool failure — transient, validation, business or permission — that tells the agent whether and how to recover.","text":"Classification of a tool failure — transient, validation, business or permission — that tells the agent whether and how to recover."},{"term":"Escalation","skill":"5.2","html":"Handing a case to a human (or higher-authority agent) — on explicit request, policy gaps, or when the agent can't make progress.","text":"Handing a case to a human (or higher-authority agent) — on explicit request, policy gaps, or when the agent can't make progress."},{"term":"Evaluator-optimizer","skill":"1.6","html":"Pattern in which one call generates and another critiques against criteria, looping until the criteria are met.","text":"Pattern in which one call generates and another critiques against criteria, looping until the criteria are met."},{"term":"Explicit criteria","skill":"4.1","html":"Concrete, checkable conditions for what to report or do, replacing vague instructions such as \"be conservative\" or \"only important issues\".","text":"Concrete, checkable conditions for what to report or do, replacing vague instructions such as \"be conservative\" or \"only important issues\"."},{"term":"Explore subagent","skill":"3.4","html":"Claude Code's built-in read-only subagent for codebase discovery; keeps verbose search output out of the main context.","text":"Claude Code's built-in read-only subagent for codebase discovery; keeps verbose search output out of the main context."},{"term":"False positive","skill":"4.1","html":"A reported finding that isn't a real problem; high false-positive rates make developers ignore all findings.","text":"A reported finding that isn't a real problem; high false-positive rates make developers ignore all findings."},{"term":"Few-shot prompting","skill":"4.2","html":"Including a small number of worked examples in the prompt to demonstrate format and judgment, especially for ambiguous cases.","text":"Including a small number of worked examples in the prompt to demonstrate format and judgment, especially for ambiguous cases."},{"term":"fork_session","skill":"1.7","html":"Agent SDK option that branches a new session from an existing one, so several approaches can be explored from the same baseline.","text":"Agent SDK option that branches a new session from an existing one, so several approaches can be explored from the same baseline."},{"term":"Forced tool choice","skill":"4.3","html":"<code>tool_choice: {\"type\": \"tool\", \"name\": \"...\"}</code> — Claude must call that specific tool.","text":"`tool_choice: {\"type\": \"tool\", \"name\": \"...\"}` — Claude must call that specific tool."},{"term":"Glob","skill":"2.5","html":"Built-in tool that finds files by path pattern (e.g. <code>**/*.test.tsx</code>); it does not search file contents.","text":"Built-in tool that finds files by path pattern (e.g. `**/*.test.tsx`); it does not search file contents."},{"term":"Graceful degradation","skill":"5.3","html":"Returning partial results with clear annotations when some components fail, instead of failing completely.","text":"Returning partial results with clear annotations when some components fail, instead of failing completely."},{"term":"Grep","skill":"2.5","html":"Built-in tool that searches file contents with regular expressions — e.g. finding all callers of a function.","text":"Built-in tool that searches file contents with regular expressions — e.g. finding all callers of a function."},{"term":"Handoff summary","skill":"1.4","html":"Structured summary passed to a human agent on escalation: customer ID, issue, what was verified and tried, amounts, recommended next step.","text":"Structured summary passed to a human agent on escalation: customer ID, issue, what was verified and tried, amounts, recommended next step."},{"term":"Hook","skill":"1.5","html":"Code that runs at a lifecycle event (PreToolUse, PostToolUse, etc.) in Claude Code or the Agent SDK and can allow, block, modify or log deterministically.","text":"Code that runs at a lifecycle event (PreToolUse, PostToolUse, etc.) in Claude Code or the Agent SDK and can allow, block, modify or log deterministically."},{"term":"Hub-and-spoke","skill":"1.2","html":"Multi-agent topology in which all communication goes through the coordinator; subagents don't talk to each other.","text":"Multi-agent topology in which all communication goes through the coordinator; subagents don't talk to each other."},{"term":"Human-in-the-loop","skill":"5.5","html":"Routing some outputs to human reviewers — by confidence, sampling or risk — before they are acted on.","text":"Routing some outputs to human reviewers — by confidence, sampling or risk — before they are acted on."},{"term":"Independent review instance","skill":"4.6","html":"A separate Claude session that reviews output without the generator's reasoning, avoiding self-confirmation bias.","text":"A separate Claude session that reviews output without the generator's reasoning, avoiding self-confirmation bias."},{"term":"Interview pattern","skill":"3.5","html":"Asking Claude to question you about requirements and edge cases before implementing, useful in unfamiliar domains.","text":"Asking Claude to question you about requirements and edge cases before implementing, useful in unfamiliar domains."},{"term":"isError","skill":"2.2","html":"MCP tool-result flag signalling that the call failed, so the client and model treat the content as an error.","text":"MCP tool-result flag signalling that the call failed, so the client and model treat the content as an error."},{"term":"isRetryable","skill":"2.2","html":"Field in a structured tool error telling the agent whether retrying the same call can succeed.","text":"Field in a structured tool error telling the agent whether retrying the same call can succeed."},{"term":"Iteration cap","skill":"1.1","html":"Maximum number of loop turns; a safety net against runaway loops, not the primary completion signal.","text":"Maximum number of loop turns; a safety net against runaway loops, not the primary completion signal."},{"term":"JSON schema","skill":"4.3","html":"Specification of an object's fields, types, required properties and enums; used in tool <code>input_schema</code> to constrain output.","text":"Specification of an object's fields, types, required properties and enums; used in tool `input_schema` to constrain output."},{"term":"--json-schema","skill":"3.6","html":"Claude Code CLI flag that constrains print-mode output to a JSON schema for machine parsing.","text":"Claude Code CLI flag that constrains print-mode output to a JSON schema for machine parsing."},{"term":"Lost in the middle","skill":"5.1","html":"Models attend less reliably to information buried in the middle of long inputs than to the beginning and end.","text":"Models attend less reliably to information buried in the middle of long inputs than to the beginning and end."},{"term":"max_tokens (stop reason)","skill":"1.1","html":"<code>stop_reason</code> meaning the output was cut off at the token limit — the answer is incomplete, not finished.","text":"`stop_reason` meaning the output was cut off at the token limit — the answer is incomplete, not finished."},{"term":"MCP (Model Context Protocol)","skill":"2.4","html":"Open protocol through which servers expose tools, resources and prompts to Claude clients such as Claude Code and the Agent SDK.","text":"Open protocol through which servers expose tools, resources and prompts to Claude clients such as Claude Code and the Agent SDK."},{"term":"MCP resource","skill":"2.4","html":"Read-only content an MCP server exposes (e.g. a document catalog or schema) that the client can load without a tool call.","text":"Read-only content an MCP server exposes (e.g. a document catalog or schema) that the client can load without a tool call."},{"term":".mcp.json","skill":"2.4","html":"Project-scoped MCP configuration in the repository root, shared with the team through version control.","text":"Project-scoped MCP configuration in the repository root, shared with the team through version control."},{"term":"Message Batches API","skill":"4.5","html":"Asynchronous API for large request sets at 50% of standard cost, processed within up to 24 hours, without latency guarantees.","text":"Asynchronous API for large request sets at 50% of standard cost, processed within up to 24 hours, without latency guarantees."},{"term":"/memory","skill":"3.1","html":"Claude Code command that shows which memory files are loaded and lets you edit them — the first step when instructions seem ignored.","text":"Claude Code command that shows which memory files are loaded and lets you edit them — the first step when instructions seem ignored."},{"term":"Multi-concern request","skill":"1.4","html":"A single customer message containing several distinct issues; decompose it, handle each, then synthesize one reply.","text":"A single customer message containing several distinct issues; decompose it, handle each, then synthesize one reply."},{"term":"Multi-pass review","skill":"4.6","html":"Reviewing in several focused passes (per-file, then cross-file) rather than one pass over everything.","text":"Reviewing in several focused passes (per-file, then cross-file) rather than one pass over everything."},{"term":"Nullable field","skill":"4.3","html":"Schema field that may be <code>null</code>, letting the model say \"not present\" instead of fabricating a value.","text":"Schema field that may be `null`, letting the model say \"not present\" instead of fabricating a value."},{"term":"Output format json","skill":"3.6","html":"<code>--output-format json</code> makes <code>claude -p</code> emit structured JSON (result plus metadata) for scripts and CI.","text":"`--output-format json` makes `claude -p` emit structured JSON (result plus metadata) for scripts and CI."},{"term":"Parallel subagents","skill":"1.3","html":"Several subagents launched by emitting multiple Task/Agent tool calls in one coordinator response.","text":"Several subagents launched by emitting multiple Task/Agent tool calls in one coordinator response."},{"term":"Path-specific rules","skill":"3.3","html":"<code>.claude/rules/*.md</code> files with <code>paths:</code> glob frontmatter that load only when Claude works on matching files.","text":"`.claude/rules/*.md` files with `paths:` glob frontmatter that load only when Claude works on matching files."},{"term":"pause_turn","skill":"1.1","html":"<code>stop_reason</code> indicating a server-side tool loop paused; send the response back so Claude can continue.","text":"`stop_reason` indicating a server-side tool loop paused; send the response back so Claude can continue."},{"term":"Per-file local pass","skill":"1.6","html":"A review or analysis pass focused on one file at a time, giving consistent depth across a large change set.","text":"A review or analysis pass focused on one file at a time, giving consistent depth across a large change set."},{"term":"Permission error","skill":"2.2","html":"Tool failure because the caller lacks rights; not retryable — escalate or request access.","text":"Tool failure because the caller lacks rights; not retryable — escalate or request access."},{"term":"Personal scope","skill":"3.2","html":"<code>~/.claude/</code> locations (CLAUDE.md, skills, commands) that apply only to one user and aren't shared via the repository.","text":"`~/.claude/` locations (CLAUDE.md, skills, commands) that apply only to one user and aren't shared via the repository."},{"term":"Plan mode","skill":"3.4","html":"Claude Code mode in which Claude explores and proposes a plan without editing files, for complex or risky changes.","text":"Claude Code mode in which Claude explores and proposes a plan without editing files, for complex or risky changes."},{"term":"PostToolUse","skill":"1.5","html":"Hook event after a tool runs; used to normalize, enrich, validate or log its result.","text":"Hook event after a tool runs; used to normalize, enrich, validate or log its result."},{"term":"PreToolUse","skill":"1.5","html":"Hook event before a tool runs; can block, modify or redirect the call — e.g. enforce a refund limit.","text":"Hook event before a tool runs; can block, modify or redirect the call — e.g. enforce a refund limit."},{"term":"Prerequisite gate","skill":"1.4","html":"Programmatic check that blocks a tool until required earlier steps (e.g. identity verification) have succeeded.","text":"Programmatic check that blocks a tool until required earlier steps (e.g. identity verification) have succeeded."},{"term":"-p / --print","skill":"3.6","html":"Runs Claude Code non-interactively: one prompt in, result out, exit — required for CI pipelines.","text":"Runs Claude Code non-interactively: one prompt in, result out, exit — required for CI pipelines."},{"term":"Progressive summarization","skill":"5.1","html":"Repeatedly summarizing conversation history to save context; tends to lose exact numbers, dates and commitments.","text":"Repeatedly summarizing conversation history to save context; tends to lose exact numbers, dates and commitments."},{"term":"Project scope","skill":"3.1","html":"Configuration in the repository (<code>CLAUDE.md</code>, <code>.claude/</code>, <code>.mcp.json</code>) shared with everyone who clones it.","text":"Configuration in the repository (`CLAUDE.md`, `.claude/`, `.mcp.json`) shared with everyone who clones it."},{"term":"Prompt chaining","skill":"1.6","html":"Fixed sequence of calls where each output feeds the next; suits predictable, multi-aspect tasks.","text":"Fixed sequence of calls where each output feeds the next; suits predictable, multi-aspect tasks."},{"term":"Provenance","skill":"5.6","html":"Where a piece of information came from and when — source, excerpt, publication or collection date.","text":"Where a piece of information came from and when — source, excerpt, publication or collection date."},{"term":"Refusal (stop reason)","skill":"1.1","html":"<code>stop_reason</code> indicating Claude declined to continue; handle explicitly rather than retrying blindly.","text":"`stop_reason` indicating Claude declined to continue; handle explicitly rather than retrying blindly."},{"term":"--resume","skill":"1.7","html":"Claude Code flag that continues a previous (optionally named) session with its history.","text":"Claude Code flag that continues a previous (optionally named) session with its history."},{"term":"Retry with error feedback","skill":"4.4","html":"Re-prompting with the original input, the failed output and the specific validation error so the model can correct it.","text":"Re-prompting with the original input, the failed output and the specific validation error so the model can correct it."},{"term":"Scenario","skill":null,"html":"One of the six contexts (support agent, code generation, research system, developer productivity, CI/CD, extraction) that frame CCA-F items.","text":"One of the six contexts (support agent, code generation, research system, developer productivity, CI/CD, extraction) that frame CCA-F items."},{"term":"Scratchpad file","skill":"5.4","html":"A file where Claude records findings during long exploration so they survive context compaction or resets.","text":"A file where Claude records findings during long exploration so they survive context compaction or resets."},{"term":"Self-correction field","skill":"4.4","html":"Extra schema field (e.g. <code>calculated_total</code> next to <code>stated_total</code>) that makes inconsistencies detectable in code.","text":"Extra schema field (e.g. `calculated_total` next to `stated_total`) that makes inconsistencies detectable in code."},{"term":"Self-review limitation","skill":"4.6","html":"A model reviewing its own output in the same session tends to confirm its earlier reasoning and miss its own errors.","text":"A model reviewing its own output in the same session tends to confirm its earlier reasoning and miss its own errors."},{"term":"Session","skill":"1.7","html":"A persisted conversation (messages, tool calls) that can be resumed or forked.","text":"A persisted conversation (messages, tool calls) that can be resumed or forked."},{"term":"Skill","skill":"3.2","html":"A folder with <code>SKILL.md</code> (frontmatter + instructions, optional scripts/resources) that Claude loads on demand or when invoked as a slash command.","text":"A folder with `SKILL.md` (frontmatter + instructions, optional scripts/resources) that Claude loads on demand or when invoked as a slash command."},{"term":"Slash command (custom)","skill":"3.2","html":"A reusable prompt stored as Markdown in <code>.claude/commands/</code> (team) or <code>~/.claude/commands/</code> (personal), invoked as <code>/name</code>.","text":"A reusable prompt stored as Markdown in `.claude/commands/` (team) or `~/.claude/commands/` (personal), invoked as `/name`."},{"term":"Stale tool results","skill":"1.7","html":"Results in a resumed session that no longer reflect the files or data; tell Claude what changed or start fresh with a summary.","text":"Results in a resumed session that no longer reflect the files or data; tell Claude what changed or start fresh with a summary."},{"term":"Stratified random sampling","skill":"5.5","html":"Sampling outputs for human review from each document type/confidence band, so rare segments are checked too.","text":"Sampling outputs for human review from each document type/confidence band, so rare segments are checked too."},{"term":"stop_reason","skill":"1.1","html":"Field on each Messages API response stating why generation stopped; the control signal of the agentic loop.","text":"Field on each Messages API response stating why generation stopped; the control signal of the agentic loop."},{"term":"Structured error response","skill":"2.2","html":"Tool error with machine-readable category, retryability, attempted operation and a human-readable message.","text":"Tool error with machine-readable category, retryability, attempted operation and a human-readable message."},{"term":"Structured outputs","skill":"4.3","html":"API feature that constrains Claude's response to a supplied JSON schema; schema compliance still doesn't guarantee correct values.","text":"API feature that constrains Claude's response to a supplied JSON schema; schema compliance still doesn't guarantee correct values."},{"term":"Subagent","skill":"1.2","html":"An agent invoked by another agent for a focused subtask, with its own context, prompt and tools; returns a condensed result.","text":"An agent invoked by another agent for a focused subtask, with its own context, prompt and tools; returns a condensed result."},{"term":"Task tool (Agent tool)","skill":"1.3","html":"The tool through which a Claude Code / Agent SDK agent spawns subagents; it must be allowed for delegation to work.","text":"The tool through which a Claude Code / Agent SDK agent spawns subagents; it must be allowed for delegation to work."},{"term":"Test-driven iteration","skill":"3.5","html":"Writing or providing failing tests first and letting Claude iterate until they pass — tests give objective feedback.","text":"Writing or providing failing tests first and letting Claude iterate until they pass — tests give objective feedback."},{"term":"tool_choice","skill":"2.3","html":"Request parameter controlling tool use: <code>auto</code> (may answer in text), <code>any</code> (must call some tool), <code>tool</code> (must call a named tool), <code>none</code>.","text":"Request parameter controlling tool use: `auto` (may answer in text), `any` (must call some tool), `tool` (must call a named tool), `none`."},{"term":"Tool description","skill":"2.1","html":"The natural-language description in a tool definition — the primary signal Claude uses to choose between tools.","text":"The natural-language description in a tool definition — the primary signal Claude uses to choose between tools."},{"term":"tool_result","skill":"1.1","html":"Content block in a user message returning a tool's output, matched to the request by <code>tool_use_id</code>.","text":"Content block in a user message returning a tool's output, matched to the request by `tool_use_id`."},{"term":"tool_use","skill":"1.1","html":"Content block (and <code>stop_reason</code>) in which Claude requests a tool call with name and input.","text":"Content block (and `stop_reason`) in which Claude requests a tool call with name and input."},{"term":"Tool overload","skill":"2.3","html":"Giving one agent too many tools; selection accuracy drops and similar tools get confused.","text":"Giving one agent too many tools; selection accuracy drops and similar tools get confused."},{"term":"Transient error","skill":"2.2","html":"Temporary failure (timeout, 503, rate limit); retry with backoff before propagating.","text":"Temporary failure (timeout, 503, rate limit); retry with backoff before propagating."},{"term":"Two-tier escalation threshold","skill":"5.2","html":"Separate policies for what the agent may handle autonomously and what always requires a human (e.g. refunds above a limit).","text":"Separate policies for what the agent may handle autonomously and what always requires a human (e.g. refunds above a limit)."},{"term":"\"unclear\" value","skill":"4.3","html":"Enum option letting the model express that the source is ambiguous instead of guessing.","text":"Enum option letting the model express that the source is ambiguous instead of guessing."},{"term":"User scope (MCP)","skill":"2.4","html":"MCP servers configured in <code>~/.claude.json</code>, available to one user across projects but not shared with the team.","text":"MCP servers configured in `~/.claude.json`, available to one user across projects but not shared with the team."},{"term":"Validation error","skill":"2.2","html":"Tool failure caused by invalid input; fix the input and retry.","text":"Tool failure caused by invalid input; fix the input and retry."},{"term":"Validation–retry loop","skill":"4.4","html":"Validate model output in code; on failure, retry with specific feedback up to a limit, then route to review.","text":"Validate model output in code; on failure, retry with specific feedback up to a limit, then route to review."}],"flashcards":[{"id":"f0","front":"<p>Which field drives the agentic loop, and which values continue vs. end it?</p>","back":"<p><code>stop_reason</code>. <strong><code>tool_use</code></strong> → execute the tools, append <code>tool_result</code> blocks, call again. <strong><code>end_turn</code></strong> → done. Other values (<code>max_tokens</code>, <code>pause_turn</code>, <code>refusal</code>) need explicit handling.</p>","skill":"1.1"},{"id":"f1","front":"<p>Three anti-patterns for deciding when an agent loop is finished?</p>","back":"<p>Parsing the text for \"I'm done\"-style phrases · stopping as soon as a text block appears · using an iteration cap as the <em>primary</em> stop signal (a cap is only a safety net).</p>","skill":"1.1"},{"id":"f2","front":"<p>Where must tool results go after execution?</p>","back":"<p>Into the conversation history as <code>tool_result</code> blocks (matching <code>tool_use_id</code>) in the next user message, so Claude can reason about them in the next iteration.</p>","skill":"1.1"},{"id":"f3","front":"<p><code>stop_reason: \"max_tokens\"</code> in the middle of an agent run means…?</p>","back":"<p>The output was <strong>cut off</strong> — not finished. Raise <code>max_tokens</code> or continue; never treat it as completion.</p>","skill":"1.1"},{"id":"f4","front":"<p>What does the coordinator do in a hub-and-spoke system?</p>","back":"<p>Decomposes the task, decides which subagents to invoke, passes them context, routes <strong>all</strong> communication, aggregates results, checks coverage and iterates.</p>","skill":"1.2"},{"id":"f5","front":"<p>A research report misses whole sub-topics although every subagent did its job well. Root cause?</p>","back":"<p>The <strong>coordinator's decomposition</strong> was too narrow — fix how it splits the question, not the subagents.</p>","skill":"1.2"},{"id":"f6","front":"<p>Why route subagent communication through the coordinator?</p>","back":"<p>Observability, consistent error handling, controlled information flow and one place to aggregate and resolve conflicts.</p>","skill":"1.2"},{"id":"f7","front":"<p>What does a subagent inherit from its parent's conversation?</p>","back":"<p><strong>Nothing</strong> by default. Everything it needs (findings, constraints, source metadata, output format) must be in the delegation prompt.</p>","skill":"1.3"},{"id":"f8","front":"<p>How do you run three subagents in parallel?</p>","back":"<p>The coordinator emits <strong>several Task/Agent tool calls in a single response</strong>; the SDK runs them concurrently.</p>","skill":"1.3"},{"id":"f9","front":"<p>Which tool must be in the coordinator's <code>allowedTools</code> to spawn subagents?</p>","back":"<p>The <strong>Task</strong> tool (the Agent tool in newer releases).</p>","skill":"1.3"},{"id":"f10","front":"<p>The three main fields of an <code>AgentDefinition</code>?</p>","back":"<p><code>description</code> (when to use it) · <code>prompt</code> (its system instructions) · <code>tools</code> (what it may use).</p>","skill":"1.3"},{"id":"f11","front":"<p>Why should subagents return structured data with metadata?</p>","back":"<p>So the coordinator can merge results reliably and keep provenance — source URL, document name, date, confidence — through synthesis.</p>","skill":"1.3"},{"id":"f12","front":"<p>\"Identity must be verified before any refund.\" Prompt instruction or gate?</p>","back":"<p><strong>Programmatic prerequisite gate</strong> — block <code>process_refund</code> until <code>get_customer</code> has returned a verified ID. Prompts only make compliance likely.</p>","skill":"1.4"},{"id":"f13","front":"<p>What belongs in a handoff summary for a human agent?</p>","back":"<p>Customer ID, the issue, what was verified, what was tried and the results, relevant amounts and dates, root cause if known, and the recommended next action.</p>","skill":"1.4"},{"id":"f14","front":"<p>A customer raises three unrelated issues in one message. Pattern?</p>","back":"<p>Decompose into separate concerns, investigate each (in parallel where independent) with shared context, then synthesize <strong>one</strong> reply.</p>","skill":"1.4"},{"id":"f15","front":"<p>PreToolUse vs. PostToolUse hook — typical use for each?</p>","back":"<p><strong>PreToolUse</strong>: block or redirect calls that violate policy (refund &gt; $500 → escalate). <strong>PostToolUse</strong>: normalize/enrich results (unify date formats) before the model sees them.</p>","skill":"1.5"},{"id":"f16","front":"<p>When is a hook the right answer instead of a prompt?</p>","back":"<p>When the business rule must hold <strong>every time</strong> (compliance, money, safety). Hooks are deterministic; prompts are probabilistic.</p>","skill":"1.5"},{"id":"f17","front":"<p>Prompt chaining vs. dynamic decomposition — when each?</p>","back":"<p><strong>Chaining</strong>: predictable, known steps (review each aspect in order). <strong>Dynamic/adaptive</strong>: open-ended investigation where the next subtasks depend on findings.</p>","skill":"1.6"},{"id":"f18","front":"<p>Reviewing a 40-file PR in one pass gives shallow, inconsistent feedback. Fix?</p>","back":"<p><strong>Per-file local passes</strong> for consistent depth, plus a separate <strong>cross-file integration pass</strong> for data flow and contracts.</p>","skill":"1.6"},{"id":"f19","front":"<p><code>fork_session</code> is for…?</p>","back":"<p>Branching from a shared baseline to explore <strong>divergent approaches</strong> (e.g. two refactoring strategies) without contaminating each other.</p>","skill":"1.7"},{"id":"f20","front":"<p>You resume a session but files changed since. Two good options?</p>","back":"<p>Tell the resumed session <strong>exactly what changed</strong> so it re-reads them, or <strong>start fresh</strong> with a structured summary of prior findings when old tool results are stale.</p>","skill":"1.7"},{"id":"f21","front":"<p>The primary mechanism Claude uses to choose between tools?</p>","back":"<p>The <strong>tool descriptions</strong> — purpose, input formats, examples, edge cases and boundaries versus similar tools.</p>","skill":"2.1"},{"id":"f22","front":"<p>Two tools with near-identical descriptions are confused. Best first fix?</p>","back":"<p>Rewrite the descriptions to state <strong>when to use each</strong> (and rename/split if they overlap) — before adding classifiers or few-shot examples.</p>","skill":"2.1"},{"id":"f23","front":"<p>Why split a generic <code>analyze_document</code> tool?</p>","back":"<p>Purpose-specific tools (<code>extract_data_points</code>, <code>summarize_content</code>, <code>verify_claim_against_source</code>) with defined inputs/outputs are selected and used more reliably.</p>","skill":"2.1"},{"id":"f24","front":"<p>The four error categories for tool failures and whether to retry?</p>","back":"<p><strong>Transient</strong> (retry) · <strong>Validation</strong> (fix input, retry) · <strong>Business</strong> (don't retry — explain/escalate) · <strong>Permission</strong> (don't retry — escalate).</p>","skill":"2.2"},{"id":"f25","front":"<p>Why is returning <code>[]</code> for a failed lookup dangerous?</p>","back":"<p>The agent treats it as \"nothing exists\" and acts on it. A failure must be an <strong>error</strong> (<code>isError: true</code>) distinct from a valid empty result.</p>","skill":"2.2"},{"id":"f26","front":"<p>What does a good structured tool error contain?</p>","back":"<p><code>isError</code>, an error category, <code>isRetryable</code>, the attempted operation, partial results if any, and a human-readable message.</p>","skill":"2.2"},{"id":"f27","front":"<p>What happens to tool-selection accuracy with 18 tools on one agent?</p>","back":"<p>It degrades. Give each agent the <strong>4–5 tools its role needs</strong>; add a narrow cross-role tool only for frequent needs.</p>","skill":"2.3"},{"id":"f28","front":"<p><code>tool_choice</code> values and their effect?</p>","back":"<p><code>auto</code> — may answer in text · <code>any</code> — must call <strong>some</strong> tool · <code>{\"type\":\"tool\",\"name\":\"x\"}</code> — must call <strong>x</strong> · <code>none</code> — no tools.</p>","skill":"2.3"},{"id":"f29","front":"<p>Guarantee that an extraction always returns structured data even when several schemas exist?</p>","back":"<p><code>tool_choice: {\"type\": \"any\"}</code> — Claude must call one of the extraction tools rather than replying in text.</p>","skill":"2.3"},{"id":"f30","front":"<p>Project-scoped vs. user-scoped MCP configuration?</p>","back":"<p><strong><code>.mcp.json</code></strong> in the repo — shared, version-controlled team servers. <strong><code>~/.claude.json</code></strong> — personal or experimental servers.</p>","skill":"2.4"},{"id":"f31","front":"<p>How do you keep an MCP server token out of <code>.mcp.json</code>?</p>","back":"<p>Environment-variable expansion: <code>\"env\": {\"GITHUB_TOKEN\": \"${GITHUB_TOKEN}\"}</code>.</p>","skill":"2.4"},{"id":"f32","front":"<p>MCP tool or MCP resource for exposing a catalog of 300 docs?</p>","back":"<p>A <strong>resource</strong> — content the client can read without exploratory tool calls; tools are for actions and queries.</p>","skill":"2.4"},{"id":"f33","front":"<p>Standard integration (GitHub, Jira) — build your own MCP server?</p>","back":"<p>Prefer an existing (official/community) server; build custom servers for team-specific workflows.</p>","skill":"2.4"},{"id":"f34","front":"<p>Grep vs. Glob?</p>","back":"<p><strong>Grep</strong> searches file <strong>contents</strong> (callers, strings). <strong>Glob</strong> matches file <strong>paths</strong> (<code>**/*.test.tsx</code>).</p>","skill":"2.5"},{"id":"f35","front":"<p>Edit fails because the anchor text appears three times. Options?</p>","back":"<p>Add surrounding context to make the anchor unique — or fall back to <strong>Read + Write</strong> of the whole file.</p>","skill":"2.5"},{"id":"f36","front":"<p>Best way to understand an unfamiliar call chain?</p>","back":"<p><strong>Incremental exploration</strong>: Grep for entry points, Read the relevant files, follow imports — not reading the whole codebase up front.</p>","skill":"2.5"},{"id":"f37","front":"<p>User-level vs. project-level CLAUDE.md?</p>","back":"<p><strong><code>~/.claude/CLAUDE.md</code></strong> — personal, not shared. <strong><code>CLAUDE.md</code> / <code>.claude/CLAUDE.md</code></strong> in the repo — team conventions via version control.</p>","skill":"3.1"},{"id":"f38","front":"<p>A new teammate doesn't get the coding conventions you see. Likely cause?</p>","back":"<p>The conventions live in <strong>your user-level</strong> <code>~/.claude/CLAUDE.md</code>, not in the project CLAUDE.md.</p>","skill":"3.1"},{"id":"f39","front":"<p>How do you check which memory files are actually loaded?</p>","back":"<p>Run <strong><code>/memory</code></strong>.</p>","skill":"3.1"},{"id":"f40","front":"<p>How do you keep a large CLAUDE.md maintainable?</p>","back":"<p>Split into focused files and include them with <strong><code>@import</code></strong>, or move topics into <code>.claude/rules/</code>.</p>","skill":"3.1"},{"id":"f41","front":"<p>Where do team-shared vs. personal slash commands live?</p>","back":"<p>Team: <strong><code>.claude/commands/</code></strong> (or <code>.claude/skills/</code>) in the repo. Personal: <strong><code>~/.claude/commands/</code></strong> (or <code>~/.claude/skills/</code>).</p>","skill":"3.2"},{"id":"f42","front":"<p>What does <code>context: fork</code> do in SKILL.md?</p>","back":"<p>Runs the skill in an <strong>isolated sub-agent context</strong>, so verbose output doesn't fill the main conversation.</p>","skill":"3.2"},{"id":"f43","front":"<p>Skill or CLAUDE.md?</p>","back":"<p><strong>CLAUDE.md</strong> for always-on conventions. <strong>Skill</strong> for on-demand, task-specific workflows.</p>","skill":"3.2"},{"id":"f44","front":"<p>Test conventions must apply to <code>*.test.tsx</code> files spread across 40 directories. Mechanism?</p>","back":"<p>A <strong><code>.claude/rules/</code> file with <code>paths:</code> glob frontmatter</strong> (e.g. <code>**/*.test.tsx</code>) — not 40 directory-level CLAUDE.md files.</p>","skill":"3.3"},{"id":"f45","front":"<p>Why do path-scoped rules save context?</p>","back":"<p>They load <strong>only</strong> when Claude works on matching files, instead of being in every session.</p>","skill":"3.3"},{"id":"f46","front":"<p>Plan mode or direct execution: migrate logging across 30 services?</p>","back":"<p><strong>Plan mode</strong> — multi-file, architectural, several valid approaches. Direct execution suits small, well-scoped changes.</p>","skill":"3.4"},{"id":"f47","front":"<p>What is the Explore subagent for?</p>","back":"<p>Verbose codebase <strong>discovery</strong> in an isolated context, returning a summary — keeps the main context clean.</p>","skill":"3.4"},{"id":"f48","front":"<p>Three iterative-refinement techniques?</p>","back":"<p>Concrete <strong>input/output examples</strong> · <strong>test-driven iteration</strong> (failing tests as feedback) · the <strong>interview pattern</strong> (Claude asks you questions first).</p>","skill":"3.5"},{"id":"f49","front":"<p>Several bugs that interact — fix one by one or together?</p>","back":"<p><strong>Together in one message</strong> (interacting issues). Independent issues can be fixed sequentially.</p>","skill":"3.5"},{"id":"f50","front":"<p>Claude Code hangs in a CI job. Missing flag?</p>","back":"<p><strong><code>-p</code> / <code>--print</code></strong> — non-interactive mode.</p>","skill":"3.6"},{"id":"f51","front":"<p>How do you get machine-parseable review findings from CI?</p>","back":"<p><code>claude -p … --output-format json</code> plus <strong><code>--json-schema</code></strong> for a fixed finding structure.</p>","skill":"3.6"},{"id":"f52","front":"<p>Re-running the CI review creates duplicate PR comments. Fix?</p>","back":"<p>Pass the <strong>previous findings</strong> into the prompt and ask for new or unresolved issues only.</p>","skill":"3.6"},{"id":"f53","front":"<p>Why use a separate instance to review generated code?</p>","back":"<p>The generating session carries its own reasoning and tends to <strong>confirm</strong> it; an independent instance catches more issues.</p>","skill":"4.6"},{"id":"f54","front":"<p>\"Be conservative and only report high-confidence issues\" didn't reduce false positives. Better?</p>","back":"<p><strong>Explicit criteria</strong>: what to report (e.g. bugs, security), what to skip (style, local patterns), severity definitions with examples.</p>","skill":"4.1"},{"id":"f55","front":"<p>Developers ignore all review comments because of noise. Fast way to restore trust?</p>","back":"<p>Temporarily <strong>disable the high-false-positive categories</strong> while improving their criteria.</p>","skill":"4.1"},{"id":"f56","front":"<p>How many few-shot examples, and which ones?</p>","back":"<p>Typically <strong>2–4</strong>, targeting <strong>ambiguous</strong> cases, showing the reasoning for the choice — not just easy, obvious ones.</p>","skill":"4.2"},{"id":"f57","front":"<p>Few-shot examples for extraction from varied document layouts help with…?</p>","back":"<p>Consistent format, handling of unusual structures, and less hallucination (showing when to return null).</p>","skill":"4.2"},{"id":"f58","front":"<p>Does a JSON schema via tool use eliminate all extraction errors?</p>","back":"<p>No — it eliminates <strong>syntax</strong> errors. <strong>Semantic</strong> errors (wrong values, totals that don't add up) still need validation.</p>","skill":"4.3"},{"id":"f59","front":"<p>The model fabricates a <code>due_date</code> when none is in the document. Schema fix?</p>","back":"<p>Make the field <strong>optional / nullable</strong> so \"not present\" is a valid answer.</p>","skill":"4.3"},{"id":"f60","front":"<p>Category enum keeps getting forced into wrong values. Pattern?</p>","back":"<p>Add <strong><code>\"other\"</code> + a detail string</strong> (and <code>\"unclear\"</code> where ambiguity is real).</p>","skill":"4.3"},{"id":"f61","front":"<p>What goes into a retry after a validation failure?</p>","back":"<p>The original document, the <strong>failed output</strong>, and the <strong>specific validation error</strong> — so the model can correct it.</p>","skill":"4.4"},{"id":"f62","front":"<p>When will retries NOT fix an extraction error?</p>","back":"<p>When the information <strong>isn't in the source</strong> (or is in an external document). Route to null/review instead.</p>","skill":"4.4"},{"id":"f63","front":"<p>How can the schema itself expose arithmetic errors on invoices?</p>","back":"<p>Add self-correction fields: <strong><code>calculated_total</code></strong> next to <strong><code>stated_total</code></strong>, or a <code>conflict_detected</code> boolean.</p>","skill":"4.4"},{"id":"f64","front":"<p>Message Batches API — cost, time window, latency guarantee?</p>","back":"<p><strong>50%</strong> cheaper · up to <strong>24 hours</strong> · <strong>no</strong> latency SLA.</p>","skill":"4.5"},{"id":"f65","front":"<p>Pre-merge review (blocking) vs. nightly audit — which uses batches?</p>","back":"<p><strong>Nightly audit</strong> → batch. Pre-merge checks block developers → <strong>synchronous</strong> calls.</p>","skill":"4.5"},{"id":"f66","front":"<p>1,000-document batch, 40 failed (oversized). Next step?</p>","back":"<p>Fix the cause (e.g. chunk the oversized docs) and <strong>resubmit only the failed <code>custom_id</code>s</strong>.</p>","skill":"4.5"},{"id":"f67","front":"<p>Before submitting 50,000 documents to a batch you should…?</p>","back":"<p><strong>Refine the prompt on a sample</strong> first, so you don't pay for 50,000 bad results.</p>","skill":"4.5"},{"id":"f68","front":"<p>Why route by confidence self-reports with care?</p>","back":"<p>Self-reported confidence is <strong>poorly calibrated</strong> unless validated on labeled data; use it for routing only after calibration.</p>","skill":"4.6"},{"id":"f69","front":"<p>Long support session forgets the refund amount agreed earlier. Fix?</p>","back":"<p>Keep a persistent <strong>case-facts block</strong> with exact amounts, dates, IDs and commitments outside the summarized history.</p>","skill":"5.1"},{"id":"f70","front":"<p>What is \"lost in the middle\" and the mitigation?</p>","back":"<p>Models attend less to the middle of long inputs → put <strong>key findings first</strong>, use clear section headers.</p>","skill":"5.1"},{"id":"f71","front":"<p>Order-lookup returns 40 fields; the task needs 5. What to do?</p>","back":"<p><strong>Trim</strong> tool output to the relevant fields before it accumulates in context.</p>","skill":"5.1"},{"id":"f72","front":"<p>Customer says \"I want to talk to a human.\" Agent action?</p>","back":"<p><strong>Escalate immediately</strong> — don't attempt to resolve first.</p>","skill":"5.2"},{"id":"f73","front":"<p>Why not escalate based on sentiment analysis?</p>","back":"<p>Sentiment doesn't measure <strong>case complexity or policy fit</strong>; angry customers with simple issues and calm ones with policy exceptions get misrouted.</p>","skill":"5.2"},{"id":"f74","front":"<p>Lookup returns two customers named \"J. Smith\". Agent action?</p>","back":"<p><strong>Ask for an identifier</strong> (email, order number) — never pick one heuristically.</p>","skill":"5.2"},{"id":"f75","front":"<p>Valid escalation triggers?</p>","back":"<p>Explicit customer request · <strong>policy gap</strong> or exception not covered · the agent <strong>can't make progress</strong> · actions above an authority threshold.</p>","skill":"5.2"},{"id":"f76","front":"<p>Subagent search times out. What should it return to the coordinator?</p>","back":"<p>Structured error context: <strong>failure type, attempted query, partial results, possible alternatives</strong> — so the coordinator can decide.</p>","skill":"5.3"},{"id":"f77","front":"<p>Two error-propagation anti-patterns?</p>","back":"<p><strong>Silently suppressing</strong> errors (returning empty as success) and <strong>terminating the whole workflow</strong> because one subagent failed.</p>","skill":"5.3"},{"id":"f78","front":"<p>Final report when one source failed?</p>","back":"<p>Publish what is supported, with a <strong>coverage annotation</strong> naming the gap.</p>","skill":"5.3"},{"id":"f79","front":"<p>Symptom of context degradation in a long exploration session?</p>","back":"<p>Claude refers to \"typical patterns\" instead of the specific classes it read earlier; answers grow inconsistent.</p>","skill":"5.4"},{"id":"f80","front":"<p>Four techniques for large-codebase exploration?</p>","back":"<p><strong>Scratchpad files</strong> of findings · <strong>subagents</strong> for verbose searches · <strong>summarize</strong> before each new phase · <strong>/compact</strong> deliberately (and crash-recovery manifests for long tasks).</p>","skill":"5.4"},{"id":"f81","front":"<p>97% overall extraction accuracy — enough to drop human review?</p>","back":"<p>Not yet. Check accuracy <strong>per document type and per field</strong>; aggregates hide weak segments.</p>","skill":"5.5"},{"id":"f82","front":"<p>How should automated results be sampled for review?</p>","back":"<p><strong>Stratified random sampling</strong> across document types and confidence bands.</p>","skill":"5.5"},{"id":"f83","front":"<p>How do you set confidence thresholds for auto-approval?</p>","back":"<p>Calibrate <strong>field-level</strong> confidence on a <strong>labeled validation set</strong>.</p>","skill":"5.5"},{"id":"f84","front":"<p>Two sources report different market sizes. What should the synthesis do?</p>","back":"<p>Keep <strong>both values with attribution</strong> (and dates/methodology) — don't silently pick one.</p>","skill":"5.6"},{"id":"f85","front":"<p>How does provenance survive multi-agent summarization?</p>","back":"<p>Subagents return <strong>claim–source mappings</strong> (source, excerpt, date) that the synthesis agent preserves.</p>","skill":"5.6"},{"id":"f86","front":"<p>Why record publication / collection dates?</p>","back":"<p>So apparent contradictions explained by <strong>time</strong> (2022 vs. 2025 data) aren't misread as conflicts.</p>","skill":"5.6"}],"pages":{"exam-guide":{"title":"CCA-F Exam Guide","summary":"Everything about the exam itself — purpose, audience, format, the six scenarios, blueprint, scoring and how to prepare — condensed from the published exam information for the Claude Certified Architect – Foundations credential.","html":"<h2 id=\"about-the-certification\">About the certification</h2>\n<p>The <strong>Claude Certified Architect – Foundations</strong> credential (launched March 2026 through the Claude Partner Network) validates that you can <strong>design and ship production-grade Claude solutions</strong> — making sound architectural trade-offs across the <strong>Claude API, the Claude Agent SDK, Claude Code and the Model Context Protocol (MCP)</strong>.</p>\n<p>Where the Developer exam asks \"can you build it?\", the Architect exam asks <strong>\"which design is right, and why?\"</strong> Almost every item presents a realistic situation and four plausible designs; the correct option is the one that addresses the <em>root cause</em> with the <em>most appropriate, proportionate mechanism</em>.</p>\n<p>Holders can demonstrate that they can:</p>\n<ul><li>Design <strong>agentic loops</strong> and <strong>coordinator–subagent</strong> systems, pass context between agents and manage sessions</li><li>Decide when a requirement needs <strong>deterministic enforcement</strong> (hooks, prerequisite gates) instead of prompt guidance</li><li>Design <strong>tool interfaces</strong>, <strong>structured error responses</strong> and <strong>MCP integrations</strong> that agents use reliably</li><li>Configure <strong>Claude Code</strong> for teams: CLAUDE.md hierarchy, rules, skills, commands, plan mode and <strong>CI/CD</strong></li><li>Produce <strong>reliable structured output</strong> with schemas, few-shot examples, validation–retry loops and batch processing</li><li>Keep long-running systems <strong>reliable</strong>: context preservation, escalation, error propagation, human review and provenance</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"intended-audience\">Intended audience</h2>\n<p>Solution architects, technical leads and senior engineers who design Claude-based systems for production — typically at Claude Partner Network organisations. Third-party guides consistently recommend <strong>6+ months of hands-on experience</strong> with the Claude API, Agent SDK, Claude Code and MCP. The exam is closed-book and taken without AI assistance.</p>\n<h2 id=\"exam-details-at-a-glance\">Exam details at a glance</h2>\n<div id=\"exam-facts-slot\"></div>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><div class=\"callout-title\">Verify administrative details</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>Anthropic distributes the exam guide to registered candidates through Anthropic Academy. Item count, duration, cut score, scenarios and domain weights are consistent across every public source; the <strong>exam code</strong> (CCA-F; some catalogues list CCAR-F), <strong>fee</strong>, <strong>retake</strong> and <strong>validity</strong> rules vary between sources. Confirm them on the Academy listing before you book.</p></div></aside>\n<h2 id=\"the-six-scenarios\">The six scenarios</h2>\n<p>Each sitting draws <strong>4 of these 6 scenarios at random</strong>. A scenario sets the context — the system, its tools and constraints — and a group of items is asked inside it. You cannot predict which four you will get, so prepare for all six. Every practice question in this guide is tagged with its scenario, and the mock exams draw scenarios the same way.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>#</th><th>Scenario</th><th>What it typically tests</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>S1</td><td><strong>Customer Support Resolution Agent</strong></td><td>Agent loop, prerequisite gates (identity before refunds), tool design, escalation and handoff, context preservation</td></tr><tr><td>S2</td><td><strong>Code Generation with Claude Code</strong></td><td>CLAUDE.md hierarchy, rules, skills and commands, plan mode vs. direct execution, iterative refinement</td></tr><tr><td>S3</td><td><strong>Multi-Agent Research System</strong></td><td>Coordinator–subagent design, context passing, parallel subagents, error propagation, provenance</td></tr><tr><td>S4</td><td><strong>Developer Productivity with Claude</strong></td><td>Built-in tools, codebase exploration, context degradation, sessions, MCP servers</td></tr><tr><td>S5</td><td><strong>Claude Code for CI/CD</strong></td><td><code>-p</code> mode, JSON output, independent review instances, explicit criteria, false positives, batch vs. synchronous</td></tr><tr><td>S6</td><td><strong>Structured Data Extraction</strong></td><td>Tool-use schemas, <code>tool_choice</code>, nullable fields, validation–retry, few-shot, human review and calibration</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"blueprint\">Blueprint</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Weight</th><th>Task statements</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>D1 Agentic Architecture &amp; Orchestration</td><td><strong>27%</strong></td><td>7 (1.1–1.7)</td></tr><tr><td>D2 Tool Design &amp; MCP Integration</td><td>18%</td><td>5 (2.1–2.5)</td></tr><tr><td>D3 Claude Code Configuration &amp; Workflows</td><td>20%</td><td>6 (3.1–3.6)</td></tr><tr><td>D4 Prompt Engineering &amp; Structured Output</td><td>20%</td><td>6 (4.1–4.6)</td></tr><tr><td>D5 Context Management &amp; Reliability</td><td>15%</td><td>6 (5.1–5.6)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>The full list of task statements with what each measures appears under <em>Blueprint weights</em> in the exam details above. Weights are published per <strong>domain</strong>; this guide splits each domain weight evenly across its task statements to size lessons and mocks.</p>\n<h2 id=\"scoring\">Scoring</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Scaled score 100–1,000; 720 to pass.</strong> The result is pass/fail with the scaled score.</li><li>Every item is <strong>single answer</strong>: one correct option and three distractors. Distractors are written to be attractive to candidates with incomplete knowledge — typically a real technique applied to the wrong problem.</li><li><strong>Unanswered items count as wrong</strong> and there is no penalty for guessing: answer everything.</li></ul>\n<aside class=\"callout callout-tip\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Tip</div><div class=\"callout-title\">How this guide scores mocks</div><div class=\"callout-body\"><p>The raw-to-scaled conversion is not published. Mocks here map about 70% correct to 720, linearly on both sides. Treat <strong>80%+ on full mocks (≈ 813)</strong> as your \"ready to book\" signal.</p></div></aside>\n<h2 id=\"how-architect-items-are-built-and-how-to-read-them\">How Architect items are built — and how to read them</h2>\n<p>Try these three; they show the patterns you will meet again and again:</p>\n<div class=\"quiz-embed\" data-qid=\"a1-gate-01\"></div>\n<div class=\"quiz-embed\" data-qid=\"a2-err-01\"></div>\n<div class=\"quiz-embed\" data-qid=\"a4-schema-01\"></div>\n<p>Notice the pattern:</p>\n<ol><li><strong>Deterministic beats probabilistic when the requirement is a guarantee.</strong> Identity verification before refunds, policy limits, compliance rules → hooks and gates, not stronger wording in the prompt.</li><li><strong>Fix the root cause at the right layer.</strong> Misrouted tool calls → better tool <em>descriptions</em>; fabricated values → <em>nullable</em> schema fields; a subagent missing facts → pass them <em>explicitly</em>.</li><li><strong>Proportionate solutions win.</strong> The simplest mechanism that fully solves the stated problem beats a more elaborate architecture (an extra classifier, a fine-tuned model, a new agent layer) that also might.</li><li><strong>Read the qualifier.</strong> \"Most effective first step\", \"most reliable\", \"lowest cost while meeting the SLA\" — several options are often <em>possible</em>; only one is <em>best</em> for the stated constraint.</li></ol>\n<h2 id=\"commonly-reported-out-of-scope-topics\">Commonly reported out-of-scope topics</h2>\n<p>Public prep material consistently lists topics the exam does <strong>not</strong> test in depth: fine-tuning and training (RLHF, constitutional AI), embedding models and vector-database internals, API authentication and billing administration, hosting/deploying MCP server infrastructure, computer use and vision specifics, streaming implementation details, and cloud-provider-specific configuration. Knowing that prompt caching and token counting exist is useful; implementation minutiae are not the focus.</p>\n<h2 id=\"how-to-prepare\">How to prepare</h2>\n<ol><li>Read this guide and take the <a href=\"#/diagnostic\">Diagnostic</a>; note the weakest domains and scenarios.</li><li>Work through the <a href=\"#/learn\">Curriculum</a> — Domain 1 first, it is 27% of the exam and underpins the rest.</li><li><strong>Build</strong> — the exam rewards experience. Do the <a href=\"#/exercises\">Build Exercises</a>: an agent loop with hooks, a coordinator with subagents, an MCP tool with structured errors, a CI review job and an extraction pipeline with validation–retry.</li><li>Drill by domain and run <a href=\"#/flashcards\">Flashcards</a> daily for patterns and anti-patterns.</li><li>Sit full <a href=\"#/mock\">Mock Exams</a> until you score 800+ consistently across different scenario draws.</li><li>Complete the official practice exam on Anthropic Academy before booking.</li></ol>","headings":[{"id":"about-the-certification","text":"About the certification"},{"id":"intended-audience","text":"Intended audience"},{"id":"exam-details-at-a-glance","text":"Exam details at a glance"},{"id":"the-six-scenarios","text":"The six scenarios"},{"id":"blueprint","text":"Blueprint"},{"id":"scoring","text":"Scoring"},{"id":"how-architect-items-are-built-and-how-to-read-them","text":"How Architect items are built — and how to read them"},{"id":"commonly-reported-out-of-scope-topics","text":"Commonly reported out-of-scope topics"},{"id":"how-to-prepare","text":"How to prepare"}]},"quick-reference":{"title":"Quick Reference","summary":"One-page cheat sheets for the Architect exam — decision rules, patterns and anti-patterns, Agent SDK and Claude Code configuration, structured output and reliability.","html":"<h2 id=\"the-ten-decision-rules\">The ten decision rules</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>If the scenario says…</th><th>The answer is usually…</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>\"must always\", \"never\", compliance, money, identity</td><td><strong>Deterministic control</strong> — hook, prerequisite gate, permission rule, schema — not a stronger prompt</td></tr><tr><td>The model picks the wrong tool</td><td>Improve <strong>tool descriptions</strong> (purpose, inputs, when to use vs. the similar tool); rename/split overlapping tools</td></tr><tr><td>A subagent lacks information the coordinator had</td><td><strong>Pass it explicitly</strong> in the delegation prompt — subagents don't inherit the parent's context</td></tr><tr><td>Output must be valid JSON for a downstream system</td><td><strong>Tool use with a JSON schema</strong> (or structured outputs) — then validate semantics in code</td></tr><tr><td>The model invents values that aren't in the source</td><td>Make the field <strong>optional/nullable</strong> and allow \"unclear\"; don't force a value</td></tr><tr><td>Same session generated and reviewed the code</td><td>Use an <strong>independent review instance</strong> without the generator's reasoning</td></tr><tr><td>Overnight / weekly / non-blocking bulk job</td><td><strong>Message Batches API</strong> (50% cheaper, up to 24 h). Blocking pre-merge check → synchronous</td></tr><tr><td>Customer asks for a human</td><td><strong>Escalate immediately</strong> — don't try to resolve first</td></tr><tr><td>A subagent's call fails</td><td>Return <strong>structured error context</strong> to the coordinator; don't fake success, don't abort everything</td></tr><tr><td>Long session keeps forgetting numbers and dates</td><td>Keep a <strong>persistent case-facts block</strong>; don't rely on progressive summaries</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"agentic-loop\">Agentic loop</h2>\n<div class=\"codeblock\"><div class=\"codeblock-head\"><span>agentic loop — the shape the exam expects</span><button class=\"copy-btn\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></div><pre><code class=\"lang-python\">messages = [{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": task}]\nwhile True:\n    resp = client.messages.create(model=MODEL, max_tokens=4096, tools=TOOLS, messages=messages)\n    messages.append({\"role\": \"assistant\", \"content\": resp.content})\n    if resp.stop_reason != \"tool_use\":          # \"end_turn\" → done; anything else → handle explicitly\n        break\n    results = [run_tool(b) for b in resp.content if b.type == \"tool_use\"]\n    messages.append({\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": results})   # tool_result blocks, matching tool_use_id</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th><code>stop_reason</code></th><th>Meaning</th><th>Loop action</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>tool_use</code></td><td>Claude wants tools executed</td><td>Run <strong>all</strong> requested tools, append results, call again</td></tr><tr><td><code>end_turn</code></td><td>Claude finished its turn</td><td>Exit the loop, return the answer</td></tr><tr><td><code>max_tokens</code></td><td>Output budget hit mid-response</td><td>Raise <code>max_tokens</code> or continue; don't treat as done</td></tr><tr><td><code>pause_turn</code></td><td>Server tool loop paused</td><td>Send the response back to let Claude continue</td></tr><tr><td><code>refusal</code></td><td>Claude declined</td><td>Surface/handle; don't retry blindly</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Anti-patterns:</strong> checking whether the reply \"sounds finished\", stopping when a text block appears, iteration caps as the <em>primary</em> stop signal (a cap is a safety net, not the control flow), dropping tool results from history.</p>\n<h2 id=\"multi-agent-orchestration\">Multi-agent orchestration</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Hub-and-spoke:</strong> the coordinator decomposes, delegates, routes <em>all</em> communication, aggregates and decides whether to iterate. Subagents never talk to each other directly.</li><li><strong>Isolation:</strong> each subagent has its own context; it sees only its delegation prompt (+ its system prompt, tools, CLAUDE.md where applicable).</li><li><strong>Parallelism:</strong> emit several Task/Agent tool calls <strong>in one response</strong> to run subagents concurrently.</li><li><strong>Context passing:</strong> include prior findings, constraints, source metadata and the expected output format in the prompt; ask subagents to return <strong>structured data with metadata</strong> (source URL, date, confidence).</li><li><strong>Coverage gaps</strong> usually come from the <strong>coordinator's decomposition</strong> (too narrow), not from the subagents.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"codeblock\"><div class=\"codeblock-head\"><span>Agent SDK subagent definition (Python)</span><button class=\"copy-btn\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></div><pre><code class=\"lang-python\">options = ClaudeAgentOptions(\n    allowed_tools=[\"Task\", \"Read\", \"Grep\", \"Glob\"],   # the coordinator needs the Task (Agent) tool to delegate\n    agents={\n        \"web-researcher\": AgentDefinition(\n            description=\"Searches the web for sources on one sub-question. Use for fresh external facts.\",\n            prompt=\"Return JSON: [{claim, source_url, published, excerpt}]. Never summarize away dates.\",\n            tools=[\"WebSearch\", \"WebFetch\"],\n        ),\n    },\n)</code></pre></div>\n<h2 id=\"enforcement-and-hooks\">Enforcement and hooks</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Need</th><th>Mechanism</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Identity verified before <code>process_refund</code></td><td><strong>Prerequisite gate</strong>: block the tool until <code>get_customer</code> returned a verified ID</td></tr><tr><td>Refunds above $500 must go to a human</td><td><strong>PreToolUse hook</strong> that blocks and redirects to escalation</td></tr><tr><td>Tools return dates as Unix time, ISO and \"MM/DD\"</td><td><strong>PostToolUse hook</strong> that normalizes before the model sees it</td></tr><tr><td>Tell the model the house style</td><td>Prompt / CLAUDE.md (guidance is fine when 100% isn't required)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"tool-design-and-mcp\">Tool design and MCP</h2>\n<ul><li>Descriptions are the <strong>primary selection signal</strong>: purpose, input formats, example queries, edge cases, boundaries vs. similar tools.</li><li>Split generic tools (<code>analyze_document</code> → <code>extract_data_points</code>, <code>summarize_content</code>, <code>verify_claim_against_source</code>); rename overlapping ones.</li><li>4–5 focused tools per agent beat 18; give cross-role agents a <strong>narrow</strong> extra tool for frequent needs.</li><li><code>tool_choice</code>: <code>{\"type\":\"auto\"}</code> may answer in text · <code>{\"type\":\"any\"}</code> must call <em>some</em> tool · <code>{\"type\":\"tool\",\"name\":\"x\"}</code> must call <em>x</em>.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"codeblock\"><div class=\"codeblock-head\"><span>structured MCP tool error</span><button class=\"copy-btn\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></div><pre><code class=\"lang-json\">{\n  \"isError\": true,\n  \"content\": [{ \"type\": \"text\", \"text\": \"{\\\"errorCategory\\\":\\\"transient\\\",\\\"isRetryable\\\":true,\\\"message\\\":\\\"Order service timed out after 5s\\\",\\\"attempted\\\":\\\"lookup_order(ORD-1182)\\\"}\" }]\n}</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Category</th><th>Example</th><th>Agent should</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Transient</td><td>Timeout, 503</td><td>Retry (with backoff)</td></tr><tr><td>Validation</td><td>Bad order ID format</td><td>Fix input, retry</td></tr><tr><td>Business</td><td>Refund exceeds policy</td><td>Explain / escalate, <strong>don't</strong> retry</td></tr><tr><td>Permission</td><td>Not authorized</td><td>Escalate, <strong>don't</strong> retry</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Empty ≠ failed:</strong> \"0 matching orders\" is a successful result; \"database unreachable\" is an error. Never return <code>[]</code> for a failure.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>MCP config</th><th>Location</th><th>Shared?</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Project scope</td><td><code>.mcp.json</code> in the repo root</td><td>Yes — version-controlled</td></tr><tr><td>User scope</td><td><code>~/.claude.json</code></td><td>No — personal / experimental</td></tr><tr><td>Secrets</td><td><code>\"env\": {\"TOKEN\": \"${GITHUB_TOKEN}\"}</code></td><td>Expanded at runtime; never commit tokens</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"built-in-tools\">Built-in tools</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Tool</th><th>Use for</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Grep</strong></td><td>Search file <em>contents</em> (function callers, error strings, imports)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Glob</strong></td><td>Find files by <em>path pattern</em> (<code>**/*.test.tsx</code>)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Read / Write</strong></td><td>Load a file / write a whole file</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Edit</strong></td><td>Targeted change via a <strong>unique</strong> anchor string; if not unique → more context or Read + Write</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Bash</strong></td><td>Run commands, tests, builds</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"claude-code-configuration\">Claude Code configuration</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>What</th><th>Where</th><th>Loaded</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Personal preferences</td><td><code>~/.claude/CLAUDE.md</code></td><td>Always, only for you (not shared)</td></tr><tr><td>Team conventions</td><td><code>CLAUDE.md</code> or <code>.claude/CLAUDE.md</code></td><td>Always, everyone (version-controlled)</td></tr><tr><td>Package/area conventions</td><td><code>&lt;dir&gt;/CLAUDE.md</code></td><td>When working in that directory</td></tr><tr><td>Conventions by file type across dirs</td><td><code>.claude/rules/*.md</code> with <code>paths:</code> globs</td><td>Only when matching files are touched</td></tr><tr><td>Modular includes</td><td><code>@path/to/file.md</code> inside CLAUDE.md</td><td>With the including file</td></tr><tr><td>Team workflow on demand</td><td><code>.claude/skills/&lt;name&gt;/SKILL.md</code> or <code>.claude/commands/*.md</code></td><td>When invoked / relevant</td></tr><tr><td>Personal workflow</td><td><code>~/.claude/skills/</code> or <code>~/.claude/commands/</code></td><td>Only for you</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"codeblock\"><div class=\"codeblock-head\"><span>.claude/rules/testing.md</span><button class=\"copy-btn\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></div><pre><code class=\"lang-markdown\">---\npaths:\n  - \"**/*.test.ts\"\n  - \"**/*.test.tsx\"\n---\nUse Vitest and Testing Library. One behaviour per test. No snapshot tests for logic.</code></pre></div>\n<div class=\"codeblock\"><div class=\"codeblock-head\"><span>.claude/skills/review-api/SKILL.md</span><button class=\"copy-btn\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></div><pre><code class=\"lang-markdown\">---\nname: review-api\ndescription: Review an API route against our REST conventions. Use when asked to review endpoints.\nargument-hint: \"[path to route file]\"\nallowed-tools: Read, Grep, Glob\ncontext: fork\n---\nReview $ARGUMENTS against docs/api-conventions.md. Report only violations, with line numbers.</code></pre></div>\n<p><strong>Plan mode</strong> → large, multi-file, architectural, ambiguous or risky changes. <strong>Direct</strong> → small, clear, single-file changes. <strong>Explore subagent</strong> → verbose discovery without filling the main context.</p>\n<h2 id=\"claude-code-in-ci-cd\">Claude Code in CI/CD</h2>\n<div class=\"codeblock\"><div class=\"codeblock-head\"><span>CI review step</span><button class=\"copy-btn\" type=\"button\">Copy</button></div><pre><code class=\"lang-bash\">claude -p \"Review the diff in this PR against CLAUDE.md. Report only bugs and security issues.\" \\\n  --output-format json --json-schema \"$(cat review-schema.json)\" &gt; review.json</code></pre></div>\n<ul><li><code>-p</code> (<code>--print</code>) = non-interactive; without it the job waits for input.</li><li><code>--output-format json</code> (+ <code>--json-schema</code>) → machine-parseable findings for inline PR comments.</li><li>Put review criteria and test conventions in <strong>CLAUDE.md</strong> so CI gets the same context as developers.</li><li>Review with a <strong>separate instance</strong> from the one that generated the code; pass <strong>previous findings</strong> so re-runs report only new or unresolved issues.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"structured-output-and-extraction\">Structured output and extraction</h2>\n<ul><li>Tool use + JSON schema → no syntax errors. Still validate <strong>semantics</strong> (totals add up, dates plausible).</li><li>Optional / nullable fields where the source may lack data; enums with <code>\"other\"</code> + detail string and <code>\"unclear\"</code>.</li><li>Few-shot: 2–4 examples of <strong>ambiguous</strong> cases, showing the reasoning; vary document formats.</li><li><strong>Retry with feedback:</strong> send the document, the failed output and the specific validation error. Retries can't recover data that isn't in the source.</li><li>Self-check fields: <code>calculated_total</code> vs <code>stated_total</code>, <code>conflict_detected</code>.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"message-batches-api\">Message Batches API</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Fact</th><th>Value</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Cost</td><td><strong>50%</strong> of standard pricing</td></tr><tr><td>Processing window</td><td>Up to <strong>24 hours</strong>; no latency SLA</td></tr><tr><td>Correlation</td><td><code>custom_id</code> per request — results may come back in any order</td></tr><tr><td>Failures</td><td>Resubmit <strong>only</strong> failed <code>custom_id</code>s (fix e.g. oversized documents first)</td></tr><tr><td>Not suited for</td><td>Blocking workflows (pre-merge checks), multi-turn tool loops within one request</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"reliability-and-context\">Reliability and context</h2>\n<ul><li><strong>Case facts block</strong>: amounts, dates, order IDs, commitments — kept verbatim outside the summarized history.</li><li><strong>Lost in the middle</strong>: put key findings at the start; use section headers in long inputs.</li><li><strong>Trim tool output</strong> to the fields the task needs before it accumulates.</li><li><strong>Escalate</strong> on: explicit human request, policy gap/exception, no progress. <strong>Not</strong> on sentiment or the model's self-rated confidence alone.</li><li><strong>Ambiguous match</strong> (two customers named J. Smith) → ask for an identifier; never guess.</li><li><strong>Human review</strong>: measure accuracy per document type and field; stratified random sampling; calibrate confidence thresholds on a labeled set.</li><li><strong>Provenance</strong>: claim → source (URL/doc, excerpt, date). Conflicting figures → keep both, attributed; explain differing dates/methods.</li></ul>","headings":[{"id":"the-ten-decision-rules","text":"The ten decision rules"},{"id":"agentic-loop","text":"Agentic loop"},{"id":"multi-agent-orchestration","text":"Multi-agent orchestration"},{"id":"enforcement-and-hooks","text":"Enforcement and hooks"},{"id":"tool-design-and-mcp","text":"Tool design and MCP"},{"id":"built-in-tools","text":"Built-in tools"},{"id":"claude-code-configuration","text":"Claude Code configuration"},{"id":"claude-code-in-ci-cd","text":"Claude Code in CI/CD"},{"id":"structured-output-and-extraction","text":"Structured output and extraction"},{"id":"message-batches-api","text":"Message Batches API"},{"id":"reliability-and-context","text":"Reliability and context"}]},"study-plan":{"title":"Study Plan","summary":"Weighted schedules for passing CCA-F — a 3-week plan (≈ 1.5 h per day) and a 10-day intensive plan — plus a readiness checklist.","html":"<h2 id=\"how-the-time-is-allocated\">How the time is allocated</h2>\n<p>The plan follows the domain weights. Domain 1 (27%) gets more than a quarter of the study time, and because its patterns — loops, delegation, enforcement — reappear in every scenario, it comes first.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Weight</th><th>Share of study time</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>D1 Agentic Architecture &amp; Orchestration</td><td>27%</td><td>~28%</td></tr><tr><td>D3 Claude Code Configuration &amp; Workflows</td><td>20%</td><td>~19%</td></tr><tr><td>D4 Prompt Engineering &amp; Structured Output</td><td>20%</td><td>~20%</td></tr><tr><td>D2 Tool Design &amp; MCP Integration</td><td>18%</td><td>~18%</td></tr><tr><td>D5 Context Management &amp; Reliability</td><td>15%</td><td>~15%</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"daily-routine-every-plan\">Daily routine (every plan)</h2>\n<ul><li class=\"task\"><input type=\"checkbox\" > 10 minutes of <strong>flashcards</strong> (due cards) — patterns, anti-patterns and configuration facts.</li><li class=\"task\"><input type=\"checkbox\" > Read the day's lessons and answer the embedded scenario questions.</li><li class=\"task\"><input type=\"checkbox\" > <strong>Drill</strong> 10–15 questions for the day's task statements (\"Unseen first\").</li><li class=\"task\"><input type=\"checkbox\" > Do at least part of one <strong>build exercise</strong>.</li><li class=\"task\"><input type=\"checkbox\" > Re-drill yesterday's <strong>missed questions</strong> and write down <em>why</em> the distractor was attractive.</li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"3-week-plan-1-5-hours-per-day\">3-week plan (≈ 1.5 hours per day)</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Day</th><th>Focus</th><th>Tasks</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>Baseline</td><td>Read the <a href=\"#/guide\">Exam Guide</a> and the six scenarios; take the <a href=\"#/diagnostic\">Diagnostic</a></td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>D1</td><td>1.1 Agentic Loops — build the stop_reason loop exercise</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>D1</td><td>1.2 Multi-Agent Orchestration; 1.3 Subagent Invocation &amp; Context Passing</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>D1</td><td>1.4 Workflow Enforcement &amp; Handoff; 1.5 Agent SDK Hooks — build the refund gate</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>D1</td><td>1.6 Task Decomposition; 1.7 Sessions; drill Domain 1 (25 q)</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>D2</td><td>2.1 Tool Interface Design; 2.2 Structured Error Responses</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>D2</td><td>2.3 Tool Distribution &amp; tool_choice; 2.4 MCP Integration; 2.5 Built-in Tools; drill Domain 2</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>Checkpoint</td><td><a href=\"#/mock\">Quick mock</a> (30 q); review every miss</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>D3</td><td>3.1 CLAUDE.md Hierarchy; 3.2 Commands &amp; Skills — configure a sample repo</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>D3</td><td>3.3 Path-Specific Rules; 3.4 Plan Mode vs Direct Execution</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>D3</td><td>3.5 Iterative Refinement; 3.6 CI/CD Integration; drill Domain 3</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>D4</td><td>4.1 Explicit Criteria; 4.2 Few-Shot Prompting</td></tr><tr><td>13</td><td>D4</td><td>4.3 Structured Output; 4.4 Validation &amp; Retry — build the extraction exercise</td></tr><tr><td>14</td><td>D4</td><td>4.5 Batch Processing; 4.6 Multi-Pass Review; drill Domain 4</td></tr><tr><td>15</td><td>D5</td><td>5.1 Preserving Context; 5.2 Escalation; 5.3 Error Propagation</td></tr><tr><td>16</td><td>D5</td><td>5.4 Large Codebases; 5.5 Human Review; 5.6 Provenance; drill Domain 5</td></tr><tr><td>17</td><td>Mock 1</td><td>Full <a href=\"#/mock\">Mock Exam</a>; review by scenario</td></tr><tr><td>18</td><td>Repair</td><td>Re-read lessons for your three weakest task statements; adaptive drill</td></tr><tr><td>19</td><td>Mock 2</td><td>Second full mock (different scenario draw)</td></tr><tr><td>20</td><td>Repair</td><td>Missed-questions drill; <a href=\"#/reference\">Quick Reference</a> end to end</td></tr><tr><td>21</td><td>Final</td><td>Flashcards (all), Anthropic Academy practice exam, rest</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"10-day-intensive-plan-3-hours-per-day\">10-day intensive plan (≈ 3 hours per day)</h2>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Day</th><th>Focus</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>Exam Guide, Diagnostic, 1.1–1.3</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>1.4–1.7; drill Domain 1</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>2.1–2.5; drill Domain 2</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>3.1–3.3; configure a sample repository</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>3.4–3.6; drill Domain 3</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>4.1–4.3</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>4.4–4.6; drill Domain 4</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>5.1–5.6; drill Domain 5</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>Full mock; repair weakest areas</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>Second full mock; Quick Reference; flashcards</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"readiness-checklist\">Readiness checklist</h2>\n<ul><li class=\"task\"><input type=\"checkbox\" > I can explain why <code>stop_reason</code> — not text content or an iteration counter — drives the agent loop.</li><li class=\"task\"><input type=\"checkbox\" > I can tell when a requirement needs a hook or prerequisite gate instead of a prompt instruction.</li><li class=\"task\"><input type=\"checkbox\" > I know what a subagent does and does not inherit, and how to pass context to it.</li><li class=\"task\"><input type=\"checkbox\" > I can design a structured MCP error (category, retryable, description) and distinguish \"no results\" from \"lookup failed\".</li><li class=\"task\"><input type=\"checkbox\" > I can place CLAUDE.md content, rules, skills and commands at the right scope (user / project / directory / path).</li><li class=\"task\"><input type=\"checkbox\" > I know when to use plan mode, the Explore subagent and direct execution.</li><li class=\"task\"><input type=\"checkbox\" > I can configure Claude Code for CI with <code>-p</code>, JSON output and an independent reviewer.</li><li class=\"task\"><input type=\"checkbox\" > I can make extraction reliable: schema via tool use, <code>tool_choice</code>, nullable fields, validation–retry, few-shot.</li><li class=\"task\"><input type=\"checkbox\" > I know which workloads suit the Message Batches API and which do not.</li><li class=\"task\"><input type=\"checkbox\" > I can design escalation triggers, error propagation, human-review sampling and provenance tracking.</li><li class=\"task\"><input type=\"checkbox\" > I score <strong>800+ on two full mocks</strong> with different scenario draws.</li></ul>","headings":[{"id":"how-the-time-is-allocated","text":"How the time is allocated"},{"id":"daily-routine-every-plan","text":"Daily routine (every plan)"},{"id":"3-week-plan-1-5-hours-per-day","text":"3-week plan (≈ 1.5 hours per day)"},{"id":"10-day-intensive-plan-3-hours-per-day","text":"10-day intensive plan (≈ 3 hours per day)"},{"id":"readiness-checklist","text":"Readiness checklist"}]}},"built":"2026-10-29"};
